where $\alpha$ is the cycle structure of $\sigma$ written in weakly increasing order.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:13:22.511122+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b65a56aa54d390b65440b64c5a88443289d68fbfa8c5ce62dcf363ec44648675","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemma technical dist of M of arXiv:2505.15940, 'Degrees of Freedom for Critical Random 2-SAT'] Let $\Phi\sim F_2(n,m)$ and $\mathcal{L}=[n]\backslash[n-f]$. If $M_k$ is the random variable denoting the number of clauses in $\Phi_k:=G_k(\Phi,f)$ for $k\in\{1,2\}$, and $M_0$ and $M_\star$ are the number of unsatisfied- and satisfied clauses, respectively, then
    $$(M_k)_{k\in K}=(M_0,\,M_1,\,M_2,\,M_\star)\sim\text{Multinomial}\big(m,\;{p}(n,f)\big),$$
    where ${p}=(p_k)_{k\in K}$ and 
    \begin{equation*}
        \begin{aligned}
            p_0(n,f)= \frac{f(f-1)}{4n(n-1)},&\quad p_1(n,f)=\frac{(n-f)f}{n(n-1)},\\
            p_2(n,f)=\frac{(n-f)(n-f-1)}{n(n-1)},&\quad p_\star(n,f)=\frac{(n-\frac{f}{4}-\frac{3}{4})f}{n(n-1)}.
        \end{aligned}
    \end{equation*}
    Furthermore
    $$\Phi_k|(M_k)_{k\in K}\sim F_k(n-f,\;M_k),\quad (k\in\{1,2\}),$$
    and $\Phi_1$ and $\Phi_2$ are conditionally independent given $(M_k)_{k\in K}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:45:32.064184+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b65a7dc6f0671fcf71486e3966daf42bca2be1331e80463b76ec1d4f2e10414a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma l.time_der_sol of arXiv:2411.14105, 'Simultaneous replica-symmetry breaking \\ for vector spin glasses'] Let $(L,\alpha)$ be a decomposition defined on $[0,T]$ satisfying that $L$ is smooth and $\alpha$ is continuous and strictly increasing. Let $\Phi=\Phi_{\mu,L,\alpha}$ be the associated Parisi PDE solution. Then, $\Phi$ is differentiable in $t$ at every $t\in[0,T]\times \mathbb{R}^{D}$ with
\begin{align*}
	\partial_t \Phi(t,x) = - \accentset{\mbox{\large\bfseries .}}{L}(t)\cdot \left(\mathbb{E} \left\langle \sigma\sigma^\intercal\right\rangle_{\mu,\pi_t,x,-L(t)} - \mathbb{E} \left\langle \sigma\sigma'^\intercal \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{h}\wedge \mathbf{h}' > \alpha(t)}\right\rangle_{\mu,\pi_t,x,-L(t)}\right)
