{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:42:47.764526+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b681292d4bed210ba9d0512740eecba31202fa8cc30e4fbc87523157ee638523","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 1 (Weak formulation) of arXiv:2604.12797, 'A free boundary problem for the mean-field limit of diffusing particles with nonlinear bou'] Let $(v,A)$ be such that (i) $t\mapsto A_t$ is continuous in $t\geq 0$, (ii) $v\geq 0$, (iii)  $(t,x) \mapsto v(t,x)$ is continuous jointly in $t>0$ and $x\geq A_t$, and (iv) $v(t,\cdot)\in L^1([A_t,\infty))$ and $v(\cdot,A_{\cdot})\in L^1([0,t])$ for all $t>0$. Let also $A_0=a_0$ and $v(t,x)\dx \rightarrow P_0$ as $t\downarrow 0$ in the topology of weak convergence of measures. Then, we say that $(v,A)$ is a weak solution of the free boundary problem \eqref{eq:classic_PDE}--\eqref{eq:classic_A_I_rate} with initial condition $(P_0,a_0)$ if 
\begin{align}
	&\int_{A_t}^\infty \!\! \phi(x)v(t,x)\dx - 	\int_{a_0}^\infty \!\! \phi(x) \di P_0(x) = \int_0^t\! \int_{A_s}^\infty \!\! \partial_{xx}\phi(x)\frac{1}{2}\sigma(s,x)^2v(s,x)\dx \ds \nonumber \\
	&+ \int_0^t \!\int_{A_s}^\infty \!\!\partial_{x}\phi(x)b(s,x)v(s,x) \dx \ds + \int_0^t  \bigl( \tfrac{1}{2} \partial_x\phi(A_s)-\gamma(s,C_s)\phi(A_s)\bigr)\sigma(s,A_s)^2v(s,A_s) \ds ,
\end{align}
for all $t\geq 0$ and all $\phi \in C^2_b(\R)$, with
\begin{equation}
	A_t=a_0 + \alpha \! \displaystyle \int_{t-\bar{d}}^{t}\varrho(t-s)I_{s} \ds, \;\;
	C_t = \!\int_{t-\bar{d}}^{t}\varrho(t-s)(I_{s}-I_{s-\bar{d}}) \ds, \;\;  I_t=1-\!\int_{A_t}^\infty \!\!\! v(t,x) \dx,
\end{equation}
for all $t>0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:27:11.450766+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b683554247465abb5d674b91a0acedd52df791d66053b3549d1084a26ce2f1c4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition p:averaging set multivariate trigonometric polynomial degree 1 of arXiv:2605.22803, 'Persistence of asymptotic variance under transport:\\ from hyperfluctuation to stealthy hy'] Suppose that $l,n\in\N$, and that $p_1,...,p_d$ are real-valued trigonometric polynomials of degrees $m^{(1)},...m^{(d)}\in\{0,1\}^l$. Then
    \begin{equation}
        \left\{\begin{pmatrix}
            p_1(2\pi j_1/n+u_1, ..., 2\pi j_l/n+u_l)\\
            \vdots\\
            p_d(2\pi j_1/n+u_1, ..., 2\pi j_l/n+u_l)
        \end{pmatrix}:j\in\{1,...,n\}^l\right\}
    \end{equation}
    is an averaging set of $(p_1,...,p_d)$ of degree $n-1$ for all $u\in\R^l$, and not of any higher degree for almost all $u\in\R^l$ if at least one trigonometric polynomial is not constant.

    \begin{proof}
        An application of Theorem~(t:averaging set multivariate trigonometric polynomial of the paper) directly yields that the set in~(equation e:averaging set degree 01 of the paper) is an averaging set of $(p_1,...,p_d)$ of degree $m-1$.
        Now, we have to show that this does not hold for any higher degree.
        In the proof of the previous Theorem~(t:averaging set multivariate trigonometric polynomial of the paper), we saw that the problem factorizes for multivariate monomials. Here, $p_k^n$ also always contains a monomial of the form $e^{im^{(k)}_1nx_1+...+m^{(k)}_lnx_l}$ for \linebreak $k\in\{1,..,d\}$. If $p_k$ is not constant, then $m^{(k)}_j=1$ for some $j\in\{1,...,l\}$. Without loss of generality, we assume $m^{(1)}_1=1$. Then,
        \begin{equation*}
            \frac{1}{(2\pi)^l}\int_{[0,2\pi)^l} e^{im^{(k)}_1nx_1+...+m^{(k)}_lnx_l}\, dx = \prod_{k=1}^l\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi} e^{im_knx}\, dx = 0,
        \end{equation*}
        and 
        \begin{equation*}
            \frac{1}{n^l}\sum_{j\in\{1,...,n\}^k} e^{im^{(1)}_1n(2\pi j_1/n+u_1)+...+im^{(1)}_ln(2\pi j_l/n+u_l)} = \prod_{k=1}^l \frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n} e^{2\pi im^{(1)}_k j+im^{(1)}_knu_k},\quad u\in\R^l.
        \end{equation*}
        On the RHS of the second equation, the first factor is equal to $e^{inu_1 }$, and the others are either equal to $1$ or $e^{inu_k }$. In total, the product is not $0$ for almost all $u\in\R^l$, which concludes the proof.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

