    \left\|v\right\|_{L^\mu_t((0, T); H^{s,r}_x(\mathbb{R}_+))}\lesssim (1+e^{cT'}(T')^{\frac12+\frac 1 \mu})\sum_{b=0}^1\|\psi_b\|_{H_t^{\frac{s+1-b}{3}}},
\end{equation} 
where $c$ and the constant of the inequality may depend only on the fixed parameters such as $s,\alpha,\beta$, and $\delta$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:37.351635+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b6b5242e8e2e52a1a31a8dc5bdf88c0af4c49e1087386526948f358a0f7b0235","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 3 of arXiv:2606.17472, '2606.17472'] Let $(A_t, t\ge 0)$ be a positive continuous additive functional of $X$, and let $\nu$ be its Revuz measure. Assume that $\nu$ is a finite measure and has compact support. Then there exists a martingale $M^\nu$ such that
\begin{align}

  \int_\R h(X_t-y)\nu(dy)=\int_\R h(x-y)\nu(dy)+M_t^\nu +A_t,\qquad t\geq 0.
\end{align}
In particular, it holds that
\begin{align}

\varphi_B(X_t)=\varphi_B(x)+M_t^{\mu_B}+\int_B L_t^y \mu_B(dy),\qquad t\geq 0.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:50:44.876524+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b6b6180b6ade94702d2f60208f8e29ba00ca8cb47737c0481d212b0796c44f83","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 59 (All-order singular lattice profiles) of arXiv:2607.16724, 'Algebraic Transfer for Operator-Valued Gaussian Chaoses: Oriented Schatten Profiles and Si'] Let $\Gamma=\mathbb Z^D$, $m\ge2$, and
\[
 \frac{(m-1)D}{2m}<\sigma\le\frac D2,
 \qquad c_x=\langle x\rangle^{-\sigma}.
\]
Put
\[
 \beta_m=m\left(\frac D2-\sigma\right),\qquad
 \gamma_m=2m\sigma-(m-1)D,
\]
and
\begin{equation}

 \Omega_{m,\sigma}(R)=
 \begin{cases}
  R^{\beta_m},&\sigma<D/2,\\
  \log(e+R)^{m/2},&\sigma=D/2.
 \end{cases}
\end{equation}
For $R_+=\max\{L,Q\}$ and $R_-=\min\{L,Q\}$,
\begin{equation}

 \Prof_{m,r}(K_{m,N}^{L,Q}(c))
 \le C\Omega_{m,\sigma}(R_+)R_-^{D/r},
 \qquad2\le r\le\infty.
\end{equation}
If $N\ge M$ and $\sigma<D/2$, then
\begin{align}

 &\Prof_{m,r}(K_{m,N}^{L,Q}(c)-K_{m,M}^{L,Q}(c))\notag\\
 &\quad\le C\Omega_{m,\sigma}(R_+)R_-^{D/r}
 \min\{1,(R_+/M)^{\gamma_m/2}\}.
\end{align}
At $\sigma=D/2$, for every $0<\vartheta<D$ the right side is replaced by
\begin{equation}

 C_\vartheta\Omega_{m,D/2}(R_+)R_-^{D/r}
 \min\{1,(R_+/M)^{\vartheta/2}\}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:11:11.705419+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b6b61fa6474ec11e167624d6143ca4c96b1b09123bd022bbef602824cb6cbb53","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:sep of arXiv:2609.00672, 'Deciding superellipticity and computing the Weierstrass normal form'] Let $t: C\to\mathbb P^1$ be cyclic of degree $n$ with branch data $(l_1,\dots,l_r)$, the point at infinity included when it is a branch point. Then there is a birational map from $C$ to a curve $v^n=h(u)$ with $k(u)=k(t)$ and $h\in k[u]$ separable if and only if there is $c\in(\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z)^\times$ such that

\begin{equation}
c\,l_j\equiv 1\pmod n\quad\text{for every $j$ with at most one exception.}
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:37:08.313301+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b6b7eba1e6adbd1559d1f5b738e2c42b1d2d68057fa6d07c6b532ad516d1c037","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma pi-t of arXiv:2608.29507, 'Denoising as Projection: Constrained Optimization with Gradient-Guided Diffusion'] Under Assumption~(assump:linear of the paper), the Stein denoising operator $\widehat\pi_t$ defined in (equation eq:pi_t of the paper) satisfies
