{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:54:28.027080+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b6e4e5f2c280db88551f7cb91c1eb71954ba7adb0809a27fa099558c2175766e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:m-OGP of arXiv:2608.09465, 'Balancing fractional Brownian motion'] Let $\{f^{(i)}_1, \ldots, f^{(i)}_n\}_{i=0}^m$ denote $(m+1)$ independent copies of the set of $n$ independent sample paths of fBm on $[0, 1]$ with Hurst exponent $H$. For any $\tau_i\in [0, \pi/2]$, denote the interpolation $g^{(i)}_j(t; \tau_i) = \cos(\tau_i)f_j^{(0)}(t) + \sin(\tau_i)f_j^{(i)}(t)$ for $i=1,\ldots, m$ and $j = 1,\ldots, n$. Fixing $0<\eta_1<\eta_2<1$, let $\mathcal S(\eta_1, \eta_2, m, a_n, \mathcal I)$ denote the set of $m$-tuples $\{\sigma^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^m$ of $\{\pm 1\}^n$ satisfying the following conditions:  
\begin{itemize}
\item (Near-optimality condition) There exist $\tau_1, \ldots, \tau_m\in\mathcal I\subseteq [0, \pi/2]$ such that $\sigma^{(i)}$ balances the set $\{g^{(i)}_1(t; \tau_i), \ldots, g^{(i)}_n(t; \tau_i)\}$ up to order $a_n$, i.e., 
\begin{align*}
\left\|\sum_{j=1}^n\sigma^{(i)}_jg^{(i)}_j(\cdot; \tau_i)\right\|_\infty\leq a_n. 
\end{align*}
\item (Pairwise overlap condition) $\{\sigma^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^m$ have intermediate correlations within $[\eta_1, \eta_2]$: 
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{n}\left|\sum_{\ell=1}^n\sigma^{(i)}_\ell\sigma^{(j)}_\ell\right|\in [\eta_1, \eta_2], \quad\quad \forall i\neq j.  
\end{align*} 
\end{itemize}
We say that the $m$-OGP with parameters $(\eta_1, \eta_2, m, a_n, \mathcal I)$ holds if $\mathbb{P}\{\mathcal S(\eta_1, \eta_2, m, a_n, \mathcal I)\neq\emptyset\} = \mathcal{O}(e^{-cn})$ for some absolute constant $c>0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:40.334200+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b6e518382021585f8f96b7946c3e5454c9f2b147d6f5d4a78ba01801be90ee53","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 13 of arXiv:2511.06196, 'Central limit theorem for high temperature spin models via martingale embedding'] Let \(A \subset \{1,\ldots,n\}\) and choose arbitrary \(\epsilon > 0\).
