{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:44:17.355177+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b70df2692ae7f699483a81cf376e70b70a7a19cfe1c6e1ed309679e4a8a7aacd","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:def-ftb of arXiv:2608.23554, 'Provably adaptive sampling with uniform and remasking \\ discrete diffusion models'] Let \(t \geq 0\) and \(b \in \cV\). Define \(\cF: \bR_{\geq 0} \times \cV \to [0, 1]\) as follows:
    \begin{equation*}
    \cF(t, b) \coloneqq \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d} t} \Pr(X_t^i = b) + \Pr(X_t^i = b) = \sum_{a \in \cV} \cQ(a, b) \Pr(X_t^i = a).
    \end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:04:30.557329+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b70effab5e04a5d005c93888f94c60c97589f4964afbd76e0d1c739a9e3a04fd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Thm: prob of A_alpha of arXiv:2411.10296, 'Parking on a random rooted binary tree'] Let $T_\infty$ be a BGW(2,1/2) tree, conditioned on non-extinction, which is constructible as in Subsection 2.1. Assume that an independent Po$(\alpha)$ number of cars arrive at each of the vertices of $T_\infty$. Let $A_\alpha$ be the event that all of the arriving cars can park on the $T_\infty$. Then
    \[\mathds{P}(A_\alpha) = \begin{cases}
        e^{\alpha/2} \sqrt{\frac{\alpha^2 - 4\alpha + 2}{2(1-\alpha)}} & 0 \leq \alpha \leq 2-\sqrt{2} \\
        0 & \alpha > 2-\sqrt{2}.
    \end{cases} \] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:48:17.123580+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b7105f1bd8b6f1e004b70043fd7ff5dc848c47a5ba320c025bcdca838d7c81ca","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 10 of arXiv:2605.27713, 'Lower path regularity in all dimensions'] Suppose that the process $X=(X_t)_{t\in [0,T]}$ satisfies Assumption \ref{a1} (i), let $q'\geq \max(Hd,1)$ and $\gamma<\frac{d}{q'}$. Then for any $0 < u < v\leq T$ and any $n \geq 1$, we have
		\begin{multline}
			\int_{[u, v]^n} \Big(\int_{(\mathbb{R}^{d})^n}  \prod_{k=1}^n\psi_\gamma(\Vert \xi_k\Vert)^{q'}\Big\lvert \mathbb{E} \exp \Big( i \sum_{j = 1}^n \langle \xi_j, X_{t_j}\rangle \Big) \Big\rvert^{q'} d\xi\Big)^{\frac{1}{q'}} dt  \\
			\leq c_7^n n^{\left(H\theta  + \frac{Hd}{q'}\right)n}(v-u)^{\left(1-\frac{Hd}{q'}\right)n}[\log(e+(v-u))]^{2 n d}
		\end{multline} 
		with a constant $c_7 > 0$ depending only on $T$, $d$, $\gamma$, $c_0$, $H$ and $q'$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:47:53.342801+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b7118d1e44c22c5d7f1feaa54e3d7c1fa20ca5aafc84a74fc2f6824ec230e8d5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 18 of arXiv:2607.14948, 'Graph alignment in sparse inhomogeneous models via self-overlap'] \begin{enumerate}
        \item Let $\eps > 0$. Then, the subgraph $K[{V_{\leq(1-\eps)}}]$ is a union of connected components of $K$, each of which is a tree supported on a vertex set of size at most $\frac{1}{\eps}$.
        \item Let $k \geq 2$, and denote by $\Ker_k (K)$ the set of vertices inside the $k$-core of $K$. Then, $V_{\geq k} \subseteq \Ker_k(K) \subseteq V_{\geq \frac{k}{2}}$.

        \item Let $\rho_m = \max_{X \subseteq V} \dfrac{|K[X]|}{|X|}$. Then, $\rho_m = \max_{v \in V} w_K(v)$, and the set $X = V_{\geq \rho_m}$ maximises $\dfrac{|K[X]|}{|X|}$.

    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:06:06.300500+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b7121feb2131b3b09b0045c44f0f653774058df4fa39e4b44df758b283ede324","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:interchange_sups of arXiv:2605.26048, 'Classification of the eternal solutions and multiple coalescing shocks in the KPZ fixed po'] Let $(f_\alpha)_{\alpha \in \mathcal I}$ be any indexed collection of functions $f_\alpha:\mathbb{R} \to [-\infty,\infty)$, and define the function $f:\mathbb{R} \to [-\infty,\infty]$ by
    \[
    f(y) = \sup_{\alpha \in I} f_\alpha(y).
    \]
    Then, for all $t > s$ and $x \in \mathbb{R}$, we have 
    \begin{equation} 
    \mathfrak{h}_t(x;f,s) = \sup_{\alpha \in \mathcal I} \mathfrak{h}_t(x;f_\alpha,s).
    \end{equation}
    Consequently, if $\varphi:\mathfrak{X} \to [-\infty,\infty)$ is such that $b(x,t) := (W \star \varphi)(x,t) < \infty$ for all $(x,t) \in \mathbb{R}^2$, then $b$ is an eternal solution.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:03:35.927073+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b71852873596c877e4629e0bb264fcaea50cfdc936f955a77ca8345c1c4acc0b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor relative-absolute of arXiv:2608.26876, 'Local Bousfield classes via homological support'] Let $\cat T\to \LAT$ be a smashing localization. Then:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item $\LAT$ has the relative h-detection property if and only if it has the absolute h-detection property.
		\item $\LAT$ has the relative h-codetection property if and only if it has the absolute h-codetection property.
