Moreover, there exist $\zeta_1^\pm$ depending only on $\zeta_2$ and the constants in the
assumptions such that the choice of $\zeta_1$ can be made to obey $0<\zeta_1^-<\zeta_1<\zeta_1^+<\zeta_2$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:28:53.670959+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b7b8ec1c1adf43c96774d71a2002936821375fd3814659df2d11f7b84aaae284","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 (transition density) of arXiv:2605.13648, 'Sticky CIR process with potential: invariant measure and exact sampling'] On $(0,\infty)$, the Lebesgue density $f$ of the CIR process defined by \cref{eq-CIR4} solves
  \[
  \partial_t f = -\partial_u\!\left(\left(\tfrac{\delta-1}{\beta u}
    -\lambda u\right)f\right) + \tfrac{1}{\beta}\partial_{uu}f.
  \]
  If $p$ denotes the density with respect to the speed measure $m$,
  then at $u=0$ the sticky (Wentzell) boundary condition holds
  \begin{equation}

    \partial_s p(t,0^+) = \tfrac{1}{\mu}\,\partial_t p(t,0),
  \end{equation}
  where $\partial_s = (1/s')\partial_u$ is the scale derivative.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:33:21.473979+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b7ba5adad4daf82cc1ff4f36dd1294ddd2973ad82d07ca0f38b957674bb077c1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:sharp-nonlinear-constant of arXiv:2606.21006, 'Law of the Iterated Logarithm for Weighted Sums of Functionals of Long-Memory Gaussian Seq'] Under the assumptions of Theorem~(thm:functional-nonlinear-lil of the paper),
\[
    \limsup_{n\to\infty}
    \frac{|S_n|}
    {A_n(2\log\log n)^{m/2}}
    =
    \Lambda_m
    \qquad\text{\rm a.s.},
\]
where the deterministic constant is given by the variational formula
\[

    \Lambda_m
    =
    \sup_{\|\xi\|_{\mathfrak H_{\mathbb R}}\le1}
    \left|
        \big\langle Q_1,\xi^{\otimes m}
        \big\rangle_{\mathfrak H_{\mathbb R}^{\otimes m}}
    \right|.
\text{      Moreover, }
    0<L_{m,\alpha,d} 
    \le
    \Lambda_m
    \le
    \frac{1}{\sqrt{m!}},
\]
where
\[
    L_{m,\alpha,d}
    :=
    \left(\frac{2-\alpha}{2(1-\alpha)}\right)^{m/2}
    \frac{
        \displaystyle
        \sum_{k=0}^{m}
        \binom{m}{k}
        \mathrm B\!\left(
            1-d+(1-\alpha)k,\,
            1+(1-\alpha)(m-k)
        \right)
    }
    {
        \sqrt{m!\,I_{m,\alpha,d}}
    },
\]
and
\[
    I_{m,\alpha,d}
    =
    \frac{
        2\,\mathrm B(1-d,\,1-\alpha m)
    }
    {
        2-2d-\alpha m
    }.
\]
Here \(\mathrm B(\cdot,\cdot)\) denotes the beta function. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:35:05.830242+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b7bc2800f6b5e2b1afd37d2ef5b55f96cb2dda9bf9dcc05c1bf6c24fbebf38f2","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def-MO-MVE of arXiv:2606.24881, 'Queues with Correlated Service Times --- the $M/M_D/c$ Model'] For positive integer $c$, a random vector $\mathbf{X} = (X_1, \ldots, X_c) \in \mathbb{R}_+^c$ follows MO-MVED, denoted by $H$,  if its survival function $\bar{H}$ is given by
\begin{equation}
\bar{H}(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbb{P}(X_1 > x_1, \ldots, X_c > x_c) = \exp\left( - \sum_{\forall A \in \mathcal{P}} \mu_A \max_{i \in A} x_i \right),
\end{equation}
where
$\mathcal{P} = 2^{\{1,\ldots,c\}} \setminus \emptyset$ is the set of all nonempty subsets of $\{1,\ldots,c\}$ (its cardinality is $2^c-1$), and $\mu_A \geq 0$ is the rate parameter associated with the shock that simultaneously affects all components in $A$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:48:17.121412+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b7bff353025a771e0c0dc3506bd9a7ff9bf3c486a2d1a481779dfa95dfada4db","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 20 of arXiv:2605.27222, '2605.27222'] For all $\phi\in C_c^\infty(I)$ and every $k\ge1$,
\begin{align}
c_k(G_\phi)
&=
-\frac{2}{\pi k}\int_I \phi(E)\sin\big(k\alpha(E)\big)\,dE,

\\
&=
-\frac{1}{\pi k}\int_I \phi(E)U_{k-1}(E/2)\sqrt{4-E^2}\,dE,

\end{align}
where $\alpha(E)=\arccos(E/2)$ and $U_{k-1}$ is the $(k-1)$st Chebyshev polynomial of the second kind. Further, the sequence $(c_k(G_\phi))_{k\ge1}$ decays faster than any negative power of $k$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.304132+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b7c08080668ce3d5891554e927692c418fa5676b8920da77a4923af43bf2c3f3","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop procov of arXiv:2411.14766, 'Axis-Driven Random Walks on $\Z^2$ \\ (transient cases)'] \begin{align}
