{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:31:49.833965+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b7dd7e7cbb41311dfda47e5f40992f798b183e4a704467aada58e4e455a91f79","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2506.12632, 'Point process convergence of extremes in $K$-symmetric exclusion'] Suppose that $f_1, f_2, g: \Z^n \to \R$ are nonnegative and bounded, where (i) $f_1$ is symmetric and positive definite, (ii) $f_2 \le f_1$ on $\Omega_n^K$, and (iii) $f_2 = f_1$ on $\Omega_n^1 = \Z^{(n)}$. Then for any $t \ge 0$, 
