{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:57:30.962984+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b7eca55a875aac0c9e436f746f40dcfca9d85905651f0a909245958ee98c1774","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 19 of arXiv:2608.12291, 'When should one stop the most exciting game?\\ {\tiny Sequential Inference for win-marting'] Let $[A,B]\subset(0,1)$ and $T_1<\infty$ be given. For any $\varepsilon>0$ such that $[A-\varepsilon,B+\varepsilon]\subset(0,1)$, there exists a $\delta>0$ such that for all $x,y\in[A,B]$ with $|x-y|<\delta$ it holds that
