{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:30:36.745988+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b894c7eef40169d7d8df62569fa8eee6d397b0fd2272911fbb1716f4a49c59bb","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 8 of arXiv:2607.08632, 'Uncountably many extremal Series--Sinai states for the Ising model on Lobachevsky lattices'] [Constants of quasi-geodicity]

    Let us suppose that \(\tan(\pi/p) < \cos(\pi/q)\).
    There exist two constants \(k_{q,p} \ge 1\) and \(c_{q,p} \ge 0\) such that for all \(\displaystyle\widehat\gamma = \bigcup_{i\in\mathbb{Z}} [u_i, u_{i+1}] \in \widehat{\Gamma}_\mathrm{red}(p,q)\) and \(f : \mathbb{R} \to \Hyp{2}\) the parametrisation of \(\widehat\gamma\) such that for all \(i \in \mathbb Z\), \(f(i\ell_{q, p}) = u_i\) and \(f\) is parametrised by arc length on \([i\ell_{q, p}, (i+1)\ell_{q, p}]\), we have
    \begin{equation}

        \forall s, t\in\mathbb R,
        \quad
        \frac{\abs{t - s}}{k_{q,p}} - c_{q,p}
        \le
        \hyperbolicDistance{f(s)}{f(t)}
        \le
        \abs{t - s}
    \end{equation}
    In particular, \(f\) is a complete \((k_{q,p}, c_{q,p})\)-quasi-geodesic.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:59:45.017255+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b8961dbb136fb2509383b3436d8d1ff033b7cd2a4ad33307cbe70e713ea03b41","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:geom-intro of arXiv:2605.15545, 'Crossover from subcritical to critical decay: \\ random walk, self-avoiding walk, percolat'] Let $d\ge 1$.
Suppose that Assumptions~(ass:Omega of the paper) and~(ass:J of the paper)
hold, but without requiring that $h^{(\mu)},J^{(\mu)}$ satisfy
(equation eq:Q_d-1 of the paper).
Then the set $\overline \Omega$ is strictly convex, and every $\mu \in \overline \Omega$ obeys $\abs \mu^2 \le M/K_{\mathrm{IR}}$.
For each $\mu \in \overline\Omega$, we have
\begin{equation} 
    \sum_{y\in {\mathbb Z^d}} J ^{(\mu)}(y) \le 1
    \quad \text{with equality if and only if $\mu\in\partial\Omega$.}
\end{equation}
For each nonzero $ x \in {\mathbb R^d}$,
there is a unique vector ${\mu_{\hat x}} \in \partial \Omega$, depending only on the direction $\hat x = x / \abs x$ of $x$, such that
\begin{equation}

\mu_{\hat x}\cdot x = \max_{\mu \in \overline\Omega }\, \mu\cdot x.
\end{equation}
The vector $\eta_{\hat x} \in {\mathbb R^d}$ with components
\begin{equation} 
\eta_{{\hat x},j}
= \sum_{y\in {\mathbb Z^d}} y_j J^{(\mu_{\hat x})}(y)
\end{equation}
satisfies $\eta_{\hat x} = |\eta_{\hat x}|\hat x$.
Also,
with the matrix $\Lambda_{\hat x}$ defined by
$\Lambda_{\hat x, j l } = \sum_{y\in {\mathbb Z^d}} y_j y_l J^{(\mu_{\hat x})}(y)$,
we have
\begin{equation} 
\mu_{\hat x} \cdot x  \asymp m_S  \abs x ,
	\qquad
|\eta_{\hat x}| \asymp m_S ,
	\qquad
k \cdot \Lambda_{\hat x} k \asymp \abs k^2
	\quad (k\in {\mathbb R^d}) ,
\end{equation}
with constants depending only on $d,M,K_{\mathrm{IR}}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:12:20.554310+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b8967dfa9cd85473082906f92793030079bb03ba40d3d5629461ecb6514c22f0","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Induced events of arXiv:2603.28936, 'Composition of random functions and word reconstruction'] For all $V_A^-,V_B^-,V_A^+,V_B^+\subseteq [n]$ and functions $\psi_a: V_A^+ \to V_A^-$ and $\psi_b: V_B^+ \to V_B^-$, we let $F(V_A^-,V_B^-,V_A^+,V_B^+,\psi_a,\psi_b)$ be the intersection of the following events (seen as events under the probability measure $\mathbb P$):
\begin{itemize}
\item[-] $\mathbf{a}(x)=\psi_a(x)$, for all $x\in V_A^+$;

\item[-] $\mathbf{b}(x)=\psi_b(x)$, for all $x\in V_B^+$;

\item[-]  $\mathbf{a}^{-1}(y)=\psi^{-1}_a(y)$, for all $y\in V_A^-$; and

\item[-]  $\mathbf{b}^{-1}(y)=\psi^{-1}_b(y)$, for all $y\in V_B^-$.
\end{itemize}
We denote $f_a^-=|V_A^-|$, $f_b^-=|V_B^-|$, $f_a^+=|V_A^+|$ and $f_b^+=|V_B^+|$. We call such an event an \emph{induced} event.  If the role of $V_A^-,V_B^-,V_A^+,V_B^+,\psi_a,\psi_b$ is irrelevant in the context, we write $F:= F(V_A^-,V_B^-,V_A^+,V_B^+,\psi_a,\psi_b)$ to keep the notation light. We also write $\textrm{Supp}(F):= V_A^-\cup V_B^-\cup V_A^+\cup V_B^+$ and $|F|:= |\textrm{Supp}(F)|$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.608090+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b897f04c6f87b8a76fa9dd2358c5e36b4798c2e4e8c3479d413ef8ef4fdda971","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 14 of arXiv:2607.08242, 'Convergence of random sums in non-commutative probability'] Let $\{a_n\}$ be self-adjoint variables, satisfying Condition \textbf{C2} with $K_n=1$, such that $a_n\stackrel{*}{\to} l$.


