    \sum_{k \in W : k \to_G z}{\ell(k \to_G z) \, w(\Phi_{\{k\}}(\mathcal{H}_u[G]))}
    & = w(\Phi_{(Z \setminus \{z\}) \cup \{u\} \to Z}(G))\,, \\
    \sum_{z \in Z}{\sum_{k \in W : k \to_G z}{\ell(k \to_G z) \, w(\Phi_{\{k\}}(\mathcal{H}_u[G]))}}
    & = w(\Phi_Z(G))\,.
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:53.186573+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9832718bb79f993e1417d8b3dadc7681c136a34115624938924aadabaabe9bc","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 14 of arXiv:2606.30364, 'Distributional comparison for non-commutative infinitely divisible probability measures'] For $\mu_1,\mu_2 \in \mathcal{P}(\R)$, we let $\Gamma_{\!\!\textrm{nc}}(\mu_1,\mu_2)$ denote the set of all tracial $C^{*}$-probability spaces $(\Ac,\varphi)$ containing variables $a_1=a_1^*$ and $a_2=a_2^*$ with analytical distributions $\mu_1$ and $\mu_2$, respectively. 

The \emph{free $p$-Wasserstein distance} between $\mu_1$ and $\mu_2$ is defined as

\begin{align}
	\freeWass{p} (\mu_1,\mu_2) \quad = \inf_{ (\Ac,\varphi) \in \Gamma_{\!\!\textrm{nc}}(\mu_1,\mu_2) } 
			\left\{ \abs{\, a_1-a_2 \,}_p  \, \left| \begin{array}{c}
			a_1=a_1^*,a_2=a_2^* \in \Ac \text{ with } \\  \varphi(a^n_i) = m_n[\mu_i] 
			\text{ for } n \geq 1 \text{ and } i=1,2			
	\end{array} \right.			
			 \right\}	
\end{align}

where $\abs{\, \cdot \,}_p $ is the the $p$-norm in a tracial $C^{\ast}$-probability spaces, see Definition \ref{def:nc_pnorm}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:30:36.800165+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b983abcabd94cdf79d1fab2663d433451423ddbafc19dd58a9458c7c989536d5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 43 of arXiv:2607.10486, 'Weak martingale solutions to stochastic Navier-Stokes-Cahn-Hilliard system with transport '] Assume that $T>0$ and the sequence $(\phi_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of continuous $\mathbb{F}$-adapted, $\newonep{V}$-valued processes satisfies the Aldous-Rebolledo condition in $\newonep{V}$, as well as the following two conditions:
