{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:35:05.829255+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9a6d9807dbc45536d9e4c1b683e35b90bc807cae8b42c7b7e69b910ac007ae7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm Tanaka of arXiv:2606.24385, 'Gradient Mean-Field Dynamics with Measure-Valued States: Well-Posedness, Chaos, and Long-T'] {revuz2013continuous}}]
Let $(Z_t)_{t\ge0}$ be a real-valued continuous semimartingale. For any $a\in{\mathbb{R}}$, there exists a stochastic process $L_t^a(Z)$ called the local time of $Z$ in $a$ such that
\begin{align}
(Z_t-a)^-
=(Z_0-a)^- -\displaystyle\int_0^t \mathbf 1_{\{Z_s<a\}}\,{\rm d} Z_s
+\frac{1}{2}\,L_t^a(Z),
\qquad t\ge0,
\end{align}
where $x^-=\max\{-x,0\}$ and $L_t^a(Z)$ denotes the local time of $Z$ at level $a$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:53:51.335083+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9a8741cf527ed34e4f3ce6451007eb6fbc05a1770294d12dfe4d4210979adf4","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:azgraph of arXiv:2605.03896, 'Dimer models on astroidal zig-zag graphs'] We call \(\mathsf G_{c, \mathsf f_0}\) an \emph{astroidal zig-zag graph} (AZ graph) if:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the medial cycle \(\partial \mathsf G_{c, \mathsf f_0}^\times\) is simple,
\item whenever a vertex of \({\mathsf{G}}\) is visited more than once as we go around \(\partial \mathsf G_{c, \mathsf f_0}\), all such visits occur consecutively along \(\partial \mathsf G_{c, \mathsf f_0}^\times\),
\item \(\mathsf G_{c, \mathsf f_0}\) is admissible.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:18:19.436906+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9a9f807931a5b889bf7c418f92812346b9bcad4e6482ccc1aa3fb153c4ab575","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:singleLoopExact of arXiv:2508.09291, '1+O(1) asymptotics for loop percolation in five and higher dimensions'] Suppose $d\ge 3$. For all $K$ with $\mathrm{diam}(K)=o\left(n\right)$ as $n\to\infty$
