{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:19:00.858165+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9e6ad57d02d2e0d7835df0650ae59be35df3197182168d7e2da1bfe29e8aff7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 14 (Event identity) of arXiv:2608.20337, 'Information on trajectories:\\ martingales and random times'] In the setting of Proposition~\ref{prop:supermart-relax}(iii), for $A \in \cF_t$ with $P(A) > 0$,
\begin{equation}
  -\log\PP(A)
  = \lambda\,\E[Y_t \mid A] - \E[\overline\Psi_t(\lambda) \mid A]
    + \KL\bigl(P(\cdot \mid A)\,\big\|\,\overline Q^\star_t\bigr)
    - \log\E_P[E_t(\lambda)],
\end{equation}
for $\dd\overline Q^\star_t/\dd P \propto E_t(\lambda)$.
Both residuals are nonnegative, and discarding them gives the event bound
\begin{equation}
  \PP(A) \leq \exp\!\bigl(-\lambda\,\E[Y_t \mid A] + \E[\overline\Psi_t(\lambda) \mid A]\bigr);
\end{equation}
if in addition $\lambda \geq 0$ and on $A$ one has $Y_t \geq x$ and $\overline\Psi_t(\lambda) \leq c$,
then $\PP(A) \leq e^{-\lambda x + c}$, at the further cost of the event relaxation $\lambda(\E[Y_t \mid A] - x) + (c - \E[\overline\Psi_t(\lambda) \mid A])$.
Since $E_t(\lambda) > 0$ a.s., the first residual is strictly positive whenever $\PP(A) < 1$: equality in~\eqref{eq:event-bound} holds only if $\PP(A) = 1$ and $\lambda Y_t = \overline\Psi_t(\lambda)$ a.s. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:52:23.601263+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9e6ade2906dbeb9e79aacbecace4dddcba767ac352647dd163be3bb2304a781","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Thm6.2 of arXiv:2602.23124, '\textsc{ Necessary and Sufficient Conditions for the Lacunary/Hereditary Laws of Large Num'] {\bf Necessity and Sufficiency in the  Classical  WLLN for I.I.D. Sequences:}
  For   real-valued I.I.D. functions $\,h_1, h_2, \cdots,$  the following are equivalent: 

  \smallskip
  \noindent
  {\bf (i)} {\rm WLLN:} There exist ``correctors"     $\,d_1, d_2, \cdots\,$ in $\mathbb R\,,$ so that 
  $ \,
\lim_{N \to \infty} \left( \frac{1}{N} \sum_{n=1}^N h_n - d_N \right) = 0\,$ holds in $\,{\mathbb P}-$probability; i.e., for each $\varepsilon >0$ we have
\begin{equation} 

\pi_N^\star( \varepsilon)\,:= \,  {\mathbb P} \bigg( \, \bigg| \frac{1}{\,N\,} \sum_{n=1}^N h_n - d_N \bigg| > \varepsilon  \bigg)\, \longrightarrow \, 0\,, \quad \text{as}~~ N \to \infty\,.
