{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:45:27.887581+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ba1a908c0143a35a0e1db0ff1301b7254fcd6f57491fc656ca9e42032f35dd5b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:flatly_parallel of arXiv:2607.02410, 'Polynomial mixing for polygonal side matchings'] Let $e_{1} = \{A,B\},e_{2} = \{C,D\} \in C_{\mathrm{cross}}$ be two chords which do not cross each other. The chords partition the sides of $P \setminus \{A,B,C,D\}$ into three disjoint sets: 
	\begin{itemize}
		\item The set $I^{e_1}$ of sides which are separated from $e_2$ by $e_1$.
		\item The set $I^{e_2}$ of sides which are separated from $e_1$ by $e_2$.
		\item The set $I^{e_1,e_2}$ of sides which are between $e_1$ and $e_2$.
	\end{itemize}
	The chords $e_{1}$ and $e_{2}$ are said to be \textit{flatly parallel} if every chord with an endpoint in $I^{e_1,e_2}$ has both endpoints in $I^{e_1,e_2}$, and none of those chords intersect each other.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:36:12.191671+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ba1b1aa103139084b1cad928040c0ddcb40763e66964aa0106af0e9a17c49519","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 18 (Trace incompatibility at the wall) of arXiv:2609.03916, 'Halfspace Theorems for Anisotropic Minimal Surfaces and Perimeter Minimizers'] Let $L\subset H$, let $G:=H\setminus L$, and suppose that on a relatively
open set $\Gamma\subset P$,
\begin{equation}
 \Tr_PL=1\qquad\HH^n\text{-a.e. on }\Gamma.
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}
 |D\chi_L|\llcorner\Gamma=\HH^n\llcorner\Gamma,
 \qquad
 |D\chi_G|\llcorner\Gamma=0.
\end{equation}
Consequently, the identity
\begin{equation}
 |D\chi_G|=\HH^n\llcorner P+|D\chi_L|
\end{equation}
cannot hold when $\HH^n(\Gamma)>0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:38:17.796069+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ba1b36469aa741243b262935e93c05e38c51cc0f025d02346c178e4ef27a3e9c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:integral_edge_crossing of arXiv:2509.23496, 'Crossing probabilities in geometric inhomogeneous random graphs'] Let $\gamma$ and $\delta$ be as in Proposition~(prop:asymp_escape_crossing_probabilities of the paper). Then, we have
