	where the constant $\mathcal{C}(\alpha)$ is defined in (equation Stable-branching of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:45:32.084039+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ba5affe69557946701d144f8ca139710b27194d4ac71023ced130981b51ba685","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn root-finding of arXiv:2411.14336, 'Finding the root in random nearest neighbor trees'] A root finding algorithm for a random tree $T_n$ generated via a geometric process on $\mathbf{T}^d$ is given inputs $\varepsilon \in (0,1)$, $d \in \mathbf{N}$, and one of the following:
\begin{itemize}
    \item (Embedded root finding) the unlabeled tree $T_n^\circ$ embedded in $\mathbf{T}^d$;
    \item (Metric root finding) the adjacency matrix of the unlabeled tree $T_n^\circ$, with corresponding edge lengths;
    \item (Graph root finding) only the adjacency matrix of $T_n^\circ$.
\end{itemize}
It outputs a subset of vertices $H_{\mathbf{T}^d}(\varepsilon, T_n^{\circ})$ of size independent of $n$ (only depends on $\varepsilon$), which contains the root with probability $1 - \varepsilon$, i.e., 
\begin{equation}
  \liminf_{n \to \infty} \mathbf{P}_{T_n}\left\{ 1 \in H_{\mathbf{T}^d}(\varepsilon, T_n^{\circ})\right\} \geq 1 - \varepsilon.
\end{equation}
We refer to $H_{\mathbf{T}^d}(\varepsilon, T_n^{\circ})$ as $H(\varepsilon, n)$ throughout the paper when the underlying metric space $\mathbf{T}^d$ and input unlabeled tree $T_n^{\circ}$ on $n$ vertices are clear from the context.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:12:46.874261+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ba5b6410cc45b516cf82d6e5f1928a95e668611d55fea73366c793e6d5ea15bc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:hill-moment of arXiv:2608.08375, 'Algebraic formulas for first-passage times of Markov processes in the linear framework: ge'] With the same assumptions as at the start of this section, choose $z \in Z$. Let $W$ be as defined in the statement of Lemma~(cor:hill-2 of the paper) and let $\Omega$ be as defined in the statement of Theorem~(thm:hill-moment-Z of the paper). Then the $r$-th moment of the conditional FPT from $u$ to $z$ is given by,
\[
\begin{split}
    \mu^{(r)}_{c,u,z}(G) = r! \sum_{(i_1, \dotsc, i_r) \in \Omega}{\left( \prod_{j=0}^{r-1}{\frac{\psi_{i_{j+1}|i_j,u} \, x_{i_{j+1}}^*(\mathcal{H}_u[G])}{\sum_{z' \in Z}{\sum_{k \in W , k \to_G z'}{\ell(k \to_G z') \, x_k^*(\mathcal{H}_u[G])}}}} \right)} \\
