{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:43:53.578018+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ba7f048dc5ad9f5c4597c232279a452ee6cb6e3495e39c2579d3116b8ad2f1e8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition char-martingales-processes of arXiv:2512.10625, 'Bessel and Dunkl processes with drift'] \begin{enumerate}\itemsep=-1pt
  \item[\rm{(1)}] Let  $(X_t)_{t\ge0}$ be a stochastic process on $\mathbb R^N$ with RCLL paths and start in $0$.
    Then  $(X_t)_{t\ge0}$ is a Dunkl process without drift if and only if
    \begin{equation}
( E_k(X_t,iz)\cdot e^{t\langle z,z\rangle/2})_{t\ge0}
\end{equation}
    is a $\mathbb C$-valued martingale (w.r.t.~the canonical filtration) for each $z\in\mathbb R^N$. Moreover, the processes 
in (equation exp-mart-dunkl-0 of the paper) are  martingales for all $z\in\mathbb C^N$.
\item[\rm{(2)}] For
  each Dunkl process $(X^\lambda_t)_{t\ge0}$ with drift $\lambda\in\mathbb R^N$ and start in $0$, and for each  $z\in\mathbb C^N$,
 \begin{equation}
\Bigl( \frac{E_k(X_t^\lambda,z)}{E_k(X_t^\lambda,\lambda)}\cdot e^{-(\langle z,z\rangle +\|\lambda\|^2)t/2}\Bigr)_{t\ge0}
\end{equation}
is a martingale.
\item[\rm{(3)}] The statements in (1) and (2) hold also for Bessel processes, when the Dunkl kernels $E_k$
  in (equation exp-mart-dunkl-0 of the paper) and
  (equation exp-mart-dunkl-lambda of the paper) will be replaced by the Bessel functions $J_k$.
\item[\rm{(4)}] For hybrid Dunkl-Bessel processes $(\tilde X^\lambda_t)_{t\ge0}$ with drift $\lambda\in\mathbb R^N$ and start in $0$, and 
  for each  $z\in\mathbb C^N$,
  \begin{equation}
\Bigl( \frac{E_k(X_t^\lambda,z)}{J_k(X_t^\lambda,\lambda)}\cdot e^{-(\langle z,z\rangle +\|\lambda\|_2)t/2}\Bigr)_{t\ge0}
\end{equation}
  is a martingale.
  \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:53:11.417762+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ba816f14dc5ddbb613b7a9be5f0f1be8ccd5b60842817191d2426883084db6d7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 (\bf Anticipative approximation) of arXiv:2606.08776, 'Approximation of certain stochastic integrals with anticipating integrands'] Assume $Z$ has the admissible representation \eqref{eqn:definition:admissible_representation_Z}
