	$(2)$ The adjoint of some polynomial can be translated into the action of a differential operator, that is, for $f,g,h \in \mathbb{C}[x_{1},...,x_{n}]$, we have $\langle h(x)f(x),g(x) \rangle = \langle f(x), h(\partial) \cdot g(x)\rangle$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.472263+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb57db14549acc561bcbc6042c4e7d1022ea98e266e729c2fdef532579b3ca05","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 45 (Evaluation bounds on the intersection branch) of arXiv:2606.29292, 'Intrinsic Gaussian Tensor Geometry and Random Operator Spectra'] For every $m\ge1$ there are constants $C_m,A_m<\infty$, depending only on
$m$, such that for $1\le p<\infty$ and $2\le r<\infty$,
\begin{align}

 \|\cT_K^{(m)}\|_{L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)}
 &\le C_m(p+r)^{m/2}\Prof_{m,r}(K),\\

 \|I_m(K)\|_{L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)}
 &\le A_m(p+r)^{m/2}\|K\|_{\mathfrak W_{m,r}}.
\end{align}
One may take $C_m\le C_0^m$ and $A_m=m^{m/2}C_m$.
The first assertion is Theorem~\ref{thm:m-finite} together with the completed
map of Proposition~\ref{prop:completed-decoupled-chaos-map}; the second is
Proposition~\ref{prop:wick-completion}.  The range $1\le p<2$ follows from
$p=2$ and monotonicity of probability norms.  Since $r\ge2$,
$r+2\le\frac43(r+p)$ in this range, so the displayed $(p+r)^{m/2}$ form is
retained after enlarging the absolute exponential base $C_0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:35:10.785398+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb583762f607c11694b69edef53bdb9d79e20019c0f76eab2b2519dbc24e1715","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 13 of arXiv:2604.00474, 'Trap behaviors for Brownian motions'] Let $Z^\dagger =\{Z_t, \, t < T_D(Z)\}$ be the part process on the bounded domain $D$ of $Z= \{Z_t\}_{t \geq 0}$ with $Z_0=x \in D$. If there exists $\beta \in (0, 1)$ such that
\begin{align}
\int_{D} \mathbf{P}_x(T_D(Z^\dagger) \leq t) \, dx   \simeq   t^\frac{\beta}{2} \quad \textrm{as } t \to 0+ 
\end{align}
then we say that $Z$ exhibits a trap behaviour on $D$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:16:53.573996+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb58fa4fb03646fe14175502515a13b229588f93a2e79157381a285185e827dc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:critgauge of arXiv:2412.04607, 'Asymptotics of the partial \(n\)-fold dimer model'] For any \(\vb*{\alpha} \in \mathcal{M}_\mathbb{R}\) and weight function \(w\) there is a unique gauge-equivalent weight function \(w'\) with the property that for all \(v\) the sum of weights of tiles containing vertex \(v\) (counted with multiplicity) is \(\alpha_v\), that is 
\(\sum_{t} t_v w'(t) = \alpha_v\).
The corresponding gauge transformations \(f\colon V\to \mathbb{R}_{>0}\) are exactly those which solve the "criticality equations" 
\begin{equation} 
\frac{x_v \partial_{x_v} P}{P} = \alpha_v
\end{equation}
with \(x_v = f(v)\). The growth rate of \(Z_{w,\vb{n},N}\) is 
\begin{equation}
\sigma(w, \vb*{\alpha}) \coloneq \lim_{n_v, N\to \infty} \frac1N \log Z_{w,\vb{n},N} = \log P(\vb{x}) - \sum_v \alpha_v \log(\mathrm{x}_v) 
\end{equation}
for any solution \(\vb{x}\) to ((eqn:crit of the paper)). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:10:43.855076+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb59970265540acb204e3514bf27d67ae6b10edb202ad43129c490237902aa19","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor22 of arXiv:2412.04334, 'Fractional counting process at L\'{e}vy times and its applications'] If $\{Y_n, n\geq 1\}$ are integer-valued discrete random variables, we set
    \begin{equation*}
        b_s^{*m} = \mathbb{P}\left[Y_1+Y_2+\cdots +Y_m= s\right],\;\; s\in\mathbb{N}_{0}.
