        Then, either $\Omega_1=\emptyset$ or $\Omega_1=\R^{d_1}.$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:34:09.911018+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bc65f77487d900e1f06592001926f039eb2fe775fc02159805f8731663d86917","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 21 of arXiv:2603.26319, 'Regularity of Gibbs measures for unbounded spin systems on general graphs'] Assume the interactions $J$ are reasonable and ferromagnetic.
    Suppose also that there exists a constant $c_0 > 0$ such that for any $j,k \geq 1$,
    \begin{equation}        
    f(k^{-r}) \geq c_0 \sqrt{\frac{\log(|B_{k+j}(o)|)}{\log(|B_j(o)|)}}.
    \end{equation}
    Then there exist $\frac{2 \beta M_f}{c}-$regular Gibbs measures $\nu^{+}_{\beta, \rho}$, $\nu^{-}_{\beta, \rho}$ such that
    \begin{equation*}
        \lim_{\Lambda \nearrow V}\nu_{\Lambda, \beta, \rho}^{\xi^{+}} =\nu^{+}_{\beta, \rho}, \qquad 
        \lim_{\Lambda \nearrow V}\nu_{\Lambda, \beta, \rho}^{\xi^{-}}=\nu^{-}_{\beta, \rho}.
    \end{equation*}
    Moreover, for any $a>0$, any $a-$regular Gibbs measure $\nu$ satisfies $\nu^{-}_{\beta, \rho} \preceq \nu \preceq \nu^{+}_{\beta, \rho}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:39:12.288337+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bc65ff65bf0ad66f5ce4ce662c1eca6386f430d4d236e2951a2f1e432896e37b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:not:linGrowth of arXiv:2412.07710, 'An H-theorem for a conditional McKean-Vlasov process related to interacting diffusions on '] Let $(\mu, \gamma)$ be a solution to the $\kappa$-MLFE{} on $[0, T]$ with potentials $(U, W)$ satisfying Assumption (as:results:lyapunov of the paper) and initial condition $\lambda \in \mathcal{Q}_{\kappa, d}$. We call $(\mu, \gamma)$ a \emph{linear growth solution} to the $\kappa$-MLFE{} if
there exists $C \in (0, \infty)$ depending only on $( \kappa, d, T, b, \lambda)$ such that\begin{equation}

        \sup_{t \in [0, T]} \operatorname*{\bar{\mu}_t-ess\,sup}_{x, y \in \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^d} \frac{|\gamma(t, x, y)|} {1 + |x| + |y|} \leq C.
    \end{equation}
    We say that the $\kappa$-MLFE{} with potentials $(U, W)$ is \emph{well-posed} if for all $\lambda \in \mathcal{Q}_{\kappa, d}$ there is a linear growth solution to the $\kappa$-MLFE{} on $[0, \infty)$ with initial condition $\lambda$ that is unique among the class of linear growth solutions.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:21:06.858121+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bc678df9836d16b44738bdc7f69b51d882b5bbae5bb43d453ac1b0025c8da677","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 22 of arXiv:2608.21063, '\textbf{Non-bijective scaling limits and phase transitions of planar maps}'] Suppose that $\nu=1$ and that condition~\eqref{eq:bwregvar} holds for some $1<\alpha<2$. Conditionally on $\widehat\xi=k$, let $\mD_{\widehat\xi}$ have the law of $\mD_k$. Define $\mD_\xi$ analogously.  Suppose that there are constants $C, \gamma_{\mathrm{dec}}>0$ and an integer $p\ge 2$ with
	\begin{align*}
		\sum_{k=1}^{m}\Ex{\mD_k^p} \le 	Cm^{1+p\gamma_{\mathrm{dec}}}
	\end{align*}
	for all large enough integers $m$. Then
	\begin{align*}
		\sup_{\substack{r>0\\\Prb{r/2<\widehat\xi\le r}>0}}
		\Exb{ \left(\frac{\mD_{\widehat\xi}}{r^{\gamma_{\mathrm{dec}}}}\right)^p \ \middle|\ r/2<\widehat\xi\le r } &<\infty,
		\\
		\sup_{\substack{r>0\\\Prb{r/2<\xi\le r}>0}} \Exb{ \left(\frac{\mD_\xi}{r^{\gamma_{\mathrm{dec}}}}\right)^p \ \middle|\ r/2<\xi\le r } &<\infty. 
	\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:49:21.124006+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bc6797d672c0fc9e2ed1153757a32445dd69b79adbcd0e4c44b28e55f3e48fc7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 31 of arXiv:2608.26642, '2608.26642'] Let $g:=\sup\{t\ge 0:\ X_t=0\}$ denote the last exit time from zero, where we use the convention that $\sup\emptyset=0$. Its law is given by
