{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:51:12.743892+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bc72fa2a812cbab7dabdac6b362c2d2414690e760f19a18ae30a0976875de5c1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 53 of arXiv:2605.02430, '\textbf{Scaling limit of the range of tree-valued branching random walks in random environ'] We fix $\alpha \ino (1, 2]$ and we assume that $\mu$ satisfies (\ref{hypostaintro}). Recall from (\ref{aennedef}) the definition of $(a_n)_{n\in \bbN^*}$. 
Let $\ttb \ino \big(1 , \infty\big)$ and $s_0 \ino (1, \infty)$ and let $\epp \ino \big( 0, 1\! -\! \frac{1}{\alpha} \big) $. 
Then there exists $c\ino (1, \infty)$ that only depends on $\mu, \ttb, s_0$ and $\epp$ such that 
\begin{equation}

\forall n \ino \bbN^*, \forall s,s^\prime\ino [0, s_0], \quad 
\bE \Big[ \big| \tfrac{1}{a_n}H_{ns^\prime} (\tau_\infty) \! -\! \tfrac{1}{a_n} H_{ns}  (\tau_\infty) \big|^\ttb \Big] 
\leq c \, \big| s^\prime \! -\! s\big|^{\ttb (1-\frac{1}{\alpha} -\epp)} \; .
\end{equation}
Moreover, for all $\gamma\ino \big( 0, 1\! -\! \tfrac{1}{\alpha}\big)$, there exists $c'\ino (1, \infty)$
that only depends on $\mu, \ttb, s_0$ and $\gamma$ such that 
\begin{equation}

\sup_{n\in \bbN^*} \bE \Big[ \Big( \mathtt{Hol}_{\gamma, s_0} \big(   \tfrac{1}{a_n}H_{ n \cdot } (\tau_\infty) \big) \Big)^{\! \ttb} \Big] \leq c' , 
\end{equation}
where we recall from (\ref{Holdnodef}) the definition of  $\mathtt{Hol}_{\gamma, s_0} (\mathtt x (\cdot))$, for all $\mathtt x \ino \bC^0 (\bbR_+, \bbR)$. In particular it implies that 
\begin{equation}

\sup_{n\in \bbN^*} \bE\Big[ \Big( \max_{s\in [0, s_0]}  \tfrac{1}{a_n}H_{ ns} (\tau_\infty) \Big)^{\! \ttb} \Big] \leq c' s_0^\gamma .
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.603806+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bc748272dbf3976a4c58bac3d382342fc6a6ea817bf665cb815a4f723ddfbd30","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 3 of arXiv:2607.07552, 'Weak convergence rate for the Cox--Ingersoll--Ross process'] Let $f\in\mathcal{C}^{(q)}((0,\infty),\mathbb{R})$ and $m\geq q$, $m\in\mathbb{N}$, be such that there exists $K>0$ with
	\begin{equation}
		|f^{(q)}(x)|\leq K(1+x^m)\quad\forall x\geq 0.
	\end{equation}
	Then the function $v:\mathbb{R}_{+}\times[0,T]\to\mathbb{R}$ defined by $v(x,t):=\mathbb{E}^x[f(X_{T-t})]$ satisfies the PDE
	\begin{equation}
		\partial_t v+b(x)\partial_x v+{\sigma(x)^2\over 2}\partial_{xx}v=0,\quad v(T,x)=f(x).
	\end{equation}
	Moreover, the derivatives of $v$ are uniformly bounded, and there exists $K>0$ such that
	\begin{equation}
		\left|\partial^{k}_x\partial^{r}_t v(x,t)\right|\leq K(1+x^{m+q+r})\quad\forall(x,t)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}\times[0,T],\quad k+2r\leq q.
	\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:19:47.248533+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bc7579f56dd45933b1bbf4b88ac6b4b978ff51f2871ced2a2fbb0d316c689faa","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Biggins_theorem of arXiv:2512.21159, '\textsc{From multitype branching Brownian motions\\ to branching Markov additive processes'] Let $\theta \in [0, \bar{\theta})$.
    On the event $\mathscr{S}$, the additive martingale $W_{\theta}(t)$ converges to $W_{\theta}(\infty)$ in $\mathcal{L}^{1}(\mathbf{P}_{x,i})$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$, $i \in \mathcal{I}$ if and only if $\theta \lambda'(\theta) < \lambda(\theta)$ and $\sum_{k\ge 1} (k \log k) \mu_j(k) < \infty$ for all $j \in \mathcal{I}$. 

    Moreover, when $W_{\theta}$ converges in $ \mathcal{L}^{1}(\mathbf{P}_{x,i})$, we have $\mathbf{P}_{x,i}\left(\{W_{\theta}(\infty)>0\} \Delta \mathscr{S}\right) = 0$, where $\Delta$ represents the symmetric difference between two sets. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:38:37.405093+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bc75ad9fcaf23ddab0c9d3913e0e315bab983c60ff463dfe7bf1ae544b44e9bf","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2605.15718, 'Mean-field derivation of a two-dimensional signal-dependent parabolic-elliptic Keller-Sege'] Let $T >0$. Under the assumptions \ref{ass_ks} it holds: \\
$\forall \alpha \in (0, \frac{1}{2} ) : \forall \beta \in (0, \frac{\alpha}{2} ) :\forall \gamma >0 : \exists C>0 : \forall \varepsilon >0, N \in \N, \text{ with } \varepsilon \geq N^{-\beta} : $
\begin{align*}
\sup_{t \in [0,T]} \pr \left( \left\lbrace \max_{1 \leq i \leq N}  \left| X^\varepsilon_{N,i} - \bar{X}^\varepsilon_{i} \right|(t) \geq N^{-\alpha} \right\rbrace \right) \leq  \frac{C}{N^\gamma}.
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:36:16.067500+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bc77565fc67f8e052d8022a56b0035a5e6204e6393ac99a68a64b9351dbf7c4d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:global_well_cutoff of arXiv:2606.28575, 'Global smooth solutions by high mode Lie-Transport noise for Logarithmically Hyperdissipat'] Let $4< p<+\infty$ and $-\frac{1}{2}<r<-\frac{2}{p}$. For all $\omega_0\in B^{-2/p}_{g,2,p},$ there exists a unique local p-solution $(\omega,\tau)$ of (equation main_ito_cutoff of the paper) satisfying $\tau=+\infty$ $\mathbb{P}-a.s.$ and
 \begin{align*}
     \omega&\in H^{\theta,p}_{loc}\left([0,+\infty);H_g^{-2\theta}\right)\cap  C\big([0,+\infty);B^{-\frac{2}{p}}_{g,2,p}\big)\quad \mathbb{P}-a.s.\quad  \text{for all } \theta\in [0,1/2).
 \end{align*}
 Moreover, for all $T>0$, there exists $C=C(T)$ such that
    \begin{align}
     \mathbb{E}\Big[\sup_{s\in [0,T]}\|\omega_s\|^p_{B^{-\frac{2}{p}}_{g,2,p}}\Big]+\mathbb{E}\int_0^T \|\omega_s\|_{H^0_g}^p \,\mathrm{d}s\leq C(T)\Big(1+\|\omega_0\|_{B^{-\frac{2}{p}}_{g,2,p}}^p\Big) .
    \end{align}
    Moreover the hidden constant $C(T)$ depends only on $\sup_{t\in [0,T]}\|\theta\|^2_{\ell^2}$, and not on the particular choice of frequency localization ($n>1$) of the noise (c.f. (hyp_noise of the paper)). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:31:49.845466+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bc78c1ec3ead7cdf956ef55d298270a4d2ee96707b2bb52db46e9729a7b5cdc8","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem: no but possibly one event in small interval of arXiv:2608.23756, 'Measure-valued fluid limits for partial service queues\\with correlated patience and servi'] Consider the process defined as:
	\begin{equation*}
		\boldsymbol{w}(t) \vcentcolon= t - \max \asetc{a_i}{i \geq 1, a_i \leq t} \quad \text{for all} \quad t \geq 0, 
	\end{equation*}
	which represents the amount of time elapsed since the last arrival at time $t$. For each $i \in \mathbbm{Z}$ and $0 \leq s < t$, the following inequality holds almost surely:
	\begin{equation}

		P\probarg*{B_i^c(s, t) | \mathcal{F}_s} \leq \frac{\bar F\left(\boldsymbol{w}(s)\right) - \bar F\left(\boldsymbol{w}(s) + t - s\right)}{\bar F\left(\boldsymbol{w}(s)\right)} + \sum_{j = 1 - M}^{\mathcal{R}(s)} H_j^1(s, t),
	\end{equation}
	where $B_i^c(s, t)$ is the complement of $B_i(s, t)$. Also, let us fix a realization and suppose that there exist a constant $\eta \in (0, \kappa)$, a compact set $(0, 0) \in C \subset U_g$ and a time $T \geq 0$ such that
	\begin{equation}

		\boldsymbol{w}(t) \leq \eta \quad \text{and} \quad \left(\boldsymbol{x}_j(t), \boldsymbol{y}_j(t)\right) \in C \quad \text{if} \quad 1 - M \leq j \leq \mathcal{R}(T) \quad \text{and} \quad t \in [0, T].
	\end{equation}
	Then there exist two deterministic constants $K \geq 0$ and $\delta > 0$, which only depend on $\eta$ and $C$, such that if $t - \delta \leq s < t \leq T + \delta$, then we have:
	\begin{equation*}
		\frac{\bar F\left(\boldsymbol{w}(s)\right) - \bar F\left(\boldsymbol{w}(s) + t - s\right)}{\bar F\left(\boldsymbol{w}(s)\right)} + \sum_{j = 1 - M}^{\mathcal{R}(s)} H_j^1(s, t) \leq K\left[1 + M + \mathcal{R}(s)\right](t - s).
	\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:35:10.786649+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bc79c1d8931ffe8513180fb910e4a0ed66b5e344661e4748068a7b2d8b34e249","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 15 of arXiv:2604.00598, 'Allowing for Imprecision in the Game-theoretic Characterisation of the Poisson Process'] Fix some \(s,t\in\posreals\) such that \(s<t\).
For all \(n\in\nats\), we define \(\Delta_n\coloneqq\gr{t-s}/n\), \(t^n_k\coloneqq s+\gr{k-1}\Delta_n\) for all \(k\in\set{1, \dots, n+1}\)  and \[A_n
\coloneqq \set{\pth\in\countpaths\colon \gr{\forall k\in\set{1, \dots, n}}~\pth\gr{t^n_{k+1}}\leq\pth\gr{t^n_k}+1 }.\]
Then
\begin{equation*}
\gr{\forall\epsilon\in\sposreals}
\gr{\exists n_\epsilon\in\nats}
\gr{\forall n\in\nats, n\geq n_\epsilon}~
\oscuprev{\indica{\compl{A_n}}}[s]
<\epsilon
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:38:23.540912+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bc7b1802988fda427f51576ee90ab23c3fd17e55589131f4502ac12f7ae67995","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma LS2 of arXiv:2510.22701, 'Gaussian fluctuations in Mean Field Stable matchings'] For $d>2$, we have that 
    \begin{align}
        \underset{n\rightarrow \infty}{\mathrm{lim}} \frac{1}{n}\sum^{n}_{k=\kappa_n+2} \bbclr{\frac{k}{n}}^{-4} I_0\bbclr{\frac{k}{n}}^2 =  \underset{n\rightarrow \infty}{\mathrm{lim}} \frac{1}{n}\sum^{n-1}_{k=\kappa_n} \bbclr{\frac{k}{n}}^{-4} I_{(\kappa_n-1)/n}\bbclr{\frac{k}{n}}^2 = d^2\gamma(d).
