{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:48:35.819042+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bcf8fa7eb8d784f7740c69091caac43bc0bfdc03002924ccddd0956c306b64bf","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition Matrix Bennett-Bernstein inequality \eqref{eqn:mbi} of arXiv:2411.09516, 'Sharp Matrix Empirical Bernstein Inequalities\footnote{NeurIPS 2025 Poster}'] Let $\overline \mathbf{X} _n$ be the sample average of independent $d\times d$ symmetric matrices $ \mathbf{X} _1,\dots,  \mathbf{X} _n$ with common mean $ \mathbb E   \mathbf{X} _i =  \mathbf{M} $, common eigenvalue upper bound $\lambda_{\max}( \mathbf{X} _i) \leqslant B$, and $ \sum_{i=1}^n \mathbb E   \mathbf{X} _i^2 \preceq n  \mathbf{V} $. For any $\alpha > 0$
