{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:44:39.743583+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bd40a544b94d26bc4c5b90c72264e704af332aa89b199f751e6e9d03a983413f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 (Resolvents) of arXiv:2608.29872, 'Local Laws and Outlier Eigenvalues for Spiked Separable Covariance Matrices'] For $z = E+ \ii \eta \in \mathbb C_+,$ we define the following resolvents for $\al=1,2$: 
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal G_{\al}(X,z):=\left({\mathcal Q}_{\al}(X) -z\right)^{-1} , \ \ \ \wt{\mathcal G}_{\al} (X,z):=(\wt{\mathcal Q}_{\al}(X)-z)^{-1} .
\end{equation}
 We denote the ESD $\rho^{(p)}$ of ${\mathcal Q}_{1}$ and its Stieltjes transform as
\be
\rho^{(p)} := \frac{1}{p} \sum_{i\in\cal I_1} \delta_{\lambda_i({\mathcal Q}_1)},\quad m^{(p)}(z):=\int \frac{\rho^{(p)}(\dd x)}{x-z}=\frac{1}{p} \mathrm{Tr} \, \mathcal G_1(z).
\ee
We also introduce the following quantities:
\begin{equation}
 m_1^{(n)}(z):= \frac{1}n\tr \left(A \mathcal G_1(z)\right) ,\quad m_2^{(n)}(z):=\frac{1}{n}\tr\left( B\mathcal G_2(z)\right). 
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:33:11.957840+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bd40bc83ab9c8c324a55fb85bcd2ca5e954bdf9cd184ee639c1a61652a2580c4","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma eq:gaussian-shift of arXiv:2607.14087, 'Stochastic Domination of Gaussian Maxima: A Resolution of the Weak Simplex Conjecture'] \label[lemma]{lem:gaussian-shift}
Let $X\sim\mathcal N(0,R)$ for a positive-semidefinite matrix $R$.  For every
$a\in\mathbb R^m$ and every nonnegative Borel function
$F:\mathbb R^m\to[0,\infty]$,
\begin{equation}
  \mathbb E\!\left[e^{a^{\mathsf T}X}F(X)\right]
  =e^{\frac12a^{\mathsf T}Ra}\mathbb E F(X+Ra).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:41:33.794479+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bd418d3d2565eec546e3b052c10d864a7ba70e52a82a914ee075ebbbe490d934","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 6 of arXiv:2604.10051, 'Ergodicity of the voter model with dynamic anti-voter bonds'] Fix~$k \in \N$. Then, for every~$(\mathbf{x},\mathsf i ,A,B) \in \mathcal S_k$, event~$\mathcal A \in \sigma( \mathbf X_s: s \ge 0)$ and stationary measure~$\mu$ for the voter model with dynamic anti-voter bonds, we have that
\begin{equation}
    \lim_{t \to \infty} \int E_{\mathbf x,\mathsf i,A,B}\big[ D(\eta,\zeta,\mathbf X_t,\mathbf i_t,A_t,B_t) \cdot \mathds{1}_\mathcal{A} \big] \; \mu \big(\mathrm{d}(\eta,\zeta)\big) = \frac{1}{2^k} \cdot P_{\mathbf x,\mathbf i,A,B}(\mathcal A).
\end{equation}    
Furthermore, for any~$(x,\mathsf i,A,B) \in \mathcal{S}_1$,
    \begin{equation}
        \mu\big( (\eta,\zeta): \eta(x) = \mathsf i, \zeta \equiv 1 \text{ on } A, \zeta \equiv -1 \text{ on } B \big) = \frac{1}{2} \cdot p^{|A|}(1-p)^{|B|}.
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:21:45.336562+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bd45840f2d6c98f62e9fedf62a3e3ce4049819d9fbb891cd2b52fad96ef15463","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 16 ({\cite[Theorem 4.12) of arXiv:2607.27150, 'Modelling Anomalous Diffusion: The Role of CTRWs and Non-Local Dynamics'] {savtoa}}]
		Let  $\alpha: \mathbb{R}\mapsto (0,1)$, with $\alpha^*=\min_{x\in \mathbb{R}}\alpha(x)>0, \max_{x\in\mathbb{R}}\alpha(x)<1$ and \[1>\lim_{x \to +\infty}\alpha(x)=\alpha_I>\alpha^*,\,\,1>\lim_{x\to -\infty}\alpha(x)=\alpha_J>\alpha^*.\] Also, let there exist $\beta_0>0$ small enough such that for all $\beta_0\geq \beta$, the set $A_\beta=\{x\in\mathbb{R}:\alpha(x)<\alpha^*+\beta<1\}$ is bounded and satisfies $0<\mathpzc{l}({A_\beta})<\infty$ and $\mathpzc{l}(\partial A_\beta)=0$. Then,
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item if $2\alpha^*<\min\{\alpha_I,\alpha_J\}$, then for all $0\leq \beta\leq \beta_0$
			\begin{equation}
				\lim_{t \to +\infty}\frac{\int_{0}^{t}\mathds{1}_{[B(L(s))\in A_\beta]}ds}{t}=1,\,\mathds{P}^{(x,0)}\text{-a.s.};
			\end{equation}
			\item  if $2\alpha^*>\min\{ \alpha_I,\alpha_J\}$, then for any $K>0$,
			\begin{equation}
				\lim_{t \to +\infty}\frac{\int_{0}^{t}\mathds{1}_{\big[B(L(s))\in A^c_\beta\cap [-K,K]^c\big]}ds}{t}=1,\,\mathds{P}^{(x,0)}\text{-a.s.}.
			\end{equation}
		\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:48:35.816629+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bd4697a71902d8106d3c0dfab297f15efb2ed293e1efe6979790912974c0d40a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:howardlieblemma of arXiv:2411.09516, 'Sharp Matrix Empirical Bernstein Inequalities\footnote{NeurIPS 2025 Poster}'] Let $\{  \mathbf{Z} _n \}$ be an $\mathcal{S}_d$-valued, adapted martingale difference sequence. Let $\{ \mathbf{C} _n\}$ be an $\mathcal{S}_d$-valued adapted process, $\{ \mathbf{C} _n'\}$ be an $\mathcal{S}_d$-valued predictable process. If 
\begin{equation}
    \mathbb E  (\exp(  \mathbf{Z} _n -   \mathbf{C} _n) | \mathcal{F}_{n-1}) \preceq \exp(   \mathbf{C} _n'  ),
\end{equation}
holds for all $n$, then the process
\begin{equation}
  \textstyle  L_n = \mathrm{tr} \exp \left( \sum_{i=1}^n  \mathbf{Z} _i -  \sum_{i=1}^n  (  \mathbf{C} _i +  \mathbf{C} _i' )  \right)
\end{equation}
is a nonnegative supermartingale. Further,
\begin{equation}
\textstyle   L_n \geqslant \exp \left( \lambda_{\max}\left(\sum_{i=1}^n  \mathbf{Z} _i \right)-   \lambda_{\max}\left( \sum_{i=1}^n (  \mathbf{C} _i +  \mathbf{C} _i' ) \right)  \right).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:00:46.837667+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bd47207eb4abdba709aa8183c81825c17db923ecbef4cfc195a0944a54d6ac74","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 18 of arXiv:2608.13359, 'Fractional moments of the Stochastic Heat Flow and\\2D Directed Polymers'] Define \(\cV_{N}^{\beta} \coloneqq \sigma^2(\beta) \EE\bigl[ (Z_N^{\beta,\w})^2 \bigr]\).
