{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:38:13.638264+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bdce479bf5e43525316397e75b12caed9cd18d3d251d23d57d1a03f5f72407d2","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 3 of arXiv:2609.00687, 'A unification of Euler-Maclaurin and Euler-Boole summation formulas'] Let $q\in\mathbb{N}$, $m\in\mathbb{N}_{0}$ and $a,b\in\mathbb{R}$ with $2\nmid q$ and $a<b$. Let $\chi$ be a Dirichlet character modulo $q$ and let $f(t)$ be an $m+1$
times continuously differentiable function on the interval $[a,b]$. Then
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\sum_{\substack{a<k\leqslant b\\k\in\mathbb{Z}}}(-1)^{k}\chi(k)f(k)\nonumber\\
&&\qquad=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{k=0}^{m}\frac{(-1)^{k}}{k!}\bigl(E_{k}(\{b\}_{\chi},\chi^{-})f^{(k)}(b)-E_{k}(\{a\}_{\chi},\chi^{-})f^{(k)}(a)\bigl)\nonumber\\
&&\qquad\quad+\frac{(-1)^{m+1}}{2m!}\int_{a}^{b}E_{m}(\{t\}_{\chi},\chi^{-})f^{(m+1)}(t)dt,
\end{eqnarray}
where
\begin{equation*}
E_{m}(\{x\}_{\chi},\chi^{-})=q^{m}\sum_{r=1}^{q}(-1)^{r-[\frac{x+r}{q}]}\chi(-r)E_{m}\biggl(\biggl\{\frac{x+r}{q}\biggl\}\biggl).
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:56:17.354964+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bdd0c6351bb1e30eac914af5c2eff67c7d0ee670fe2fcc89adda8ada3d73a020","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:primal-optimizers-finite-p of arXiv:2605.14149, 'The mean-field limit of online stochastic vector balancing'] Let $p\in[2,\infty)$ and $\delta\in[0,\infty)$, and write $q=p/(p-1)$ for the conjugate exponent. The optimization problems $V_{p,\delta}^{\infty}$ and $P_{q,\delta}$ admit unique optimizers $\alpha$ and $Y$. Denoting $X=X^\alpha$ and $Y(t)={\mathbb E}[Y\,|\,\mathcal{F}(t)]$, we have
    \begin{equation}
        0 < \|X(1)\|_p <\infty,\qquad \int_0^1 \|Y(t)\|_2\,dt <\infty,\qquad \|Y(t)\|_2 > 0\quad\text{for $t>0$},

    \end{equation}
    and the following duality relations hold almost surely:
    \begin{equation}
        Y = \frac{X(1) |X(1)|^{p-2}}{\|X(1)\|_p^{p-1}}, \qquad \alpha(t) =-\lambda(t)Y(t)\quad\text{for a.e.\ $t\in(0,1)$},

    \end{equation}
    where $\lambda(t) \in (0,\infty)$ is defined by
    \begin{equation}
        \lambda(t) \coloneqq \min\bigg(\frac{c_0}{\|Y(t)\|_2},\, \frac{1}{\delta}\bigg) ,\qquad t\in(0,1).

    \end{equation}
    If $\delta=0$, we interpret the minimum as $\lambda(t)=c_0/\|Y(t)\|_2$. Lastly, the function $t\mapsto \lambda(t)$ is continuous, and if $\delta>0$, it is bounded from above. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:00.618429+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bdd18733409a5a6876c652413a4cda6ff2c81328b9babdb2f6422dfcc29e91c4","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 77 of arXiv:2510.27589, 'Geodesic switches and exceptional times in dynamical Brownian last passage percolation'] There exist constants $g,C,c>0$ such that for all $\delta>0$, all $n$ and all $m$ satisfying $0\leq m< n^{1-3\delta/2}$, we have
