{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:28:04.863426+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bef36724a744484a8816a58ca69a43d5c703588bc7347a48ce9c95870d1198b4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 24 of arXiv:2511.22156, ''] Let $B=B(x,r)$, there exist constants $C_7,C_8,C_9>0$, such that   
\begin{equation}
P^x(\tau_B\leq t)\leq C_7\exp(-C_8(\frac{r^{\beta}}{t})^{\frac{1}{\beta-1}}),\qquad C_9t>r>1.
\end{equation}

\begin{proof}
Let $\tau=\tau_B$. Notice that $\tau\leq t+(\tau-t){1}_{\{\tau>t\}}$. By \eqref{e:6.7}, 
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
C_5^{-1}r^\beta\leq E^x[\tau]&\leq t+E^x[{1}_{\{\tau>t\}}E^{W_t}[\tau]]\\
&\leq t+C_5 P^x(\tau>t)r^{\beta},\qquad r>1.        
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
Thus 
\[
C_5^{-1}r^\beta\leq t+C_5 P^x(\tau>t)r^{\beta},\qquad r>1,
\]

which gives 
\begin{equation}
P^x(\tau\leq t)\leq c_1+c_2r^{-\beta}t,\qquad r>1,
\end{equation}
for $c_1\in (0,1),\ c_2>0$. Let $n\geq 1$, $b=\frac{r}{n}$ and $s_0=0,\ s_{i+1}=\inf\{t>s_i:|W_t-W_{s_i}|=b\},\ \xi_i=s_i-s_{i-1},\ i\geq1$. By \eqref{e:6.13} and strong markov property,
\[
P^x(\xi_{i+1}\leq t|\mathcal{F}_{s_i})\leq c_1+c_2r^{-\beta}t,\qquad b>1.
\]
Then by \cite[Lemma 3.14. p32]{barlow2006diffusions}, 
\begin{equation}
\log P^x(\tau\leq t)\leq c_3(r^{-\beta}n^{1+\beta}t)^{\frac{1}{2}}-c_4n,\qquad r>n,
\end{equation}
where $c_4,c_3>0$. If $r^{\beta}>2c_3c_4^{-1}t>r$, then take $n=(c_4r^{\beta}/(2c_3t))^{\frac{1}{\beta-1}}\in (1,r)$, and by \eqref{e:6.14}
\begin{equation*}
P^x(\tau\leq t)\leq c_5 \exp(-c_6(\frac{r^{\beta}}{t})^{\frac{1}{\beta-1}}).
\end{equation*}
While if $2c_3c_4^{-1}t\geq r^{\beta}$, then we obtain the same bound, on adjusting the constant $c_5$. 
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:17.750050+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bef45baf926a3aaf84750c0a8f2f4a8ea1d03b760c85d3afa45acea14f48002a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 28 of arXiv:2511.01456, 'Zero distribution of multiplicative Hermite and Laguerre polynomials'] Recall that $m_k(\gamma)$ is the $k$-th moment of $\nu_{\beta, \gamma}$. Then,
