{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:30:28.827692+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bf9d385212b0287e92943ce6bbd310f322b9f58840ae63f6c76ac8e57abadc88","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 18 of arXiv:2609.02105, 'Contact fundamental forms and \\ adjoint varieties'] In the notation of Definition \ref{d.Spencer}, we have the following.
\begin{itemize} \item[(i)] For each $1 \leq b \leq m$, 
for any  $\varphi \in \sO_{\sH,h}$ and $\sigma \in \sO(J^k)_h$,
\begin{eqnarray*} D_{X_b} (\varphi \sigma) & = & (X_b \varphi) \pi^k_{k-1} \sigma + \varphi  \ D_{X_b} \sigma \\
D_{Y_b} (\varphi \sigma) & = & (Y_b \varphi) \pi^k_{k-1} \sigma + \varphi  \ D_{Y_b} \sigma \\
D_T (\varphi \sigma) & = & (T \varphi) \pi^k_{k-2} \sigma + \varphi  \ D_{T} \sigma. \end{eqnarray*}
\item[(ii)] 
An element $\sigma \in \sO(J^k)_h$ is of the form $j^k(\varphi)$ for some $\varphi \in \sO_h$ if and only if $$ D_{X_b} \sigma \ = \ D_{Y_b} \sigma \ = \ D_T \sigma \ =0$$ for all $1\leq b \leq m$. \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:49:21.129907+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bfa2ac92a4b8ad1e058c1599fe171b01e795335db1ed457929109ffb3160f938","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 (Exact spectral representation) of arXiv:2608.26693, '2608.26693'] For nondegenerate Gaussian inputs, the optimal action decomposes as
\[
\Acal_*=\Acal_{\rm mean}+\Acal_{\rm cov},
\]
where \eqref{eq:mean-action} holds and the covariance contribution is
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\Acal_{\rm cov}
={}&\frac{\tau_0}{2}\log\det\Sigma_0
+\frac{\tau_1}{2}\log\det\Sigma_1
+\frac{\tau_1-\tau_0}{2}\log\det C_1 \\
&+\frac{\tau_0-\tau_1}{2}\log\det \mathsf S
+\frac{T}{2}\log\det(\mathsf S-r_1I).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Equivalently, if $\lambda_1(B),\ldots,\lambda_p(B)$ are the eigenvalues of $B$, then
\begin{equation}
\Acal_{\rm cov}
=\frac{\tau_0}{2}\log\det\Sigma_0
+\frac{\tau_1}{2}\log\det\Sigma_1
+\frac{\tau_1-\tau_0}{2}\log\det C_1
+\sum_{j=1}^p\Phi_{\tau_0,\tau_1}(\lambda_j(B)),
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{\tau_0,\tau_1}(t)
=\frac{\tau_0-\tau_1}{2}\log s(t)
+\frac{T}{2}\log(s(t)-r_1).
\end{equation}
In addition, $\mathsf S-r_1I\succ0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:33:01.794113+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bfa4b4498b0bc73af49a49bb563794b132dbd3602591d1600e67ea1e9f91f433","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 77 (Antipode) of arXiv:2605.14630, '2605.14630'] The \emph{antipode} $\cA:\spanF\to\spancF$ is defined inductively by $\cA(\unit)=\unit$ and 
\begin{align}
 \cA(\Gamma) &= -\Gamma - 
 \sum_{\substack{\unit\neq\overline\Gamma\subsetneq\Gamma, \overline\Gamma \in \Fminus}} 
\cA(\overline\Gamma)\cdot(\Gamma/\overline\Gamma)\\
&= - \Gamma - \cM(\cA\otimes\id) \DeltaCKred(\Gamma)\;.

\end{align} 
Here $\DeltaCKred = \DeltaCK - \Gamma\otimes\unit - \unit\otimes\Gamma$ 
denotes the \emph{reduced coproduct}, and the map $\cM:\spancF\otimes \spancF\to\spancF$ denotes multiplication, defined 
by $\cM(\Gamma_1\otimes\Gamma_2) = \Gamma_1 \cdot \Gamma_2$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:14:37.597670+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bfa62b9b6d0663cc0ee9eaaf37f804d684a0ffbbc4a65f2bbe3a923f376d4156","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 9 of arXiv:2608.18688, 'Regularity Preservation for Jump-Type Stochastic Transport Equations with Singular Drift'] A local $\mH^{m,\alpha}$-process is called a local $\mH^{m,\alpha}$-martingale if, for any $x\in\mR^d$,  the stopped process $\partial^kX_t^{\tau_n(x)}(x)$, $0\leq |k|\leq m$, $n=1,2,\cdots$ are  martingales. It is called a local $\mH^{m,\alpha}$-processes of bounded variation if, for any $x\in\mR^d$,  $\partial^kX_t^{\tau_n(x)}(x)$, $0\leq |k|\leq m$, $n=1,2,\cdots$ are bounded variation. Furthermore, it is classified as a  local  $\mH^{m,\alpha}$-semimartingale if it can be expressed as the sum of a  local  $\mH^{m,\alpha}$-martingale and a  local  $\mH^{m,\alpha}$-process of bounded variation. 	Additionally, it is designated as a continuous local  $\mH^{m,\alpha}$-semimartingale 
	if both components in the sum are continuous in $(t,x)$.

	If $\tau(x)=\infty$ for any $x$ a.s., these are called global $\mH^{m,\alpha}$-martingale etc.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:23:03.033279+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bfa79862e666fcec8ddd616ff596016a99db1517ba8b96434b3c9aa0d0b2b4eb","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 7 (Fixed-time regularity of functionals of the decoupled flow) of arXiv:2605.03057, 'Uniform-in-time quantitative fluctuations of large scale interacting particle systems'] Let Assumption \ref{ass:flow-smothness} hold. Let $ \varphi \in C_b^7\left( \R^d \right) $.
For $ t \ge 0 $, $ x \in \R^d $, and $ \mu \in \Ptwo $, let $ X_t^{x,\mu} $ be the decoupled flow
associated with the McKean--Vlasov equation, and define
\begin{equation*}
u_t\left( x,\mu \right) = \E\left( \varphi\left( X_t^{x,\mu} \right) \right).
\end{equation*}
Then, for every fixed $ t \ge 0 $, all multi-indices $ \gamma $ and $ \beta $, and
  every integer $ n \ge 0 $ with $1 \le \abs{\gamma} + \abs{\beta} + n
  \le 7$, the mixed derivative
\begin{equation*}
\partial_x^\gamma \partial_v^\beta \partial_\mu^n
u_t\left( x,\mu \right)\left( v_1,\dots,v_n \right)
\end{equation*}
exists and is continuous in $\left( x,\mu,v_1,\dots,v_n \right)$. More precisely, every such derivative is a finite sum of terms of the form
\begin{equation}

\E\left(
\nabla^r \varphi\left( X_t^{x,\mu} \right)
\left[
\partial_x^{\gamma_1} \partial_v^{\beta_1}\partial_\mu^{n_1}
X_t^{x,\mu}\left( \mathbf{v}_1 \right),\dots,
\partial_x^{\gamma_r} \partial_v^{\beta_r}\partial_\mu^{n_r}
X_t^{x,\mu}\left( \mathbf{v}_r \right)
\right]
\right),
\end{equation}
where $ 1 \le r \le 7 $, for each $1 \leq j \leq r$, $\mathbf{v}_j$
denotes an $n_j$-tuple of Lions directions, and each
$\partial_x^{\gamma_j} \partial_v^{\beta_j}\partial_\mu^{n_j}
X_t^{x,\mu}\left( \mathbf{v}_j \right)$
is a mixed derivative of the flow of positive total order $1 \le \abs{\gamma_j} + \abs{\beta_j} + n_j
  \le 7$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:06:39.287235+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bfaa7e5d5bfc67b786a7f531cece38ec1c4789ad9b553d1c789d8962c77d47b5","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:collapse of arXiv:2607.10801, 'Relaxation times of non-reversible\\ Markov processes'] For $i\in\{1,2\}$, the non-positive definite self-adjoint linear operators $({\mathcal{C}}_i,\dom({\mathcal{C}}_i))$ on $\mathbb{H}^l$ defined by 
    \begin{eqnarray*}
        \langle f,-{\mathcal{C}}_ig\rangle &=& \mathcal{E}_i^\mathcal{L}(f,g)\qquad\text{for all }f\in\dom(\mathcal{E}_i^\mathcal{L})\cap\mathbb{H}^l\text{ and }g\in\dom({\mathcal{C}}_i)\,  ,\\
        \dom({\mathcal{C}}_i) &=& \left\{  g\in \mathbb{H}^l:\exists\, h\in \mathbb{H}^l\text{ s.t.\ }\mathcal{E}_i^\mathcal{L}(f,g)=\langle f,h\rangle \text{ for all }f\in\dom(\mathcal{E}_i^\mathcal{L})\cap\mathbb{H}^l\right\}
    \end{eqnarray*}
    are called the
    \emph{symmetric first- and second-order collapse of $\mathcal{L}$}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:44:28.081548+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bfab709c969b38b676ab2b1db597811f25812b299f71dc610b14b778ebede4da","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 9 of arXiv:2607.13849, 'Rearranged Stochastic Heat Equation with an Entropy Gradient Structure'] The family $\{({\zeta}_t^{(h)})_{0 \leq t \le T}\}_{{h \in T/{\mathbb N}^*}}$ given by \eqref{interpo1} induces a tight family of probability measures on the space $\mathbb{D}([0,T],\mathcal{H}^{-2}_{\mathrm{Sym}}(\mathbb{S}))$ of c\`adl\`ag functions from $[0,T]$ to $\mathcal{H}^{-2}_{\mathrm{Sym}}(\mathbb{S})$.
In addition, there exists a constant $C$ such that, for any $h \in {T/{\mathbb N}^*}$, 
    \begin{equation}
    \begin{split}
	 \mathbb{E}\Big[ \sup_{0\leq t\leq T}\|{\zeta}_t^{(h)}\|_{2,-2}^{2} \Big]&\leq C (1+\mathbb{E}\|X_0\|_2^{2})
    \\
	\mathbb{E}\|\zeta_{t}^{(h)}-\zeta_{s}^{(h)}\|_{2,-2}^{2} &\leq  C(t-s)  + {\mathcal O}_h(1), \quad 0 \leq s < t \leq T,
    \end{split}
\end{equation}
where the Landau term 
${\mathcal O}_h(1)$ tends to $0$ with $h$, and is independent of the parameters 
$s$ and $t$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

