{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:32:28.992626+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c060aac79136828e4a5414a85f06b259575785e7ad8e7ae4f4f45fa9fcc9b660","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:degree-three-passage of arXiv:2608.23545, 'Eulerian walkers on $\Z^2$ have range exponent $2/3$'] Let $G$ be a doubly periodic graph in $\mathbb R^2$ of maximum degree three, equipped with an
		arbitrary rotor mechanism and independent uniform initial rotors.
		There are constants $c,C>0$ such that, for every directed edge $u\to v$ and
		every integer $R\geq1$,
		\begin{equation}
			\mathbb P\left\{
			\begin{array}{c}
				\text{a live path starts with $u\to v$ and contains a vertex}\\
