{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:42:57.240174+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c3f3a6167aed2bd8803c98f45501bd3628c5ed2b8fa58363ba74d4b0433accc4","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eqn:corrisexpdist of arXiv:2608.19082, 'Learning Random Geometric Graphs Drawn in Probabilistic Metric Spaces'] $\delta({\cal G}_{S, \boldsymbol{V}}(\boldsymbol{R}^{(m)}, \tau), {\cal G}_{S, \boldsymbol{V}}(\boldsymbol{R}^{(m^{\prime})}, \tau))$, is computed using the set of probability values that are computed using samples (generated in $n_{i,j}$ trials, with Rejection Sampling) of the edge variables relevant to each RGG, where we normalise each computed probability by the maximal - out of all computed - probability values. Additionally, the inter-observable correlation matrices of datasets ${\bf D}_{\boldsymbol{W}^{(m)}}$ and ${\bf D}_{\boldsymbol{W}^{(m^{\prime})}}$ can be learnt using the closed-form posterior density of the inter-observable correlation matrix discussed in Section~(sec:corr of the paper). Then the $m,m^{\prime}$-th element of the inter-component correlation $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_m$ is modelled as:

