{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:53:30.651483+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c448cd39977da9ecdad807e18bb1d6fcb11157078d8a14cd135cc94aa8f40e22","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn Pure Partition Functions of arXiv:2506.01306, 'Asymptotic of Coulomb gas integral, Temperley-Lieb type algebras and pure partition functi'] The functions $\mathcal{Z}_\alpha: \mathfrak{X}_n \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^+$, indexed by link patterns $\alpha \in \mathrm{LP}(n,m)$, are called \emph{pure partition functions}. They are a collection of positive solutions to the null vector equation, subject to boundary conditions specified by their asymptotic behavior, which is determined by the link pattern $\alpha$
\begin{itemize}
    \item[\textnormal{(ASY)}] \textbf{Asymptotics:} For all $\alpha \in \mathrm{LP}_n$, $j \in \{1, \ldots, n\}$, and $\xi \in (\theta_{j-1}, \theta_{j+2})$, the following limit exists:
    \[
    \lim_{\theta_j, \theta_{j+1} \rightarrow \xi} \frac{\mathcal{Z}_\alpha\left(\theta_1, \ldots, \theta_n\right)}{(\theta_{j+1} - \theta_j)^{\frac{6 - \kappa}{\kappa}}}
    =
    \begin{cases}
        0, & \text{if } \{j, j+1\} \notin \alpha, \\
        \mathcal{Z}_{\hat{\alpha}}\left(\theta_1, \ldots, \theta_{j-1}, \theta_{j+2}, \ldots, \theta_n\right), & \text{if } \{j, j+1\} \in \alpha,
    \end{cases}
    \]
\end{itemize}
where $\hat{\alpha} = \alpha / \{j, j+1\} \in \mathrm{LP}(n-2,m-1)$ denotes the link pattern obtained from $\alpha$ by removing the link $\{j, j+1\}$ and relabeling the remaining indices as $1, 2, \ldots, n-2$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:26:02.555746+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c448d7aaf0fa073be7cebdacc8a8243e5aa3591b8c28094b0d931fd66b009ec5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition decC of arXiv:2608.30747, 'Compact homogeneous complex manifolds'] As a manifold, any C-space as in (equation gprime of the paper) admits the following decomposition: 
\begin{equation*}
M=G/K=\widetilde{G}/\widetilde{H}\times \underline{G}/\underline{K}\times\overline{G}, 
\end{equation*}
where $\widetilde{G}/\widetilde{H}$ is a flag manifold, $\underline{G}/\underline{K}=\underline{G}_f/\underline{K}\times T^z$ is a strict C-space and $\overline{G}$ is a compact semisimple Lie group. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:25:40.077290+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c44c38170877359c0f2219d25c1b137398254ce34fef61aff1b79d29f0e74b56","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 25 (\cite{LecturesonPP}, Theorem~12.7) of arXiv:2510.26216, 'A functional Breuer-Major theorem with Poisson noise'] Suppose $f_i \in L^1\left(\mathbb{R}^{a_i}\right)$ for $i \in\{1, \ldots, \ell\}$. If
        \begin{equation*}
            \int_{\mathbb{R}^{|\sigma|}}\left(\otimes_{i=1}^\ell|f_{i}|\right)_\sigma dx<\infty, \quad \forall\sigma \in \Pi(\vec{a}),
        \end{equation*}
    then we have
        \begin{equation*}
            \mathbf{E} \bigg[\prod_{i=1}^{\ell} I_{a_i}\left(f_i\right) \bigg] = \sum_{\sigma \in \Pi_{\geq 2} (\vec{a})} \int_{\mathbb{R}^{|\sigma|}} \left( \otimes_{i=1}^\ell  f_{i}\right)_\sigma dx.
        \end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:12:46.875146+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c44d34dd2f7101b324b57f13cf9392bf4571be55a1c50e770885d0c6234fe683","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm_var_est of arXiv:2608.08529, 'Maximum Spanning Trees of Random Geometric Graphs With Independent Edge Weights'] Let~\(\epsilon_1,\epsilon_2\) be as in~((f_eq of the paper)) and suppose that~\(nr_n^2p_n \geq M\log{n}\) for some constant~\(M > 0.\) Also suppose that the ccdf scaling condition~((dilpax of the paper)) in Theorem~(thm_mst_max of the paper)\((b)\) holds for some constant~\(s > 3\) strictly. There is a constant~\(\gamma> 0\) such that for any~\(M > \gamma,\)~\(L = L(n) >0\) and~\(2 < s_1 < s,\) the variance~\[var(\tau_n) \leq  \gamma n (\sigma^2_{loc} + \sigma^2_{wt}),\] where
\begin{equation}
\sigma^2_{loc} :=  (nr_n^2p_n)^2 \left(L^2 + n^{4}r_n^2p_n F_c(L) + n^2F_c^{1-2/s_1}(L)\right) + n^{6}e^{-\gamma^{-1}nr_n^2p_n}
\end{equation} is the scaled contribution due to randomness in the vertex locations and \[\sigma^2_{wt} := H^2(nr_n^2p_n)\] is the scaled contribution due to randomness in edge states and weights. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:17.369917+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c44d72afcbf02656c83f1fc2b3c2f9ecae484311c7c3887b04f22a1af9285366","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 18 of arXiv:2606.03534, 'Construction and simulation of a path-valued model of dendrite development'] Define the processes
    \begin{align*}
        A_t = \int a \, d\mu_t \,, \quad I_t = \int 1-a \, d\mu_t \,,
    \end{align*}
    which count the number of active and inactive branches respectively. 
    Then $A_t$ is equal to the number of dendrite tips and $I_t$ is equal to the number of branch points.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:22:20.960533+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c44dc5ef4ccbf0f3718a5ca8297b4c4597895b204293ba7e9775abc4a9a0bbf9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 24 of arXiv:2604.20634, 'Distributional Statistical Models:\\ \large Weak Moments, Cumulants, and a Central Limit T'] Let $\varphi \in \mathcal{S}(\R)$ with $\varphi(x) > 0$ for all~$x$, and suppose that there exist constants $a > 0$ and $C > 0$ such that
\begin{equation}
  \varphi(x) \leq C\,e^{-a|x|}
  \qquad \text{for all } x \in \R.
\end{equation}
Let $f_1, f_2$ be measurable functions with
$f_1 - f_2 \in L^2(\R, \varphi(x)\,dx)$.  If
\[
  \int_{\R} x^n f_1(x)\varphi(x)\,dx
  = \int_{\R} x^n f_2(x)\varphi(x)\,dx
  \qquad \text{for all } n \geq 0,
\]
then $f_1 = f_2$ almost everywhere.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:51:30.072214+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c44dc87bd6e4f782e127b0403ef44dbffd660699748fe2e1f97205354b17b240","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 24 of arXiv:2606.01832, 'MA(1) processes with uniform innovations conditioned to stay positive in the non-expanding'] In the orange region, for $m\ge1$, it holds that
