{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:13:31.923803+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c48e7d0cc5b23e6dd11ad8c6c0dd55609b73781836943426028f08b2d7ff8261","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm critical_weights_unique of arXiv:2506.12171, 'The multinomial dimer model'] {KenyonPohoata}}]
    Fix a finite graph $G = (V,E)$, a weight function $w: E \to \mathbb{R}_+$, $\alpha_v \in [0,1]$ feasible and a sequence of multiplicites ${\mathbf N}$ with $N_v/K\to \alpha_v$ as ${\mathbf N}\to \infty$ for all $v\in V$. Then there exist a \textit{critical weight function} $\mathbf x:V\to \mathbb{R}_+$ such that for each $v\in V$,
\begin{equation}
\sum_{u:(u,v)=e\in E} w_e x_u x_v  = \alpha_v P(\mathbf x).
\end{equation}
Further, the critical weights $\mathbf x$ are unique up to the operation of multiplying by a constant on all black vertices,
and the inverse of this constant on white vertices. In particular, the \textit{critical edge weights} $w_e x_u x_v$ for $(u,v)\in E$ are unique. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:52:08.671335+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c49318f6c319563ee734b109f003dd679deb5b9c49ce5eb6a638a09d3fea41dc","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 30 (Scalar quotient recursion and convergence) of arXiv:2608.30027, 'Under Ricci flow, a 3-torus goes flat'] Fix \(C_0\geq0\), and let \(b_k\geq0\), \(k\geq0\), be a summable
sequence.  Suppose that the sequences \(E_k\geq0\) and \(\ell_k>0\),
\(k\geq0\), satisfy
\begin{equation}
        E_{k+1}\leq \frac{100E_k}{1+99E_k/2}+C_0b_k,
        \qquad
        \ell_{k+1}\leq(1+C_0b_k)
        \left(\frac{1+99E_k/2}{100}\right)^{1/2}\ell_k.
\end{equation}
Then \(\sup_kE_k<\infty\) and \(\sup_k\ell_k<\infty\), with a bound depending
only on \(E_0\), \(\ell_0\), \(C_0\), and \(\sum_k b_k\).  If, in addition,
\begin{equation}
        \inf_k\ell_k>0,
\end{equation}
then there is a number \(\ell_\infty>0\) such that
\begin{equation}
        \ell_k\longrightarrow\ell_\infty,
        \qquad E_k\longrightarrow2.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:08.432598+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c495d09dcec973ca1db454600025369fdf67e22ecef168fff0ca8aef44483f07","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma SemiHyperplanes of arXiv:2609.02180, 'Helly-Type Theorems for Splitting Point Sets \thanks{A preliminary version of this article'] For any positive integer $d$, and any positive numbers $\alpha\in (0,1/2]$ and $\epsilon\in (0,\alpha)$, one can fix a positive integer $c=c(\epsilon,d)<\infty$ with the following property.

For any finite point set $P$ in ${\mathbb R}^d$, there exists a semi-algebraic set $\Gamma_{\alpha,\epsilon}(P)\subseteq {\mathbb R}^d$, whose description complexity is bounded by $c$, so that the following statements hold.

\begin{enumerate}
	\item Any $\alpha$-splitting hyperplane $h$ for $P$ that does not pass through the origin yields a point $h^\ast\in \Gamma_{\alpha,\epsilon}(P)$.
	\item Any point $(a_1,\ldots,a_d)\in \Gamma_{\alpha,\epsilon}(P)$ describes a hyperplane that $(\alpha- \epsilon)$-splits $P$ (and does not pass through the origin).
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:02:11.259607+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c49adaafbfe2937aaea670f09a71bf0384e698774853306d7af98def5865ea7a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:stability_condition of arXiv:2509.08430, 'One-dimensional particle clouds with elastic collisions'] Let $N \in {\mathbb N}$, $m_i \in (0,\infty)$ for all $i \in [N]$,
and $a_i, b_i \in {\mathbb R}_+$ for all $i \in [N]$.
With $U_k, M_k$ defined at~(equation eq:U-M-def of the paper), suppose that
\begin{equation}

\frac{U_k}{M_k}<\frac{U_N}{M_N} , \text{ for all } k \in [N-1].
\end{equation}
Then the system is a single stable cloud in the following sense.
\begin{thmenumi}[label=(\alph*)]
    \item 

    {\bf {Limiting speed.}}
For every $i \in [N]$, there holds the strong law of large numbers 
    \begin{equation}

 \lim_{t \to \infty} t^{-1} {X_i (t)} = - \frac{U_{N}}{M_{N}}, \ \text{a.s.}     \end{equation}
    \item 

    {\bf {Relative stability.}} 
      There exist constants $C \in {\mathbb R}_+$ and $\delta > 0$ (depending on the $a_i, b_i$, and $m_i$)
    such that 
 \[
\sup_{t \in {\mathbb R}_+} \Pr \left[ \Delta_{[N]} (t) \geq s \right] \leq C \Bigl[ 1 + {\mathrm{e}}^{C \Delta_{[N]} (0) } \Bigr] {\mathrm{e}}^{-\delta s} , \text{ for all } s \in {\mathbb R}_+,      \]
    and, moreover,
    \[ \limsup_{t \to \infty} \frac{\Delta_{[N]} (t)}{\log t} < \infty , \ \text{a.s.}\]
\end{thmenumi}
Furthermore, condition~(equation eq:stability_condition of the paper) is necessary for~(thm:stability_condition-b of the paper) to hold. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

