{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:38:23.501836+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c4c0a6a104c65f199a0f87342476bb3b0a3c5074c0dacd57586f3a87356fe0ba","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:necessary-rigid of arXiv:2510.18392, 'Hyperuniformity of random measures, transport and rigidity'] Assume the spectral density $    \mathsf s$ satisfies for some $ c,p,\varepsilon >0$:\begin{itemize}
\item $    \mathsf s$ has finitely many zeros $ u_{ 1},\dots ,u_{ m}\in \mathbb{R}^{ d}$ and they have finite order, i.e. 
\begin{align*}
 \int_{B(u_{ i},\varepsilon )}\frac{ \|u-u_{ i}\|^{ p}}{   \mathsf s(u)}du<\infty 
\end{align*}
\item $    \mathsf s$ does not vanish too fast at $ \infty $: for $ u \notin \bigcup _{ i}B(u_{ i},\varepsilon )$, $ 
