{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:56:36.590964+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c4f1c57620c5304694d5b9bddecac982910920525c1925b795e77834004cfc84","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma WPD-NHS-AA of arXiv:2608.30582, 'The flexible part of intermittent Onsager theorem for the two-dimensional stochastic incom'] For any $N \in \left\{ {0,1, \cdots ,10} \right\}$ and $t \in \mathop  \cap \limits_{j = 0}^{q} \left[ {0,{\mathfrak{t}_j}} \right] \cap \left[ {{t_k},{t_{k + 1}}} \right)$, there exists a real number $\alpha \in \left( {\frac{\beta }{{b}},\beta } \right)$ such that the backward flow ${{\tilde \Phi }_k}\left( {t,x} \right)$ and the Lagrangian flow ${{\tilde X}_t}$ satisfy
\begin{equation}
{\left\| {\nabla {{\tilde \Phi }_k}} \right\|_N} + {\left\| {{{\left( {\nabla {{\tilde \Phi }_k}} \right)}^{ - 1}}} \right\|_N}  \lesssim \lambda _q^N \lambda _{q + 1}^{ - \alpha }   ,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
{\left\| {D{{\tilde X}_k}} \right\|_N} \lesssim  \max \left\{ {1,\lambda _q^N \lambda _{q + 1}^{ - \alpha }} \right\}  ,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
{\left\| {{{\bar D}_{t,\Gamma }}\nabla {{\tilde \Phi }_k}} \right\|_N} + {\left\| {{{\bar D}_{t,\Gamma }}{{\left( {\nabla {{\tilde \Phi }_k}} \right)}^{ - 1}}} \right\|_N} \lesssim \delta _q^{\frac{1}{2}}\lambda _q^{N + 1}   ,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
{\left\| {{\partial _{tt}}{{\tilde \Phi }_k}} \right\|_N} \lesssim \delta _q^{\frac{1}{2}}\lambda _q^{N + 1}  ,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
{\left\| {{\partial _{tt}}{{\left( {\nabla {{\tilde \Phi }_k}} \right)}^{ - 1}}} \right\|_N} \lesssim \delta _q^{\frac{1}{2}} \lambda _q^{N + 3}   ,
\end{equation}
where ${{\bar D}_{t,\Gamma }} \triangleq {\partial _t} + {{\bar v}_{q,\Gamma }} \cdot \nabla $ is the material derivative corresponding to ${{\bar v}_{q,\Gamma }}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:44.528677+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c4f235472beaf69cb28b30b7c114832c3e9c25a17a909d90d4d29c5d3ff09fc7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 7 of arXiv:2604.01852, 'The on/off Brownian snake'] Let $(\mathcal{Z}^{(\rm d)}_r, \mathcal{Z}^{(\rm a)}_r)_{r \geq 0}$ be the process defined in Theorem \ref{theorem ooSBM ffd convergence} and let $s \geq s' \geq 0$. Firstly we have $\text{supp}(\mu_{\rm d}) \subseteq \text{supp}(\mathcal{Z}^{(\rm d)}_s)$ $\mathbb{P}_{(\mu_{\rm d}, \mu_{\rm a})}$-a.s. and secondly we have $\text{supp}(\mathcal{Z}^{(\rm d)}_{s'}) \subseteq \text{supp}(\mathcal{Z}^{(\rm d)}_s)$ $\mathbb{P}_{(\mu_{\rm d}, \mu_{\rm a})}$-a.s.
    \begin{proof}
        The first part follows immediately from the construction of $\mathcal{Z}^{(\rm d)}_s$ in Theorem \ref{theorem ooSBM ffd convergence}. The second part follows from the first part and the Markov property of ooSBM.
