{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:25:49.877338+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c55b848f5169db5c85e949cd51e0c8d3ab1ed4e71fbee313f2ab4a72989e901e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 of arXiv:2609.02065, '\textbf{From Bowditch's question to Goldman's conjecture for type-preserving representatio'] \cite[Theorem 1.1]{ryu}
    Let $\Sigma=\Sigma_{g,p}$ be a connected and oriented punctured surface with genus $g\geqslant 1$ and $p\geqslant 1$ punctures.  Let $n\in \mathbb{Z}$ and $s\in \{\pm 1\}^p.$ Then the space $\mathcal R_n^s(\Sigma)$ of type-preserving representations with relative Euler class $n$ and sign $s$  is non-empty if and only if the pair $(n,s)$ satisfies the following inequality: 
    $$\chi(\Sigma) + p_+(s)\leqslant n\leqslant -\chi(\Sigma) - p_-(s).$$
    Each non-empty $\mathcal R_n^s(\Sigma)$ above is connected. Further, a representation $\rho$ is a holonomy representation of the surface iff $e(\rho)=|\chi(\Sigma)|$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:36:23.137124+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c55fb6644fd4474d53a77264cbf599c6c09dd8cb04bb60efe7b0d23d087a58b0","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem eq:strassens of arXiv:2507.01236, 'Disintegration theorem for multifunctions, \\ with applications to empirical Wasserstein d'] Let $(\mathfrak{X}, \mathcal{A}, \mu)$ and $(\mathfrak{Y}, \mathcal{B}, \nu)$ be Polish probability spaces, and let $\omega$ be a nonempty, closed subset of $\mathfrak{X} \times \mathfrak{Y}$.  Then there exists a probability measure $\lambda$ on $\mathfrak{X}\times \mathfrak{Y}$ (with its Borel $\sigma$-algebra) with marginals $\mu$ and $\nu$ such that $\lambda(\omega) = 1$ if and only if
    \begin{equation}
    \mu\big(\pi_{\mathfrak{X}}(\omega\cap(\mathfrak{X}\times U))\big)\geq \nu(U)
    \end{equation}
    for all open sets $U\subseteq \mathfrak{Y}$, where $\pi_{\mathfrak{X}}:\mathfrak{X}\times \mathfrak{Y}\rightarrow \mathfrak{X}$ is the projection of $\mathfrak{X} \times \mathfrak{Y}$ onto $\mathfrak{X}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:20:07.940222+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c561b81ddd2e2815958298f7a804185cbba6da745997e1eb61ba51b331ba9c01","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:lines of arXiv:2608.30254, 'Exact Recovery Thresholds for Weighted Data Selection\\ in Vector-Valued Linear Regression'] Let the features span $\mathbb{R}^d$ and $\tau(D)=(m+1)d=k$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item every data point with $x_i\ne0$ belongs to some positive circuit of
  dyads supported on a single feature line and of size exactly $m+1$;
\item there is no positive circuit of dyads whose features span a subspace of
  dimension $\ge2$, and no line circuit of size $\le m$; in particular no
  point has $x_i\ne0$ and $\rho_i=0$;
\item the nonzero features lie on exactly $d$ linearly independent lines.
\end{enumerate}
Consequently, any dataset whose nonzero features are \emph{not} of this
$d$-line form satisfies $\tau(D)\le(m+1)d-1$ and is recovered exactly within
budget $(m+1)d-1$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.602962+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c561edd84b3190de994c555e0d02818e997432c48290e599257ad4f719ef4104","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 44 of arXiv:2607.07283, 'Multi-time Markov renewal chains and stratified renewal theorems'] For $x\in C$, the first-entrance kernel into $D$ is

\begin{equation*}
g_D=\psi^C*q^{C,D}.
\end{equation*}

In particular,

\begin{equation*}
g_D(k)(x,D)=\Pp_x\left(\tau_D<\infty,\ S_{\tau_D}=k\right),
\qquad k\in\N^d.
\end{equation*}

Consequently, for an initial law $\nu$ on $C$, the distribution of the first entrance over the lower rectangle with upper corner $k$ is

\begin{equation*}
F_{\nu,D}(k)=\int_C\sum_{r\leqd k}g_D(r)(x,D)\nu(\dd x),
\end{equation*}

and the reliability surface is

\begin{equation*}
R_{\nu,D}(k)=1-F_{\nu,D}(k).
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:38:13.630984+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c56448f219cd33ee1f0f94e8f7bca9f3e504f3b8dcc404f91b7e33b2fb6a1731","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 12 of arXiv:2609.00598, 'Supercritical mean--field limit for magnetized Hamiltonian dynamics'] With the same assumptions and notations of {\rm Theorem {\rm\ref{main thm 1}}}, the following identity holds for all $t\in \mathbb{R}_{+}${\rm:}
\begin{align}
\frac{\dd}{\dd t}\mathcal{E}_{\varepsilon}(t)=&-\int_{\mathbb{T}^{2}\times \mathbb{R}^{2}} (\xi-v)^{\otimes 2}:D^{\mathbf{s}}_xvf_{\varepsilon}\ \dd x\dd \xi +\int_{\mathbb{T}^{2}\times \mathbb{R}^{2}} (\xi-v)\cdot \nabla_{x} p f_{\varepsilon}\ \dd x\dd \xi \notag
\\
&+\varepsilon\int_{\mathbb{T}^{2}} D^{\mathbf{s}}_{x}v:\nabla_{x} \Phi_{\varepsilon}^{\otimes 2}\ \dd x. 

\end{align}
Consequently, the following estimate holds{\rm:} 
\begin{align}
\mathcal{E}_{\varepsilon}(t)\leq \mathcal{E}_{\varepsilon}(0)+2\left\Vert D^{\mathbf{s}}v\right\Vert_{\infty} \int_{0}^{t}\mathcal{E}_{\varepsilon}(\tau)\ \dd \tau+O(\sqrt{\varepsilon}) 
\qquad \mbox{for all $t\in [0,T]$},      
\end{align}
so that
\begin{align}
 \underset{t\in [0,T]}{\sup}\mathcal{E}_{\varepsilon}(t)\underset{\varepsilon\rightarrow 0}{\longrightarrow}0 
 \qquad \mbox{provided}\ \mathcal{E}_{\varepsilon}(0)\underset{\varepsilon\rightarrow 0}{\longrightarrow }0.      
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:57:59.894615+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c565b2695729c582deb44fe038bcbe089a5bc335651a34891e58684a9f5425af","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition of arXiv:2607.09946, 'Statistics on Yau's conjecture: Variance asymptotics'] Let $(M,g)$ be a compact Riemannian manifold and let $0< \eta_\ell\le \ell$ be a real sequence such that $\exists\lim_{\ell\to +\infty} \ell^{-1}\eta_\ell =1-\alpha $. Assume that $\varepsilon_\ell=\varepsilon(\phi_{I_\ell}^M)<1$. Then, 
\begin{equation} 
\mathbb{E}\left\{ Y_M([\ell-\eta_\ell,\ell]) \right\}=1+\mathrm{O}(\varepsilon_\ell).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:49:15.411300+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c566f8034e68545a6c9f400503089a7d89e2e2688b4cb59b577f1d35280174d6","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Determinant lemma of arXiv:2608.27248, 'Optimal Transport and the ABP Method in Higher Codimension'] Set $Z=\nabla^\Sigma\log f+H\in\mathbb{R}^{n+4}$.  For every $(x,y)\in\mathcal{C}$,
\begin{equation}
 \operatorname{tr} Q_x(y)=nf(x)^{\frac{1}{n-1}}
 -\big(h_{\mathcal E}(Z(x))+\langle Z(x),\Phi(x,y)\rangle\big),
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 0\leq\bigl(\det Q_x(y)\bigr)^{\frac{1}{n}}
 \leq\frac{\operatorname{tr} Q_x(y)}n\leq f(x)^{\frac{1}{n-1}}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:44:27.968984+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c566fc9d41f4292cf924c2b0eb4fbf8beeac5f401418b5cd4ae286c564b6cbc3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2607.13273, 'Asymptotics of transition densities for L\'evy processes with stochastic resetting'] Let $\boldsymbol{Y}$ be the Brownian motion in $\Rd.$ Then
    for all $\lambda>0, t>0$ the density $p_\lambda$ of the
    corresponding process $\boldsymbol{X}$ with resetting exists and, for any $x\in\Rd$,
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item For every $M>\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\lambda}}$ we have
        \begin{equation}
          p_\lambda(t,x,y) \sim \frac{\lambda^{\frac{d+1}{4}}}{2^{\frac{d+1}{4}}\pi^{\frac{d-1}{2}}} \frac{e^{-\sqrt{2\lambda}|y|}}{|y|^{\frac{d-1}{2}}}, \quad \text{ on } \quad \frac{t}{|y|}\geq M.
        \end{equation}
        \item For every $0<  M <\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\lambda}}$ we have
        \begin{equation}
            p_\lambda(t,x,y) \sim e^{-\lambda t}p(t,y) \left(\frac{2\lambda}{(|y|/t)^2-2\lambda}+e^{\frac{-|x|^2+2\langle x,y
            \rangle}{2t}}\right) 
            , \quad \text{ on } \quad 0< \frac{t}{|y|} \leq M.
        \end{equation}
        \item For $\frac{t}{|y|}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\lambda}}$ we get
        \[
             p_\lambda(t,x,y) \sim \frac{\lambda^{\frac{d+1}{4}}}{2\cdot 2^{\frac{d+1}{4}}\pi^{\frac{d-1}{2}}} \frac{e^{-\sqrt{2\lambda}|y|}}{|y|^{\frac{d-1}{2}}}.
        \]
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:24:25.990681+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c56acb70617d95029d03012549bec5bc38884156f438889167ae628a64926ed4","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 98 (Mixing time) of arXiv:2507.07702, 'Observables of random spanning trees in random environment'] Define the (uniform) mixing time as
    \begin{equation*}
    \tmix(G, \weight) = \tmix := \min \left\{ t \geq 0 : \max\limits_{u,v \in V} \left| \frac{q_t(u,v)}{\pi(v)} - 1\right| \leq \frac{1}{2}\right\}.
