{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:05:28.197718+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c5ae5ebcb809c5705f5404bcdc4ac28d8316871f98d3657a19548f663e616d4d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: continuum Z introduction of arXiv:2602.04429, 'Disordered systems and (subcritical) polynomial chaos\\ with heavy-tail disorder'] Let \(\boldsymbol\zeta\) be a \(\gamma\)-stable Lévy white noise with \(\gamma \in (1,2)\).
Assume that there is some \(q\in (\gamma,2]\) such that for every $C \, \geqslant\, 0$,
\begin{equation}

	\sum_{k\geq 1} C^k \|\boldsymbol\psi^{(k)}\|_{q} <+\infty \,,
\end{equation}
where \(\|\cdot\|_{q}\) denotes the symmetric \(L^q \)-norm (see its definition (equation eq: def of symmetric norms of the paper) below).
Then, the continuum partition function 
\begin{equation}

	{\ensuremath{\mathcal Z}}_{\Omega}^{\boldsymbol\zeta,\hat{\beta}} = 1 + \sum\limits_{k \, \geqslant\, 1} \frac{\hat{\beta}^k}{k!} \int_{\Omega^k} \boldsymbol\psi^{(k)}(x_1,\dots,x_k) \prod\limits_{j=1}^k \boldsymbol\zeta(\mathrm{d} x_j)
\end{equation}
is a well-defined (and non-degenerate) \(\boldsymbol\zeta\)-measurable random variable in \(\mathbb{L}^p\) for every $ p \in [0,\gamma) $. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:09.066503+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c5b3258e4e646f76680b54106b1885fcfa5c3f960e2d786c03c5c782ba2b539c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 8 of arXiv:2606.27520, 'Critical curve of loop percolation on the $d$-regular tree'] Let $\kappa\geq \kappa_c$. For a finite subset of vertices $F$,
\begin{equation}
    \mu_\kappa(\ell : \ell \cap F \neq \emptyset)
    =
    \log \det\big( G^\kappa|_{F} \big),
\end{equation}
where $G^\kappa$ is the Green function viewed as a matrix $(G^\kappa(x,y))_{x,y}$, and $G^\kappa|_{F}$ is its submatrix indexed by $F \times F$.
In particular, for $n$ distinct vertices $v_1, \ldots, v_n$,
\begin{equation}
    \mu_\kappa\big( \ell : v_i \in \ell \text{ for } i \in \{1,\ldots,n\} \big)
    =
    \sum_{\substack{A \subset \{v_1,\ldots,v_n\} \\ A \neq \emptyset}}
    (-1)^{\# A + 1}
    \log \det\big( G^\kappa|_{A} \big).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:27:14.949591+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c5b6f80b337d1ac846d769f2e58327853659130ea4359568592cb2c01507916f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 7 of arXiv:2608.24352, 'Second moments associated with finite free multiplicative convolution'] \cite[Theorem 6.2.3, Remark 6.2.4] {ceccherini1945harmonic}
	Let $0\leq h\leq \min(k,l)$ and $\lambda = (k+l-h,h)$. Let $I$ be a $k$-element subset of $[k+l]$. Write $\delta_{I,x}$ for the function on $S_{k+l}$ whose value on $\sigma \in S_{k+l}$ is 1 if $|\sigma(I) \cap I| = k-x$ and 0 otherwise. The spherical function $\omega_h$ associated to the irreducible representation of $S_{k+l}$ indexed by the partition $\lambda$ 
	\begin{equation*}
		\omega_h = \sum_{x=0}^{\min(k,l)} Q_h(x;l,k) \delta_{I,x},
	\end{equation*}
	where the $Q_h(x;l,k)$ are a family of orthogonal polynomials known as {\rm Hahn polynomials}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:32:18.845391+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c5b9da70ea1a68531bd11299c86edc425cb75f43dd904f6f413909d625a680d8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 6 (Expansion of the collision operator) of arXiv:2609.03847, 'A counterexample to McKean's conjecture for the Landau-Coulomb equation'] The collision operator has the expansion
	\begin{equation}
		Q(f_R,f_R)(v)	=
		\bigl(Q_{R,1}+Q_{R,2}+Q_{R,3}\bigr)(v/R)	+
		\mathcal E_R^Q(v) \qquad\text{for all }v \in \Omega_R^{\rm out},
	\end{equation}
	where
	\begin{equation}
		Q_{R,1}	=R^{-K-6}\frac{\Pi^\perp_v}{|v|}:\nabla^2G,
		\qquad	Q_{R,2}	=\frac{R^{-K-8}}{|v|^3}
		\lp3\frac{v\otimes v}{|v|^2}-\Id	\rp:\nabla^2G,
		\qquad	Q_{R,3} 	=R^{-2K-6}Q(G,G).
	\end{equation}
	For every $N\geq0$, there exist constants $c_N,C_N>0$ such that, for all
	sufficiently large $R$,
	\begin{equation}
		\int_{\Omega_R^{\rm out}}\left(
		\frac{|\mathcal E_R^Q(v)|^2}{g_R(v)}
		+	|\mathcal E_R^Q(v)|\langle v\rangle^N	\right)\d v
		\leq
		C_N \exp \lp -c_NR^{6/5} \rp.
	\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:09:23.969825+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c5ba064614d198201139c3c335c2a10343a0a0a1c8ae0df995928ca59b33a1b4","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 20 (Verification theorem) of arXiv:2607.22416, 'A multi-player optimal stopping game based on a spider Brownian bridge'] Suppose that for $j \neq i$, Player $j$ uses the threshold strategy $\tau_j :=\inf\{s\geq 0: R_{s}\geq  d_j \sqrt{1-s}, \Theta_{s} = \theta_j \}$. Under Assumptions~\ref{ass:Gamma_N} and \ref{ass:spidersupermartingale}, the value function $\overline{V} = \overline{V}(t,r,\theta)$ defined in \eqref{Valuefunc_P1_E} coincides with the value function $V_{*} = V_{*}(t,r,\theta)$ given by \eqref{Expression_VE_star}. Moreover, the stopping time
