{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.827199+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c608bd48c7a20d926ef0eb1603268fb29f1f05d84622f25f720ce4a2f89c703a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:conditional-limit of arXiv:2609.01690, 'Iterated-sumset spectra: The complete exponent law and its rank geometry'] Fix $k\geq3$, and take the limits first as $q\to\infty$ and then as
$h\to\infty$.  If $A$ is uniform in $\binom{[q]}k$, conditional on $A$
not being a $B_h$-set, then
\begin{equation}
 \frac{\tau(A)}h\ \Longrightarrow\ X_k,
 \qquad \mathbb P(X_k\leq t)=t^{k-2}\quad(0\leq t\leq1).
\end{equation}
Equivalently, $X_k$ has the $\operatorname{Beta}(k-2,1)$ law.  Moreover,
\begin{equation}
 \frac{|hA|}{M_{h,k}}\ \Longrightarrow\
 Y_k:=1-(1-X_k)^{k-1},
\end{equation}
whose distribution function is
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb P(Y_k\leq y)=
 \left[1-(1-y)^{1/(k-1)}\right]^{k-2}
 \qquad(0\leq y\leq1).
\end{equation}
Furthermore,
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb E Y_k
 =1-\frac{(k-2)!(k-1)!}{(2k-3)!}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:58:40.816432+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c6093902c9a0861567da82fac67939a1dc41bec8d49a68951aa1a1e7580e716e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem local1 of arXiv:2605.10309, 'Exponential Decay of $L^2$-Solutions to Stochastic Nonlinear Schr\"odinger Equations Drive'] Assume (H1) and (H2), and let $1<\alpha <1+\frac{4}{d}$.
Then for any $x\in L^2$ and $0<T<\infty$, there exists a sequence of $L^2$-solutions $(X_n,\tau_n), n\in\mathbb{N}$ of (equation SNLS of the paper) where $(\tau_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ is a sequence of increasing stopping times.
For every $n\ge 1$, it holds ${
{\mathbf{P}}\text{-a.s.}}$ that
\begin{align}
X_n|_{[0,\tau_n]}\in C([0,\tau_n];L^2)\cap L^{q}(0,\tau_n;L^{\alpha+1}),
\end{align}
and uniqueness holds in $C([0,\tau_n];L^2)\cap L^{q}(0,\tau_n;L^{\alpha+1})$. Here, $(q,\alpha+1)$ is a Strichartz pair, where $q=\frac{4(\alpha+1)}{d(\alpha-1)}\in (2+\frac{4}{d},\infty).$
Moreover, defining $\displaystyle \tau^*(x)=\lim_{n\to\infty}\tau_n$ and $\displaystyle X=\lim_{n\to\infty}X_n\mathbf{1}_{[0,\tau^*(x))}$, we have the blowup alternative, that is, for ${
{\mathbf{P}}\text{-a.s.}} \ \omega$, if $\tau^*(x)(\omega)<T$, then
\[ \lim_{t\to \tau^*(x)(\omega)}\|X(t)(\omega)\|_{L^2}=\infty. \] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:45:17.650532+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c609795893489b3d6fcdfa284fbc95f893ab307c67552277cefc442648ac36df","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eqn: IIC-conv of arXiv:2604.08462, 'Convergence of $k$-point functions in\\ high dimensional percolation'] Let $\mathbf{e}_1=(1,0,\ldots,0)$. The Incipient Infinite Cluster measure $\nu_x$ is defined by
    \begin{equation}
    \nu_x = \lim_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{P}(\cdot \mid x \leftrightarrow  n \mathbf{e}_1) \ ,
    \end{equation}
    where the limit is in the weak sense. We write $\nu = \nu_0$.
    In [CCHS1], it was shown that the point-to-point conditioning of (equation eqn: IIC-conv of the paper) may be replaced by conditioning on any long open connection from $x$. If $(V_n)$ and $(\mathcal{D}_n)$ are sequences of subsets of $\mathbb{Z}^d$ with $\limsup_n V_n = \limsup_n \mathcal{D}_n = \varnothing$, and if $x$ and $V_n$ are in the same connected component of $\mathbb{Z}^d \setminus \mathcal{D}_n$ for all $n$, we have
    \begin{equation}

        \nu_x = \lim_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{P}\left( \cdot\  \left|\, x \sa{\mathbb{Z}^d \setminus \mathcal{D}_n} V_n\right.\right)\ .
    \end{equation}

    The cluster of $x$ under $\nu_x$, which is a.s.~the unique infinite open cluster, is denoted by $W_x$, or $W$ when there is no ambiguity. The cluster $W$ almost surely has a single topological end. We often consider up to three independent copies of the IIC, which we decorate with tildes: $\nu_x$, $\widetilde{\nu}_y$, and $\widetilde{\widetilde{\nu}}_z$. The infinite clusters corresponding to $\widetilde{\nu}_y$ and $\widetilde{\widetilde{\nu}}_z$ will be denoted by $\widetilde{W}$ and $\widetilde{\widetilde{W}}$ (respectively).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:56:37.664371+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c60b58bdf804527d76a6d1ae15745d30271acf869b714840be9021487a76d2c9","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem block-cut of arXiv:2609.00110, 'Phase Transition and Fluctuation Results for First-Passage Percolation on Spread-Out Cycle'] Assume that $b>2\ell$. Then, with $S_n^{(b)}$ as defined in~(equation eq:block-cut-sum of the paper), we have
\begin{align}
 0\leqslant S_n^{(b)}-T_{[n]} \leqslant \sum_{i=1}^{q-1}(Y_{i,-}^{(\ell)} + Y_{i,+}^{(\ell)}).
