{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:53:30.546926+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c6160f3137adf8a9f641d9cb9c424d675b66e3bc421191d57d461463142fb766","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def-quadratic variation of arXiv:2505.24043, 'Weak solutions of Navier-Stokes Equation with purely discontinuous L\'evy Noise'] If $M$ is a  square integrable  martingale taking values in a Hilbert space $X$ with norm $\normb{\cdot}{}{}$, then  
the quadratic variation process of $M$ is an $\mathbb{R}$-valued process $[M,M]$   such that for every $ t\in [0,T]$, $[M,M](t) $  is  the limit in $L^1=L^1(\Omega,\mathscr{F},\mathbb{P})$ over the partitions of $[0,T]$ with mesh converging to $0$, i.e. 
\begin{equation}
[M,M](t) = L^1-\lim \sum_{i=1}^n \left\vert M(t_i^{n} \wedge t )-M(t_{i-1}^{n} \wedge t )\right\vert^2.
