{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:00:22.780782+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c69e14885c3594e59f51bcaf7de6b93eaddf7fc2ee28fa965929b7db42acb052","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm Main Theorem Statement of arXiv:2411.01633, 'On the Limit of the Tridiagonal Model \\for $\beta$-Dyson Brownian Motion'] Fix a $k \in \mathbb{N}$ and let $\{a_j\}_{j=1}^n, \{b_j\}_{j=1}^{n-1}$ be the tridiagonal entry (processes) as in (equation eq:tridiagonalmatrix of the paper), from the $n\times n$ tridiagonalized GOE process. Define 
\begin{equation*}
E_n(t):=\left(a_1(t),\dots, a_k(t), \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}(b_1(t)^2-n),\dots ,\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}(b_k(t)^2-n)\right).
\end{equation*} 
Then, $E_n(t)$ converges weakly in $C[0,\infty)$ to a vector process $E(t)=(A_1(t),\dots,A_k(t),B_1(t),\dots,B_k(t))$ as $n\to\infty$, where
\begin{equation*}
    \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}E(t) \sim \mathbf{OU}_{2k}(1,3,\dots,2k-1,2,4,\dots,2k).
\end{equation*} 
More explicitly, the components $A_j(t) \in C[0,\infty)$ and $B_j(t) \in C[0,\infty)$ are jointly-independent centered Ornstein--Uhlenbeck processes with covariances given as $\mathrm{Cov}(A_j(t),A_j(s))=2e^{-(2j-1)|t-s|}$ and $\mathrm{Cov}(B_j(t),B_j(s))=2e^{-2j|t-s|}$, for $j=1,\dots,k$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:37:08.312487+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c6a03edff661e098dd4db0d96833fa8c7d1631eb093d7f7bd4654b22239a3bd7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma e_t of arXiv:2608.29507, 'Denoising as Projection: Constrained Optimization with Gradient-Guided Diffusion'] Define 
    \begin{align*}
        e_t:= \frac{\eta L^2}{2(1-\eta\mu c)} \|v_t\|^2,
    \end{align*}
    where $v_t$ is defined as in (equation eq:def-u-v of the paper), then under the conditions specified in Theorem (theorem: convergence-guided-diffusion of the paper) and for $\widetilde{\eta}$ defined as in (equation eq:subspace-dynamics of the paper), we have that 
    \begin{align*}
        e_{t-1} \le  (1-\widetilde{\eta} \mu c)e_t -  \frac{\widetilde{\eta} L^2}{2}\|v_t\|^2
    \end{align*}
    \begin{proof} For $\|v_t\| = 0$ the statement is trivial. For $\|v_t\|>0$ we have
        \begin{align*}
            &e_{t-1} \le  (1-\widetilde{\eta} \mu c)e_t -  \frac{\widetilde{\eta} L^2}{2}\|v_t\|^2\\
            \stackrel{\textup{Lemma (lemma:guided-diffusion-u-v of the paper)}}{\Longleftrightarrow}\quad& \frac{\eta L^2}{2(1-\eta\mu c)} \frac{\sigma_{t-1}^2}{\sigma_t^2} \le (1-\widetilde{\eta} \mu c)\frac{\eta L^2}{2(1-\eta\mu c)} -  \frac{\widetilde{\eta} L^2}{2}\\
            \Longleftrightarrow\quad & \frac{\sigma_{t-1}^2}{\sigma_t^2}\le (1-\widetilde{\eta} \mu c) - \frac{\widetilde{\eta} (1-\eta\mu c)}{\eta}\\
            \Longleftrightarrow\quad & (1-\epsilon)^2=\frac{\sigma_{t-1}^2}{\sigma_t^2}\le (1-\widetilde{\eta} \mu c) - \epsilon (1-\eta\mu c) = 1-\epsilon\eta\mu c -\epsilon + \epsilon\eta\mu c = 1-\epsilon,
        \end{align*}
        given that $\epsilon \in (0,1)$, it completes the proof.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

