{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:24:20.642782+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c6c4e508813fcd747fa1a140f9129949bae663592dae2f24539dd73bf41e2d5e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] The sequence of free cumulants $\kappa_1,\kappa_2,\kappa_3,\kappa_4,\ldots$ is as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
\item For $\mu=\delta_c$ the free cumulants are $c,0,0,0,\ldots$

\item For $\mu=\gamma_t$ the free cumulants are $0,t,0,0,\ldots$

\item For $\mu=\pi_t$ the free cumulants are $t,t,t,t,\ldots$

\item For $\mu=\beta_t^2$ the free cumulants are $0,t,0,t,\ldots$
\end{enumerate}
Also, for the compound free Poisson laws the free cumulants are $\kappa_n(\pi_\nu)=M_n(\nu)$.","status":"unverified"}

