{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:01:01.828612+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c7639126d52e60acfc71d43ba24e219f449fda7dac023607e50316275b6fe4ac","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem: bound_int_t-harmonic_by_distance of arXiv:2609.00257, 'Stability estimates for the initial-to-final-state inverse problem'] Let $n\geq2$ and $V_1, V_2\in L^1(\mathbb{R}^n)\cap L^\infty(\mathbb{R}^n)$ with $\|V_j\|_{w_\delta} < \infty$, for $j\in \{1, 2\}$. Let also $\psi_1\equiv \psi_\lambda^{V_1}$ and $\psi_2 \equiv \psi_\lambda^{V_2}$ be the time-harmonic solutions (equation eq: time-harmonic solutions of the paper) to the Schr\"odinger equation, as constructed in subsection (sec: stationary states of the paper) for potentials $V_1$ and $V_2$ respectively. Then, there holds

\begin{equation}
\Big| \int_\Sigma (V_1-V_2) \psi_1 \overline{\psi_2} \Big| \leq T \mathrm{dist}_\delta (\mathcal{U}_T^1, \mathcal{U}_T^2) \|\psi_1\|_{C_TL^2_{-\delta}} \| \psi_2\|_{C_TL^2_{-\delta}}. 
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:57:16.077717+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c765b01fb3a3428c29230e0334267995e539a6c178db7685c4c438ab8b23a31a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo formule of arXiv:2507.00737, 'Dispersion models on a circle: universal properties and asymptotic results'] For all bounded and Lebesgue measurable function $g:[0,1]\to \mathbb{R}^+$,
	\begin{equation}\langle g, M_{\lambda}({\sf e}) \rangle  = \int_{ \lambda}^{+\infty} \sum_{k\geq 0} g( {\ell_t(k)})\, {\ell_t(k)} \,   \mathrm{d} t, ~~~\textrm{ a.s.}     \end{equation}

	In particular, for $f = {\sf Id}/2$, we have: 
	\begin{eqnarray}\langle f, M_{\lambda}({\sf e}) \rangle 
	&=&\int_{0}^1 {\sf e}_\lambda(s)-\underbar{{\sf e}}_\lambda(s)\, \mathrm{d} x
	= \frac12\int_{ \lambda}^{+\infty} \sum_{k=1}^{+\infty}  {\ell_t(k)}^{2} \mathrm{d} t.       \end{eqnarray} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:59:53.362334+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c767b4540f6b3e4abba1248f0ccd1dc6fe5e6743b9c966bdb809ceb474e1f08f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:carpet-information of arXiv:2607.24705, 'Derangetropy Operators'] For every $f \in \mathcal{D}_{+}$:
		\begin{enumerate}[label=\normalfont(\roman*), leftmargin=*]
			\item the time-averaged carpet is the tent modulation,
			\begin{equation}
				2\pi\int_{0}^{1/2\pi} f_{\tau}\,d\tau
				= \rho_{\Lambda}[f],
				\qquad
				\Lambda(z) = 4\min(z,\,1-z);

			\end{equation}
			\item the time-averaged chi-square divergence from the base law is
			universal,
			\begin{equation}
				2\pi\int_{0}^{1/2\pi}
				\chi^{2}\big(f_{\tau}\,\|\,f\big)\,d\tau
				= \frac{2}{3},
				\qquad
				\chi^{2}\big(f_{\tau}\|f\big)
				= \int_{0}^{1}\big(w_{s} - 1\big)^{2}dz;

			\end{equation}
			\item information breathes but never grows:
			$D(f_{\tau}\,\|\,f) \le \log\big(1 + \chi^{2}(f_{\tau}\|f)\big)$,
			and $\sup_{\tau}\chi^{2}(f_{\tau}\|f)$ is a finite absolute
			constant.
		\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:31:20.585884+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c76878d7463ffc61f70d4739210f9f58dc103661d7649983a6ed9367b04b0526","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 2 (Closed-form gradient refinement) of arXiv:2608.25116, '\method: Gradient Refinement and Progress-Aware Exploitation for Query-Efficient High-Dime'] Let $k_\calD(\cdot,\cdot)$ denote the GP posterior kernel after observing
$\calD$. Then $\alpha_{\mathrm{ref}}(\bz)$ is independent of the unobserved
value $y_\bz$ and equals
