{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:24:20.640420+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c77a842e4b567f49c1f46809bc64cb583b9052ee45d1f2c84cda27c525ef5220","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] Let $A$ be a $C^*$-algebra, given with a positive trace $tr:A\to\mathbb C$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item The elements $a\in A$ are called random variables.

\item The moments of such a variable are the numbers $M_k(a)=tr(a^k)$.

\item The law of such a variable is the functional $\mu_a:P\to tr(P(a))$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:33:01.793835+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c77d5de45e90becab56fc26484a660c356ab1638dd9b1414bb72d8741778cff0","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 76 (Connes--Kreimer extraction-contraction coproduct) of arXiv:2605.14630, '2605.14630'] The \emph{Connes--Kreimer extraction-contraction 
co\-product} $\DeltaCK: \spanF \to \spancFminus \otimes \spanF$ is defined by 
\begin{equation}

 \DeltaCK(\Gamma) = \Gamma\otimes\unit + \unit\otimes\Gamma 
+ \sum_{\substack{\unit\neq\overline\Gamma\subsetneq\Gamma, \overline\Gamma \in \Fminus}} 
\overline\Gamma\otimes(\Gamma/\overline\Gamma)\;, 
\end{equation} 
where the sum ranges over all \emph{divergent} subgraphs $\overline\Gamma$, and 
$\Gamma/\overline\Gamma$ denotes the graph obtained by replacing $\overline\Gamma$
by a single vertex. The subgraphs have to be \emph{full}, in the sense that if an edge
$e$ belongs to $\overline\Gamma$, all edges connecting the same vertices also belong 
to $\overline\Gamma$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:43:19.042607+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c781ec51218483998af86f298fdace9eae5e7e09ca919587be7d17f7c9bfc513","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm thm-P2 of arXiv:2609.02552, 'Resolvent Estimates for the Stokes Operator \\ in a Three-Dimensional Lipschitz Domain'] Let $d=3$ and $\lambda\in \Sigma_\theta$.
Let $(u, \phi )$ be the same as in Theorem (thm-P1 of the paper).
Suppose $x_0 \in \partial\Omega$ and $0< r<  r_0$.
Then, for $2\le q< 3+\varepsilon$ and $p=\frac{2q}{3}$, 
\begin{equation}
\left(
\fint_{B(x_0, r)\cap \Omega} |\phi -\beta|^q \right)^{1/q}
+\left(\fint_{B(x_0, r)\cap \partial\Omega}
|\phi -\beta|^p \right)^{1/p} 
\le C M_p(|\nabla u|) (r),
\end{equation}
where 
