		\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:51:15.029276+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c8ce9d1f4523bfdb2604da817e7b06c3ff2ee0df8d64ed6c83dc808fd006e655","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:untied-strong-recovery of arXiv:2609.03858, 'High-Dimensional Learning Dynamics of \\ Attention-Indexed Models'] Suppose that the conditions of Theorem (theo:untied-SGD of the paper) and Assumption (assum:untied_effective_pl of the paper) hold. Let $n_0:=\left\lceil\frac{T_0d}{\alpha_d}\right\rceil$.
Then, for every fixed target accuracy \(\epsilon>0\), there exists
\begin{equation}
n_\epsilon=n_0+O\left(\frac{d}{\alpha_d}\log_+\frac1\epsilon\right)

\end{equation}
such that
\begin{equation}
\lim_{d\to\infty}\mathbb P\left(\Psi_d(U^{(n_\epsilon)},V^{(n_\epsilon)})-\Psi^\star>\epsilon\right)=0,

\end{equation}
where $\log_+(x):=\max\{0,\log x\}$. In particular, if \(T_0=\Theta(1)\), reaching an \(\epsilon\)-suboptimal population loss requires at most $O\left(\frac{d}{\alpha_d}\log\frac1\epsilon\right)$ additional samples after entering the strong-recovery regime.
For \(\alpha_d=\Theta((d\log d)^{-1})\), this becomes
$O\left(d^2\log d\,\log\frac1\epsilon\right)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.481991+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c8cfcede5ba9a23a877fa5c913c0c07d422d7baafea949486fc8fb126ca9603c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 119 (High moments of one-dimensional Gaussian chaoses) of arXiv:2606.29292, 'Intrinsic Gaussian Tensor Geometry and Random Operator Spectra'] Let $g_{\mathbb R}$ be a standard real Gaussian and let $g_{\mathbb C}$ be a
standard circular complex Gaussian.  For independent copies
$g_1,\ldots,g_m$ of the indicated type,
\begin{align}

  \lim_{p\to\infty}
  \frac{\|\prod_{\nu=1}^m g_{\mathbb R,\nu}\|_{L^p}}
       {p^{m/2}}
  &=e^{-m/2},\\

  \lim_{p\to\infty}
  \frac{\|\prod_{\nu=1}^m g_{\mathbb C,\nu}\|_{L^p}}
       {p^{m/2}}
  &=(2e)^{-m/2}.
\end{align}
If $H_m$ is the probabilists' Hermite polynomial, then
\begin{equation}

  \lim_{p\to\infty}
  \frac{\|H_m(g_{\mathbb R})\|_{L^p}}
       {p^{m/2}}
  =\left(\frac me\right)^{m/2}.
\end{equation}
Consequently,
\begin{equation}

  \lim_{p\to\infty}
  \frac{\|H_m(g_{\mathbb R})\|_{L^p}}
       {\|\prod_{\nu=1}^m g_{\mathbb R,\nu}\|_{L^p}}
  =m^{m/2}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

