{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:11:02.395314+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c908cd9c976ce32f754f4281c057c8db790bebae6b492394ef4a1b457a0a86c9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma of arXiv:2507.19554, 'Full extremal process in four-dimensional membrane model'] For $i=1,...,K^4$, let $z_i$ be as in (prop:aux of the paper) and $z_i' \in V_{N}^{K, \delta, i}$ be measurable with respect to $\{h_x^f: x \in V_N^{K, i}\}$ such that $\lv z_i - z_i'\rv = o(N/K)$ as $N,K \rightarrow \infty$. Then for any $A \subset [0,1]^4$ open and non-empty 
    \begin{equation}
        \lim_{K\rightarrow \infty}\lim\sup_{N\rightarrow \infty} d\lp \max\{h_{z_i}^f + h_{z_i}^c: B^{K, i}\subset A\}, \max\{h_{z_i}^f + h_{z_i'}^c: B^{K, i}\subset A\}\rp = 0.
    \end{equation}
    where $d$ is the Levy metric for $\mbR$-valued random variables. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:53.156453+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c90d1e7def40dad65bf216745dc95a3e43d3524b0d37cace0f339cd474f29b81","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 (Normalization) of arXiv:2607.02108, 'A Two-Color Lift of the Shifted \texorpdfstring{$t$}{t}-Schur Measure'] The normalizing constant is
\begin{equation}
        Z_q(X,Y)=\Ht(qX;Y)\Ht(X;Y)=\Ht(X+qX;Y).
\end{equation}
For finite alphabets this is
\begin{equation}
        Z_q(X,Y)
        =\prod_{i,j}
        \frac{1+x_i y_j}{1-x_i y_j}
        \frac{1+q x_i y_j}{1-q x_i y_j}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:19:56.691205+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c915c127c05a7a24bbefdc261f25b4c491f4a54275950dc0b3273c22c16619f5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 9 of arXiv:2608.30967, 'B-Frames, B-Riesz basis, and Their tensor products'] \begin{enumerate}
\item  Let $\{x_k\}_{k\in \mathcal{K}} \subset \mathcal{B}$ and let $\mathfrak{b}: \mathcal{H} \times \mathcal{B} \to \mathcal{Z}$ be a continuous bilinear mapping.

    $(\{x_k\}_{k\in \mathcal{K}},\mathfrak{b})$ is a b-frame (b-Riesz basis) if and only if $\{\Lambda_k: z  \rightarrow \langle z / x_k \rangle \}_{k\in \mathcal{K}}$ is a g-frame (g-Riesz basis) with respect to the sequence of Hilbert spaces $ \{\mathcal{Z}_k = \overline{range(\Lambda_k)}\}_{k\in \mathcal{K}} $.
\item  Let $ \{\mathcal{Z}_k \}_{k\in \mathcal{K}} $ be a sequence of Hilbert spaces, let $\{\Lambda_k  \in \mathcal{L}(\mathcal{Z},\mathcal{Z}_k)\}_{k\in \mathcal{K}} $, and  Let\\ $ \mathcal{H}=\bigoplus_{k>0} \mathcal{Z}_k.$

$\{\Lambda_k \}_{k\in \mathcal{K}} $  is a g-frame (g-Riesz basis) with respect to  $ \{\mathcal{Z}_k \}_{k\in \mathcal{K}} $ if and only if\\  $(\{x_k:\mathcal{H} \to \mathcal{Z} \}_{k\in \mathcal{K}},\mathfrak{b}_0)$ is a b-frame (b-Riesz basis), where $\forall h \in \mathcal{Z}_k, x_k(h)=\Lambda_k^*(h)$ and $\ker (x_k)= \mathcal{Z}_k^\perp$.
\end{enumerate}
For each of the Statements (1) and (2),  the b-frame and g-frame share the same bounds and the b-frame operator coincide with the  g-frame operator.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:34:33.546233+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c91a33dd26e09647913750e6546827f782fc3bb7eb98c96a2c77f6129815e8c5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2603.29446, 'Law of large numbers for stochastic multiscale spatial gene networks'] Let $0\leq \alpha, \beta, \gamma\leq 1$, satisfying one of the following assumptions
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] If $1/2<\alpha\vee\beta\leq 1$, and $\gamma< \alpha\wedge\beta$, 
\item[(ii)] If $\alpha\vee\beta\leq 1/2$, and $\gamma < \alpha+\beta-1/2$, 
\end{enumerate}
Then, there exists $C>0$ such that for all $u,v\in H_N$,
$\|uv\|_{H_N^\gamma} \leq C  \|u\|_{H_N^\alpha}\|v\|_{H_N^\beta}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:53.164522+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c91a9d1c915e923b2667ae096966a4e563fe83325e09965ff9eba3be1a0457a8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 11 of arXiv:2607.02777, 'Hypocoercivity for Hamiltonian Diffusions \\ with Singular Drift'] Let $P_S$ and $P$ denote the orthogonal projections of $H$ onto $L^2(\mu_1)$ and $\{f\in L^2(\mu_1) : \SPH{f}{1}=0\}$ respectively. Then for $f\in H$ it holds
        \begin{equation*}
            P_S f = \int_{\mathbb{R}^{d_2}} f\,\dmui{2} \defeq 
			\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d_2}}f(\cdot,y)\,\dmui{2}(y) \qquad\text{and} \qquad Pf = P_S f-\SPH{f}{1}.
        \end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:24:20.957186+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c91bed117d48ba040af5aa099e1aa52c8cd9aed6fe9ab4340564be2a6c185c18","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma add:lem2 of arXiv:2508.12464, 'On the Fitness Landscape in the $\NK$ Model'] If $\alpha_*\leq \alpha\leq 2-\sqrt{2},$ then
	\begin{align*}
		\min_{t\in [0,1-\alpha]}f_s(t)>0,\,\,\forall 0<s<\beta_c.
	\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:48:24.617403+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c91df249fc5c28363147cf786b5a42262c23e580a12941022eccc380522840d8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:hl of arXiv:2601.04997, 'Fluctuations of the Boundary-Driven Symmetric Zero-Range Process from the NESS'] Let $\gamma:[0,1]\to[0, \infty)$ be a  measurable function and let $\{\mu^N\}_N$ be a sequence of probability measures on $\Omega_N$ associated to $\gamma$ and satisfying the two conditions 
\begin{equation*}
    H(\mu^N|\bar\nu_N)\lesssim N \quad \text{and} \quad \mu^N\le \bar\nu_N.
\end{equation*}
Then, for $\theta\ge1$ and for every $t\in [0,T]$, the sequence of empirical measures $\{\pi^{N}_t\}_N$ converges in probability to the absolutely continuous measure $\rho(t, u)\de u$, that is, for any $\varepsilon>0$ and for any $H\in C^2(\mathbb{T})$, we have that
\begin{equation*}
    \mylim_{N\to\infty}\mathbb{P}_{\mu^N} \left\{\left|\langle \pi^{N}_{t},H\rangle-\int_0^1\rho(t,u)H(u)\de u \right|>\varepsilon\right\} =0,
\end{equation*}
where $\rho(t,u)$ is the unique weak solution of (equation eq:pde_zr of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:29:08.756412+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c922a63e72120ffdba4695ccb5ddec16b558dd2cc9540ccc4b7e89d173f92e11","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem ratesHaarU of arXiv:2412.00593, 'A new approach to strong convergence II.\\The classical ensembles'] There exists a linear functional $\mu_m$ on $\mathcal{P}$ for every $m\in\mathbb{Z}_+$ 
such that for every $q\in\mathbb{N}$ and $h\in \mathcal{P}_q$
\begin{equation}

	|\mu_m(h)| 
	\le 
	\bigg(
	(C\tilde q\tilde q_0)^m
	+\frac{(C\tilde q\tilde q_0)^{2m}}{m!} 
	\bigg)
	\sobnorm{h}{0}{[-K, K]},
\end{equation}
and such that for all $N\in\mathbb{N}$
\begin{equation}

	\left| \mathbf{E}[\mathop{\textnormal{tr}} h(X^N)]- \sum_{k=0}^{m-1} \frac{\mu_k(h)}{N^k} 
	\right| \leq 
	\frac{1}{N^m}
	\bigg(
	(C\tilde q\tilde q_0)^m
	+\frac{(C\tilde q\tilde q_0)^{2m}}{m!} 
	\bigg)
	\sobnorm{h}{0}{[-K, K]}.
\end{equation}
Here $C$ is a universal constant,
$\tilde q:=q(1+\log q)$, and $\tilde q_0:=q_0(1+\log q_0)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:10.892887+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c922cc365670ace6f1967a3cd062e9858df3f6f8867af6138a68669005eb124c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 of arXiv:2508.04938, 'Perfect t-embeddings of doubly periodic Aztec diamonds'] Let~$\cT_N$ and~$\cO_N$ denote the perfect t-embedding and associated origami map of the Aztec diamond of size~$2\ell N$ with~$(2\times\ell)$-periodic weights described above and let~$(\xi,\eta)$ and~$(u,v)$ be as above.\\ 
1) If~$(\xi,\eta)$ is a point in the liquid region~$\mathcal F_R$, then
\begin{equation*}
(\cT_N(u,v)),\cO_N(u,v)))\to \left(\mathcal Z(\Omega(\xi,\eta)),\vartheta(\Omega(\xi,\eta))\right)
\end{equation*}
as~$N\to \infty$, where~$\Omega:\mathcal F_R\to \mathcal{R}_0$ 
is a diffeomorphism (precisely defined in Proposition~\ref{prop:diffeomorphism_2xell}) known as the critical point map, and the convergence is uniform on compact subsets of~$\mathcal F_R$; \\
2) If~$(\xi,\eta)$ is in a gas or frozen region, let~$(\xi',\eta')\in \mathcal F_R$ be a sequence in the liquid region converging to a point~$(\xi_0,\eta_0)$ on the boundary of the liquid region and the gas or frozen component containing~$(\xi,\eta)$, then
\begin{equation}
\lim_{N\to \infty}(\cT_N(u,v),\cO_N(u,v))=\lim_{(\xi',\eta')\to (\xi_0,\eta_0)} \left(\mathcal Z(\Omega(\xi',\eta')),\vartheta(\Omega(\xi',\eta'))\right);
\end{equation}
3) The map~$\mathcal R_0\ni q\mapsto (\mathcal Z(q),\vartheta(q))$ defines a conformal parameterization of a space-like surface in~$\mathbb R^{2,2}$ with zero mean curvature and with the boundary given by a quadrilateral in~$\mathbb{C}\times\mathbb{R}$ with vertices at 
\[
(0,0), \quad
\left((a+\i)\sqrt{a^2+1},-(a^2+1)\right), \quad
\left(2a\sqrt{a^2+1} ,\,0\right), \quad

\left((a-\i)\sqrt{a^2+1},-(a^2+1)\right),
\]
and~$\ell-1$ cusps with apices~$P_1, \dots, P_{\ell-1}$, where~$P_i$ is the right hand side of \eqref{eq:gas_frozen_limt_intro} with~$(\xi_0,\eta_0)$ in the boundary of the~$i$th gas region. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:23:27.789129+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c924106fcdd9f8d05353d380d7dde37d2a0cc10f2e202388d5e9349b9d1eb0ee","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem bulk-KR of arXiv:2606.09712, 'Nearest zero-critical point distances for Gaussian SU(2) polynomials'] Let $R>1$ be fixed. Let \(I\) be uniformly distributed on \(\{1,\ldots,n\}\), independently of
\(p_n\), and let \(z_I\) be the corresponding zero.  Condition on the event
\(z_I\in K_R\).  Let
\(\beta_I\) be the nearest critical point to \(z_I\) in
Fubini--Study distance.  Then, in the affine coordinate, we have \begin{equation}
        n(\beta_I-z_I)
        +\frac 1{ V_{\mathrm{FS}}(z_I)}\to 0

\end{equation}
in probability, conditionally on \(z_I\in K_R\).  Consequently, the Fubini--Study distance satisfies 
