{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:15:09.027947+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c93d84a02f4beda3cc6dd375c6c15fc09e2983e0cb1c879d742253825fa78b5e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop IXHGest of arXiv:2411.10829, 'Airy$_\beta$ line ensemble and its Laplace transform'] Take any constants $C_1, D, d>0$.
Take any limiting blocks $(\vec \mathbf p, \vec\boldsymbol{\upsilon},\mathbf H)$ that is $d$-well-spaced, with $\boldsymbol{\delta}, \mathbf p^0, \mathbf H<C_1$.
Take any $(\vec p, \vec\upsilon, \mathcal H)\in \mathscr L_\epsilon$, and assume that $(\vec p, \vec\upsilon, \mathcal H)$ and $(\vec \mathbf p, \vec\boldsymbol{\upsilon},\mathbf H)$ are $D$-close, and that $\mathcal L^{-1}(\vec p, \vec\upsilon, \mathcal H)$ is non-empty.
Also take $\vec \boldsymbol{\tau}\in \mathbb R_{\ge 0}^m$ such that $\boldsymbol{\tau}_1\le \cdots \le \boldsymbol{\tau}_m<C_1$, and $|N_\ell-N+\boldsymbol{\tau}_\ell N^{2/3}|<C_1$ for each $\ell\in\llbracket 1, m\rrbracket$.
Then there exists $C_2=C_2(C_1, D, d)$ such that
\begin{multline*}
