{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:04:39.222026+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c9db13248b8c8b7e0dff2bcf928fe6b4fed585f5837590fe9c02be7ef14ed68f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 24 of arXiv:2608.13976, 'Boundary Harnack principle for diffusion with jumps on metric measure spaces'] There is a symmetric and positive function $J(x, y)$ in $\sX\times \sX$ such that  $J(dx, dy)=J(x, y) \,m(dx)\, m(dy)$ in $\sX\times \sX.$
Moreover, there are positive constants $C_k, k=1, 2$ so that
\begin{equation}
 \frac{C_1 }{V(x,  d(x, y))\phi_j  (d(x, y) )}\leq
J(x, y)  \leq \frac{C_2 }{V(x,  d(x, y))\phi_j  (d(x, y) )}
\quad \hbox{for   } (x, y) \in \sX\times \sX \setminus {\rm diag}.
