{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:45:05.415562+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ca01eedc5af45b5c02e9697cf4c065177f2d95777ff8263ebc8569b17efe9039","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 140 (Running-source stability and the intrinsic defect) of arXiv:2608.04993, '2608.04993'] Fix an admissible driver $F$ in the sense of
\Cref{def:admissible-backward-driver}.  The raw source map
$B^{F,0}$ on the testing graph has a unique continuous extension
\begin{equation}
 B^F:\mathscr W_{Q,\cP}^{1,2}\longrightarrow\mathbb S_{\cP}^2,
 \qquad B^F|_{\mathfrak G_{Q,\cP}^{\rm bw,0}}=B^{F,0}.
\end{equation}
For a stable lift $(Y,Z)$ define its intrinsic defect by
\begin{equation}
 K_t^F[Y,Z]:=Y_0-B_t^F[Y,Z]+I_t(Z)-Y_t.
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{align}
 &\|B^F[Y,Z]-B^F[\widetilde Y,\widetilde Z]\|_{\mathbb S_{\cP}^2}
 \notag\\
 &\qquad\le
 LT\|Y-\widetilde Y\|_{\mathbb S_{\cP}^2}
 +L\sqrt T\|Z-\widetilde Z\|_{\mathbb H_{\cP}^2},
 \\
 &\|K^F[Y,Z]-K^F[\widetilde Y,\widetilde Z]\|_{\mathbb S_{\cP}^2}
 \notag\\
 &\qquad\le
 (2+LT)\|Y-\widetilde Y\|_{\mathbb S_{\cP}^2}
 +(2+L\sqrt T)\|Z-\widetilde Z\|_{\mathbb H_{\cP}^2}.

\end{align}
In particular $B^F$ is the response-completed running-source channel and
$B^F,K^F$ are continuous first-jet readouts.  For a completed lift, the occasional notation
$\int_0^tF(r,\cdot,Y_r,Z_r)\,\dd r$ means this continuous extension; it does
not assert a distinguished pointwise representative of the energy class $Z$.

The cone
\[
 \mathbb K_{\cP}^{2,\uparrow}
 :=\{K\in\mathbb S_{\cP}^2:K_0=0
       \text{ and }K\text{ has a quasi-sure continuous nondecreasing version}\}
\]
is closed in $\mathbb S_{\cP}^2$.  Suppose
$R:=Y+B^F[Y,Z]$ belongs to the declared response-supermartingale class under
every $P\in\cP$ and the common upper-energy class in \textup{(B4)} of
\Cref{def:backward-compatible-interface} is the specified first-jet
coordinate, $Z^R=Z$ in $\mathbb H_{\cP}^2$.  Then
$K^F[Y,Z]\in\mathbb K_{\cP}^{2,\uparrow}$.  Moreover, for every $P\in\cP$
and every deterministic $s\le t$,
\begin{equation}
 Y_s-E^P\!\left[Y_t+B_t^F-B_s^F\mid\mathcal F_s^0\right]
 =E^P[K_t^F-K_s^F\mid\mathcal F_s^0],
\end{equation}
so conditional exactness under $P$ is equivalent to $P$-flatness of the defect
on $[s,t]$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:43:53.643791+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ca022c2b440a51d35745de9bb00d8080f3384bb6af17ae8e59f1478cca2c36ae","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:part-fun of arXiv:2512.17747, 'On exponentially height-penalized random trees'] Let $\mu=\mu_n$ be such that $\mu\gg1/\sqrt{n}$. Then the partition function $Z_n$ as defined in~(equation eq:part fun of the paper) behaves according to the following regimes.
    \begin{itemize}
        \item[$\mathbf{(1)}$] If $1/\sqrt{n}\ll\mu\ll n^{1/4}$, we have
        \begin{align*}
            Z_n=\big(1+o(1)\big)4^ne^\mu(e^\mu-1)\frac{\pi^{5/6}}{2^{1/3}3^{1/2}}\frac{\mu^{1/3}}{n^{5/6}}\exp\left(-3\left(\frac{\pi^2\mu^2n}{4}\right)^{1/3}\right)\,.
        \end{align*}
        \item[$\mathbf{(2)}$] If $(\log^{3/2}n)/\sqrt{n}\ll\mu\ll n$, we have
        \begin{align*}
            Z_n=\big(1+o(1)\big)4^ne^{\mu}(e^\mu-1)\frac{\mu}{2n}e^{-n\lambda_{\mu/n}(t_{\mu/n})}\sum_{m\in\mathbb Z}\exp\left(-\left(\frac{3}{2}+o(1)\right)\left(\frac{\mu^4}{2\pi^2n}\right)^{1/3}(m-t_{\mu/n})^2\right)\,.
        \end{align*}
        \item[$\mathbf{(3)}$] If $n^{1/4}\ll\mu$, we have
        \begin{align*}
            Z_n=\big(1+o(1)\big)4^ne^{2\mu}\sum_{m=\max\{\lfloor t_{\mu/n}\rfloor,3\}}^{\lceil t_{\mu/n}\rceil}\frac{\tan^2\left(\frac{\pi}{m}\right)}{m}e^{-n\lambda_{\mu/n}(m)}\,.
        \end{align*}
    \end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:56:00.630971+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ca051702cabc4d01897beb167249d6b7c06b4c2990ea1ecb0dc43de9a4a16b27","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem: facts of T(z) of arXiv:2603.10738, 'Pairwise Negative Correlation for Uniform Spanning\\ Subgraphs of the Complete Graph'] The formal power series $T(z)\coloneq \sum_{n\;\geqslant\;1} n^{n-1}\frac{z^n}{n!}$ has the following properties:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item[(1)] It is well-known (see, e.g., equation~(44), p.~127 of [FS2009]) that $T(z)$ satisfies 
		\begin{equation}
		T(z)=ze^{T(z)}\,.
		\end{equation}


		\item[(2)] The derivative of $T(z)$ satisfies(see, e.g., formula~(2.7) in the proof of Lemma~2.1 of [Stark2011])
		\begin{equation}
		T'(z)=\frac{T(z)}{z\big[1-T(z)\big]}\,.
		\end{equation}

		\item[(3)] For an integer $k\in[1,n]$, we have\footnote{Here and in the sequel, we use $\big[z^n\big]f(z)\coloneq f_n$ to denote the coefficient of $z^n$ in the formal power series $f(z)=\sum_{n\;\geqslant\;0}f_nz^n$.}  (see, e.g., Proposition~1 of [FGKP1995])
		\begin{equation}
		\big[z^n\big]T^k(z)=\frac{kn^{n-k-1}}{(n-k)!}.
		\end{equation}

		\item[(4)] For an integer $k\in[1,n]$, we have (see, e.g.,  formula~(2.13) in Lemma~2.3 of [Stark2011]) 
		\begin{equation}
		\big[z^n\big]\frac{T^k(z)}{1-T(z)}=\frac{n^{n-k}}{(n-k)!}\,.
		\end{equation}
	\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.814140+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ca06d7ec7212d977cec9a88b51efba3971ece356283309f5345c683054e55ea9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:subdivision of arXiv:2609.01672, '\textbf{All-Minors Matrix-Tree Theory for Superport Networks:\\ Completed Quotient-Inciden'] For every \(X\subseteq V\), put \(R_X:=V\setminus X\),
