    \vert\kern-0.25ex\vert\kern-0.25ex\vert}_N
\end{equs}
uniformly in $\mu\in(0,1]$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:58.439962+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ca83de13f13125dd5643ce7a2768fc1a1c0e24c04c6584c305cc664856613e2c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def.finite-volHamiltonian of arXiv:2604.24743, 'The impact of disorder and non-convex interactions on delocalisation of height functions'] Let $\Lambda \subseteq \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}^d}_n$ be a finite set, and let $(J_{xy})_{ \{ x, y \} \in E(\Lambda)} \in [0, \infty)^{E(\Lambda)} $ be a collection of non-negative conductances. We define the Wells' disorder to be the probability distribution on the space of site percolation configurations $\{0 , 1\}^{\Lambda \cap \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}^d}}$ given by the identity: for any $r \in \{0 , 1\}^{\Lambda \cap \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}^d}}$
    \begin{equation} 
        \nu_{\Lambda, J} (\{ r \}) := \frac{Z_{\Lambda, J,r}^{\mathrm{XY}}}{\bar Z_{\Lambda, J}^{\mathrm{XY}}} ~~\mbox{where} ~~ \bar Z_{\Lambda, J}^{\mathrm{XY}} = \sum_{ r \in \{0 , 1\}^{\Lambda\cap \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}^d}}} Z_{\Lambda, J,r}^{\mathrm{XY}}.
    \end{equation}
When the set $\Lambda$ is the box $\Lambda_L^n,$ we denote this measure by $\nu_{L, J}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:31:20.886508+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ca85b651665c66f5bf561299ecafd0875a7afc16e2c2c2f446260ce56013eb88","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposizione 8 of arXiv:2506.11937, 'Random rotational invariance of integration by parts formulas within a Bismut-type approac'] Given a differential operator \eqref{L}
        with $ a(x,t) \in Mat(n,n)$ positive semidefinite, if there exist $\sigma, \tilde{\sigma} \in Mat(n,m)$ such that for every $ (x,t) \in \mathbb{R}^n \times [0,T]$
    \[ \sigma(x,t) \sigma(x,t)^T=a(x,t)=\tilde{\sigma}(x,t) \tilde{\sigma}(x,t)^T,\]
   then there exists an orthogonal matrix $\rho(x,t) \in O(m)$ such that
    \[ \tilde{\sigma}=\sigma \rho.\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:38:23.548694+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ca88efed1bf9359ff12cf1683a65d34a70bc5e6d8f112468e8fdb8bb5169b146","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thrm:bound of arXiv:2511.01058, 'Limit profiles and cutoff for the Burnside process on Sylow double cosets'] Fix \(p \ge 11\), \(1 \le k <p\) and \(\sigma \in S_{pk}\). If \(|H\sigma H|=p^a\), then for all \(t \ge 1\)
    \begin{equation}
        \left|  \Vert P^t_\sigma - \pi\Vert_{\mathrm{TV}} - 1+\left(1-\left(1-\frac{1}{p}\right)^t\right)^{2k-a}\right| \le \frac{4p^4}{(p-1)!} + \frac{t}{(p-2)!}.
    \end{equation}
    In particular,
    \begin{equation}
        \Vert P^t_\sigma - \pi \Vert_{\mathrm{TV}}  \le 1-\left(1-\left(1-\frac{1}{p}\right)^t\right)^{2k-a}+\frac{4p^4}{(p-1)!} + \frac{t}{(p-2)!}.
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:45:32.101722+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ca8972839b67b071b811bdb9c965a5edc66e658e30d660f91e2dedc228dac2a4","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma density Theta1 of arXiv:2411.14350, 'Randomized Geodesic Flow on Hyperbolic Groups'] For all $\xi,\eta\in \partial G$, we have 
\[
{\rm e} ^{-(2 M + 8\delta)}\, {\rm e} ^{2(\xi,\eta)^{{\mathcal G}}_{o}}\leq 
\frac{{\rm{d}} \Theta_1}{{\rm{d}}(\nu_0\otimes\nu_0)}(\xi,\eta) \leq {\rm e} ^{2\delta}\, {\rm e} ^{2(\xi,\eta)^{{\mathcal G}}_{o}}
\]
where $M$ is the constant such that $(G,d_{{\mathcal G}})$ is $M$ quasi-ruled in the sense of Remark~(rmk-qr of the paper). In particular this also means that the limit in the definition of $\Theta_1$ exists, i.e. that $\Theta_1$ is well defined.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:15:15.525239+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ca8a9afb36c8ba8e6cd07d119cae38f660616ecc909f7d8469ec7c9631bc8465","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma frechet of arXiv:2507.01406, '{\Large On Iterated Lorenz Curves with Applications: The Multivariate Case }'] Let $A\in \mathcal{A}$ and $h\in C([0,1])$. Then 
\begin{equation}
DT_{A}[h](x)=\frac{-\int_{0}^{q(x)}(2-2t)\,h(t)\,dt+\tau
(x)\int_{0}^{1}(2-2t)\,h(t)\,dt}{M}.  
\end{equation}
Consequently, 
\begin{equation}
\Vert DT_{A}\Vert _{\infty \rightarrow \infty }\leq \sup_{x\in \lbrack 0,1]}
\frac{(1-\tau (x))p(x)+\tau (x)(1-p(x))}{M}.  
\end{equation}
For the indexed orbit this becomes 
\begin{equation}
K_{n}(x)=\frac{(1-\tau _{n}(x))p_{n}(x)+\tau _{n}(x)(1-p_{n}(x))}{M_{n}}
,\qquad \Vert DT_{A_{n}}\Vert _{\infty \rightarrow \infty }\leq \sup_{x\in
\lbrack 0,1]}K_{n}(x).  
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:31:20.595916+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ca8d55a3456df5523acd34daa5a3afc9d3be5bfab23be0d9c672f071fd6503ba","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 21 (A localized comparator for a conjugate prior) of arXiv:2608.25706, 'Fast rates in Bayesian online learning with approximate posteriors'] \label[lemma]{lem:coordinate-comparator}
Let $\Pi$ be a distribution with $\Pi_\kappa(\d\theta)\propto \exp(\kappa A'(0)\theta-\kappa A(\theta))\ind_{[-B,B]}\d\theta$ with  $\kappa\ge1$. Assume \eqref{eq:A-regularity}. For $|\theta^\star|<B$ and $n\in\bN$, there exists a probability measure $\rho$ on $[-B,B]$ such that
    \begin{align}
    \KL(\rho\|\Pi)
    &\le C\left\{1+\log\left(1+\frac n\kappa\right)+\kappa(\theta^\star)^2\right\},
    \\
    \int D_A(\theta,\theta^\star)\rho(\d\theta)
    &\le\frac C{\kappa+n},
    \end{align}
for some absolute constant $C>0$, where
$D_A(\theta,\theta'):=A(\theta)-A(\theta')-A'(\theta')(\theta-\theta')$.","status":"unverified"}

