{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:33:21.479490+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_caa393bfee521e921b546103e44051d66a061262de097f122a97eee1208401fe","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Def Def_strongly_convex_3 of arXiv:2606.22183, 'Expected loss of quasi-arithmetic means of exchangeable random variables'] Let $I\subseteq \mathbb{R}$ be a nondegenerate interval and $\mu\in\mathbb{R}_+$.
A function $L:I\to\mathbb{R}$ is called strongly convex with modulus $\mu$ if
 \begin{align*}
  L(tx+(1-t)y) \leqslant t L(x) + (1-t)L(y) - \mu \cdot t (1-t) (x-y)^2,\qquad t\in[0,1], \; x,y\in I.
 \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:06:07.094471+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_caa620231663af27b9d3cd21d798da84654f2c009ea34430b345a0df79d8e2b0","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi 37 of arXiv:2608.28148, 'Flows and homotopies of Banach Lie algebroids'] A map $a \colon [0,1]\times[0,1]\to A$ such that $a_\varepsilon := a(\varepsilon,\cdot)$ is an admissible curve for every $\varepsilon \in [0,1]$ is called a \emph{variation of admissible curves} if $\g_\varepsilon:=\pi\circ a_\varepsilon \colon [0,1]\rightarrow M$ satisfies\begin{itemize}
\item $\g_\varepsilon(0)=x$ and $\g_\varepsilon(1)=y$, and both $x,y$ fixed for all $\varepsilon\in [0,1]$
\item
$\rho(a_\varepsilon)=\dis\frac{d\g_\varepsilon}{dt}$ for all $\varepsilon\in [0,1]$
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:23:27.793400+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_caa7da6bfedc063c51dd4d95b022495428b57fd1326b65d2bd1a1eb34387c166","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn defOUGFF of arXiv:2606.11043, 'Thick points under Gaussian free field dynamics'] Let $(\alpha_k(t))_{k\in\mathbb N}$ be an i.i.d. sequence of Ornstein-Uhlenbeck processes with rate $1/2$ and initial condition a standard Gaussian distribution. The Ornstein-Uhlenbeck GFF (from now on OU-GFF) is defined as 
    \begin{equation}

        \varPhi(t) = \sum_{k\in \mathbb N} \alpha_k(t) e_k,
    \end{equation}
    where $(e_k)_{k\in\mathbb N}$ is an orthonormal basis for $H_0^1(D)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:00:46.818294+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_caaaaf56bf333f278f9c506c2a2729e380761d2803849c36e737d84990fe345f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 33 of arXiv:2608.12804, 'Scaling Limits for Ising Models on Inhomogeneous Random Graphs and Applications'] Suppose that $\{X_N\}_{N\ge 1}, \{Y_N\}_{N\ge 1}$ are sequences of random variables all defined on a common probability space $\Omega$. Suppose that there exists $\delta>0$ such that for every $t\in (-\delta,\delta)$,  $$\E \left(e^{tX_N}\Big |Y_N\right) \xrightarrow{P} \E e^{tV}$$ for some random variable $V$. Then,
   \begin{enumerate}
       \item $X_N \xrightarrow{d} V$,
       \item For every $p\ge 1$, $\E (X_N^p|Y_N) \xrightarrow{P} \E V^p$. 
   \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:31:45.220959+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_caabbb2f170cda2f589b31a5ac62a2dce53c5d19ec013484d2dfba58b3ccc773","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem main of arXiv:2412.06086, 'Zeros of conditional Gaussian analytic functions, random sub-unitary matrices and q-series'] Let $\varepsilon=-\log q$. If $(x,u)\in D=(0,1]\!\times \!(0,+\infty)\cup (1,+\infty)\! \times \![u_{-1}(x), +\infty)$
 then, in the scaling limit $\varepsilon\to  0^{+}$, $t \varepsilon=u>0$,
\begin{align}
F\left(x,\frac u \varepsilon ;q\right)= \displaystyle{\frac{\exp\left\{{\varepsilon^{-1} \big[\log x\log z_s+{\operatorname{Li}}_2(z_s)+u(1-z_s)-\pi^2/6\big]}\right\}}{\sqrt{1+(1-u)(1-z_s)/z_s}}} \left(1+O(\varepsilon)\right),
\end{align}
where
\begin{align}

z_s=
\begin{cases}
z_0(x,u) &  \text{if }  x\not=1 \lor (x=1\land u\ge 1) \\
z_{-1}(x,u) & \text{if }  x=1\land u\in (0,1)
\end{cases}
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:14:46.348768+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_caae517a8de5e2834e8ce95cd63c283052d63c68d6c083d23b4613ab8c39f791","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:regularity of arXiv:2608.10184, 'The sharp SAT/UNSAT phase transition in random ellipsoid fitting'] Fix $\theta>0$.  There is $C_\theta<\infty$ such that for every unit vector $y\in\mathbb{R}^n$ there is $J\subseteq[n]$ with $|J|\le C_\theta d\log d$ for which, writing $r=\|y_{J^c}\|_2$, one of the following holds:
\begin{align}
&r\le d^{-2};\\
&r>0\quad\text{and}\quad \left\|\frac{y_{J^c}}r\right\|_\infty \le\frac{\theta}{\sqrt d}.

\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:49:06.298179+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cab10e71b18d9a8234b6a0f8fcd285407e2e65df12ac4a465ae439d0cf277d5c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem: lemma B1 from 8 of arXiv:2601.05361, 'Noise sensitivity in last-passage percolation'] Let $(\omega_x)_{x\in{\mathbb{Z}}^2}$ and $(\tilde\omega_x)_{x\in{\mathbb{Z}}^2}$ be two collection of (deterministic) weights satisfying the relation $\omega_{i\mathbf{e}_1}\ge\tilde\omega_{i\mathbf{e}_1}$ and $\omega_{i\mathbf{e}_2}\le\tilde\omega_{i\mathbf{e}_2}$ for $i\ge0$, and $\omega_x=\tilde\omega_x$ for $x\ge \mathbf{e}_+$.

    Let $T(0,x)$ and $\tilde{T}(0, x)$ denote the travel time between the origin and $x$ with respect to $\omega$ and $\tilde\omega$, respectively. Then, for all $x\in{\mathbb{Z}}^2_{\geq 0}$ we have
    \begin{eqnarray*}
    \tilde{T}(0, x+\mathbf{e}_2)-\tilde{T}(0, x)\geq T(0, x+\mathbf{e}_2)-T(0, x);\\
    \tilde{T}(0, x+\mathbf{e}_1)-\tilde{T}(0, x)\leq T(0, x+\mathbf{e}_1)-T(0, x).
    \end{eqnarray*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:06:55.290147+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cab2eb5c3ca9ee3f5c649cedfe13cf29658cf6bd300373f926f627dff4570e43","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem symGen of arXiv:2602.17579, 'Non-equilibrium functional inequalities for finite Markov chains'] Let $M$ be a generator of an irreducible Markov chain on a finite state space $\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}$ and $\zeta\in \mathcal{P}_+(\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}})$. 
    The symmetrised generator $M^\zeta$~(equation eq:symGen of the paper) is irreducible and is reversible with respect to its steady state $\zeta$. Consequently, $\zeta$ satisfies the PI and the standard LSI, i.e., 
    \begin{equation}
        \forall f\in \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^{|\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}|}_{>0}: \ \ \operatorname{var}_\zeta(f)\leq \frac{1}{\lambda_{\zeta}}\mathscr{E}_\zeta(f,M^\zeta), \ \ \mathrm{Ent}_\zeta(f^2) \leq \frac{1}{\alpha_{\sLSI} (M^\zeta)}\mathscr{E}_\zeta(f,M^\zeta),
    \end{equation}
    where $\mathrm{Ent}_\zeta(\cdot)$ is the centred entropy~(equation eq:CentEnt of the paper),  $\mathscr{E}_\zeta(\cdot,M^\zeta)$ is the Dirichlet form using the generator $M^\zeta$, and $\lambda_{\zeta} = \alpha_{\PI}(M^\zeta) = \alpha_{\gPI}(\zeta, M) > 0$ is the spectral gap for the generator $-M^\zeta$. Furthermore, we have the relation
    \begin{equation}
        \frac{\lambda_{\zeta}}{2+\log\dfrac{1}{\zeta_*}}\leq \alpha_{\sLSI}(M^\zeta) \leq 2\lambda_{\zeta},
    \end{equation}
    where $\zeta_*=\min_z\zeta(z)$.","status":"unverified"}

