{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:44.526686+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cacd4da58b5ef2e86c87240f21a19ccb9cb9389c15af503cff076e29d5f8d435","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 17 of arXiv:2604.01701, '\bf Chung-type laws of the iterated logarithm for $m$-fold weighted integrated fractional '] Let $  X(t)$ be a continuous, centered, self-similar Gaussian process of index $\tau>0$.
Suppose that $1< q\le\infty$ and
\begin{equation}-\log \pr\Big(\sup_{0\le t\le 1} \left|  X(t)\right|<\epsilon\Big)\le c_0\epsilon^{-1/\tau}+o(\epsilon^{-1/\tau}).   
\end{equation} 
 Assume that  $\int_0^t|w(s)|s^{\tau}ds<\infty$, and $\Delta$ is a sub-interval of $(0,\infty)$. Suppose that $\Delta$ is bounded   when $q<\infty$.
 Then   
\begin{equation}-\log \pr\left( \left\|I\big(wX\big)\right\|_{L^q(\Delta)}<\epsilon\right)\le C\|w\mathbbm{I}_{\Delta}\|_{r,\tau+1,q}^{1/(\tau+1)}\cdot \epsilon^{-1/(\tau+1)}+o\big(\epsilon^{-1/(\tau+1)}\big),
\end{equation}
where $\frac{1}{r}=\tau+1+\frac{1}{q}$ and the constant $C>0$ does not depend on $w$.   Moreover,  we assume that $w$ is almost everywhere continuous on $\Delta$ when $q=\infty$, and $\Delta$ is bounded when $1<q<\infty$. Then
\begin{equation}\log \pr\left( \left\|I\big(wX\big)-wI(X)\right\|_{L^q(\Delta)}<\epsilon\right)=o\big(\epsilon^{-1/(\tau+1)}\big) \text{ as }  \epsilon\to 0,
\end{equation}
if $\|w\mathbbm{I}_{\Delta}\|_{r,\tau+1,q}<\infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:25:44.992144+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cad22d678b78af862a589c8b1b4b2fee4ed9ee08f8a39f111c46ee20bb9a1bfd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 3 of arXiv:2605.11801, 'A non-local singular non-linear Fokker-Planck PDE'] Let $f\in \shc^{\g}$ for some $\gamma \in \R$. Then for any $\theta\geq 0$ there exists a constant $c$ such that
