{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:55:37.876553+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbca456dc612a95f47db629b505614b60d72f7b7b71102c04fe222fc53e6ed6d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm Thomeo of arXiv:2605.07920, 'Primitive Sequences for Probability Measures on Compact Intervals'] Equip $\pazocal{P}_{[a,b]}$ with the weak topology and $\pazocal{S}^{\mathrm{adm}}_{[a,b]}$ with the subspace topology inherited from the product topology on $\prod_{n = 0}^\infty [0, (b-a)^n/n!]$. Then the primitive sequence map $S$ is a homeomorphism between $\pazocal{P}_{[a,b]}$ and $\pazocal{S}^{\mathrm{adm}}_{[a,b]}$; in particular, for $\mu_k, \mu \in \pazocal{P}_{[a,b]}$ with $k \geq 1$, 
    \begin{equation*}
        \mu_k \rightsquigarrow \mu \quad \Longleftrightarrow \quad \varepsilon_n(\mu_k) \to \varepsilon_n(\mu), \quad n \geq 0.
    \end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:23.334804+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbcc797246f8a7a8508b4d0e8deeb1729f63ee512c6ab94fe3b2490303389252","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 19 of arXiv:2512.05466, 'Stochastic Flows and Marked Stable Processes'] The following statements hold almost surely, for all $(a,r),(a',r')\in (0,\infty)\times \bR$ and $(b,x)\in \bR_+\times (r,\infty)$: 
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item  We have $(b,x)\in \cT_{(a,r)}$ if and only if $(a,r)$ is an ancestor of $(b,x)$ for both $\cB$ and $\cR$. In particular, there is the identity
\begin{equation}
    \cT_{(a,r)}=\{(b,x)\in \bR_+\times (r,\infty)\,:\, a=\cB^*_{-x,-r}(b)=\cR^*_{-x,-r}(b)\}.
\end{equation} 

\item   If $(b,x)\in \cT_{(a,r)}$, then the points  $(\cR^*_{-x,-y}(b),y)$ and $(\cB^*_{-x,-y}(b),y)$ for $y\in (r,x)$ also belong to $\cT_{(a,r)}$.
\item  $\cT_{(a,r)}$ is bounded.
    \item Either $\cT_{(a,r)}$ and $\cT_{(a',r')}$ are disjoint or one is included in the other.

\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.608882+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbcda38dc7cf354f851f761cde05a4a508982b22f999ffc723c7a5bf08c7410c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 7 of arXiv:2607.08314, 'Berry-Heisenberg random waves'] Let $f\in L^{2}(\Hei, dg)$, where $dg$ denotes the Haar measure. Then 
    \begin{align*}
        f(g) = \int_{0}^{\infty} \left( \mathcal{P}_{\lambda }f \right)  (g) d\mu(\lambda),
    \end{align*}
    where $d\mu(\lambda)$ denotes the Plancherel measure on $\widehat{\Hei}= \R^{2} \, \dot{\cup} \, \R^{\ast}$
    \begin{align*}
        & d\mu(\lambda) := \frac{1}{(2\pi)^{2}} \vert \lambda \vert d\lambda \, 
    \end{align*}
    which is supported on $\R^{\ast}$, and 
    \begin{equation}  \mathcal{P}_{\lambda} f = \frac{\pi^{2}}{4} f \ast F_{\lambda},
    \end{equation}
    where $F_{\lambda}$ is given by \eqref{e.cov.H}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:53.158730+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbceaae40cd5f2e6a9185f2ce059ab8787dca21148c280e088a5dcf25ff1cef8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 6 of arXiv:2607.02406, 'On the range of competing random walks'] We have the following decomposition formula for $\R_n^k$
    \begin{equation}

        \R_n^k=\left|X^k(0,n)\right|-\sum_{j\ne k}\R_n^{j\rightarrow k}+\varepsilon_n^k,
    \end{equation}
    where $\varepsilon_n^k$ is an error term such that 
    \begin{equation}

        \lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\frac{h(n)^4b(n)^{2d}}{n^4}\EE\left[\left(\varepsilon_{n}^k\right)^2\right]=0.
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:00:30.494551+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbd3baeedcc621b44b9adfa3f40817e25d85eff239fbe45337e7a6e1a9c019ac","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 24 (Excavation form of octahedron recurrence) of arXiv:2607.04138, 'Hives from deformed GUE minor processes'] Let $v$ be an element of the triangle $T$.  Then there is an explicit finite family ${\mathcal W}_{v} \colon \GT_{\diag(\ast) \rel \ast} \times \GT_{\diag(\ast) \rel \ast} \to \R$ (defined in Section \ref{recurrence-sec}) of linear functionals on $\GT_{\diag(\ast) \rel \ast} \times \GT_{\diag(\ast) \rel \ast}$, such that whenever $(h,g) = \oct(g_1,g_2)$ is the image of the octahedron recurrence for some $g_1, g_2 \in \GT_{\diag(\ast) \rel \ast}$, then
\begin{equation}
 h(v) = \max_{w \in {\mathcal W}} w( g_1, g_2 ).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:38:25.218774+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbd4887b5e02467cb35b9d667776a5a2c80cbcc2e71f152733b900ad961f4200","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:type FL, duality group, type F of arXiv:2609.00233, 'A survey on mapping class groups of 3-manifolds'] We say that a group $G$ is:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item of \emph{type FL} if there is a finite-length resolution of $\mathbb{Z}$ as a $\mathbb{Z}G$-module by free finitely-generated $\mathbb{Z}G$-modules;
        \item a ($\mathbb{Z}$-)\emph{duality group} if there exists a (right) $\mathbb{Z}G$-module $D$ and $n \in \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}$ such that for all $k$ and all $\mathbb{Z}G$-modules $M$, we have natural isomorphisms
        \[H^k(G;M) \cong H_{n-k}(G;D \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}} M)\]
        (in this case say that the \emph{dimension} of $G$ is $n$);
        \item \emph{geometrically finite} (or \emph{of type F}) if there exists an aspherical finite CW-complex with fundamental group $G$, i.e.~there is a finite $K(G, 1)$-complex.
    \end{itemize}
    For any of these properties $P$, we say that $G$ is \emph{virtually P} if it has a finite-index subgroup which is $P$ (and we say $G$ is of \emph{type VFL} if it is virtually of type \emph{FL}).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:54:28.026163+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbd800408527421903fed07f65fd14858945ba437f7ba28d1ffc8453237f9ffb","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lm:corB of arXiv:2608.09465, 'Balancing fractional Brownian motion'] Let $B(t) = (B_1(t), B_2(t))^\top$ be a planar Brownian motion started from the origin with constant correlation $\rho\in (-1, 1)$. Define 
\begin{align}
&\mathcal R(\rho)\coloneqq T(\rho)^{-1}([-1 ,1]^2)\subset\mathbb{R}^2, &T(\rho) = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ \rho & \sqrt{1-\rho^2}
\end{pmatrix}. 
\end{align}
Let $0<\lambda_1(\rho) < \lambda_2(\rho)\leq \dots$ be the Dirichlet eigenvalues of the negative Laplacian $(-\Delta)$ on $\mathcal R(\rho)$, and let $\{\phi_k(\cdot; \rho)\}_{k=1}^\infty$ be the corresponding $L^2$-normalized eigenfunctions. The following results hold:
\begin{enumerate} 
\item [(i)] Fixing $\delta>0$, the small-ball probability asymptotics hold uniformly for $|\rho|\leq 1-\delta$ as $\varepsilon\to 0$:  
\begin{align}
\mathbb{P}\left\{B(t)\in [-\varepsilon, \varepsilon]^2, \forall t\in [0, 1]\right\} = C_1(\rho)\exp\left\{-\frac{\lambda_1(\rho)}{2\varepsilon^2}\right\} (1 + o(1))
\end{align}
where the constant $C_1(\rho)$ is strictly positive and given by
\begin{align}
C_1(\rho)= \phi_1(0; \rho) \int_{\mathcal R(\rho)} \phi_1(x; \rho) \mathrm{d} x, 
\end{align}
and the $o(1)$ term depends on $\delta$. 
\item [(ii)] The principal eigenvalue $\lambda_1(\rho)$ is real-analytic, even, concave for $\rho\in(-1,1)$, and satisfies the following lower bound:
\begin{align}
\lambda_1(\rho)\geq\frac{\pi^2}{4}(1+\sqrt{1-\rho^2}). 
\end{align}
Moreover, $\lambda_1'(0)=0$, $\lambda_1'(\rho)<0$ for $0<\rho<1$, $\lambda_1'(\rho)>0$ for $-1<\rho<0$, and $-\lambda_1''(0)\in [1.965,1.966]$. 
\item [(iii)] $C_1(\rho)$ is continuous for $\rho\in(-1, 1)$. 
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:54:15.702641+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbdb3ee23fd2dbcc390719983f90bb16ad290796e6af3da6aa327bee2934a094","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2608.09840, 'Langevin dynamics along the zero set of real-analytic potentials'] For each singularity type $(\lambda,m)$, let $\Dcal_{\lambda,m}$ be the set of all $f \in C^1_c(D)$ whose gradient is tangent to $Z_{\lambda,m}$ and whose support does not meet any deeper strata.
Then for any $f,g \in \Dcal_{\lambda,m}$ one has $c_\beta \Ecal_\beta(f,g) \to \Ecal_{\lambda,m}(f,g)$ as $\beta \to \infty$, where
\[
c_\beta = \beta^\lambda (\log \beta)^{1-m}
\]
and
\[
\Ecal_{\lambda,m}(f,g) = \int_{Z_{\lambda,m}} \nabla f \cdot \nabla g \, d\mu_{\lambda,m}
\]
for a Radon measure $\mu_{\lambda,m}$ on $Z_{\lambda,m}$. The bilinear form $\Ecal_{\lambda,m}$ with domain $\Dcal_{\lambda,m}$ is closable in $L^2(Z_{\lambda,m}, \mu_{\lambda,m})$, and the closure is a strongly local regular Dirichlet form. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:38:37.404238+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbdb8af95b4eb47a09ff02f281c3aa1301b6cebeea99a0713b3b5dee4b638e4d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 15 of arXiv:2605.15501, 'Well-posedness of the obstacle problem for generalized Dean--Kawasaki equation\thanks{ The'] For the lower obstacle problem, in which we require $u\geq\psi$ on $Q_T$ rather than $u\leq\psi$, the corresponding approximating penalized equation \eqref{eq:appro penalized eq} becomes
\begin{align}
\mathrm{d}u_{\alpha,n,\varepsilon} & =\Big[\Delta\Phi(u_{\alpha,n,\varepsilon})+\alpha\Delta u_{\alpha,n,\varepsilon}+\frac{1}{2}\nabla\cdot(F_{1}[\sigma_n^{\prime}(u_{\alpha,n,\varepsilon})]^{2}\nabla u_{\alpha,n,\varepsilon}+\sigma_n(u_{\alpha,n,\varepsilon})\sigma_n^{\prime}(u_{\alpha,n,\varepsilon})F_{2})\nonumber \\
 & \quad-\nabla \cdot g(u_{\alpha,n,\varepsilon})+\frac{1}{\varepsilon}(u_{\alpha,n,\varepsilon}-\psi)^{-}\Big]\mathrm{d}t-\nabla\cdot(\sigma_{n}(u_{\alpha,n,\varepsilon})\mathrm{d}\xi^{F}).
\end{align}
A priori estimates for the solution $u_{\alpha,n,\varepsilon}$ to \eqref{eq:appro penalized eq lower obstacle} remain valid under additional assumptions that $|\sigma(r)|\leq C(1+|r|)$ and $p=2$ using Gronwall inequality instead of \cite[Lemma 5.4]{fehrman2021wellarxiv}.
This includes the important Dean--Kawasaki equation
\begin{align*}
\mathrm{d}u & =\Delta\Phi(u)\mathrm{d}t+\nabla\cdot\sqrt{\Phi(u)}\circ\mathrm{d}\xi^{F},
\end{align*}
provided that $|\Phi(r)|\leq C(1+|r|^{2})$ for all $r\in\mathbb{R}$, and thus in particular covers the case $\Phi(r)=r$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

