    converges almost surely in \( X \). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:31:20.885448+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cdcb4647a25085d118c995e28fe42493f9c00982053094bbbdc29e7637b9dbc9","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definizione 1 (Weak and strong solutions) of arXiv:2506.11937, 'Random rotational invariance of integration by parts formulas within a Bismut-type approac'] Let us fix a finite time horizon $\ T >0.$  Given $\ s \rightarrow \mu(X_s,s)=(\mu_i(X_s,s))_{i=1,...,n}$ and $\ s \rightarrow \sigma(X_s,s)=(\sigma_{ij}(X_s,s))_{i=1,...,n \ \ j=1,...,m}$ processes in $\ M^1[0,T]$, $\ M^2[0,T]$ respectively, given $\ x \in \mathbb{R}^n,$ we say that the $ SDE_{\mu,\sigma}$
    \begin{equation}
        dX_t=\mu(X_t,t)dt + \sigma(X_t,t) dW_t, \quad X_0=x
    \end{equation}
    admits $ X$ as weak solution  if  there exist a filtered probability space  $\ (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathcal{F}_t, \mathbb{P})$ and an $\mathcal{F}_t$-Brownian motion $ W$ taking values in $\mathbb{R}^m$ such that, for any $ t \in [0,T],$
    \begin{equation}  
     X^i_t-x^i=\int_0^t \mu_i(X_s,s)ds + \int_0^t \sigma_{ij}(X_s,s) dW^j_s.
    \end{equation}
    In this case we say that $ X$ is a weak solution to $SDE_{\mu,\sigma}$ driven by $ W$. \\
Differently, we say that \eqref{SDE} admits $X$ as  strong solution if \eqref{sol} is satisfied for every filtered probability space $(\Omega, \mathcal{F},\mathcal{F}_t,\mathbb{P})$ and for every $\mathcal{F}_t-$Brownian motion $W$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:47:01.659890+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cdcc4f623143446dfc1e9701d91686f1b0267f557bbcef04e8adb814b4fcd949","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 28 (Spectral concentration of the rescaled fluctuation matrix) of arXiv:2604.23461, 'Scaling limit of Sinkhorn-rescaled Random Matrices \\ via Stability of Static Schrödinger '] Suppose Assumption \ref{assump:random_matrix} holds, and let $s_{\max}$ be the maximum variance defined in \eqref{eq:def_s_star}, $\A$ and $\check{\A}$ be the rescaled and comparison fluctuation matrices defined in \eqref{eq:def_check_A_and_A}. Let $t_D$, $\tau$, and $\eps_{\textup{pot}}$ be as in Theorem \ref{thm:first_approximation_rescaled}, and assume $\eps_{\textup{pot}} \le 1$. Then, on the event $\mathcal{E}_{2} \cap \{\|\X-\bLambda\|_2 \le t_D\}$,
	\begin{align}

		&\|\A\A^T - \check{\A}\check{\A}^T\|_2 \le \eps_{\textup{cov}} := \frac{3\, e^{4K}\, N^2}{(m+n)\,s_{\textup{max}}\,\|\bLambda\|_1^2}\, \eps_{\textup{pot}}\, t_D^2\, (2 + 3\eps_{\textup{pot}}).
	\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.651131+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cdcd55ec5b409045977dcfe798e724fd844c340cf66badbf39a15f5fde059af1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop bdy-sin of arXiv:2602.06011, 'Excursion decomposition of the XOR-Ising model'] Let $\alpha\in(0,1]$. Let $D$ be a bounded, simply connected domain. Fix any $A\subseteq \partial D$ to be a (deterministic) closed subset of the boundary such that $\mathrm{Leb}(A)=0$. For any continuous, bounded  function $f:\mathbb C\to\mathbb R$,
\begin{align}
    \lim_{n\to \infty} (:\! \sin(\alpha \phi) \!: , f\1_{[A]_n}) = 0, 
\end{align}
where the limit is almost sure and in $L^2(\mathbb P)$.
In particular, for any $u\in[-\pi/2,\pi/2]$,
\begin{align}
    \lim_{n\to \infty} (\field{\overline{\mathrm{cos}}(\alpha\phi+u)}, f\1_{[A]_n}) = 0,
\end{align}
where the limit is, again, almost sure and in $L^2(\mathbb P)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.280003+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cdcda9a4c969d4be865dbac502f0b9e5fd6aba3ebb2c35f6abb6ccd78b91d872","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Main1 of arXiv:2603.14302, 'On Partition Functions for Time-Inhomogeneous Branching Random Walks'] \textsc{(Phase Transition for Time-homogeneous BRWs)}
For a BRW on a supercritical Galton–Watson tree with mean offspring number $\mathbb{E}[D_1] = d > 1$, there exists a phase transition for the non-degeneracy of $W_\infty^\beta$, i.e.,
\[
\beta_c := \sup\Bigl\{\beta \geq 0 \mid \mathbb{P}\{W_\infty^\beta = 0\} > 0\Bigr\}
\]
is well-defined and equals $\sqrt{2\log d}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:25:44.580524+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cdce791e6fb16a62e0de7a55aa5312d2ef292d3ee2847dacb09565bb2326846e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem lemma:many_to_two of arXiv:2509.06730, 'Hyperbolic branching Brownian motion: \\ the empirical limit measure'] {Fix $r\geq 0$,} under $\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}^r$, let $(X_s^1,Y_s^1)_{s\geq 0}$ and $(X_s^2,Y_s^2)_{s\geq 0}$ be two hyperbolic Brownian motions that move together until time $r$ and afterwards move independently. Then we have for any interval $I\subseteq \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}$ that 
    \begin{equation*}
        \ensuremath{\mathbb{E}}\left[\mu_\infty(I)^2\right] = 2\beta\int_0^\infty\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}^r\left(X^1_\infty,X_\infty^2\in I\right) e^{-\beta r}dr.
    \end{equation*}
    Similarly for any $K>0$,
        \begin{align*}
        &\ensuremath{\mathbb{E}}\left[\mu_\infty^K(I)^2\right] = 2\beta\int_0^\infty\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}^r\bigg(X^1_\infty,X_\infty^2\in I, \\ 
        & \hspace{4cm}\forall s\geq K: \log(Y_s^1)+s/2, \log(Y_s^2)+s/2 \in [-s^{2/3},s^{2/3}]\bigg) e^{-\beta r}dr.
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:43:18.913440+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cdd014052a1d3d34ec2a58ea3899b22b3e9c3b4f9ca3b8fef8e6d889c64c0493","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop five hodge no psi local model of arXiv:2609.02409, 'Membranes and Maps'] For $Z=\mathbb{C}^{5}$, (conj:index of the paper) is equivalent to the identity
    \begin{equation}
        \mathsf{H}(~;\epsilon_1,\ldots, \epsilon_5;u)
        =
        \frac{1}{2}
        \sum_{g>1}
            \frac{B_{2g-2}}{2g-2}
            \left[
                \frac{\sum_{i=1}^5 2\sinh(u\epsilon_i)}
                {\prod_{i=1}^5 2\sinh(\tfrac{u\epsilon_i}{2}) }
            \right]_{2g-2} u^{2g-2}  \,,
