{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:13:31.943658+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cdddc984e4aecc3d419dc240aa61e8944726ee1e6528e4493dd12719c32b77b7","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor limit_shape of arXiv:2506.12171, 'The multinomial dimer model'] (Limit shape.) 
Fix $\Lambda$ and $(R,b)$ as in Theorem~(thm:ldp of the paper). First, the rate function $I$ has a unique minimizer $\omega_{\min}\in \text{AF}(\Lambda,R,b)$. Second, given $\varepsilon>0$ define the event 
    \begin{align*}
        V_\varepsilon = \{ \omega: d_W(\omega_{\min},\omega)>\varepsilon\}. 
    \end{align*}
    For any sequence of multinomial measures $\rho_{n,N}$ as in Theorem (thm:ldp of the paper), dimer covers sampled from $\rho_{n,N}$ concentrate exponentially fast on $\omega_{\min}$ in the iterated limit as $N$ and then $n$ go to infinity. In other words there is a constant $C>0$ determined by $(R,b)$ such for $n,N$ sufficiently large,
    \begin{align}
        \rho_{n,N}(V_\varepsilon) < C^{-Nn^d}. 
    \end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.473108+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cde1c5719be9eaf2a15e76e47cc6d6806fe744c2f7817dcb97f260f442f9f917","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 51 (Concrete quotient-sum model) of arXiv:2606.29292, 'Intrinsic Gaussian Tensor Geometry and Random Operator Spectra'] Let $1\le r\le2$.  For $S\subset[m]$, set
\[
 \mathfrak X_{S,r}
 :=\{K\in\mathscr K_m:\Flat_S(K)\in
 \Sch_r(\conj{\cH_S}\otimes\cC,
             \cH_{S^c}\otimes\cE)\},
 \qquad
 \norm K_{\mathfrak X_{S,r}}:=\norm{\Flat_S(K)}_{\Sch_r}.
\]
Define
\begin{equation}

 \mathfrak T_{m,r}^{\Sigma}
 :=\sum_{S\subset[m]}\mathfrak X_{S,r},
 \qquad
 \norm K_{\mathfrak T_{m,r}^{\Sigma}}
 :=\inf_{K=\sum_{S\subset[m]}K_S}
       \sum_{S\subset[m]}\norm{\Flat_S(K_S)}_{\Sch_r},
\end{equation}
where $K_S\in\mathfrak X_{S,r}$.  Equivalently,
$\mathfrak T_{m,r}^{\Sigma}$ is the quotient of the finite
$\ell^1$-sum of the $\mathfrak X_{S,r}$ by the kernel of the summation map
into $\mathscr K_m$.

In the same-field setting, label the stochastic legs as before and define
$\mathfrak W_{m,r}^{\Sigma}$ as the completion of symmetric algebraic
same-field kernels in the norm
\begin{equation}

 \norm K_{\mathfrak W_{m,r}^{\Sigma}}
 :=\norm{\Dec_mK}_{\mathfrak T_{m,r}^{\Sigma}}.
\end{equation}
Equivalently, after labelling, it is the closed range of the finite-group
symmetrizer on $\mathfrak T_{m,r}^{\Sigma}$.
Only the total kernel is required to be symmetric; the summands in
\eqref{eq:low-all-cut-sum} are not required to be symmetric.  At order
zero, put $\mathfrak T_{0,r}^{\Sigma}
=\mathfrak W_{0,r}^{\Sigma}=\Sch_r$.

For later local reconstruction, we also write
\begin{equation}

 \mathfrak T_{m,r}^{\cap}
 :=\overline{\mathcal K_{\mathrm{alg}}}^{\,\Prof_{m,r}},
 \qquad
 \mathfrak W_{m,r}^{\cap}
 :=\overline{\{K\in\mathcal K_{\mathrm{alg}}:K\text{ is symmetric}\}}^{\,
       K\mapsto\Prof_{m,r}(\Dec_mK)}.
\end{equation}
These max-cut completions are auxiliary on the low side.  They record
simultaneous local profiles, whereas
$\mathfrak T_{m,r}^{\Sigma}$ and $\mathfrak W_{m,r}^{\Sigma}$ are the
intrinsic coefficient spaces detected by Gaussian evaluation.  At $r=2$
both constructions agree isometrically with the Hilbert tensor spaces.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:14:57.636918+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cde502370de6be1df01231b3b03ee9f02ad9d974036e5217eb8bdcff01270860","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem: factorial approximation for the first representation and its conjugate of arXiv:2605.28770, 'Cutoff profiles for conjugacy invariant random walks on symmetric groups'] Let $n \geq 3$ and $t\geq 0$. Then
