{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:11:57.313235+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cde66eb2219d0178a69b321401fec47d73bb7e7dac82351425e27d43d7e66a47","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 of arXiv:2607.23305, 'On extremes of a random walk with positive drift\\ over an intermediate regularly varying '] Assume that $a:=\E\xi>0$ and that the distribution of $\tau$ is intermediate regularly varying at infinity.
If
\begin{eqnarray}
x \P\{\xi>x\} &=& o(\P\{\tau>x\})\quad\mbox{as }x\to\infty,
\end{eqnarray}
then 
\begin{eqnarray}
\P\{M_\tau>x\} &\le& (1+o(1))\P\{\tau>x/a\}\quad\mbox{as  } x\to\infty.
\end{eqnarray}
If
\begin{eqnarray}
\int_x^\infty \P\{\xi<-y\}dy &=& o(\P\{\tau>x\})\quad\mbox{as }x\to\infty,
\end{eqnarray}
then 
\begin{eqnarray}
\P\{S_\tau>x\} &\ge& (1+o(1))\P\{\tau>x/a\}\quad\mbox{as  } x\to\infty.
\end{eqnarray}
If both conditions, \eqref{F.tau.cond.sstar.upper} and \eqref{F.tau.cond.sstar.lower}, hold true then 
\begin{eqnarray}
\P\{M_\tau>x\}\ \sim\ \P\{S_\tau>x\} &\sim& \P\{\tau>x/a\}\quad\mbox{as  } x\to\infty.
\end{eqnarray} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:33:01.781824+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cdeaabada1425ff8810bedf34c328c9218784640d098ab18e4892a0fe91c12e1","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 34 (Multiplication between the $n$th and first chaos) of arXiv:2605.14630, '2605.14630'] Assume $f\in\H^{\otimes n}$ and $g\in\H$. Then 
\begin{equation}
 \hat I_n(f)\hat I_1(g) 
 = \hat I_{n+1}(f\otimes g) + \hat I_{n-1}(f\star_1g)\;,
\end{equation} 
where $\star_1$ denotes the \emph{contraction operation} 
\begin{equation}

 (f\star_1g)(i_1,\dots,i_{n-1}) 
 = \sum_{\Sigma\in\frakS(1,n)}
 \sum_{j=1}^N f(\Sigma(j,i_1,\dots,i_{n-1}))g(j)\;. 
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:06:39.318972+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cdeac178c459231b9c99aae56cfd752581a572d0c169cced135be0eedd22f88e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:higher_queue of arXiv:2607.13748, 'Load Balancing with Individually Heterogeneous Servers'] For any $h\in \mathbb{R}^{\infty}$ and $y\in \mathbb{D}_{\mathbb{R}}^{\infty}[0,\infty)$, the following system of equations 
     \begin{align}
         x_1(t) &= x_2(t) = 0,\\
         x_i(t) & = h_i + y_i(t) - \mu_{\min}\int_0^t x_i(s)ds + \mu_{\min}\int_0^t x_{i+1}(s)ds, \mbox{ for all }i\geq 3
     \end{align}
     has a unique solution $x\in \mathbb{D}_{\mathbb{R}}^{\infty}[0,\infty)$ and there exists a well-defined mapping $f:\mathbb{R}^\infty\times \mathbb{D}_{\mathbb{R}}^{\infty}[0,\infty)\to \mathbb{D}_{\mathbb{R}}^{\infty}[0,\infty)$. Furthermore, $f$ is a continuous mapping and $x=f(h,y)$ is continuous if $y$ is continuous. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

