{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:49:06.345002+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ce5ad1ad848bf82d579ee8d6eafdd867661646b72953aaad05f270c0660ce6dd","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem sec3-thm.1 of arXiv:2602.04303, '\Large\textbf{Strong solutions to SDEs with singular drifts driven by fractional Brownian '] Assume (equation cond1 of the paper), $p,q\geq2$ and $H<1/2$. Then, for $b(t,\cdot)\in L^p_xL^q_t$, under a new probability measure $\mathrm{d} \tilde{\mathbb{P}}=\xi_T\mathrm{d} \mathbb{P}$, the shifted process $\tilde{B}_t^H:=B_t^H+\int_0^tb(s,B_s^H)\mathrm{d} s$ is a $\mathcal{F}_t^{B^H}$-fractional Brownian motion. Here, $\xi_T$ is defined by (equation Girsanov of the paper) and for any $r\geq1$, there exists a constant $C=C(H,d,p,q,T,\Vert b\Vert_{L_x^pL_tq},r)$ such that
			\begin{equation}
				\mathbb{E}^{\tilde{\mathbb{P}}}[\xi_T^{-r}]=	\mathbb{E}^{\tilde{\mathbb{P}}}\left[\frac{\mathrm{d} \mathbb{P}}{\mathrm{d}\tilde{\mathbb{P}}}\right]^r\leq C
			\end{equation}
			Moreover, the equation (equation sec1-eq.1 of the paper) has a weak solution, and any two weak solutions have the same probability law.","status":"unverified"}

