{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:02:11.314541+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ced35cc1b6c8073b744d9f12e4a93f26e0dfca5c5ad858e9a8b197f445dafc25","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem LDP-relating-X1-X of arXiv:2510.26123, 'Directed distances in bipolar-oriented triangulations:\\ exact exponents and scaling limit'] Let ${\operatorname{XDP}} = \operatorname{LDP}$. Define the KMSW encoding walk $\mathcal Z  :\mathbbm Z\to\mathbbm Z^2$, the infinite directed triangulations $M_{0,\infty} \overset{d}{=} M_{1,\infty}$, and their associated Busemann functions $\mathcal X$ and $\mathcal X^1$ as at the beginning of Section~(sec-recursive of the paper).

    \begin{itemize}
        \item If $\mathcal Z(1)-\mathcal Z(0)=(1,-1)$, then 
            \begin{equation} 
            \Big(\mathcal X(-1)\,,\,\mathcal X(1)\Big)=\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft(\mathcal X^1(-2) - \max\{\mathcal X^1(-1),1\}\,,\,  - \max\{\mathcal X^1(-1),1\}\aftergroup\egroup\originalright). 
            \end{equation}
        \item If $\mathcal Z(1)-\mathcal Z(0)=(-1,0)$, then 
           \begin{equation} 
           \Big(\mathcal X(-1)\,,\,\mathcal X(1)\Big)=\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft( - \mathcal X^1(1)-1\,,\,-1\aftergroup\egroup\originalright).
           \end{equation}
        \item If $\mathcal Z(1)-\mathcal Z(0)=(0,1)$, then 
           \begin{equation}  
           \Big(\mathcal X(-1)\,,\,\mathcal X(1)\Big)=\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft(\mathcal X^1(-1) \,,\, \mathcal X^1(2) \aftergroup\egroup\originalright).
           \end{equation}
    \end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:34:45.714765+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ced7b727e9c48befb2c3a93b373c90c3bc2ef0ef2f97fc71a8a804db1e42b2f1","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Lemma-Laplace-Kernel of arXiv:2609.01964, 'The critical exponent for the two-dimensional semilinear wave equation with strong damping'] For $s>0$ and $r>0$, one has
	\begin{align}
		\widetilde G_1^{(2)}(s,r)=\frac{1}{2\pi(1+s)}K_0\left(\frac{rs}{\sqrt{1+s}}\right),
	\end{align}
	where $K_0$ denotes the modified Bessel function of the second kind  of order zero. Furthermore, for every $r>0$, the right-hand side of (equation Laplace-G2 of the paper) is completely monotone as a function of $s$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:17:04.321975+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ced87455ca23e6a5594540197ac34cb8ff3f972c6889429dd75e2fa01bc65765","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary main global corollary of arXiv:2606.01683, 'Singular-value gap of nonreversible Markov processes'] Assume the global singular-value gap $\gamma$ defined in (equation gap definition continuous time of the paper) 
