{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:19:47.250233+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cee4a089d2eba6687c6dbb789a8a71bf617e1c17b25570c1c3848c52a2e9c41d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:spine-decomp-2 of arXiv:2512.21159, '\textsc{From multitype branching Brownian motions\\ to branching Markov additive processes'] Let $x\in \mathbb{R}$ and $i\in \mathcal{I}$. Under $\widetilde{\mathbf{P}}_{x,i}^{\uparrow}$, 
the branching MAP is described
as follows.
\begin{itemize}
     \item There is a spine starting from position $x$ with type $i$ and moves according to a MAP conditioned to stay positive defined by (equation eq:MAP-cond of the paper). 
     \item Given the type process $\Theta$ of the spine, the branching rate at time $t\ge 0$ is given by $\beta_{\Theta_t} m_{\Theta_t}$;
     when it splits, it gives birth to an offspring of the same type at the same position. The number of children is given by the size-biased law $\widetilde{\mu}_j(k) := \frac{k\mu_j(k)}{m_j}$, for $k\ge 1$ and $j\in \mathcal{I}$. 
     \item Choose one child uniformly at random, which continues as the spine; each of the other children leads a subpopulation of the original law $\mathbf{P}$, independent of each other. The spine continues in a similar way.  
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:23:03.043322+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cee63260ded5e78997624b707c81c94e1f327dfb9d22b13e84d3f8a6bbc87a2c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 70 of arXiv:2605.03236, 'Stochastic It\^o Equations and Parabolic Second-Order Equations with singular Drift'] Let $\tau=0$, $\cA=\Omega$, so that
\eqref{10.16.1} becomes
\begin{equation}

