{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:17:04.325432+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cf4e8f77247cc7167ddb65d2938b268e5ce9c056e7b6f98c9a8236962c9d07ae","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:domregime of arXiv:2606.01866, 'Phase Transition of a Semiflexible Membrane in Two Dimensions'] For $d=2$, let $\{\phi_x\}_{x\in V_N}$ be as in (thm:compregime of the paper). Fix $\delta\in(0,1)$. Then the following holds for $x,y\in V_N^\delta$:
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
        \item(DGFF domination regime) If $\lambda<0$, then
            \[\operatorname{Cov}(\phi_x,\phi_y)=\operatorname{Cov}^\nabla_N(\phi_x,\phi_y)+O(N^\lambda).\]
        \item(MM domination regime) If $\lambda>2$, then
        \[\operatorname{Cov}(\phi_x,\phi_y)=N^{-\lambda}\operatorname{Cov}^{\Delta}_N(\phi_x,\phi_y)+O(N^{4-2\lambda}\log N).\]
    \end{enumerate}
    Here, $\operatorname{Cov}^{\Delta}_N(\phi_x,\phi_y)$ denotes the covariance of the two-dimensional MM with zero boundary conditions. It coincides with the Green function $G^{\Delta}_N(x,y)$ of the following boundary value problem: for any fixed $y\in V_N$,
    \begin{equation}
        \begin{aligned}
            \Delta^2 G^{\Delta}_N(x,y)&=\delta_y(x),&&\quad \forall x\in V_N,\\
            G^{\Delta}_N(x,y)&=0,&&\quad \forall x\in\partial_2 V_N.
        \end{aligned}
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:55:42.094982+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cf4ff4c1d472042ac79631c460bd8a9478b3f91855465508879b92f09e23efd1","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Had1 of arXiv:2411.15830, 'Deformations of biorthogonal ensembles and universality'] For a $k\times k$ matrix $M$, denote its $j$-th column by $M_j \in \mathbb{C}^k$, and for a vector $v=(v_1,\cdots, v_k)^T\in \mathbb{C}^k$ denote

    $$
    \|v\|_2\mathrel{\vcenter{\baselineskip0.5ex \lineskiplimit0pt
                     \hbox{\scriptsize.}\hbox{\scriptsize.}}}
                     = \left(\sum_{i=1}^k |v_i|^2 \right)^{1/2},\quad \|v\|_\infty \mathrel{\vcenter{\baselineskip0.5ex \lineskiplimit0pt
                     \hbox{\scriptsize.}\hbox{\scriptsize.}}}
                     = \sup_{1\leq i\leq k} |v_i|.
    $$

    Then for any $k\times k$ matrices $M$ and $L$, the inequalities

\begin{equation}
    |\det M|\leq \prod_{i=1}^k \|M_i\|_2
