{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:17.367211+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cfe75e5a9eda94416b2073ea059033eaf3ab5a6b6a24299bcd5455dce7909f1a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 7 of arXiv:2606.02856, 'Geometric Routing in Geometric Inhomogeneous Random Graphs'] Let \(w_0, D_0\) be sufficiently large constants. The geometric routing protocol finds a path from a vertex of weight at least \(w_0\) to a vertex within distance \(D_0\) of \(t\), which constitutes the main part of the routing, whose length is upper-bounded by
\begin{equation}
\frac{1 + \oh{1}}{\abs{\log(\tau - 2)}} \left( \log \log_{w_0} (D_{s}^d) + \log \log_{D_0} (D_{s}) \right) + \Oh{f_0(n)},
\end{equation}
where \(D_s = \norm{x_s -x_t}\) and \(\om{1} \leq f_0(n) \leq o(\log\log n)\). As \( D_s \leq n^{1/d}\), the bound evaluates to \[\frac{2+\oh{1}}{\abs{\log(\tau-2)}}\log \log n.\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.609389+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cfe85bc9d3f172e8ecd5ea725d99c0cb56f7433b8edc309b87e0250dd22977f7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma E:5.67a of arXiv:2412.08964, 'Phase transition and critical behavior in hierarchical\\integer-valued Gaussian and Coulom'] For each~$\alpha\in(-\ffrac12,\ffrac12)$ there exists~$\tilde \epsilon>0$ and~$\widetilde C,\widetilde\eta>0$ such that for all~$\beta>\beta_\text{\rm c}$ satisfying $1/\beta>1/\beta_\text{\rm c}-\tilde\epsilon$,
