{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:48:25.155984+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d069f7c7a08328df7be4de473538ec0082e814cb1ba6331d371c5ea1beb7482c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:maincgce of arXiv:2607.05671, 'Bi-infinite systems of singularly interacting Brownian particles and the KPZ equation'] As $\varepsilon \to 0$, $\tilde \mathcal{E}^{\varepsilon}_{\theta}$ converges to $u$, in distribution, in $C([0, \infty); \mathcal{C}(\mathbb{R}))$.
Furthermore, for any $r \ge 1$, $T<\infty$ and compact $K\subset \mathbb{R}$,
