    satisfies $\var D(R)\sim C R^d$, $C>0$, and $\frac{D(R)}{\sqrt{\var D(R)}}$ converges in 1-Wasserstein distance to a standard Gaussian random variable as $\R\to\infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:50:48.843456+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d08115545df271d08fb27337d0091cdf3498b768530b5364f3b25976b670d2fc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:max.ent.POC of arXiv:2607.07598, 'Universal Central Limit Theorem for non-exchangeable interacting diffusions'] Suppose Assumption (assum.main of the paper) holds with $k \ge 0$. Then, for each $t\in[0,T]$:
			\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
				\item \emph{(Maximum three-particle entropy.)}
				\begin{align*} 
					\max_{\substack{i,j,k\in[n]\\ i\neq j,\ i\neq k,\ j\neq k}}
					H(P_t^{ijk}\,|\, \mu_t^{\otimes 3})
					\lesssim
					\max_{i, j \in [n]}\xi_{ij}^2.
				\end{align*}
				\item \emph{(Average two-particle entropy.)}
				\begin{align*}
					\frac{1}{n(n-1)}\sum_{\substack{i, j \in [n] \\ i \neq j}} H(P_t^{ij}\,|\, \mu_t^{\otimes 2})
					\lesssim
					\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n\Big(\sum_{j=1}^n(\xi_{ij}^2+\xi_{ji}^2)\Big)^2.
				\end{align*}
			\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:51:00.235943+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d0831441e9760bf2815d7d8d181d9862acabec0c58c5709474d2278bf3e9811a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:barb of arXiv:2604.22510, 'Asymptotics of Multi-Scale McKean--Vlasov Diffusions with Super-Linear Kernels: a Lifted S'] $(i)$ $($Coercivity$)$ For any $x\in {\mathbb R}^n, \mu\in \mathscr{P}_{\kappa}({\mathbb R}^n)$, we have
		\begin{align}
			\langle \bar{b}(x,\mu), x\rangle \lesssim \big(1+|x|^2+M_2(\mu)\big).
		\end{align}
		$(ii)$ $($Exponential ergodicity$)$ There exist  positive constants $C_{\kappa}, \beta$ such that for any $x\in {\mathbb R}^n, \mu\in \mathscr{P}_{\kappa}({\mathbb R}^n), \gamma\in \mathscr{P}_{\kappa}({\mathbb R}^m), z\in {\mathbb R}^m$,
		\begin{align}
			\big|\tilde{\mathbb{E}}\,b\big(x,\mu,Y_t^{\mu,\gamma,z},\mathscr{L}_{Y_t^{\mu,\gamma}}\big)-\bar{b}(x,\mu)\big|\lesssim\big(1+|x|^{\kappa}+|z|^{\kappa}+M_{\kappa}(\mu)+M_{\kappa}(\gamma)\big)e^{-\beta t}.
		\end{align}
		$ (iii)$ $($Local Lipschitz$)$ For any $x_1,x_2\in{\mathbb R}^n$, $\mu, \nu\in\mathscr{P}_{\kappa}({\mathbb R}^n)$, it holds that
		\begin{align}
			&|\bar{b}(x_1,\mu)-\bar{b}(x_2,\nu)| \nonumber\\
		\lesssim&\big(1+M_{\kappa}^{\frac{1}{2}}(\mu)+M_{\kappa}^{\frac{1}{2}}(\nu)\big)\big|x_1-x_2\big| \nonumber\\
		&+\big(1+|x_1|^{\frac{\kappa}{2}}+ |x_2|^{\frac{\kappa}{2}}+M_{\kappa}^{\frac{1}{2}}(\mu)+M_{\kappa}^{\frac{1}{2}}(\nu)\big)\big(1+M_{\kappa}^{\frac{1}{2}}(\mu)+M_{\kappa}^{\frac{1}{2}}(\nu)\big)  \mathbb{W}_2(\mu,\nu).
			\end{align}
		In particular, we obtain the growth of $\bar{b}$ in the sense that for any $x\in {\mathbb R}^n, \mu\in \mathscr{P}_{\kappa}({\mathbb R}^n)$, it holds that
		\begin{align}
			|\bar{b}(x,\mu)|\lesssim \big(1+M_{\kappa}^{\frac{1}{2}}(\mu)\big)|x|
			+\big(1+|x|^{\frac{\kappa}{2}}+M_{\kappa}^{\frac{1}{2}}(\mu)\big)\big(1+M_{\kappa}^{\frac{1}{2}}(\mu)\big)  M_2^{\frac{1}{2}}(\mu).
		\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:26.254654+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d083f58f7eecb46c9ace4bb308be0103b56312d4c68c791d9405c589cc488090","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 22 of arXiv:2605.23308, 'Convergence rate estimates for semigroups and heat kernels associated with resistance form'] For $\calX_n=(X_n,d^{X_n},\mu_{X_n}),\;\calX=(X,d^X,\mu_X)\in\frK(\tau)$,
the following conditions are equivalent to each other.
\begin{itemize}
  \item [$\mathrm{(i)}$]There exist a compact metric space $(M,d^M)$ and isometric embeddings $f_n:X_n\to M,\;f:X\to M$
  such that $f_n(X_n)\to f(X)$ in the Hausdorff topology, and $\mu_{X_n}\circ f_n^{-1}\to \mu_{X}\circ f^{-1}$ weakly.  
