{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.679872+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d0993f2971ae3d27cc3be5839d0faf2abfd74936070b8c48f119cb71e1412089","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:manifoldsSP of arXiv:2412.09235, 'A semiconcavity approach to stability of entropic plans and exponential convergence of Sin'] Let $M$ be a compact smooth Riemannian manifold and assume that $\nu$ satisfies (eq:TI of the paper). Then for every $\varepsilon \in (0,1]$ Sinkhorn's algorithm converges exponentially fast. Moreover, as soon as $\varepsilon$ is small enough (cf.~(equation eps:small:manifold:gen of the paper)) the exponential convergence rate equals
\begin{equation*}
1-\frac{\varepsilon^2}{\varepsilon^2+  2\tau C'(\varepsilon+{\textrm{diam}}(M)^2) + \tau C(\varepsilon+\kappa^-\varepsilon(1+ \varepsilon)+1) }
\end{equation*}
where $\kappa \in \mathbb{R}$ denotes a lower bound for the Ricci curvature and $C,C'$ are constants depending on the dimension, Ricci and sectional curvatures of $M$. Finally, if $M$ has non-negative Ricci curvature, then the exponential convergence of Sinkhorn's algorithm holds for any regularizing parameter $\varepsilon>0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:25:06.156126+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d09a533ca47e561a8eada1b92b37dc419b84e642e1c1debc288fb4eb3e0ca59e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 19 of arXiv:2608.22534, 'Entropy power inequalities in compact groups'] Let $G$ be a connected compact abelian group, let $\mu$ be its 
Haar probability measure and $Y$ be a random variable on $G$ with 
density $f_Y$. Let $S$ be a set of positive Haar measure 
and $g_S = \frac{1}{\mu(S)}\mathbb{I}_S$ be the density of a uniform 
random variable $U_S$ on $S$. For $t>0$, also define
    \begin{equation*} 
    g^t(y) = \frac{t+f_Y(y)}{1+t}, \quad y \in G.
    \end{equation*}
    Then, for all $t > 0 $
\begin{equation*} 
    \chi^2(f_Y \ast g_S \| g^t  \ast g_S) \leq \eta_S\frac{t+\|f_Y\|_{\infty}}{t}\chi^2(f_Y\|g^t),
\end{equation*}
where
$$\eta_S = \left(\frac{\sin(\pi \mu(S))}{\pi \mu(S)}\right)^2.$$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:24:25.931818+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d09abe470cb2f643e78106e404f11c15d670359bed68d46a254da00bd63686ff","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 22 (Decomposition of $\TLis{k}$ at the root) of arXiv:2506.19123, 'The longest increasing subsequence of Brownian separable permutons'] Let $k \geq 1$. Then the law of $\left( \lis(\TLis{k}_{\lfa}), \lis(\TLis{k}_{\rga}), D \right)$ is described as follows:
    \begin{enumerate}
		\item  With probability $\frac{1}{2 q(1)} \mathds{1}_{k=1}$, the tree $\TLis{k}$ consists of a single vertex, which is a leaf.
		\item  For all $1\leq i < k$, with probability $\frac 1 2 (1-p) q(i)$, the root $\rho_k$ is negative, $\lis(\TLis{k}_{\lfa})=i$ and $\lis(\TLis{k}_{\rga})=k$. 
        Moreover, in this case $D=\rga$. 
		\item  For all $1\leq i \leq k$, with probability $ \frac 1 2 (1-p) q(i)$, the root $\rho_k$ is negative, $\lis(\TLis{k}_{\lfa})=k$ and $\lis(\TLis{k}_{\rga})=i$. Moreover, in this case $D=\lfa$.
		\item  For all $1 \leq i \leq k-1$, with probability $ \frac p 2  \frac{q(i) q(k-i)}{q(k)}$, the root $\rho_k$ is positive, $\lis(\TLis{k}_{\lfa})=i$ and $\lis(\TLis{k}_{\rga})=k-i$. Moreover, conditionally on this, the direction $D$ is $\lfa$ with probability $\frac{i}{k}$ and $\rga$ with probability $\frac{k-i}{k}$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Moreover, conditionally on $\{( \lis(\TLis{k}_{\lfa}), \lis(\TLis{k}_{\rga}), D )=(k_1, k_2, d)\}$, the trees $\TLis{k}_{\lfa}$ and $\TLis{k}_{\rga}$ are independent trees with respective laws $\TLis{k_1}$ and $\TLis{k_2}$, and $L^k$ is a uniform leaf of $(\TLis{k}_{d})^{\lmax}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:45:07.262929+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d09c8b7c864143606c19f9c8c7aaff3d2bf133956edbf53e68b154e5f03c3251","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 25 of arXiv:2604.19738, 'Phase Transitions in the Fluctuations of Functionals of Random Neural Networks \\'] Let $\widehat \kappa_L$ be as in \eqref{defkappahatL} and assume \Cref{ass:cri-pot}. Then for $q\ge2$ we have the following.

