{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:47:12.032522+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d154e5688ed4158aaaee7de13d13343538757eed28d8d1af3fb4545ea4a1037b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem-Lemma 4.1 of arXiv:2512.17727, 'Stochastic transport equation with L\'evy noise'] Assume that $Y$ is a normed vector space and $\gamma \in (0, 1]$.
If   $f \in C^{1+ \gamma}_\mathrm{b}(\mathbb R^d,Y)$ and  $x, y, z \in \mathbb R^d$, then
 \begin{equation}
 | f(x+y) - f(y) - f(x+z) + f(z) | \le
 [ Df ]_{\gamma}
 \, \vert y- z| \, |x|^{\gamma}.
\end{equation}
If $\delta \in (0,\gamma]$,   $f \in C^{\gamma}_\mathrm{b}(\mathbb R^d,Y)$ and  $x \in \mathbb R^d$, then
 \begin{equation}
 [ \tau_x f - f ]_{\delta} \leq  2 \vert x \vert^{\gamma-\delta} [f]_{\gamma},
 \end{equation}
i.e., for all $x, y, z \in \mathbb R^d$,
 \begin{equation}
 \vert  f(x+y)- f(y)
 -  f(x+z)+ f(z)
 \vert \leq  2 \vert x \vert^{\gamma-\delta} [f]_{\gamma} \vert y-z\vert^{\delta}.
 \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:48:35.818764+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d156de97011f067841692ae4e0613083035f469b3ab9d1d64c0391cc672aeb44","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:consistency-var of arXiv:2411.09516, 'Sharp Matrix Empirical Bernstein Inequalities\footnote{NeurIPS 2025 Poster}'] Let $ \mathbf{X} _n$ be adapted to $\mathcal{F}$ with constant conditional mean $ \mathbb E [ \mathbf{X} _n|\mathcal{F}_{n-1}] =  \mathbf{M} $ and conditional variance $ \mathbb E [( \mathbf{X} _n -  \mathbf{M} )^2|\mathcal{F}_{n-1}] =  \mathbf{V} $. Further, assume that
    \begin{equation}
        \sum_{n=1}^\infty n^{-1-\delta}  \mathbb E [ \| \mathbf{X} _n \|^{2+2\delta} | \mathcal{F}_{n-1}  ] < \infty
    \end{equation}
    almost surely for some $\delta \in (0,1]$.
    Then, the sample variance
    \begin{equation}
        \overline  \mathbf{V} _n = \frac 1 n \sum_{i=1}^n ( \mathbf{X} _i - \overline{ \mathbf{X} }_n)^2
    \end{equation}
    converges to $ \mathbf{V} $ almost surely.","status":"unverified"}

