{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:05:28.219406+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d17da52fc7f87be3792f62219c96207b15c1294f2e9f93473f1751177c5b9f80","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: convergence of the pinning measure of arXiv:2602.04429, 'Disordered systems and (subcritical) polynomial chaos\\ with heavy-tail disorder'] {}
	Under the same assumptions as Theorem (thm: convergence of the partition function of the pinning model of the paper), for every $\hat{\beta} \in [0,\infty)$ and \(\hat{h} \in {\ensuremath{\mathbb R}} \), there exists a random probability measure ${\ensuremath{\mathbf Q}} _{\hat{h}}^{\boldsymbol\zeta,\hat{\beta}}$ on ${\ensuremath{\mathcal C}} _{\infty}$ such that the following convergence in distribution holds in ${\ensuremath{\mathcal M}} _1({\ensuremath{\mathcal C}} _{\infty})$,
