{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:53:10.210410+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d19d76f091f7a3272a2748630f3b67d0ecd992c6c5cd6423d75792b4dc49c801","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm of arXiv:2608.29013, 'Weighted Homology and Cohomology of Weighted Polyhedra'] Let $G$ be a discrete group acting on a space $Y$. Assume that $Y$ admits an equivariant triangulation and that the isotropy group at each point is finite. Let $X=Y/G$, and define a weight function $\lambda$ on $X$ to be the order of the local group at each point. Then we have
     \begin{equation*}
         H_*(Y; \mathbf{m}_{\mathbb Z}) \cong \mathcal{H}_*^W(X, \lambda). 
     \end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:40.362356+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d19e1da8a09ff81e07d353b2cc462bc6d2283ca6b07e4eff5f6f745eb974e196","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 64 of arXiv:2511.09517, 'From Cannings model to Brownian motion conditioned on local time profile'] For any $\delta>0$, $k\ge1$, and sequences $(a_i^n)$, $(a_i)$, $(b_i^n)$, $(b_i)$ satisfying the above assumptions, we have uniformly for all $s\in (\delta,h(\ell)-\delta)\cap\frac{1}{n}\mathbb{Z}$,
\begin{align*}
        \mathbb{P}_n\left(Y^n(s-\tfrac{1}{n})=y^n(s-\tfrac{1}{n})\,|\,B_s\right)
        =\,\begin{cases}
            1+o_n(1),&\quad\text{if }s-\tfrac{1}{n}\in \{a^n_1,\ldots,a^n_k\},\\
            \dfrac{\binom{y^n(s)}{2}\sigma_n(s)^2}{q_n(s)}\big(1+o_n(1)\big),&\quad\text{if }s-\tfrac{1}{n}\in \{b^n_1,\ldots,b^n_m\},\\
            1-\dfrac{\binom{y^n(s)}{2}\sigma_n(s)^2}{q_n(s)} \big(1 + o_n(1)\big),&\quad\text{otherwise}.
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:51:26.682562+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d19e2544bf14b2ee9c3a02cc968d9aac87ed36d6691e3c539c1c9c616e5bd94e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:linear-critical-window of arXiv:2606.06612, 'The limit shape and emergence of the \\ Discrete Gaussian level lines'] Fix a constant $\alpha \in (0, 1)$. Set $\lambda_* = $ $\tfrac{1}{\beta}(2\tau(0) + \tfrac{\mathsf w_1}{2})$. For any loop $\gamma$ in the unit square with an interior area of $|A(\gamma)| \geq \alpha$, we have that
    \[\ensuremath{\mathcal F}_\lambda(\gamma)\leq \begin{cases}
			\beta(\lambda - \lambda_*), & \text{if $\lambda \geq \lambda_*$}\\
            -\beta(\lambda_* - \lambda)\alpha, & \text{if $\lambda < \lambda_*$ and $1 - (4\tau(0)^2- \tfrac{\mathsf w_1^2}{4})\tfrac{1}{\beta^2\lambda^2} \geq \alpha$.}
		 \end{cases}\]
    Moreover, if $\lambda \geq \lambda_*$, then $\sup_\gamma\ensuremath{\mathcal F}_\lambda(\gamma) = \ensuremath{\mathcal F}_\lambda({\ensuremath{\mathscr L}}(\lambda))$ and $\ensuremath{\mathcal F}_\lambda({\ensuremath{\mathscr L}}(\lambda)) \geq (\beta - (4\tau(0)^2- \tfrac{\mathsf w_1^2}{4})\tfrac{1}{\beta\lambda^2})(\lambda - \lambda_*)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:53:54.836563+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d19ebd7388cccc3701b26128e5aac78fde032f3fa794371a91f558e487fabc6d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 18 (Subcritical and critical component density) of arXiv:2607.16833, 'Dynamical Equations for Poisson Galton--Watson Trees and Component Densities of Sparse Inh'] Suppose $u\kappa\in L^1(\pi\otimes\pi)$ and $\cT_u$ becomes extinct almost
surely.  Then $q_u=1$ $\pi$-almost everywhere, $c_u=\pi$, and
\begin{equation}
 m_\pi(u):=\iint_{S^2}u\kappa(x,y)\pi(\dd x)\pi(\dd y)<\infty.
\end{equation}
In this case
\begin{equation}
 \gamma(u)=\bE_\pi\frac1{T_u}=1-\frac{m_\pi(u)}2,
\end{equation}
and the BJR graph in Theorem~\ref{thm:IRG-general} satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \frac{K(G_n)}n\longrightarrow1-\frac{m_\pi(u)}2
 \qquad\text{in probability and in $L^1$.}
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:49:17.498858+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d19fb6cb2291056a2b7e3129e885554d4440757ac7af42c973081c7937bf2a86","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 (Dynamics at $\hat{J}=+\infty$) of arXiv:2605.29608, '2605.29608'] For $\hat{J}=+\infty$ (critical point), the Wolff dynamics reaches the set $\{-\textbf{1},+\textbf{1}\}$ in finitely many steps almost surely. Moreover, once the chain enters $\{-\textbf{1},+\textbf{1}\}$, it remains in this set thereafter. More precisely, if the initial configuration $Y_{1}\in\{-\textbf{1},+\textbf{1}\}$, then for any $k\geq 1$
	\begin{equation*}
		Y_{k}=
		\begin{cases}
			Y_{1}, & k \ \text{is odd},\\
			-Y_{1}, & k \ \text{is even}.
		\end{cases}
	\end{equation*}
	Furthermore, for any initial configuration $\tilde{\sigma}\in\Omega$ with $\tilde{\sigma}\notin\{-\textbf{1},+\textbf{1}\}$, we have
	\begin{align*}
		\mathbb{P}(Y_{\tilde{\mathcal{C}}^{\tilde{\sigma}}}\in\{-\textbf{1},+\textbf{1}\}\mid Y_{1}=\tilde{\sigma})=1,
	\end{align*}
	and consequently, for any $k\geq 0$,
	\begin{align*}
		\mathbb{P}(Y_{\tilde{\mathcal{C}}^{\tilde{\sigma}}+k}\in\{-\textbf{1},+\textbf{1}\}\mid Y_{1}=\tilde{\sigma})=1.
	\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:09.860888+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d1a0c0a2bcae53e22da1b0cd36f68183e10fcfc920350750bb5da1244a9df9f0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2605.21209, 'Sensitivity analysis of Stochastic Fluid Models: \\Stationary and transient quantities wit'] For $s$ with $Re(s)>0$, the matrix $\frac{\partial}{\partial\btheta}\bXi(s;\btheta)$ is the solution of the equation
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\bf A}{\bf X}+{\bf X}{\bf B}&=&-{\bf D},
\end{eqnarray*}
where
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\bf A}&=&
{\bf J}(s;\btheta)
,\\
{\bf B}&=&
{\bf I}\otimes {\bf U}(s;\btheta)
,\\
{\bf D}&=&
\frac{\partial
{\bf Q}_{-+}(s;\btheta)
}{\partial \btheta}
+
\frac{\partial {\bf Q}_{--}(s;\btheta)}
{ \partial \btheta}
\times\left(
{\bf I}\otimes \bXi(s;\btheta)
\right)
+
\bXi(s;\btheta)
\times
\frac{\partial  {\bf Q}_{++}(s;\btheta)}
{\partial \btheta}
\\
&&
+
\bXi(s;\btheta)
\times
\left\{
\frac{\partial {\bf Q}_{+-}(s;\btheta)}
{ \partial \btheta}
\times\left(
{\bf I}\otimes \bXi(s;\btheta)
\right)
\right\}
.
\end{eqnarray*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:18:54.371684+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d1a2a53b381e747fdc83fe8b5aed2d95b226abe24506ba4856ec15c4ffe2399a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] We have an isomorphism $L^2(\mathbb R)_{per}\simeq l^2(\mathbb Z)$, as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Associated to $f\in L^2(\mathbb R)_{per}$ are its Fourier coefficients, given by:
$$\widehat{f}(n)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^\pi f(t)e^{int}dt$$

\item Associated to $g\in l^2(\mathbb Z)$ is the series $S_g(t)=\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}g(n)e^{-int}$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:58.364969+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d1a541d4cba7a219171e8c6a830c4ed2363d6a3d790570a640d45f48491d188c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 14 of arXiv:2506.20146, 'Parabolic Anderson Model in the Hyperbolic Space. Part I: Annealed Asymptotics'] One has 
