{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:48:35.782858+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d290f927a033c8fb62d0118efe2c07df65065de785ee3b0259f13dca31f37f0b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thmIgfpmf of arXiv:2411.09257, 'Iterated Generalized Counting Process and its Extensions'] The state probabilities $\hat{q}^\alpha(n,t)=\mathrm{Pr}\{\hat{M}^\alpha(t)=n\}$, $n\ge0$ of time-changed IGCP are given by
{\footnotesize
	\begin{equation*}
		\hat{q}^\alpha(n,t)=\sum_{\Omega(k,n)}\Big(\prod_{j=1}^{k}\frac{\lambda_{j}^{n_{j}}}{n_{j}!}\Big)\sum_{\sum_{j_0=1}^{k_0}r_{j_0}=z_{k}}z_{k}!\Big(\prod_{j_0=1}^{k_0}\frac{j_0^{r_{j_0}}}{r_{j_0}!}\Big)\sum_{\substack{x_{j_0}\ge0\\1\le j_0\le k_0}}\Big(\prod_{j_0=1}^{k_0}\frac{x_{j_0}^{r_{j_0}}(\mu_{j_0}t^\alpha e^{-j_0\lambda})^{x_{j_0}}}{x_{j_0}!}\Big)z_{k_0}! E_{\alpha,\alpha z_{k_0}+1}^{z_{k_0}+1}(-\mu t^\alpha),
\end{equation*}}
where  $\Omega(k,n)=\{(n_1,n_2,\dots,n_k):\sum_{j=1}^{k}jn_j=n,\, n_j\in\mathbb{N}_0\}$, $z_k=n_1+n_2+\dots+n_k$ and $z_{k_0}=x_1+x_2+\dots+x_{k_0}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.472529+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d291d3eb3550adad979214d5ebb9092eb5e385b9b4ced6bb7b1cb4e3aa592a4e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 48 (Wick polarization) of arXiv:2606.29292, 'Intrinsic Gaussian Tensor Geometry and Random Operator Spectra'] Let $m\ge1$ and let $K$ be a symmetric algebraic order-$m$ kernel over
$\cH_{\R}$, and let $W_1,\ldots,W_m$ be independent copies of the
isonormal process.  For $\varepsilon\in\{-1,1\}^m$, set
\[
 W_\varepsilon
 :=m^{-1/2}\sum_{j=1}^m\varepsilon_jW_j.
\]
Then every $W_\varepsilon$ is isonormal and
\begin{equation}

 \cT_{\Dec_mK}^{(m)}(W_1,\ldots,W_m)
 =\frac{m^{m/2}}{2^m m!}
 \sum_{\varepsilon\in\{-1,1\}^m}
 \left(\prod_{j=1}^m\varepsilon_j\right)
 I_m(K;W_\varepsilon).
\end{equation}
Consequently, for $1\le p<\infty$ and every $1\le r\le\infty$ for which
the algebraic evaluations are measured in $\Sch_r$,
\begin{equation}

 \norm{\cT_{\Dec_mK}^{(m)}}_{L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)}
 \le\frac{m^{m/2}}{m!}
 \norm{I_m(K)}_{L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)}.
\end{equation}
For $2\le r<\infty$, both statements extend by density to
$K\in\mathfrak W_{m,r}$.  On the quotient-sum completion, the algebraic
identity is used below; after Theorem~\ref{thm:low-Schatten-Fock}
establishes continuity of both evaluations, it extends there by density.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:40:26.993656+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d292bff501bb17b0740a88fb0b15e1f2dcb45c9f368fc38a1dca4e9f6fee945b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem-exponentiallytight of arXiv:2604.14318, '\large The free energy of the interacting Bose gas:\\\medskip a variational description \\'] Suppose that Assumption (V) holds. Then the following holds.
\begin{enumerate}
\item For any $R,L\in \mathbb{N}$, the set $K_{R}^{{{\scriptscriptstyle{({\mathcal S }})}}}(L)$ is closed in the weak topology. 
Furthermore, the set
\begin{equation}
