{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:32:28.993471+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d2bca0dea846038c9b35463c407940dada402efb0fa86cc5c6c7c440fda834e1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop magnetization of arXiv:2608.25362, 'Fluctuations of the free energy of the Sherrington--Kirkpatrick model with ferromagnetic i'] The following statements hold:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{\roman*)}]
    \item For any $\beta\geqslant0$ and $0<\gamma\leqslant1$, we have $\Omega(\beta,\gamma)=\{0\}$.
    \item For any $\beta\leqslant1$ and $\gamma>1$, we have $0\notin\Omega(\beta,\gamma)$.
    \item For $\gamma>1$, let $\theta=\theta(\gamma)>0$ be the unique positive solution of $\tanh(\gamma\theta)=\theta$, and let $q = q(\beta)$ be the largest solution in $[0,1)$ of $q = \E\tanh^2(\beta\sqrt q\, z)$, where $z$ is a standard Gaussian random variable; thus $q(\beta) = 0$ for $\beta\leqslant1$ and $q(\beta) \in (0,1)$ is unique for $\beta>1$. Suppose
    \begin{align*}
        \ln\cosh(\gamma\theta) - \frac{\gamma\theta^2}{2} \;>\; \E\ln\cosh\big(\beta\sqrt q\, z\big) + \frac{\beta^2}{4}\,(1-q)^2 .
    \end{align*}
    Then $0\notin\Omega(\beta,\gamma)$. In particular, $0\notin\Omega(\beta,\gamma)$ whenever
    \begin{align*}
        \gamma \;>\; \min\Big\{ \frac{\beta^2}{2} + 2\ln2,\ \sqrt{\frac{2}{\pi}}\Big(2\beta + \frac1\beta\Big) \Big\}.
    \end{align*}
    \item For $\beta>1$ and $\gamma\geqslant\beta^2$, we have $0\notin\Omega(\beta,\gamma)$.
    \item For $\gamma>1$ and $\theta=\theta(\gamma)$ as in (iii), suppose
    \begin{align*}
        \ln\cosh(\gamma\theta) - \frac{\gamma\theta^2}{2} > \beta E_0,
        \qquad\text{where}\quad
        E_0 := \lim_{N\to\infty} \frac1N\, \E \max_{\boldsymbol{\sigma}\in\Sigma_N} \frac{1}{\sqrt N}\sum_{i<j} g_{ij}\sigma_i\sigma_j
    \end{align*}
    is the ground-state energy of the SK model. Then $0\notin\Omega(\beta,\gamma)$. Moreover, $E_0 \leqslant \sqrt{\ln 2}$, so the same conclusion holds under the explicit condition $\ln\cosh(\gamma\theta) - \frac{\gamma\theta^2}{2} > \beta\sqrt{\ln2}$. 
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:56:11.139796+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d2c262aff15611b7cd771faa58d7568e943913e0ea64df342d2b8f1054ee0bd4","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 24 (One-sided endpoint domination) of arXiv:2608.10992, '2608.10992'] Fix $s\geq0$ with $\mathbb E[L_s]<\infty$ and $K\geq1$. Let
$q^{s,K}$ be the tail flow of Lemma~\ref{lem:tail-flow-general-data}
with initial data
\[
q_k^{s,K}(0)
:=
\mathbb P(L_s\wedge K\geq k).
\]
Then, for every $k\geq1$ and $t\geq0$,
\begin{equation}

q_k^{s,K}(t)
\leq
\mathbb P(L_{s+t}\geq k).
\end{equation}
Consequently, with $m_{s,K}:=\mathbb E[L_s\wedge K]$,
\begin{equation}

t_{\infty}^{\mathrm{iso}}
\leq
s+2\log\left(1+\frac1{m_{s,K}}\right).
\end{equation}
In particular,
\begin{equation}

X_t\preceq L_t,
\qquad
t_{\infty}^{\mathrm{iso}}
\leq t_{\infty}^{\mathrm{os}}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

