Then, for all $\mathfrak{r}>0$, it holds with probability at least $1 - \varepsilon^M$ as $\varepsilon \to 0$, at a rate which is universal, that the following is true. For each $z \in B_{\mathfrak{r} \varepsilon^{-M}}(0)$, there exist 
\begin{equation*}
    r \in [\varepsilon^2 \mathfrak{r},\varepsilon \mathfrak{r}] \cap \{2^{-k} \mathfrak{r}\}_{k \in \NN}, w \in B_{\mathfrak{r} \varepsilon^{-M}}(0) \cap \left(\frac{\varepsilon^2 \mathfrak{r}}{4} \ZZ^2\right)
\end{equation*}
such that $E_r(w)$ occurs and $z \in B_{\mathfrak{r} \varepsilon^2 / 2}(w)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:42:55.321105+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d363bc597b93dc0af630cce9fa2e481f558a9af5fd0a7730d76c0af1b6d3469b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 33 (Exact evaluation of $\mathcal{U}$) of arXiv:2609.02398, '\textbf{Convex Order Comparisons for Sub-Gamma Random Variables}'] Write $a_0 \coloneqq (2\log 2 - 1)/\left((1 - \log 2)\sqrt{2\log 2}\right)$. The function $\mathcal{U}$ of \eqref{eqn:sgM} is given by
