{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:54:57.520479+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d393a8174af2ed8f485a2f45311f48e8175b851914d5f2fb06aba7bee90ae588","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm : LDP/ExpEquiv_LDP of arXiv:2412.03320, 'Large deviation principle at speed $n$ for the random metric in first-passage percolation'] Let $(\mathbb{X}, \mathrm{d}_\mathbb{X})$ be a metric space.
	\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
		\item If the processes $(X_n)_{n\ge 1}$ and $(Y_n)_{n\ge 1}$ on $\mathbb{X}$ are exponentially equivalent and $(X_n)_{n\ge 1}$ follows a LDP with a good rate function, then $(Y_n)_{n\ge 1}$ also follows a LDP, with the same rate function.
		\item Assume that the processes $(X_n^{(b)})_{n \ge 1}$ on $\mathbb{X}$ are exponentially good approximations of $\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft( X_n \aftergroup\egroup\originalright)_{n\ge 1}$ as $b\to \infty$, and for all $b \ge 1$, $(X_n^{(b)})_{n \ge 1}$ follows the LDP with a rate function $I^{(b)}$. Then $(X_n)_{n\ge 1}$ follows the weak LDP with the rate function 
		\begin{equation}
			I : x \longmapsto \lim_{\varepsilon \to 0}\!\!\uparrow\! \liminf_{b\to\infty} \inf_{\hat x \in \ball[\mathbb{X}]{x, \varepsilon} } I^{(b)}(\hat x). 	
		\end{equation}
		If furthermore $I$ is a good rate function and satisfies, for all closed sets $F$,
		\begin{equation}
			\inf_{x\in F}I(x) \le \limsup_{b\to\infty} \inf_{x\in F} I^{(b)}(x),
		\end{equation}
		then $(X_n)_{n\ge 1}$ follows the LDP with the rate function $I$.
	\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:51:26.605869+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d393f412becd0ccd80fbb3f371eb09b1f026d9f0b9152199814af4889254e141","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition omega of arXiv:2606.03079, 'Mean Field Limits for Stochastic, Underdamped Reactive Langevin Dynamics Models'] Consider a fixed reaction $\mathcal{R}_{\ell}$, with $\nu$ a fixed particle distribution given by (equation ncen of the paper) with representation (equation eq:densitymeasdefmargrep of the paper). Using the notation of Definition (D:indexSpace of the paper), we define the allowable substrate index sampling space $\Omega^{(\ell)}(\nu) \subset \mathbb{I}^{(\ell)}$ as
