{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:47:12.037793+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d3ae602c062043b402961acf0d49410a5f35d9c30f2bc2c12b107328b1cff1f2","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def-transport-weak-2 of arXiv:2512.17727, 'Stochastic transport equation with L\'evy noise'] A weak$^\ast$-$\mathrm{L}^{\infty}$-solution to the stochastic transport equation
(equation eqn-transport-strong of the paper)  is
\begin{trivlist}
\item[(o)] a measurable and essentially bounded function
\begin{equation}
u:
[0,T]\times\mathbb{R}^d\times \Omega \to \mathbb{R}
\end{equation}
\end{trivlist}
{
in the sense  that there exists $M>0 $ satisfying
  \begin{align}
    \sup_{t\in[0,T]}\| u(t, \cdot, \omega) \|_{L^\infty(\mathbb{R}^d)} \leq M,\quad\mathbb{P}\text{-a.s.}
