{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:01:22.599874+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d44e63b20cdd50fb32904ca4bcbe3f8c95b78a53168ef5dbb2e7adbd56144d08","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition of arXiv:2606.10257, 'Downward conditional monotonicity gives survival and extinction for contact processes in r'] Consider the CPMRE having parameters $\beta,\mu,T$ as above, and with $\delta=1$. We have established the following results. 
	\begin{longlist}
		\setlength{\itemindent}{28pt}

		\setlength{\labelsep}{0.4em}
		\item The model is infection-DCM when $0<\lambda\leq \gamma$. 
		\item  If $\gamma>\lambda_c(2+\sqrt2)$, then the interval $(\lambda_\mathcal S, \gamma]$ is nonempty, and, for every $\lambda$ in this interval, the CPMRE is infection-DCM and dominates a supercritical contact process. Then, by monotonicity in $\lambda$, the CPMRE survives for all $\lambda>\lambda_\mathcal S$ (with $\lambda_\mathcal S$ defined in (equation eq:lamS of the paper)).
		\item The model is recovery-DCM when $\gamma\geq1$, in which case it goes extinct when $\lambda\leq\lambda_\mathcal E$ (with $\lambda_\mathcal E$ defined in (equation eq:lamE-def of the paper)). 
	\end{longlist} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:49:15.413592+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d44f08f0ea162f6ba7ef13b130a54225d2dd42cc9c1022f6e5f62d38e876710b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:nf of arXiv:2608.27771, 'A normal form for the Thin Flat Surfaces category'] Let $f \colon n \to m$ be a connected viewable tf-cobordism with interval cycles
$\lambda_1, \ldots, \lambda_p$, where $\lambda_k$ involves $a_k$ incoming and $a'_k$
outgoing intervals, genus $g \geq 0$, and $c \geq 0$ holes.  Fix an ordering of the
cycles as in Section~(sec:ordering of the paper).  The \emph{normal form} of $f$ is the
tf-cobordism
\begin{equation}

  \mathrm{NF}(f)
  \;:=\;
  \Gamma(c,\, g)
  \;\circ\;
  \bar\rho(f)
  \;\circ\;
  K_p\!\bigl(\lambda_1, \ldots, \lambda_p\bigr)
  \;\circ\;
  \bar\sigma(f).
\end{equation}
For a general (not necessarily connected) viewable tf-cobordism $f = f_1 \otimes
\cdots \otimes f_c$ decomposed into connected components, the normal form is the
monoidal product
\[
  \mathrm{NF}(f) \;:=\; \mathrm{NF}(f_1) \otimes \cdots \otimes \mathrm{NF}(f_c).
\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:57:16.067152+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d45204f266e80cbf4b028839f19b3f0686076caebfd05e59ca7754e789c1450b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem-orbit-expectation of arXiv:2506.12752, 'Strong Detection Threshold for Correlated \ER Graphs with Constant Average Degree'] {}
Recall (equation eq-def-p-rho of the paper). For any $\sigma\in\mathfrak S_n$, let $\pi = \pi^* \circ \sigma$ and let $\Pi,\Sigma$ be the bijection on $\operatorname{U}_n$ induced by $\pi,\sigma$, respectively. Then for any legal realization $J$ of $\mathcal J_{\sigma}$ and any orbit $O \in \mathcal O_{\sigma}, O \not \subset J$, we have
