{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:55:27.259858+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d4e111302e0c7bbd67f5c76338a4415adfb82963d7eb5905bbf4d02829c32492","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm Subcritical2 of arXiv:2410.23859, 'Subcritical regimes in the Poisson Boolean percolation on Ahlfors regular spaces'] Let $\sigma > 1$. Let $(S,d)$ be a doubling, $\sigma$-uniformly perfect metric space. Let $\mu$ be an infinite and boundedly finite measure on $S$. Let $\rho$ be a probability measure on $(0,\infty)$. Suppose that there exists $C_\textup{V} \geq 1$ and $s > 0$ such that $\mu(B(x,r)) \leq C_\textup{V} r^s$ holds for all $x\in S$, $r >0$. 
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
        \item  If $\int_0^\infty R^s\rho(dR) < \infty$, then there exists $\lambda_0 >0$ such that for all  $\lambda \in (0,\lambda_0)$, we have
        \begin{equation*}
            \lim_{r \to \infty}\sup_{x \in S} \mathbb{P}_\lambda(M(x) > r) = 0. 
        \end{equation*}
        \item If there exists $\beta >0$ such that $\int_0^\infty R^{s+\beta} \rho(dR)< \infty$, then There exists $\lambda_0 >0$ such that for all  $\lambda \in (0,\lambda_0)$, we have
        \begin{equation*}
            \sup_{x \in S} \mathbb{E}_\lambda[M(x)^\beta]< \infty.
        \end{equation*}
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:42:08.412580+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d4e15b030d436c30d5ac08af4547fe7004976e6b781f85387907cbff75174bc7","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma L: test function increasing of arXiv:2511.11036, 'Analysis of a class of Recursive Distributional Equations Including the Resistance of the '] Fix $T,A > 0$ and let $F : \mathbb{R} \times (0,T) \to [0,A)$ be an upper semicontinuous function that is nondecreasing in the spatial variable.  Fix $(x_{0},t_{0}) \in \mathbb{R} \times (0,+\infty)$ and let $\varphi$ be a smooth function defined in some neighborhood of $(x_{0},t_{0})$ such that $\varphi(x_{0},t_{0}) < A$ and $\partial_{x} \varphi(x_{0},t_{0}) > 0$.  If $\varphi$ touches $F$ from above at $(x_{0},t_{0})$, then there is an $r > 0$, a nondecreasing $C^{2}$ function $\psi_{1} : \mathbb{R} \to [0,1]$ with $\psi_{1}(x) = \psi_{1}(x_{0} - r)$ for each $x \leq x_{0} - r$ and $\psi_{1}(x) = A$ for each $x \geq x_{0} + r$, and a $C^{2}$ function $\psi_{2} : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ with $\psi_{2}(t_{0}) = 0$ such that if $\psi$ is the function
	\begin{align*}
		\psi(x,t) = \psi_{1}(x) + \psi_{2}(t),
	\end{align*}
then
	\begin{align*}
		F(x,t) \leq \psi(x,t) \quad \text{for each} \, \, (x,t) \in \mathbb{R} \times [t_{0}-r,t_{0}+r]
	\end{align*}
and equality holds if and only if $(x,t) = (x_{0},t_{0})$.  Further, for any $\delta > 0$, it is possible to construct $\psi$ in such a way that 
	\begin{gather*}
		\psi(x_{0},t_{0}) = \varphi(x_{0},t_{0}), \quad \partial_{x} \psi(x_{0},t_{0}) = \partial_{x} \varphi(x_{0},t_{0}), \quad \partial_{t} \varphi(x_{0},t_{0}) = \partial_{t} \varphi(x_{0},t_{0}), \\
		\left| \partial_{x}^{2} \psi(x_{0},t_{0}) - \partial_{x}^{2} \varphi(x_{0},t_{0}) \right| \leq \delta.
	\end{gather*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:42:08.464581+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d4e1c50007ab52eb8c847a0e778a7024c1729c18fb4edc65f8168ed7a23aaee3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma notation:LT of arXiv:2511.15893, 'On a Class of Dynamical Poisson-Voronoi Tessellations'] For any $\gamma\geq 0$, the Laplace transform of $H_{\scriptscriptstyle V}^2$, under the Palm probability distribution $\mathbb{P}^0_{{\mathcal A}_{\scriptscriptstyle V}}$ of ${\mathcal A}_{\scriptscriptstyle V}$, is given by 

