{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:41:03.288731+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d523e0c3610ea88e12579393b1cede378f449475bb67f821746fb6f25b057368","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor corollary of arXiv:2411.08796, 'Optimal stopping for Markov processes \\ with positive jumps'] Consider a strong Markov process $X$ with no negative jumps
and a continuous positive reward function $g$ that belongs to $\mathcal{D}_{\alpha}$ with $f=-A_{\alpha} g=(\alpha-A)g$, satisfying
\begin{itemize}
\item[\rm (i)] $\lim_{t\to \infty}\Ex{x}{e^{-\alpha{t}}g(X_t)}=0$,
\item[\rm (ii)] $\Ex{x}{\int_0^{\infty} e^{-\alpha{s}}|f(X_s)| ds}<\infty$.
\end{itemize} 

Suppose that $x^*$ is a solution of
\begin{equation*}
g(x^*)=\int_{x^*}^\infty(\alpha-A)g(y)G_{\alpha}(x^*,dy),
\end{equation*}
such that $(\alpha-A)g(y) \geq 0$ for all $x\geq x^*$.
Define 
\begin{equation*}
V_{\alpha}(x)=\int_{x^*}^\infty(\alpha-A){g}(y) G_{\alpha}(x,dy).
\end{equation*}

If $V_{\alpha}(x) \geq {g}(x)$ for all $x\leq x^*$ then the OSP (equation eq:osp of the paper) is right-sided, $x^*$ is an optimal threshold and $V_{\alpha}$ is
the value function. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:21:45.283269+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d5241506108fd184001bb2736e8d6b0b8d5b3d1466c02d3db02b53fc9672141d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 11 of arXiv:2607.26944, 'Improved bounds on the inradius and inverse inradius process of the convex hull of planar '] It holds that
