{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:55:42.109744+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d567d129ff4f3a0c639a153581ba66d8959b00df456965fa01d81e7049b189a9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm1 of arXiv:2411.15881, 'Stable Approximation for Call Function Via Stein's method'] Let $X_1,X_2,\ldots$ be independent and identically distributed random variables defined on a common probability space, and suppose that $X_1$ has
a distribution of the form ((px of the paper)) with $B(x)$ satisfying ((bound of the paper)).
Set $\sigma=\left(A\alpha\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \frac{1-\cos y}{|y|^{1+\alpha}}dy\right)^\frac1\alpha$ and
\begin{equation}
S_n=\frac{1}{\sigma n^{\frac{1}{\alpha}}}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(X_i-\mathbb{E}[X_i]).
\end{equation}
Then we have
\begin{align*}
\left|\mathbb{E}\left(S_n-M\right)_{+}-\mathbb{E}\left(S_\alpha (1,\delta)-M\right)_{+}\right|\leq c_{1}{R_{n}},
