{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:51:52.062451+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d5759cd354ad24a3438c2d229465734df4dea120e4ed6b56ba7cb6263c7a8f03","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:Russo of arXiv:2607.14993, 'Sharp phase transition for percolation with short-range dependencies'] Let $\Omega = \{0, 1\}^E$ with $E$ at most countable and, for $p \in [0, 1]^E$, let $\mathbb P_p$ be the product probability measure on $\Omega$ such that for all $e \in E$ we have $\mathbb P_p(\omega(e) = 1) = p_e$. Then, if $A$ is an increasing event and~$e\in E$,
  \begin{equation}
    \partial_{p_e} \mathbb P_p(A) = \mathbb P_p(e \text{ pivotal for } A)
  \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:32:56.080485+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d575b7e2ed3922a2a9d5bd50ea32c090b2f307b1129533d61b1bcb925667b40f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: moments of arXiv:2411.12474, 'Multi-type branching processes with immigration generated by point processes'] In the case of immigration spanned by a $(\Lambda, K)$-DPP,	\begin{enumerate}
		\item  we have that
		\[\Ebb{{Z}_i(t)} = \int_{(0,t]}
		K(x,x)\Ebb{Z_{\times,i}(t-x)}\Lambda(\mathrm{d} x),\]
		\item and if we assume (equation eq:strong of the paper),
        \begin{align*}
			Cov(Z_i(t), Z_j(t))=&
			\int_{(0,t]} K(x,x)
		 \Ebb{Z_{\times, i}(t-x) Z_{\times, j}(t-x)} \Lambda(\mathrm{d} x) \\
& \! - \int_{(0,t]^2} K^2(x,y) \Ebb{Z_{\times, i}(t-x)} \Ebb{Z_{\times, j}(t-y)} \Lambda(\mathrm{d} x) \Lambda(\mathrm{d} y).
		\end{align*}
	\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.479365+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d578e37b6f9fbfccfdad6e4c6576123429e753cc72557ed9e7f34e36f9b51f44","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 106 (Reconstructed covariance-profile Wick multipliers) of arXiv:2606.29292, 'Intrinsic Gaussian Tensor Geometry and Random Operator Spectra'] Assume the hypotheses of
Theorem~\ref{thm:geom-covariance-block-transfer} and suppose
\begin{equation}

 \mathfrak c(R)^{m/2}\lesssim R^\beta\ell_R^\kappa,
 \qquad R\in\mathbb D,
\end{equation}
for some $\beta,\kappa\ge0$.  Let $2\le r<\infty$ and assume
\begin{equation}

 a>\beta,\qquad b>\beta,\qquad
 a+b>\beta+\frac dr.
\end{equation}
Then the coefficient series
\begin{equation}

 \mathbf K_\nu^{a,b}:=\sum_{L,Q\in\mathbb D}K_{\nu,L,Q}^{a,b}
\end{equation}
converges unconditionally in the same-field coefficient space, uniformly
in $\nu$, and
\begin{equation}

 I_m(\mathbf K_\nu^{a,b})
 =\Lambda_g^{-a}M_{\Phi_{m,\nu}}\Lambda_g^{-b}
 \quad\text{in }L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)
\end{equation}
for every finite $p$.  Uniformly in $\nu$,
\begin{equation}

 \|I_m(\mathbf K_\nu^{a,b})\|_{L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)}
 \lesssim (p_*+r)^{m/2},\qquad p_*:=\max\{p,2\}.
\end{equation}
If the family is ultraviolet Cauchy at order $m$, then
$\mathbf K_\nu^{a,b}$ converges in the coefficient space and the operators
in \eqref{eq:geom-general-smooth-sandwich} converge in every finite
$L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)$.  The limit is independent of the regularization
inside every jointly covariance-compatible class and independent of the
admissible dyadic resolution.

If, in addition,
$\varepsilon_{N_1,N_2}^{(m)}\lesssim N_1^{-\vartheta}$ for dyadic
$N_2>N_1$, then for some $\eta>0$,
\begin{equation}

 \|I_m(\mathbf K_N^{a,b}-\mathbf K^{a,b})\|_{L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)}
 \lesssim N^{-\eta}(p+r)^{m/2},\qquad p\ge2,
\end{equation}
and the convergence is almost sure in $\Sch_r$ along dyadic cutoffs.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:57:07.196566+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d57fc308d9764eec61a144ac0cb4ea71f7841ff960b37abf55b7363ee34b080f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:cigar-shape of arXiv:2509.04333, 'The limiting law of the Discrete Gaussian level lines'] Fix $A, B$ with angle denoted $\theta_{A, B}$ and $B_1 - A_1 = M_{A, B}$. Define the curves $\ensuremath{\mathcal C}^\pm_{A, B}$ by
