{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:36:31.230392+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d5a6022668549ebeb5ea8641307f36769dfde6f436aa2238a93d5da42eefd223","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 16 of arXiv:2609.00537, 'Explicit Exponential Sum Estimates and Approximate Functional Equations for the Zeta Funct'] .
Let $s = \sigma + it$ with $ 1/2\leq \sigma \leq 1$ and $|t| \geq t_0 \geq 2\pi$. Also, assume that $x,y$ are in $\mathbb{Z}+\frac12$ and satisfy $2\pi xy = |t|$ and $x,y\geq h\geq 1.5$. 
We have the Approximate Functional Equation:
\begin{equation}
\zeta(s) = \sum_{1 \le n \le x} n^{-s} + \chi(s)\sum_{1 \le m \le y} m^{s-1} + \mathcal{E}(\s,t,x,y),
\end{equation} 
where the error term $\mathcal{E}(\s,t, x, y)$ satisfies the following bounds:
\begin{equation}
\left|\mathcal{E}(\s,t, x, y)\right|
\leq 
\begin{cases}
  \Big(  \frac{\log y}{\pi}+ \mathcal{E}_0(\sigma,h,t_0) \Big) \Big(\frac{t}{2\pi}\Big)^{1/2-\sigma} y^{\sigma-1}  &\text{if}\ x \ge  y, \\
\left(\frac{C_0(\sigma,t_0)}{\pi} (\log x) +\mathcal{E}_0(\sigma, h, t_0) \right)x^{-\s} &\text{if}\ x < y.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
with
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{E}_0(\s,h,t_0) =
\begin{cases}
    A_{0}(1-\sigma,h,t_0)C_0(\sigma,t_0)  + B_{0}(1-\sigma,t_0) &\text{if}\ x \ge y,
  \\
  A_0(\s,h,t_0)  + C_0(\s, t_0) B_0(\s,t_0) \ &\text{if}\ x < y.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Here, $(A_0, B_0, C_0)$ are defined in \eqref{def-A0},  \eqref{def-B0}, and \eqref{def-C0} respectively. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:53:31.975769+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d5a9dc115f9ff84300ecf507070ccc643fb11902597743b428bcf774702512ea","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 of arXiv:2606.11271, '\LARGE The $K$-th nearest neighbor random walk on a Poisson point process gets trapped'] For any $\varepsilon >0$, $A>0$ and $n\in \N$, $X_n$ is called an $(\varepsilon, A)$-pioneer point if there exist some $\rho \in [0,A]$ and some $c\in \R^d$ such that 
\begin{itemize}
\item[$(i)$] $B(c,\varepsilon) \subset B(X_n, \rho) \cap (\R^d \setminus E_n)$;
\item[$(ii)$] $| (\chi \smallsetminus \{X_n\})\cap \left(   B(X_n, \rho) \cap E_n  \right) | < M$.
\end{itemize}
The set of $(\varepsilon, A)$-pioneer points is denoted by
$$
\V_p (\varepsilon, A) \coloneqq \{ x\in \chi \,:\, \exists n \in \N \,,\, X_n =x \textrm{ and $X_n$ is $(\varepsilon, A)$-pioneer}\} \,.
$$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:35:10.784910+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d5aa1ce5814f9ec3e5cb76dce34983bc4c721e2ae8cb69ddcca184e7de3a39db","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 8 of arXiv:2604.00474, 'Trap behaviors for Brownian motions'] If $D \in  \mathcal{D}$, then reflecting Brownian motion $X^+$ in $D$ starting from $x \in D \cap \partial_L D $  has a semimartingale decomposition 
\begin{align}
X^+_t = x + W_t + N_t, \quad t\geq 0
\end{align}
where $W_t$ is a d-dimensional Brownian motion, $N_t =\int_0^t n(X_s)d\gamma^+_s$ and the local time $\gamma^+$, is a non-decreasing continuous process that does not increase when $X^+$ is not in $\partial L_D$, that is 
\begin{align*}
\int_0^\infty  1_{ (\partial_L D)^c }n(X^+_t)d\gamma^+_t=0
\end{align*}
(here $n$ is the unit inward normal vector field of $D$ on $\partial_L D)$.
The Revuz measure of $\gamma^+$ for the process $X^+$ is the surface measure on $\partial_L D$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:25:45.002612+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d5ac1004aa68ec7fdaf71d4ef6df39a5ff19b201cc9d213d63d1a856a1cfc8ed","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 34 of arXiv:2605.11801, 'A non-local singular non-linear Fokker-Planck PDE'] We define the domain 
   \begin{equation*}
