{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:04:30.545950+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d5cf10d3df2f7500e2787529200d9effb08c88d22323100bdeac0c93b7ee6d05","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: A.s. singularity extremals of arXiv:2411.10271, '$A$-localized states for clock models on trees and their extremal decomposition into glass'] Consider a $\mathbb{Z}_q$-valued nearest-neighbor clock model with Hamiltonian given in (equation def: Hamiltonian of the paper) and a potential defined in (equation eq: Potential ferr n.n. model of the paper) fulfilling the $u,U,d$-bounds in (equation eq: u,U-bounds of the paper). Furthermore, let $\beta$ be large enough, $A\subsetneq \mathbb{Z}_q$ such that $|A|\geq 2$ and denote with $\mu_A$ its corresponding $A$-localized state, see Proposition (prop: Bounds A-loc. states of the paper). Then, for $\mu_A \otimes \mu_A$-a.e. pair $(\omega,\omega')$ the extremal Gibbs measures $\pi(\cdot|\omega)$ and $\pi(\cdot|\omega')$ are singular with respect to each other, i.e.
