{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.478476+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d60cd602f76f32d95dbb01a167e0fd44d44bed979d07fdf58cef1a60287d8b2a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 101 (Parameter-uniform spectral-multiplier bounds) of arXiv:2606.29292, 'Intrinsic Gaussian Tensor Geometry and Random Operator Spectra'] Fix $0<c<C<\infty$.  Let $R\ge2$ and let
$\beta_R\in C_c^\infty((0,\infty))$ satisfy
$\operatorname{supp}\beta_R\subset[cR,CR]$.  If $\mathcal K_R$ is the
kernel of $\beta_R(\Lambda_g)$, then for every $J\ge0$ and every fixed
differential operators $D,E$ of orders $q_x,q_y$ there exist $K_*$ and
$C_{J,D,E}<\infty$ such that
\begin{equation}

 |D_xE_y\mathcal K_R(x,y)|
 \le C_{J,D,E}
 \max_{0\le j\le K_*}\sup_{\lambda>0}
 R^j|\partial_\lambda^j\beta_R(\lambda)|
 R^{d+q_x+q_y}(1+Rd_g(x,y))^{-J}.
\end{equation}
In particular, there are $K_{\mathrm{Sch}}$ and $C_{\mathrm{Sch}}$ such that
\begin{equation}

  \sup_x\int_M|\mathcal K_R(x,y)|\,dV_g(y)
  +\sup_y\int_M|\mathcal K_R(x,y)|\,dV_g(x)
  \le C_{\mathrm{Sch}}
  \max_{0\le j\le K_{\mathrm{Sch}}}
  \sup_{\lambda>0}R^j|\partial_\lambda^j\beta_R(\lambda)|.
\end{equation}
Thus uniformly rescaled multiplier families are bounded on both
$L^1(M)$ and $L^\infty(M)$.  The analogous fixed low-frequency statement
holds for $R=1$.

If $F_R(x,\cdot)$ is spectrally supported in a fixed enlargement of the
$R$-shell, then every differential operator $E$ of order $q$ satisfies
\begin{equation}

  \|E_yF_R\|_{L^2(M\times M)}
  \lesssim_E R^q\|F_R\|_{L^2(M\times M)},
\end{equation}
with the corresponding fixed low-frequency statement at $R=1$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:24:25.989110+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d60d2f207570507728169a3214363ca6638f4f5a0690a3359ed8394b86e55cf0","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 76 of arXiv:2507.07702, 'Observables of random spanning trees in random environment'] Let $(G, \weight)$ be a finite connected weighted graph and let $\mathcal{K}$ be the kernel of $G$. Assign each edge $(U,V) \in E(\mathcal{K})$ the weight
