{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:18:19.444555+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d629f4d9d466d84a21fb3c1881c0c7cde693e5491cf0f0b7b5a5c743e4e43796","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition ass:ft of arXiv:2508.17838, '\bf Edge Universality for Inhomogeneous Random Matrices'] There exist constants $ \alpha \in (0, 2]$, $c_0 > 0$, $\varepsilon > 0$, and $0 < \rho < 1$ such that the following assumptions are satisfied:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item (normalization) $f$ is a probabiltiy density
  \begin{equation}
    f(x)\ge 0, \quad \int_{\mathbb R^d} f(u)\,du \;=\; 1;
  \end{equation}
  \item (small-frequency control) for all $|\xi|\le \varepsilon$,
  \begin{equation}
    |\widehat f(\xi)| \;\le\; \exp\!\bigl(-c_0 |\xi|^\alpha \bigr);
  \end{equation}
  \item (high-frequency contraction) for all $|\xi| > \varepsilon$,
  \begin{equation}
    |\widehat f(\xi)| \;\le\; \rho < 1;
  \end{equation}
  \item (regularity/decay) $f$ is a continuous function and for some   constants $T, K > d$ and $C_T, C_{K} > 1$,   
  \begin{equation}
      f(x)\le \frac{C_T}{(1+|x|)^{T}},
    \qquad |\widehat f(\xi)| \;\le\; \frac{C_K}{(1+|\xi|)^{K}} 
      \qquad  x,\xi \in \mathbb R^d.
  \end{equation}
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:50:48.846866+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d62a89bd6053752125a5839b2866e0e2a6fc921eed00c8739e1a01ec90520454","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop lemma-invariance-principle of arXiv:2607.08187, 'Asymptotic behaviours of critical branching random walk in $\mathbb{R}^d$'] For any $\mathbf{y} \in \overline{D}_1$ and $\gamma , \theta \in  [0,\theta_0]$, it holds that 
	\begin{align}
		V_{(\gamma,\theta)}(\mathbf{y})
		&=\gamma\, \mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{y}}\big(\tau_{1}^W\big)+\theta
		-\mathcal{C}(\alpha)\, \mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{y}}\bigg[\int_{0}^{\tau_{1}^W} V_{(\gamma,\theta)}^\alpha(W_s)\,\mathrm{d}s\bigg],
	\end{align}
	where  $\mathcal{C}(\alpha)$ is the constant in (equation Stable-branching of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:29:35.080482+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d6338b5985780d949f5aa372345776b09007d30016c940486a382657808c92d3","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 18 of arXiv:2609.03793, 'Typical martingale diverges on a co-$\sigma$-porous set'] Let $\f\in\M_\infty$, $m\in\N$, $u\in2^m$ and $n,k\in\N_0$ be arbitrary. Let us further denote
\begin{equation*}
M\coloneqq\bs{v\in2^{l}\cap u\appinf;l\geq m,\forall j\in[m:l]:f_j(v)\geq \f(u)-2^{-n}}
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
s\coloneqq\sup\s{\f(v);v\in M}.
\end{equation*}
Then given any $v\in M$ such that $s-\f(v)\leq2^{-k-n}$, it holds that
\begin{equation*}
\forall w\in2^{\leq k}:s-\f(v\app w)\leq2^{-n},\quad\text{in particular}\quad v\app w\in M.
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:18:19.426587+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d63429377dd53164d67ddc70a713f6e831367644245e4b4fe0fa68189a31c238","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:singleCol of arXiv:2508.03577, 'Phase Transitions in a Particle Model\\ for the Self-Adaptive Response to Cancer Dynamics'] Given $\alpha>0$ the speed of the updates to 1 and $p$ the probability of deletion, as described in Definition (def:singleCol of the paper). Let $\tau$ be the first time the column consists of a row of only ones. Choose $a=\tfrac{p M}{\alpha q}$ and assume that $a\in (0,\infty)$ is fixed as $M\to\infty$. We then have that 
    \begin{equation}
        \mathbb{E}\left[\tau|X_0=0\right]\sim  \frac{M^{a+1}}{\Gamma(a+1)a}\, ,
    \end{equation}
    and
    \begin{equation}
        \mathbb{P}\rk{\left| \tau-\mathbb{E}\left[\tau\right] \right|\gg M^{(a+1)/2}}=o(1)\, ,
    \end{equation}
    as $M\to\infty$. This means that we observe a cut-off phenomenon on the square-root scale.

    Finally, we also have the following transitions based on the value of $a$: let $\pi$ be the invariant distribution of the chain and let $\mathbb{P}_\pi$ be the distribution of the chain started from the invariant distribution. There then exists $C>0$ such that
    \begin{equation}
                C^{-1}k^{a-1}\le \frac{\mathbb{P}_\pi\rk{ k\textnormal{ zeros}}}{\mathbb{P}_\pi\rk{\textnormal{ no zeros}}}\le C k^{a-1}\, ,
        \end{equation}
        as $M\to\infty$.

        Hence 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item if $a>1$: most of the times we have a large number of ones,
        \item if $a=1$, ones and zeros balance,
        \item if $a<1$, most of the times, there is a large number of zeros.
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:24:20.981053+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d6373c5f71e2cf6258a4e403216bc36c3e5e76e1fed852e0366ab4219220e938","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop finitary_mean_field of arXiv:2508.18809, '{\bf Critical long-range percolation III: The upper critical dimension}'] Consider percolation on any transitive weighted graph. The bound
