{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:33:17.671699+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d6eb9f2a193bf854844140fc7a46d68f1d0976134a55690e93e613706ee90a36","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 of arXiv:2607.10725, 'From the Airy line ensemble to the Gaussian free field'] The {\em Airy line ensemble} $\mathcal{A} = \{\mathcal{A}_i\}_{i \geq 1}$ is a non-intersecting line ensemble in the sense of Definition \ref{Def.LE}. Its law is uniquely characterized by the following property. For any finite set $\mathsf{S} = \{s_1, \ldots, s_m\} \subset \mathbb{R}$ with $s_1 < \cdots < s_m$, the random measure on $\mathbb{R}^2$ given by
