{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:31:49.839942+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d6ec439a7a1ce1d58013f839a4d7fd862f5906574067a53bbb44377856dd0f7d","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi 12 of arXiv:2506.13587, 'Non-exchangeable mean-field theory for adaptive weights: propagation of dissociatedness an'] If, for a sequence of random events $A_k$, $k \to \infty$, there exist constants $C_1 > 0, C_2 > 0,$ and $\theta > 0$ such that for all $k$ (or for all $k \geq k_0$ for some initial $k_0 \in \N$),
\begin{equation*}
\Pb(A_k) \geq 1 - C_1 \exp(-C_2 k^{\theta}),
\end{equation*}
we say that the sequence of events $A_k$, $k \to \infty$ is $k$-certain.","status":"unverified"}

