{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:13:22.522002+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d72629115cb035c914d8f190a533daad69e1c34a38e1b84714651c2cdce59b3c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:string-plaquette-operation of arXiv:2505.16585, 'Expanded regimes of area law for lattice Yang--Mills theories'] Let $s \in \mc{S}_{\Lambda}$ be non-null and $K: \mc{P}_{\Lambda}^{+} \backslash P \to \N$ be a plaquette count supported on good plaquettes. We make the following definitions.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item If $s$ contains a good edge $\mbf{e}$, then $(s',K')$ is a positive merger of $(s,K)$ at $\mbf{e}$ if $s' \in \mbb{M}_+(s,\mbf{e})$ and $K'=K$. The set of all positive mergers of $(s,K)$ at $\mbf{e}$ is denoted by $\mbb{M}_+((s,K),\mbf{e})$.
    \item The sets $\mbb{M}_-((s,K),\mbf{e})$, $\mbb{S}_+((s,K),\mbf{e})$, and $\mbb{S}_-((s,K),\mbf{e})$ may be similarly defined.
    \item If $s$ contains a good edge $\mbf{e}$, we say that $(s',K')$ is a positive (resp. negative) deformation of $(s,K)$ at $\mbf{e}$, if we can express $s'=s \oplus_{\mbf{e}} p$  (resp. $s'=s \ominus_{\mbf{e}} p$) for some plaquette $p$, and moreover $K'$ 
    is given by (here $|p|$ denotes the positively oriented version of $p$, which may be $p$ itself if it is already positively oriented)
    \begin{equs}
    K'(q) = \begin{cases}
        K(q) & q \in \mc{P}_\Lambda^+ \backslash \{|p|\}, \\
        K(p) - 1 & q  = |p|.
        \end{cases}
    \end{equs}
    The set of all such $(s',K')$ is denoted $\mbb{D}_+((s,K),\mbf{e})$ (resp. $\mbb{D}_-((s,K),\mbf{e})$). 
    \item We say that $(s',K')$ is a revival of $(s,K)$ with revival set $R$ if $s' \in \mc{R}(s,R)$, $K'(p)=K(p)$ for $p \in \mc{P}_{\Lambda}^+ \backslash R$, $K'(p)=0$ for all $p \in R$, and the plaquette count $J : o(R) \ra \N$ such that $s' = s \Delta_R J$ satisfies $J(p) + J(p^{-1}) \leq K(p)$ for all $p \in \mc{P}_\Lambda^+$. In this case, we write $(s', K') = (s, K) \Delta_R J$. The set of all revivals of $(s,K)$ with revival set $R$ is denoted $\mc{R}((s,K),R)$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:02:11.352331+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d7267515c13706be23870377e2c3e8c039e4933c34b2f80d9446374cdb3f5d33","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem abel of arXiv:2510.26123, 'Directed distances in bipolar-oriented triangulations:\\ exact exponents and scaling limit'] For every $t>0$,
\begin{equation}
\sum_{k\ge0} f(k)\sin(tk)
= \sum_{k\ge0} S(k+1)\big(\sin((k+1)t)-\sin(kt)\big).
\end{equation}
Equivalently,
\begin{equation}
\sum_{k\ge0} f(k)\sin(tk)
= \int_{0}^{\infty} S\big(\lfloor u/t\rfloor+1\big)\cos u\,du .
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:18:13.240554+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d726ee85fa3e61a6741140ef341095417551ea6395c44442818c7c0d21a09785","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm hyc of arXiv:2507.02518, '{\bf Exponential Ergodicity in Relative Entropy and $L^2$-Wasserstein Distance for non-equ'] Assume  {\bf(B)} and $\sigma\sigma^\ast$ is invertible. Then (equation Degth1 of the paper) has a unique invariant probability measure $\bar{\mu}\in\mathscr P_2(\mathbb R^{2d})$ and there exists a constant $t_1>0$ such that $P_{t_1}^\ast $, the adjoint operator of $P_{t_1}$ in $L^2(\bar{\mu})$, is hypercontractive, in the sense that
\begin{align}
\|P_{t_1}^\ast\|_{L^{\frac{4}{3}}(\bar{\mu})\rightarrow L^{2}(\bar{\mu})}= 1.
\end{align}
Consequently, there exist constants $c,\tilde c,\lambda>0$ such that
\begin{align} \mathrm{Ent}(\mathscr L_{(X_t^{\mu_0},Y_t^{\mu_0})}|\bar{\mu})\leq c\text{\rm{e}}^{-2\lambda t}\mathrm{Ent}(\mu_0|\bar{\mu}),~ t\geq 0, \mu_0\in\mathscr P(\mathbb R^{2d}),
\end{align}
and
\begin{align}\mathbb W_2(\mathscr L_{(X_t^{\mu_0},Y_t^{\mu_0})},\bar{\mu})\leq \tilde c\text{\rm{e}}^{-\lambda t}\mathbb W_2(\mu_0,\bar{\mu}),\ \ t\geq 0, \mu_0\in\mathscr P_2(\mathbb R^{2d}).
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:10.887513+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d7281bb2650cae544e6a170ec492ac25536b0cb7e0515bf1a8a17bccbc3d85e2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 15 (Harris--FKG--Inequality) of arXiv:2504.02435, 'Poisson-Voronoi percolation in higher rank'] In the above setting, $\omega_p^{(\lambda)}$ satisfies the Harris-FKG-Inequality, i.e.~
