{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:23.338345+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d7667a680ea3cf66803f710a569b161d1c39de89261080486a62c23508649679","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 of arXiv:2506.22213, 'No arbitrage assumption implies the differentiability of derivative pricing function'] Let $\hat{X}$ be an $m$-dimensional continuous Markov semimartingale starting at $0$, then for $\hat{X}$, we have 
    \begin{align*}
        \hat{X}_t=A_t+M_t,
    \end{align*}
where $A$ is the finite variation part and $M$ is the local martingale with quadratic variation $N$. For such a process, there exists a system of local characteristics $(V,b,c)$ with $V$ is a strictly increasing continuous process, and $b,c$ are Borel measurable functions such that
\begin{align*}
    A_t=\int_0^tb(\hat{X}_s)dV_s,\quad\text{and}\quad N_t=\int_0^tc^2(\hat{X}_s)dV_s.
\end{align*}
Moreover, under some filtration $(\scG_t)_t$, we have an adapted stochastic process $X$ as a time-changed $\hat{X}$ such that
\begin{align*}
    X_t=\int_0^tb(X_s)ds+\int_0^tc(X_s)dW_s
\end{align*}
is an It\^{o} process.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:13:22.533108+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d769a1010684a098fd45ac0d812937c1467f317fc5dda11cc0c26a68532c0a6c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem.detouring_path of arXiv:2506.07158, 'The diffusivity of supercritical Bernoulli percolation is infinitely differentiable'] Every ${\scaleobj{1}{\square}} \in \mathcal{G}^{(N)}_k$ is $N$-$\mathsf{stable}$ for the statement: for every ${\scaleobj{1}{\square}}' \in \mathcal{P}^{(N,k)}_{h}({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}) $ with $0 \leqslant h \leqslant k-1$, and every ${x \in \frac{16}{15}{\scaleobj{1}{\square}}' \cap {\scaleobj{1}{\square}}}$, the following properties hold.
