{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:28:34.939525+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d7cedfb05050859cdcd3bacf03e113596480ebd19294bc4ac06e0aef165bee4f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemma_hopf of arXiv:2412.05709, 'Incipient infinite clusters and volume growth for Gaussian free fields and loop soups on m'] Let $\mathbf{X}$ and $\mathbf{Y}$ be two spaces, where $\mathbf{Y}$ is equipped with a $\sigma$-finite measure $\mu_{\mathbf{Y}}$. Let $\mathcal{K}:\mathbf{X} \times \mathbf{Y}\to (0,\infty)$ be a positive kernel. Define the linear operator $\mathcal{K}f(\cdot)$ as (where $f:\mathbf{Y}\to [0,\infty)$ is a function on $\mathbf{Y}$)
\begin{equation*}
	\mathcal{K}f(x):= \int_{\mathbf{Y}}\mathcal{K}(x,y)f(y)d\mu_{\mathbf{Y}}(y). 
\end{equation*}
If there exists $\kappa>0$ such that
\begin{equation}
	\frac{\mathcal{K}(x,y)\mathcal{K}(x',y')}{\mathcal{K}(x,y')\mathcal{K}(x',y)}\le \kappa^2, \ \ \forall x,x'\in \mathbf{X}\ \text{and}\ y,y'\in \mathbf{Y},
\end{equation}
then for any two functions $f_1:\mathbf{Y}\to [0,\infty)$ and $f_2:\mathbf{Y}\to (0,\infty)$, one has  
\begin{equation*}
\mathrm{osc}\big(\frac{\mathcal{K}f_1}{\mathcal{K}f_2}\big)	\le  \frac{\kappa-1}{\kappa+1}\cdot  \mathrm{osc}\big(\frac{f_1}{f_2}\big), 
\end{equation*}
	where $\mathrm{osc}(g)$ for $g:\mathbf{Z}\to [0,\infty)$ is defined as $\mathrm{osc}(g):=\sup_{z_1,z_2\in \mathbf{Z}} | g(z_1) - g(z_2) |$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:57:59.894226+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d7d2604ae44127792915adbf75f0f1e3523528b465f0747e6797bf9447c88a2f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:hoermplaw of arXiv:2607.09946, 'Statistics on Yau's conjecture: Variance asymptotics'] {Keeler}}{}]
For any $M$ compact Riemannian manifold, 
\begin{equation} 
E^M_{[0,\ell]}(x,y) = \ell^n\left( E_{[0,1]}^{n}(\ell\dist xy)+R_{[0,\ell]}^M(x,y)\right),
\end{equation}
with remainder of order $R_{[0,\ell]}^M(x,y)=\mathrm{O}(\ell^{-1})$. Precisely, the estimate continues to hold for the derivatives in the following sense: for $\rho(M)$ small enough, for any $k\in \mathbb{N}$,
\begin{equation}
\sup_{\mathrm{dist}(x,y)\le \rho(M)}\max_{|a|,|b|\le k} \frac{\| \nabla^a \nabla^b R_{[0,\ell]}^{M}(x,y)\|}{\ell^{|a|+|b|}}=\mathrm{O}(\ell^{-1}). 
\end{equation}
In particular, it follows that: 
\begin{equation} 
\sigma(\phi_{[0,\ell]}^M)^2=\ell^{n}\left( \frac{b_n}{(2\pi)^n}+\mathrm{O}(\ell^{-1})\right),\quad \lambda(\phi_{[0,\ell]}^M)^2=\ell^2\left(\frac{n}{n+2}+\mathrm{O}(\ell^{-1})\right).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

