{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:36:12.193531+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d83406a64cdcc88ff7610a2c5553b19918ce3fe1736ebce54ec65e2bfc37eed0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 13 of arXiv:2609.03924, 'Beyond normal fluctuations in local laws for Wigner matrices'] Let $W$ be an $N\times N$ GOE/GUE matrix. For any fixed $D\in\N$, $n_1,n_2\in\N\cup\{0\}$ and multi-indices $\bm{k}\in \N^{n_1},\bm{l}\in\N^{n_2}$, it holds that
\begin{equation}
\E\left(\bm{k},\bm{l}\right) = \sum_{d=0}^{D-1} \frac{1}{(N\eta)^d}\Phi_d\left(\bm{k},\bm{l}\right) + \mathcal{O}\left(\frac{1}{(N\eta)^D}+\eta\right),

\end{equation}
where the implicit constant in $\mathcal{O}(\cdot)$ is uniform in $z\in\mathcal{D}$. For $d\in \N\cup\{0\}$, the coefficients $\Phi_d$ depend only on $\Re z$, $\bm{k}$, $\bm{l}$ and $\beta$, and satisfy the following recurrence relations for $\bm{k}\neq\emptyset$:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\Phi_d(\bm{k},\bm{l})=&\frac{2\ma_0}{\beta}\Im m_0 \sum_{j=1}^{n_2} \frac{\ii^{k_1-l_j-1}}{2^{k_1+l_j-1}} l_j\binom{k_1+l_j-1}{k_1-1} \Phi_d\left(\bm{k}^{[1]},\bm{l}^{[j]}\right)+\bm{1}_{\beta=1}\ma_0 k_1\Phi_{d-1}\left(\big(k_1+1,\bm{k}^{[1]}\big),\bm{l}\right)\\
+& \ma_0 \sum_{u=1}^{k_1} \Phi_{d-1}\left(\big(u,k_1+1-u,\bm{k}^{[1]}\big),\bm{l}\right)+\frac{2\ma_0}{\beta} \sum_{i=2}^{n_1} k_i \Phi_{d-1}\left(\big(k_1+k_i+1,\bm{k}^{[1,i]}\big),\bm{l}\right)\\

+&\frac{2\ma_0}{\beta} \sum_{j=1}^{n_2} \sum_{u=1}^{k_1} l_j \frac{(-1)^{k_1-u}}{(2\ii)^{k_1+l_j+1-u}} \binom{k_1+l_j-u}{l_j}\Phi_{d-1}\left( \big(u, \bm{k}^{[1]}\big), \bm{l}^{[j]}\right)\\
+&\frac{2\ma_0}{\beta} \sum_{j=1}^{n_2} \sum_{v=1}^{l_j+1} l_j\frac{(-1)^{k_1}}{(2\ii)^{k_1+l_j+1-v}}\binom{k_1+l_j-v}{k_1-1}\Phi_{d-1}\left(\bm{k}^{[1]},\big(v,\bm{l}^{[j]}\big)\right).
\end{split}

\end{equation}
Here we additionally set $\Phi_{d'}:=0$ for $d'<0$ and $\Phi_0(\emptyset,\emptyset):=1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:28:04.861127+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d8384fc948c1a951ccf71bf6c2d570c9f0152ab97be6c3d3d97b68e9b3222c9a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prp 34 of arXiv:2511.21496, 'Optimal local central limit theorems on Wiener chaos'] Let $M \in \N$, $g(x) \coloneqq x$, and $T \in \cS_{-\alpha}$ with some $\alpha \in \N_0$. 
Take $p,q \in (1, \infty)$ such that $s_{\alpha + M} = s_{\alpha + M}[p,q] > 1$, where $\{s_j\}_{j \in \N}$ is defined by \eqref{def s_j}.
Assume that $F \in \bW^{\infty}$ and $\Delta_F^{-1} \in L^q(\Omega)$. 
Then, we have
\begin{align}
    \dpair{\bW^{-\alpha -1, s_{\alpha+1}}}{(gT)(F)}{1}{\bW^{\alpha +1, s_{\alpha+1}'}}
    &= \sum_{i=0}^{M-1}\frac{\kappa_{i+1}(F)}{i!}\dpair{\bW^{-\alpha - i, s_{\alpha + i}}}{(\sD^{i}T)(F)}{1}{\bW^{\alpha + i, s_{\alpha + i}'}}\\
    &\quad + \dpair{\bW^{-\alpha-M, s_{\alpha + M}}}{(\sD^M T)(F)}{\Gamma_M(F)}{\bW^{\alpha + M, s_{\alpha +M}'}},
\end{align}
where $\dpair{\bW^{0, s_{0}}}{T(F)}{1}{\bW^{0, s_{0}'}}$ is understood as $\E[T(F)]$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:19:46.235931+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d83c4cc42ec03f8cffa6943d020ec1752645930d90303d8e568130d5527c527b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 87 of arXiv:2507.08909, 'Annealed almost periodic entropy'] If~\eqref{eq:traces-fast} holds, then the maps in~\eqref{eq:conv-comb-with-phi} satisfy
\begin{equation}
\rmh_{\bspi}^0(\phi) \le \rmh_{\bspi}^\ann(\phi_t)\le \rmh_{\bspi}^0(\phi) + k \log (1 + t\cdot \tr_k\phi(e)) \qquad (0 < t < 1).
\end{equation}
As a result, either $\rmh_{\bspi}^0(\phi)$ equals $-\infty$ and so does $\rmh_{\bspi}^\ann(\phi_t)$ for every $t$, or these quantities are all finite and
\[\rmh_{\bspi}^\ann(\phi_t) \to \rmh_{\bspi}^0(\phi) \qquad \hbox{as}\ t \downarrow 0.\]

The same conclusions hold if $\rmh^\ann_{\bspi}$ and $\rmh_{\bspi}^0$ are replaced with $\ul{\rmh}^\ann_{\bspi}$ and $\ul{\rmh}_{\bspi}^0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:28:32.000717+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d83c5f666a5c10c651e634ba99bdc2bd778c233bd5816e2de79144e44b5498d9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Coro 12 of arXiv:2512.02766, 'Fine-Graining and Continuous Space Scaling Limit of the $H^{2|2}$ Model on the Hierarchica'] On the Dyson hierarchical lattice $\mathbb{X}$ with spectral dimension $d_s=2$, the $\widetilde{\beta}$-field and the $\widetilde{u}$-field (defined in Sec. \ref{sec:find-grain-coupling}) of the $H^{2|2}$-model are stationary and ergodic with respect to the group of automorphisms of $\mathbb{X}$. Especially, we have the following zero-one law on the limit martingale (defined in \eqref{eq:mart-eux}:
\begin{equation*}
    \mathbb{P}\left[\left\{\forall x\in[0,1), \varphi_{x}=0\right\}\right],  \mathbb{P}\left[\left\{\forall x\in[0,1), \varphi_{x}>0\right\}\right]\in\{0,1\}.
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:45:01.898236+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d83d1e886fcec09acdf5d24f0b937b5d3cff7a24a6754265d51f8b606259e8bd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition Hgenerating of arXiv:2512.19897, 'On the convoy of the ASEP speed process'] The generating function of $H_n\left(x|q\right)$ is the following.
    \begin{equation}
    \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{H_{n}\left(\cos\theta \mid q\right)}{\left(q;q\right)_{n}} t^{n} = \frac{\left(t; q\right)_{\infty}}{\left(e^{i\theta}t, e^{-i\theta}t; q\right)_{\infty}}.
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:55:27.165013+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d83f1acfad4f61faf850c01298266252b5fea0f48b43b7764a4731cc88ee6ef3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq generic automorphism def of arXiv:2609.04072, 'Oort's conjecture for split unitary Shimura varieties'] Let $N=gm(n-m)-n+1$. If $n \nmid g$ we define
\begin{equation}
    \Gamma_{\operatorname{gen}} := \mathbb{Z}_p^\times + \pi^N \operatorname{Mat}_g(\mathcal{O}_D)=\mathbb{Z}_p^\times.V_N,
\end{equation}
and if $n \mid g$ we define
\begin{equation}
    \Gamma_{\operatorname{gen}}:=\mathbb{Z}_p^\times + \pi^{N-1}\mathcal{O}_D+\pi^N\operatorname{Mat}_g(\mathcal{O}_D)=(\mathbb{Z}_p^\times + \pi^{N-1}\mathcal{O}_D).V_N.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:58:38.071996+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d83fdd283103fd84e5cee3dca760bc429e91ab5c555c26d29954d2a16879965e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:unlabeled-delta-BL-p of arXiv:2606.21556, 'Mean field limit of non-exchangeable interacting diffusions on co-evolutionary networks'] For any two fibered probability measures \(\mu,\bar{\mu} \in \mathcal{P}_{\nu}(\mathcal{X}\times [0,1])\), we define
	the \emph{unlabeled} version of the distance \(d_{\text{BL},p}\) as 
	\begin{equation} 
		\delta_{\text{BL},p}(\mu,\bar{\mu}):= \inf_{\varphi \in S_{[0,1]}} d_{\text{BL},p}(\mu,\bar{\mu}^{\varphi}),
	\end{equation}
	where \(S_{[0,1]}\) is the set of bijective measure-preserving maps from \([0,1]\) to itself, and \(\bar{\mu}^{\varphi}\)
	is the fibered probability measure rearranged by \(\varphi\), that is, \(\bar{\mu}^{\varphi,\xi}=\bar{\mu}^{\varphi(\xi)}\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:48:47.287901+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d8419e48118af6a2cef446e961146805bc749d1d19f19506ab099a3394c53a86","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Hopf Lax of arXiv:2604.20660, 'The Legendre structure of the TAP complexity\\ for the Ising spin glass'] Let $\mu$ be a probability measure on $\mathbb{R}$ with support bounded from below and let $t>0$.
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item Let $x\in\mathbb{R}$ and set $\omega := \omega_{\mu,t}(x)\in\mathbb{C}^+\cup\mathbb{R}$. Define the characteristic path $(z_s)_{s\in [0,t]}$ by
			\begin{equation}
				z_s = \omega + s\, G_\mu(\omega).
			\end{equation}
			If $x\leq \ell(\mu\boxplus\sigma_t)$, then for every $s\in [0,t]$,
			\begin{equation}
				x \leq z_s \leq \ell(\mu\boxplus\sigma_s).
			\end{equation}
			If instead $x$ lies in the interior of $\mathrm{supp}(\mu\boxplus\sigma_t)$, then for every $s\in [0,t)$,
			\begin{equation*}
				\Im\bigl(\omega + s\,G_\mu(\omega)\bigr) > 0.
			\end{equation*}

			\item For every $x\leq \ell(\mu\boxplus\sigma_t)$,
			\begin{multline}
				\int \log|\lambda-x|\,\mathrm{d} (\mu\boxplus \sigma_t)(\lambda) = \int \log |\lambda-\omega_{\mu,t}(x)|\,\mathrm{d} \mu(\lambda) + \frac{\Re\bigl(\omega_{\mu,t}(x)-x\bigr)^2 - \Im\bigl(\omega_{\mu,t}(x)\bigr)^2}{2t}\\
				= \inf_{u\leq \ell(\mu)}\;\sup_{\substack{v\geq 0 \\ u+iv\in \overline{\Omega_{\mu,t}}}} \Bigl(\int \log |\lambda-(u+iv)|\,\mathrm{d} \mu(\lambda) + \frac{(x-u)^2-v^2}{2t}\Bigr).
			\end{multline}
			Moreover, the infimum in (equation eq:ch of the paper) is uniquely attained at $(u,v)=\bigl(\Re(\omega_{\mu,t}(x)),\,\Im(\omega_{\mu,t}(x))\bigr)$.
		\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:48:27.282100+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d8441a98570fd5b5982bad10fa28834e85d52e853327b6afd9cca4c9603ef134","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 of arXiv:2604.25336, 'Central limit theorem for slow-fast system under mixed fractional Brownian motion'] Under assumptions \textbf{H1}-\textbf{H3}. Then for any function \(h\in C^{2,2+\gamma}\) with \(\gamma\in(0,1]\) which is ``centering" that is \(\bar h = \int_{\mathbb{R}^{m}} h(x,y)\mu^x(dy)=0\), there exists a unique solution $u$ satisfying the following Poisson equation in \(\mathbb{R}^m\) which depends on the parameter \(x\), 
\begin{equation}
-\mathcal{L}^x(y)u(x,y) = h(x,y).
\end{equation}
Here, \(\mathcal{L}^x\) is the infinitesimal generator of the fast process \(Y\) where \(x\) is considered as a parameter. 
Moreover, \(u(x,y)\in C^2\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

