{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:38:57.119785+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d8d72dce08192765b21a2b3c9a7c24140de7c2d7ca72815d6c67d08cb1a41b85","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 7 of arXiv:2604.04840, 'Bounding the Gap Between Zeros of the Variable-\\Parameter Confluent Hypergeometric Functi'] Let $a_{k+1}^* < a_k^*$ be two consecutive zeros of $\Phi(a,b,z_l)$ for the given values $(b, z_l) \in \mathbb{R}^+$. Suppose that $a_k^*$ lies in the interval $[-N,-N+1]$ for some positive integer $N$ so that there is a sequence of $N$ values $0<z_1<z_2 < \cdots < z_N$, of which $z_l$ is a member, that satisfy the equation $\Phi(a_k^*,b,z_n) = 0$, $n=1,\dots,N$. Now let $a^*(z)$ be the solution to the initial value problem (IVP)

	\begin{equation}

		\frac{\partial a^*}{\partial z} = \left( \frac{a^*}{b} \right)^2 z^b e^{-z} \frac{\Phi^2(a^*+1,b+1,z)}{\int_0^z t^{b-1} e^{-t} \Phi^2(a^*,b,t)dt}, \quad a^*(z_l) = a^*_{k+1}
	\end{equation}\\
	\noindent Then when $z_l \neq z_N$, $a^*(z_{l+1}) = a_k^*$. If $z_l = z_N$, $a^*(z) \rightarrow -N$ as $z \rightarrow \infty$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:20:07.901282+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d8d785e5e18761e97d0c7e492d0f73599aff1f60b06530e893f0c020adc687c1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma normbound:hadamard of arXiv:2608.23765, '\texorpdfstring{Tight Majorizations and Convergence Rates\\ of Nuclear Norm Minimization I'] Let $\lambda_1, \ldots, \lambda_d$ be a sequence of positive numbers.
	Set $\boldsymbol{\lambda} := (\lambda_1, \ldots, \lambda_d)$ and $\lambda_{\max} := \max_{i \in [d]} \lambda_i$.
	Let $q \in [-\infty, \infty]$ and $\widetilde{\mathbf{H}}_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{(q)} \in \R^{d \times d}$ 
	be the matrix defined by
	\begin{equation*}
		(\widetilde{\mathbf{H}}_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{(q)})_{ij} 
		:= 
		\mathcal{M}_q (\lambda_i, \lambda_j)
		\quad
		\text{ for }
		i,j \in [d],
	\end{equation*}
	where $\mathcal{M}_q$ is the power mean as in Definition (def:powermean of the paper). Recall $c_q$ from (eq:c_q:def of the paper), i.e., for $q \in [-\infty, \infty]$,
	\begin{equation*}
	c_q =
	\begin{cases}
	1, & \text{ if } q \in [-\infty,1], \\
	2^{2-1/q}-1, & \text{ if } q \in (1,\infty), \\
	3, & \text{ if } q = \infty.
	\end{cases}
	\end{equation*}
	Then for any unitarily invariant norm ${\left\vert\kern-0.25ex\left\vert\kern-0.25ex\left\vert \cdot 
    \right\vert\kern-0.25ex\right\vert\kern-0.25ex\right\vert}$ on $\R^{d \times d}$ and any matrix $\mathbf{B} \in \R^{d \times d}$, it holds that
	\begin{equation*}
	\vertiii{ \widetilde{\mathbf{H}}_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{(q)} \circ \mathbf{B} }
	\le c_q \lambda_{\max}\, \vertiii{ \mathbf{B} }
	\leq
	\begin{cases}
	\lambda_{\max}\, \vertiii{ \mathbf{B} }, & \text{ if } q \in [-\infty,1], \\
	3 \lambda_{\max}\, \vertiii{ \mathbf{B} }, & \text{ if } q \in (1, \infty].
	\end{cases}
	\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:00:22.665792+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d8dc2ac887d5601a0d1fe069a654346f5ed3f153363fc1b90e87bfff4e317636","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:2s5c-moment-low of arXiv:2411.00930, '\bf \Large Asymptotic Product-form Steady-state for Multiclass Queueing Networks: A Reentr'] Under Assumption~(assumption:2s5c-t-moment of the paper) and~(assumption:multi-scale-2s5c of the paper), there exists $r_0\in(0,1)$ and $\epsilon_0>0$ such that
