{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:39:12.332067+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d8ebedffa877bb53bfa3cc2f683a119640ef25a19e97c87d75fcfb6b7450e39b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:wp:entropyEstimates of arXiv:2412.07710, 'An H-theorem for a conditional McKean-Vlasov process related to interacting diffusions on '] Let $m \in \mathbb{N}$ and $\lambda_0 \in \mathcal{P}(\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^m)$. Suppose $b^1, b^2:[0, T] \times \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^m \rightarrow \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^m$ are measurable and satisfy a linear growth condition, that is there exists $C \in (0, \infty)$ such that
\begin{equation*}
    \sup_{(t, x) \in [0, T] \times \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^d} |b^i(t, x)| \leq C(1 + |x|), \quad i = 1, 2.
\end{equation*}
For $i = 1, 2$, suppose $(\Omega^i, \mathcal{F}^i, \mathbb{F}^i, \mathbb{P}^i)$ is a filtered probability space supporting an $m$-dimensional Brownian motion $B^i$ and a continuous $m$-dimensional $\mathbb{F}^i$-adapted process $Z^i$ satisfying
\begin{equation*}
    dZ^i(t) = b^i(t, Z^i(t)) dt + \sqrt{2}dB^i(t),
\end{equation*}
with $\mathcal{L}(Z^1(0)) = \mathcal{L}(Z^2(0)) = \lambda_0$. Then the following identity holds: 
\begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{H} \big( \mathcal{L}(Z^1[T])\,|\, \mathcal{L}(Z^2[T]) \big) = \frac{1}{2} \mathbb{E}^{\mathbb{P}^1} \Bigg[ \int_0^T |b^1(t, Z^1(t)) - b^2(t, Z^1(t))|^2 dt\Bigg]. 
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:27:56.778074+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d8eccd393907807b1c1a975aa9d5f2d2da7af10017c992b33efc5c9da4e1b8e8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 13 of arXiv:2608.31171, '\bf Finite-energy spacetimes with torus symmetry. \\ \Large Cauchy stability of Einstein--'] The \textbf{particle-production Einstein--Navier--Stokes model} associated with a Navier--Stokes triple $(\mathscr F,\chi,\mathscr B)$ is the Einstein--matter system
\bel{eq:pot-production-system}
G^{\alpha\beta}=T_\chi^{\alpha\beta},
\qquad
\nabla_\alpha T_\chi^{\alpha\beta}=0,
