{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:33.741486+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d9bac7f5575a6b801e768d6ad55e62deaaf748bfa3bc27c5379258dffa9c3a94","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Openquestion 34 (Reversal amplitude) of arXiv:2601.16580, 'Coin flipping and waiting times paradoxes \\ Why fair coins are exceptional'] Fix a tie convention.  Among all pairs with $\mathbb{E}[\tau_U]\ge \mathbb{E}[\tau_V]$, how large can the head‑to‑head advantage of $U$ over $V$ be?  Define the \emph{reversal gap}
\[
\Delta_{L_1,L_2}(p)\;:=\;\sup_{|U|=L_1,\,|V|=L_2,\ \mathbb{E}[\tau_U]\ge \mathbb{E}[\tau_V]}\Bigl(\Pr(U\!>\!V)-\tfrac12\Bigr).
\]
Determine (or bound) $\Delta_{L_1,L_2}(p)$ and its maximisers; relate them to border polynomials.
In particular, for fixed $(L_1,L_2)$ and $p$, can $\Delta_{L_1,L_2}(p)$ be bounded away from $1/2$, or can it approach $1/2$ (i.e.\ can a ``slower'' string win with probability arbitrarily close to $1$)? (Stated as a question by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:40.333311+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d9bd5cdab126b27f2cbdefcd04955d9de015cdbcc4ab2e412568a5fb670ff79c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2511.06196, 'Central limit theorem for high temperature spin models via martingale embedding'] Let $X$ follow the Ising model \cref{eq:Isingmodel} with $0\preceq A\prec I_n$. Let $\theta=(\theta_1,\dots, \theta_n)^\top\in \IR^n$ be a unit vector, that is, $|\theta|=1$. Let
\ben{
W_n:=\theta^\top X,\quad \mu_n:=\E(W_n),\quad \sigma_n^2:=\Var(W_n).
}
Then, we have
\besn{

&\mathcal{W}_2 (\mathcal{L}(W_n), N(\mu_n, \sigma_n^2))\leq 14\left\{\frac{1}{1-\|A\|_{\text{op}}} \sup_{h\in \IR^n} \sum_{k=1}^n \bigg[\sum_{i=1}^n \Big(\sum_{j=1}^n \theta_j B_{ij}^{(h)}\Big)\Big(\sum_{l=1}^n \theta_l B_{ilk}^{(h)}\Big) \bigg]^2 \right\}^{\frac{1}{14}}
}
provided that the right-hand side is sufficiently small, where
\besn{
&B_{ij}^{(h)}:=\Cov(X_i, X_j),\\
&B_{ilk}^{(h)}:= \E (X_i-\E X_i)(X_l-\E X_l)(X_k-\E X_k),
}
$N(\mu_n, \sigma_n^2)$ denotes the normal distribution with mean $\mu_n$ and variance $\sigma_n^2$,
the supremum in \cref{eq:thm1} is over all possible external fields $h\in \IR^n$,
and the expectations and covariances in \cref{eq:Bilk} are computed under the model \cref{eq:Isingmodel} with the external field $h$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:38:25.223701+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d9bde4c4dadbd20f80158623086d89c7408ffc3e38d3bac7423c5dce979f69ab","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2609.00233, 'A survey on mapping class groups of 3-manifolds'] A sequence of groups and homomorphisms
