 \right]\dd\mu_t.
 \end{aligned}
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:37:08.320829+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dab79c91af9249476761b1e020098891940b55fb277d28488dfe96bf2e1d2dd7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:upper_bounds of arXiv:2608.31071, 'Global smooth solutions to the inhomogeneous Landau--Fermi--Dirac equation'] Fix any $f_{\rm in} \in L^\infty_m$ for some $m > 2$ satisfying $0 \le f_{\rm in} \le 1$. Any classical solution $f\colon[0,T]\times \Omega \times \mathbb R^3 \to [0,1]$ to (equation e.LFD of the paper) with initial data $f_{\rm in}$ satisfies the pointwise bound
    \begin{equation*}
        \left\vertf(t,x,v)\right\vert \le e^{Kt} \frac{\norm{f_{\rm in}}_{L^\infty_m}}{\left\vertv\right\vert^m} \qquad \text{for each }(t,x,v) \in [0,T]\times \Omega\times \mathbb R^3.
    \end{equation*}
    Here $K = K(m)$ depends only only on $m$. The spatial domain $\Omega$ may be either $\mathbb T^3$ or $\mathbb R^3$.
    Consequently, the mass and energy densities satisfy
    \begin{equation}
        \rho(t,x) = \int_{\mathbb R^3} f(t,x,v) \, {\rm d} v \lesssim  e^{Kt} \|f_{\rm in}\|_{L^\infty_m}
        \qquad \text{and} \qquad E(t,x) = \int_{\mathbb R^3} \left\vertv\right\vert^2 f(t,x,v) \, {\rm d} v \lesssim e^{Kt}\|f_{\rm in}\|_{L^\infty_m},
    \end{equation}
    provided $m > 5$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:50:48.850007+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dab88b5e822b96c8cae25ad846d4e0f45ee92b74f6bff3575177dd2398756dfa","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem recur:lowerBound of arXiv:2607.09841, 'Mixing and cutoff for the systematic scan dynamics of the mean-field ferromagnetic Potts m'] Let $\{x_t\}_{t \geq 0}$ be a sequence of real numbers define for $t \geq n$ by
\begin{align*}
    x_t &= \frac{\beta}{nq}\sum_{i = t-n}^{t-1} x_i - \frac{C}{n} \sum_{i = t-n}^{t-1} \gamma_n^{2i} - \frac{\tilde C}{n},
\end{align*}
where $C, \tilde C > 0$ are constants. If for all $i \in \{0,\dots,n-1\}$, there exists a constant $\rho > 0$ such that
$x_i \geq \rho$, then there exists a constant $\hat C > 0$ such that
\begin{align*}
     x_t  \geq  (\rho - \hat C C) \gamma_n^t - \frac{\tilde C \hat C}{n}.
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.294955+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dabba4a47002b5cd0e6b88140456d35b21545d8be7b09113e144f73830ba60be","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop occupation of arXiv:2411.05441, 'Sticky diffusions on star graphs : characterization and It\^o formula'] For every bounded and measurable function $f \colon \mathbb{R}_+ \to \mathbb{R}$ the time-changed process $X$ satisfies
		\begin{equation}
		\int_0^t f(s) \mathbf{1}_{\{X(s) = \mathsf{v}\}}\, ds = \eta \int_0^t f(s) d\ell_X(s) \quad \textnormal{almost surely for every } t > 0,
		\end{equation}
		where the integral in the right-hand side must be understood in the Lebesgue-Stieltjes sense. In particular, the occupation time of $X$ at $\mathsf{v}$ is given by
		\begin{align}

		\int_0^t \mathbf{1}_{\{X(s) = \mathsf{v}\}}\, ds = \eta \ell_X(t) \quad \textnormal{almost surely for every } t > 0.
		\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:00.617111+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dabbbc8f9855871d779fb4bdeec51f66ef63a3adc4758f257359dbaeb49ea961","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 58 of arXiv:2510.27589, 'Geodesic switches and exceptional times in dynamical Brownian last passage percolation'] Fix $k\in \NN,t\geq 1$ and let $\mathbf{a}\in \RR$ be such that $(\mathbf{a},\mathbf{a}+t)\subseteq [-n^{1/10},n^{1/10}]$. Define $U(\mathbf{a})=[\![1,k]\!]\times (\mathbf{a},\mathbf{a}+t)$. Then there exists a random sequence of continuous functions $\fL^{\mathbf{a}}=\fL^{t,k,\mathbf{a}}$ such that the following hold:
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item Almost surely, $\fL^{\mathbf{a}}$ satisfies $\fL_i^{\mathbf{a}}(r)>\fL_{i+1}^{\mathbf{a}}(r)$ for all $(i,r)\notin U(\mathbf{a})$.
  \item The line ensemble $\fL^{\mathbf{a}}$ satisfies the following Gibbs property. For $1\leq m\leq n+1$ and $-n^{1/3}/2<a<b<\infty$, set $S=[\![1,m]\!]\times [a,b]$. Then conditional on the data given by $\fL_i^{\mathbf{a}}(r)$ for $(i,r)\notin S$, the law of $\fL_i^{\mathbf{a}}(r)$ for $(i,r)\in S$ is given by independent Brownian bridges $B_1,\dots,B_\ell$ from $(a,\fL_i^{\mathbf{a}}(a))$ to $(b,\fL_i^{\mathbf{a}}(b))$ for $i\in [\![1,k]\!]$, additionally conditioned on having $B_i(r)>B_{i+1}(r)$ for all $(i,r)\notin U(\mathbf{a})$.
  \item There exists a constant $c_k$ for which we have
    \begin{equation}

      \PP(\fL_1^{\mathbf{a}}>\fL_2^{\mathbf{a}}>\dots>\fL_{n+1}^{\mathbf{a}})\geq e^{-c_kt^3}.
    \end{equation}
  \item There is an event $\cE$ which is measurable with respect to the data given by $\cP_i(r)$ for $(i,r)\notin U(\mathbf{a})$, satisfying for a positive constant $c_k$, the inequality
    \begin{equation}

      \PP(\cE^c)\leq e^{-c_kn},
    \end{equation}
    such that if we condition $\fL^{\mathbf{a}}$ on the event $\{\fL_1^{\mathbf{a}}>\fL_2^{\mathbf{a}}>\dots>\fL_{n+1}^{\mathbf{a}}\}$, then the resulting ensemble has the same law as $\cP$ conditioned on the event $\cE$. 
  \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:48:47.304461+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dac0a9d14df832e601183543ed44355994eeba5a7dc9ef5f8cf5d9d109bb0af4","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition of arXiv:2604.22130, 'Reflected Stochastic Differential Equations Driven by $G$-Brownian Motion with Nonlinear C'] Given two reflected $G$-BSDEs with parameters $(x^i,f^i,h^i,g,l,r)$, $i=1,2$ satisfying the assumptions in Theorem (thm5.9 of the paper), we additionally suppose the following condition.
\begin{itemize}
\item  For each $t\in[0,T]$, $g$ does not depends on $x$ and either $f^1(t,\cdot),g^1(t,\cdot)$ or $f^2(t,\cdot),g^2(t,\cdot)$ are nondecreasing.

\end{itemize}
Let $(X^i,A^i)$ be the solution to reflected $G$-SDE with parameters $(x^i,f^i,h^i,g,l,r)$ and $(A^{i,l},A^{i.r})$ be the associated constraining processes, $i=1,2$. Then, we have
\begin{itemize}
\item[(1)] $A^{1,l}_t\leq A^{2,l}_t\leq A^{1,l}_t+\hat{X}_t+(x^2-x^1)$;
\item[(2)] $A^{2,r}_t\leq A^{1,r}_t\leq A^{2,r}_t+\hat{X}_t+(x^2-x^1)$,
\end{itemize}
where $\hat{X}_t=\int_0^t f^2(s,X^2_s)-f^1(s,X^1_s)ds+\int_0^t h^2(s,X^2_s)-h^1(s,X^1_s)d\langle B\rangle_s$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:32:11.124226+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dac1ab5a163b7bc0c8fef51e76cdc7d8b56d16a54a603b32520ff620c14672fb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma def:irreducibility22 of arXiv:2510.00748, '\bf On irreducible central limit theorems'] Let the assumptions and conventions of the present section hold. Then, the sequence $\{Z_n\}$ is reducible in the sense of Definition (def:irreducibility of the paper) (see also Remark (r:reducible of the paper)-{\rm (redCLTconda of the paper),(redCLTcondb of the paper),(redCLTcondc of the paper)}) if and only if there exist partitions $\{B_1,\ldots,B_{m_n}\} = \{B^{(n)}_1,\ldots,B^{(n)}_{m_n}\}$ of the vertex sets $V_n$, such that, as $n \to \infty$,
    \begin{enumerate}[label={\rm (\roman*')}]
    \item  $m_n\to \infty$;
    \item  $\sum_{i=1}^{m_n} E(B_i,B_i) \sim |\mathcal{E}_n|$;
    \item  $\max_{i=1,...,m_n} E(B_i,B_i) = o\big(|\mathcal{E}_n|\big)$.  
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:38:11.738589+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dac29044d01c6f6994e32f471c0f90c3766b8282b5a3db9a96e6d4aadd442619","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma CVtG of arXiv:2504.00512, 'Localization-delocalization transition for a random block matrix model at the edge'] Under the assumptions of Theorem (MixEV of the paper), suppose $\mathfrak{t}= N^{-1/3+\mathfrak c}$ for a constant $\mathfrak c \in (0,1/10)$. Then, for any fixed $n\in \mathbb{N}$, there exist a constant $c_n=c_n(\mathfrak c,\delta_A,\varepsilon_A)>0$ such that 
\begin{align}
&\left|	\mathbb{E} O\pa{\gamma_+  \left({DN}\right)^{2/3}\pa{E^+-\lambda_1^{{\mathfrak t}}},\ldots,\gamma_+ \left({DN}\right)^{2/3}\pa{E^+-\lambda_n^{{\mathfrak t}}}}\right.\nonumber\\
&\left.-\mathbb{E} O\pa{ \left({DN}\right)^{2/3}\left({2-\mu_1}\right),\ldots, \left({DN}\right)^{2/3}\left({2-\mu_n}\right)}   \right|\le N^{-c_n}   , 
\end{align}
where $\lambda_1^{{\mathfrak t}}\ge\cdots\ge \lambda_n^{{\mathfrak t}}$ and $\mu_1\ge\cdots\ge \mu_n$ denote respectively the largest $n$ eigenvalues of ${H_{\Lambda}}({\mathfrak t})$ and a $DN\times DN$ GUE. 
The corresponding result also holds for $\gamma_-(DN)^{2/3}(\lambda_{DN}^{{\mathfrak t}}-E^-,\ldots,\lambda_{DN-n}^{{\mathfrak t}}-E^- )$ at the left edge $E^-$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:24:25.989547+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dac3f0d280f949f60068461e3e1ce0378ce547dd0a8771f3336189713f66b866","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 82 of arXiv:2507.07702, 'Observables of random spanning trees in random environment'] For disjoint sets $A, B \subset V$, we define the effective resistance between $A$ and $B$ by 
\begin{equation} 
    \effR{G, \weight}{A}{B} := \frac{1}{\sum_{a \in A} \weight(a) Q_{a}(\tau_B < \tau_A^+)}, 
\end{equation}
where $Q_a$ is the law of the lazy random walk started at $a$, $\tau_B$ is the first hitting time of $B$, and $\tau_A^+$ is the first return time to $A$. When $A=\{a\}$ and $B = \{b\}$ are singletons, we will just write
$\effR{G, \weight}{a}{b}$ instead. When the underlying graphs and weights are clear we will often drop either one or both of the superscripts.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:32:28.995703+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dac4487c667e850a3378fa6d1321a2687ddd5c658d77b2c821762dc3449df51c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm T th gau of arXiv:2608.25471, 'Exact Hausdorff measures and fine geometry of collisions of independent Markov processes'] There exist a deterministic constant $c_1\in(0,\infty)$
  and a deterministic function $c_2\colon (0,\infty)\to(0,\infty)$ such that,
