{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:19:00.866291+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dae48ed0ea1503290ab332d9eb99528c3d06ba109d5a16734661381ce4ea4c19","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 20 (Discrete-time crossing of a curved boundary) of arXiv:2608.20337, 'Information on trajectories:\\ martingales and random times'] Let $X=N+A$ be a discrete class-$(\Sigma)$ process and let $\varphi\colon\R_+\to(0,\infty]$ be Borel with $I:=\int_0^\infty\varphi(x)^{-1}\dd x<\infty$.
Write $w(x):=\exp\bigl(-\int_x^\infty\varphi(z)^{-1}\dd z\bigr)$, $\zeta:=1-w$ and $h:=w/\varphi$, so that $w'=h$ and $\zeta'=-h$, and assume $h$ is bounded.
Then $M_t:=\zeta(A_t)+h(A_t)X_t$ is nonnegative with $M_0=1-e^{-I}$, the crossing event is exactly
\begin{equation}
  \bigl\{\exists\,t:\ X_t>\varphi(A_t)\bigr\}
  =\bigl\{\sup\nolimits_t M_t>1\bigr\},
\end{equation}
and $M$ decomposes pathwise as
\begin{equation}
  M_t=M_0+\sum_{s\le t}h(A_{s-1})\,\Delta N_s+\sum_{s\le t}R_s,
  \qquad
  R_s:=h(A_{s-1})\,\Delta A_s-\bigl(w(A_s)-w(A_{s-1})\bigr),
\end{equation}
the first sum a martingale and $R_s=\int_{A_{s-1}}^{A_s}\bigl(h(A_{s-1})-h(z)\bigr)\dd z$ the quadrature residual of $h$ across the jump of $A$.
If in addition $h$ has finite total variation $\mathrm{var}(h)$ on $\R_+$, the jumps of $A$ are uniformly bounded, $A_t\to\infty$, and $M_t\to0$ off the crossing event, then with $T:=\inf\{t:X_t>\varphi(A_t)\}$ and overshoot $J:=M_T-1\ge0$,
\begin{equation}
  \PP\bigl(\exists\,t:\ X_t>\varphi(A_t)\bigr)
  =1-e^{-I}
   +\E\Bigl[\sum_{s\le T}R_s\Bigr]
   -\E\bigl[J\,\ind\{T<\infty\}\bigr].
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:52.746888+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dae88120d9c6de968551d926f95ecbb2718c537c10a98f9ab513df7a899e89d1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2506.10522, 'On the Law of the Iterated Logarithm for $m$-dependent stationary random variables under s'] Consider a subspace of $\mathcal{H}$ as
