{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:49:49.199380+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dbaaf8960705a5aef44fad2018a311c253048cf46300a0881d52a9258d0eee04","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 22 ({\cite[Theorem 4.9) of arXiv:2604.26549, 'Existence and uniqueness results of a stochastic nonlinear heat equation with a constraint'] {ZB+GD-21}}]
		Let $\mathcal{X}$ be a topological space such that there exists a sequence $\{f_m\}_{m\in\N}$ of continuous functions $f_m:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}$ that separates points of $\mathcal{X}$. Let us denote by $\mathcal{S}$, the $\sigma-$algebra generated by the sequence of maps $\{f_m\}_{m\in\N}$. Then
		\begin{itemize}
			\item[(a)] every compact subset of $\mathcal{X}$  is metrizable,
			\item[(b)] if $\{\nu_m\}_{m\in\N}$ is a tight sequence of probability measures on $(\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{S})$, then there exists a subsequence $\{m_k\}_{k\in\N}$, a probability space $(\Omega,\Fn,\Pr)$ with $\mathcal{X} - $valued Borel measurable random variables $\xi_k,\xi$ such that $\mu_{m_k}$ is the law of $\xi_k$ and $\xi_k$ converges to $\xi$ almost surely on $\Omega$. Moreover, the law of $\xi$ is a Radon measure.
		\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:32:35.571285+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dbb0cee5babd1262d216058396c9193c8ccbbad0b8e433f73f01169743bcdb04","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:extension.Luk.1994.Lemma.2.4 of arXiv:2606.04859, 'Stein's method for the Wishart distribution'] Let $\alpha\in (d - 1, \infty)$ and $\Sigma\in \mathcal{S}_{++}^d$ be given, and set $\Sigma_t \vcentcolon= (1 - e^{-2t})\Sigma$. For the matrix Wishart process defined in (equation eq:Wishart.process of the paper), with transition kernel on $\mathcal{S}_{+}^d$, for every initial state $W\in \mathcal{S}_{+}^d$ we have
\begin{align}
\mathfrak{W}_t \mid \{\mathfrak{W}_0 = W\} &\sim \mathcal{W}_d(\alpha, \Sigma_t, e^{-2t} \Sigma_t^{-1} W), \qquad t > 0,  \\
\mathfrak{W}_t \mid \{\mathfrak{W}_0 = W\} &\stackrel{\mathrm{law}}{\longrightarrow} \mathfrak{W}_{\infty} \sim \mathcal{W}_d(\alpha, \Sigma), \qquad t\to \infty. 
\end{align}
In particular, let $(\mathcal{P}^{\mathcal{W}}_t)_{t\geq 0}$ be the transition semigroup with kernel $P_t(W, \, \mathrm{d} Y)$ on $\mathcal{S}_{+}^d$, so that, for every bounded Borel measurable function $h:\mathcal{S}_{+}^d\to\mathbb{R}$,
\begin{equation}
(\mathcal{P}^{\mathcal{W}}_t h)(W) \vcentcolon= \int_{\mathcal{S}_{+}^d} h(Y) \, P_t(W, \, \mathrm{d} Y) = \mathsf{E}\big[h(\mathfrak{W}_t)\mid \mathfrak{W}_0 = W\big].
\end{equation}
When $h$ is only defined on $\mathcal{S}_{++}^d$, we use the same notation for $t > 0$ and $W\in \mathcal{S}_{+}^d$ by choosing any bounded Borel extension of $h$ to $\mathcal{S}_{+}^d$ since $P_t(W, \partial\mathcal{S}_{+}^d) = 0$. For $\mathfrak{S}_t\sim \smash{\mathcal{W}_d(\alpha, I_d, e^{-2t} \Sigma_t^{-1/2} W \Sigma_t^{-1/2})}$, the above shows
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{P}_t^{\mathcal{W}} h(W) = \mathsf{E}\big[h(\Sigma_t^{1/2} \mathfrak{S}_t \Sigma_t^{1/2})\big].
\end{equation}
Moreover, given a probability measure $\mu$ on $\mathcal{S}_{+}^d$, the push-forward measure $\mu \mathcal{P}^{\mathcal{W}}_t$ is defined by
\[
(\mu \mathcal{P}^{\mathcal{W}}_t)(A) \vcentcolon= \int_{\mathcal{S}_{+}^d} P_t(W, A) \, \mu(\mathrm{d} W), \qquad A\subseteq \mathcal{S}_{+}^d \text{ Borel}.
\]
Hence, for $\xi \vcentcolon= \mathcal{W}_d(\alpha, \Sigma)$, we have the invariance
\begin{equation}
\xi \mathcal{P}^{\mathcal{W}}_t = \xi.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:52.231503+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dbb195783757746cdc2f7f84cf6732bbc9f65245ddf396bb14d7224df1052f86","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 43 of arXiv:2512.09136, 'On the Green's functions and Martin boundary structure of a planar diffusion in a disconti'] Let $f :\R \longrightarrow \R^+$ be a continuous function and $\phi(z):= \int_\R e^{xz}f(x)dx$. Suppose that there are $a < 0 < b$ such that $\phi$ converges absolutely in $a<\Re(z)<b$. For $\delta > 0$ and $s \in \C$, let $G^+_\delta(s) = \{z \in \C, \Re(z) > 0, z\neq s, |\arg(z-s)| > \delta\}$ (where the complex argument is taken in $(-\pi,\pi]$). Suppose that there exists $0 \leq \delta< \pi/2$ such that
\begin{itemize}
\item Function $\phi$ has a holomorphic continuation on $G^+_\delta(b)$.
\item The following asymptotics hold: $$\phi(z) \underset{|z|\to + \infty\atop z \in G^+_\delta(b)}\longrightarrow 0.$$
\item There exists $\lambda \in \R$ and constants $c \in \C$, $A \neq 0$ such that
\begin{equation}(\phi(z) -c)(b-z)^\lambda \underset{z\to b\atop z\in G^+_\delta(b)}{\longrightarrow}A.\end{equation}
\end{itemize}
Then: $$f(x) \underset{x \to +\infty}{\sim}\frac{A}{\Gamma(\lambda)}e^{-bx}x^{\lambda - 1} $$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:17.755809+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dbb2e7f39d55cad3c04662dc519ca1bd8c6b234416aa72a8a744e5e62409e0a6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 13 of arXiv:2511.04938, 'Uniform dimension theorems for parabolic SPDEs'] For every $T > 0$,
	\begin{equation}\begin{aligned}
		&\Var(H_1(t\,,x)\mid H_1(t_1\,,x_1),\dots,H_1(t_m\,,x_m))\\
		&\hskip1in \asymp 
			\min\left\{ \sqrt{t}\, , \min_{1\le j\le m}\left( |t-t_j|^{1/2} + |x-x_j| \right)\right\}, 
	\end{aligned}\end{equation}
	uniformly for all $m\in\N$ and $(t\,,x), (t_1\,,x_1), \dots, (t_m\,,x_m) \in (0\,,T] \times \T$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:25:06.155107+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dbb786ba98e3aac3e1b0303b34a05f0f0124a70ba0282881056962b8f2f83b25","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 13 of arXiv:2608.22534, 'Entropy power inequalities in compact groups'] Let $G$ be a compact and metrizable topological abelian group
equipped with its Borel $\sigma$-algebra, and let $\mu$ denote the Haar 
probability measure on $G$. 
For any $G$-valued random variables $X, Y$ with finite entropies 
there is a non-empty, closed subset $S$ of $G$ of positive Haar 
measure such that, if $U_S$ is uniformly distributed on $S$, then,
for any constant $C\geq 4$,
\begin{equation} 
\dR(U_S, Y) \leq (C+2)\dR(X, Y) + H_2(1 - 2/C), 
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\log \frac{\mu(S-S)}{\mu(S)} \leq (2C+4)\dR(X, Y) + 2H_2(1 - 2/C).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:26.258187+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dbb787cb9e46dc59086e568b861405adcc0a5349762ceb89d819d983d0196409","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 75 ({\cite[Theorems 1.5(ii) and 1.7) of arXiv:2605.23308, 'Convergence rate estimates for semigroups and heat kernels associated with resistance form'] {BTM24}}]
Let $\alpha>2$ and $E\in \left(0,\frac{\alpha-2}{10(2\alpha-1)}\right)$.
Then it holds for any $K\in (0,\infty)$ and $0<T_1<T_2<\infty$ that
\begin{equation*}
  \sup_{x\in \R}|P_0(X_1\le x)-P^n_0(X^n_1\le x)|=O(n^{-\theta}),
