{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:57:16.061433+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc4c30458e060faf00013cf06ce1b5b2c5bed376b3117c706bcc4eab87fdb2bf","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem many_to_one of arXiv:2506.10623, 'Polynomial slowdown in an angle-dependent 2d branching Brownian motion'] Let $t \geq s_0 \geq 0$ and $f \colon \mathcal{C}([s_0,t],\mathbb{R}^2) \to [0,\infty)$ be a measurable functional. Then, for any $x,y \in \mathbb{R}^2$,
	\begin{align*}
	    & \mathbb{E}_{(s_0,x,y)} \mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft[\sum_{u \in \mathcal{N}(t)} f\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft((X_u(s),Y_u(s))_{s\in [s_0,t]} \aftergroup\egroup\originalright) \aftergroup\egroup\originalright] \\
	    & = \mathbb{E}_{(s_0,x,y)} \mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft[f\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft((X_s,Y_s)_{s\in [s_0,t]} \aftergroup\egroup\originalright) \exp\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft( \int_{s_0}^t b(\theta_s) \mathop{}\mathopen{}\mathrm{d} s \aftergroup\egroup\originalright) \aftergroup\egroup\originalright],
	\end{align*}
	where $(X_s,Y_s)_{s\in [s_0,t]}$ is a Brownian motion on $\mathbb{R}^2$ starting from $(x,y)$ at time $s_0$ under $\mathbb{P}_{(s_0,x,y)}$, and $(R_s,\theta_s)_{s\in [s_0,t]}$ is a representation of its polar coordinates. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:43:53.603240+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc4cabb2c8473c5b398b5fb8f77d54b03de0a05169f89c20be7ebcba1a19bdbb","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:conv_mart of arXiv:2512.12902, 'Non-equilibrium fluctuations for the \\stirring process with births and deaths'] For $H \in\mathbb S$, the sequence of martingales $\{M_t^\epsilon(H);t\in [0,T]\}_{\epsilon}$ converges in the topology of $\mathcal  D([0,T], \mathbb R)$, as $\epsilon\to 0$, towards a mean-zero Gaussian process $ W_t(H)$ with  quadratic variation given by 
