{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:23.338436+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc75293c7c2a61428bea3e47224595489f1f0f1de6beff26e3cbb4b72696b831","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 7 of arXiv:2506.22213, 'No arbitrage assumption implies the differentiability of derivative pricing function'] Let $\hat{X}$ be an $m$-dimensional continuous Markov semimartingale starting at $x\in\bbR^m$ and satisfy SDE
    \begin{align}

        d\hat{X}_t=b(V_t,\hat{X}_t)dV_t+\sigma(V_t,\hat{X}_t)dN_t;\quad \hat{X}_0=x,
    \end{align}
    where $N$ is an adapted local martingale with $\langle N,N \rangle_t=V_t$, and $V$ is an adapted strictly increasing continuous finite variation process. Suppose $b,\sigma$ satisfy Assumption \ref{sde assumption}. We further assume SDE \eqref{ito sde} with coefficients $(b,\sigma)$ has a unique strong solution, for instance, $\sigma$ is Lipschitz. Then $(f(V_t,\hat{X}_t))_{t\geq 0}$ is a semimartingale if and only if $f\in \hat{V}^L_\mu(loc)$, and it has decomposition
\begin{align}

    f(V_t,\hat{X}_t)=f(0,\hat{X}_0)+\int_0^t f_x(V_s,\hat{X}_s)d\hat{X}_s+ A_t^f
\end{align}
where $A^f$ is uniquely determined by the relation
\begin{align}
    E\int_0^\infty\psi(V_s,\hat{X}_s)dA_s^f=\iint\psi(s,x)\nu^L_f(ds,dx)
\end{align}
for each bounded continuous function $\psi$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:15:15.481007+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc76c06648661f8b7a16973c4d4e002b25adcf85d5d047c4990368e33cc821d9","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:PCM of arXiv:2507.01406, '{\Large On Iterated Lorenz Curves with Applications: The Multivariate Case }'] \medskip \noindent  A random vector $X=(X_{1},\ldots ,X_{d})$
is \textit{paired countermonotone}, or PCM, with respect to the pairing (\ref
{2050}), if:

\begin{enumerate}
\item for each $r=1,\ldots ,m$, the pair $(X_{2r-1},X_{2r})$ is
countermonotone in the bivariate sense;

\item the blocks 
\begin{equation*}
(X_{1},X_{2}),\ (X_{3},X_{4}),\ldots ,(X_{2m-1},X_{2m})
\end{equation*}
are independent.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:31:49.848213+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc782527bea603b69b5a21f91f601d126f2952648ca2d5eb430621d0877aa9eb","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem: threshold for null initial condition of arXiv:2608.23756, 'Measure-valued fluid limits for partial service queues\\with correlated patience and servi'] Recall from (equation eq: equilibrium threshold of the paper) that
	\begin{equation*}
		y_* \vcentcolon= \inf\asetc{y \geq 0}{\lambda_0\int_0^y S_g(u, u)du \geq 1},
	\end{equation*}
	where $y_* \vcentcolon= \infty$ if the integral is strictly less than one for all $y \geq 0$. Suppose that $\boldsymbol{\mu} \in \mathcal{S}^+$ has initial condition the null measure. Then
	\begin{equation*}
		\bar y_*(\boldsymbol{\mu}_t) = \begin{cases}
			\infty 	& \text{if} \quad 0 \leq t < y_*, \\
			y_* 	& \text{if} \quad y_* < \infty \quad \text{and} \quad t \geq y_*.	
		\end{cases}
	\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:08.770568+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc79ac59a4ca35da8a71fd7e32d80392ee9bbcf587a75cea0f12d04b70a0f06d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Satz thm:sufficiency-log of arXiv:2411.18155, 'Besov regularity of random wavelet series'] Let $d,k\in\mathds{N}$, $s,\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\theta\in\mathds{R}$, and $p,q\in (0,\infty]$.
Assume that $a$ follows a Besov sequence prior and $f$ follows a Besov prior, both with parameters $(\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\theta,k)$ and template random variable $X$, satisfying
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases} \:\exists\: \eps>0:\,\mathds{E}\bigl[\left\lvertX\right\rvert^p \cdot \log_2^+(\left\lvertX\right\rvert)\bigr]< \infty&\text{if }p\in (0,\infty),\\
\:\exists\:  R>0:\, \left\lvertX\right\rvert\stackrel{a.s.}{\leq}R&\text{if }p=\infty.\end{cases}
\end{equation}
If Property A holds, then $a$ almost surely belongs to $b^s_{p,q}(\mathds{R}^d)$.
Moreover, if Properties A and B are satisfied, then $f$ belongs almost surely to $B^s_{p,q}(\mathds{R}^d)$.","status":"unverified"}

