{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:31:49.844632+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dca45e9adf1435c04359de6b860b9d26cc717fb84fa71465571b99b1b7bd1fe9","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def: hazard rate of arXiv:2608.23756, 'Measure-valued fluid limits for partial service queues\\with correlated patience and servi'] The survival function associated with $g$ is defined as
	\begin{equation*}
		S_g(x, y) \vcentcolon= \int_{[x, \infty) \times [y, \infty)} g(u, v)dudv \quad \text{for all} \quad x, y \in \mathbbm{R}_+.
	\end{equation*}
	Consider the open set $U_g \vcentcolon= \setc{(x, y) \in \mathbbm{R}_+^2}{S_g(x, y) > 0}$. If the survival function $S_g$ is differentiable, then we define the hazard rate vector field $\map{h_g}{\mathbbm{R}_+^2}{\mathbbm{R}_+^2}$ as:
	\begin{equation*}
		h_g(x, y) \vcentcolon= -\nabla \left[\log S_g\right](x, y) \quad \text{if} \quad (x, y) \in U_g \quad \text{and} \quad h_g(x, y) \vcentcolon= 0 \quad \text{otherwise}.
	\end{equation*}
	The first and second components of $h_g$ are denoted by $h_g^x$ and $h_g^y$, respectively.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:25.272750+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dca60109d34adf93e149ea5a4dff4ecb978438dca055121c865d63df4299c49f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 14 of arXiv:2606.21111, 'On Elephant Random Walk with Delayed Amnesia'] Let $0\le p<(4a-1)/4a$. Then,
