{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:12:19.463672+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dcd2d7ef27706dd55b62e243d26a80dda3829472a201d3b81c714ba49312b6d1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 8 (Fundamental estimate with localization parameter) of arXiv:2608.17540, 'Pathwise stability for one-dimensional SDEs driven by Brownian motion and a symmetric stab'] Assume Assumptions~\ref{ass:baseline}--\ref{ass:solutions}. Then there exists a constant
\[
C=C\bigl(T,\alpha,\wt\eta,\|\ell_b\|_{L^1(0,T)},\|\ell_a\|_{L^2(0,T)},\|\ell_\sigma\|_{L^\alpha(0,T)}\bigr)
\]
independent of $\delta$ and $\eps$ such that, for every $\delta\ge2$ and $\eps\in(0,1)$,
\begin{align}
\sup_{0\le t\le T}\E|X_t-\wt X_t|^{\alpha-1}
\le C\Bigg[&|x_0-\wt x_0|^{\alpha-1}
+\eps^{\alpha-1}
+\frac{\delta}{\log\delta}\frac{B}{\eps^{2-\alpha}} \notag\\
&+\frac{\delta^2}{\log\delta}\frac{A^2}{\eps^{3-\alpha}}
+\frac{\delta}{\log\delta}\frac{S^\alpha}{\eps}
+\frac{\delta^2}{\log\delta}\eps^{\alpha-1}
+\frac{\eps^{\alpha\wt\eta-1}}{\log\delta}
\Bigg]. 
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:01:22.627864+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dcd3b747b357783ad3490ae5816f407a9ded699022901f2a82044c2df465ffa2","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Th th_original_threshold of arXiv:2606.23648, '\textsc{Optimal Stopping for a Diffusion with Unobserved Bernoulli Drift}'] Assume that the hypotheses of Theorem~(th_2d_structure of the paper) hold. Then
    \begin{equation}
        \mathcal C^0
        =
        \{x\in\mathcal I:\kappa(x)>0\}.
    \end{equation}
    In particular, for every \(x\notin\mathcal C^0\), immediate stopping is optimal and \(V(x)=k(x)\). For every \(x\in\mathcal C^0\), we have
    \begin{equation}
        V(x)=Q(x)G(0)+R(x),
    \end{equation}
    with the functions $Q$ and $R$ defined in (equation def_q_R_coefficients of the paper), and an optimal stopping time is
    \begin{equation}
        \tau_x^*
        =
        \inf\{t\ge0:X(t)\notin(x+a(x),\,x+b(x))\}.
    \end{equation}

    If, in addition, (ass_3 of the paper) holds, then the function $\kappa(\cdot)$ of (equation def_kappa_threshold of the paper) is non-decreasing on \(\mathcal I\), and \(\mathcal C^0\) is either empty or a right interval. More precisely, with
    \begin{equation}
        x^*
        \coloneqq
        \inf\{x\in\mathcal I:\kappa(x)>0\}
    \end{equation}
    and the convention \(\inf\emptyset=\infty\), immediate stopping is optimal for every \(0\le x<x^*\), while \(x\in\mathcal C^0\) holds for every \(x>x^*\).

    Finally, assume that \(J\subset\mathcal C^0\) is an open interval on which the relevant two-dimensional boundaries are of class \(C^1\). Then \(V\) is \(C^1\) on \(J\), with
    \begin{equation}
        V'(x)=Q'(x)G(0)+R'(x),
        \qquad x\in J.
    \end{equation}
    If \(x^*<\infty\), \(V(x^*)=k(x^*)\), and \((x^*,0)\) is a regular horizontal crossing point, then \(V\) satisfies smooth fit at the original threshold:
    \begin{equation}
        V'(x^*+)=k'(x^*).
    \end{equation}
    If \(x^*>0\), then \(V'(x^*-)=k'(x^*)\) holds as well, so \(V\) is continuously differentiable at
    \(x^*\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:30.010463+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dcd44189e1617a41de835d62e03d818bb29606ba096125fd91924fd02b14eb86","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2605.04168, 'Error analysis for learning fractional stochastic differential equations with applications'] Suppose that the assumptions of Section \ref{sec:assumptions} hold, in particular, \ref{H0} holds for both $H$ and $\HeM$, with $\hat{H}_M$ an estimator of the Hurst parameter. Let $\epsilon,\epsilon_0>0$, $s,s_2,\tilde{\delta}\in(0,1)$ with $\tilde{\delta}<\HeM$, and denote $\tilde\sigma = \sigma - \sigma_n$ and $\tilde b = b - b_n$. Then, for any 
$q>\frac{d}{1-s}$, $q_2>\frac{1}{1-s_2}, \rho > \frac{1}{1-\alpha}$ such that $q\ge\rho$, and any $\alpha \in (1-H\wedge\HeM, \, \min(1/2,s\tilde{\delta},s_2))$, we have for almost all realisations:
    \begin{align*}
        \mathcal{E}_{\texttt{appr},\alpha}(n) &\leq  C_\omega\left[ \Vert\partial_t\nabla_z\tilde\sigma\Vert_{(q_2,q)} +\Vert\nabla_z\tilde\sigma\Vert_{(q_2,q)} + \Vert\partial_t\tilde\sigma\Vert_{(q_2,q)} + \Vert\tilde\sigma\Vert_{(q_2,q)}\right.  \\
    &+\left.\Vert \nabla_z \tilde b\Vert_{(\rho,q)}  + \Vert  \tilde b\Vert_{(\rho,q)}\right]\\
        \mathcal{E}_{\texttt{fit},\alpha}(M) &\leq C_\omega|\HeM-H|^{\frac12}.
    \end{align*}

    Furthermore, for any $\eta>0$ there exists $\Delta_0 >0$ and $\Omega_0\subseteq \Omega$ such that $\P(\Omega_0)>1-\eta$ and, for any $\Delta t\leq \Delta_0$:
    \begin{align*}
        \mathcal{E}_{\texttt{time},\alpha}(\Delta t) & \leq C_{\epsilon_0}\,  \Delta t^{2\hat{H}_M-1-\epsilon_0}.
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:57:20.591367+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dcd5b4512707e6d9d25be146851bef887caa0b4c48546deecdb3c71b5a87f0ce","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn of arXiv:2411.09988, 'A Study in Markov Chains, Loop-Erased Random Walk and Loop Soups'] A discrete-time \textit{(time-homogeneous) Markov chain} on $A$ is a sequence of random variables $X_0, X_1, X_2, . . .$ taking values in a countable (finite or countably infinite) state space $A$ that satisfies the following \textit{(time-homogeneous) Markov property}:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{split}
\mathbb{P}\{X_{n+1} = y \mid & X_0 = x_o,\ldots,X_n = x_n\}\\
& = \mathbb{P}\{X_{n+1} = y \mid X_n = x_n\}\\
& = \mathbb{P}\{X_{1} = y \mid X_0 = x_n\}.
\end{split}
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:45.028171+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dcd922cf67249d06e650dd81fcdf60d560b2ba6f86e19c21ca67a981e542cec9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 12 of arXiv:2606.06859, 'The rectangular finite free heat flow'] Let $p(x,t) = \prod_{i=1}^d (x - r_i(t)^2)$ be a polynomial of degree $d$ with nonnegative real roots that satisfies the partial differential equation
\begin{equation} 
{\partial_t} p(x,t) = -2\left( x {\partial_x^2} + (n+1) {\partial_x} \right) p(x,t).
\end{equation}
Then the Cauchy transform of the empirical root distribution \[
G(x,t) = \frac{1}{d}\sum_{i=1}^d \frac{1}{x - r_i(t)^2}
\]
satisfies the partial differential equation
\begin{equation} 
\partial_t G + 2 \partial_x \left( d x G^2 + (n+1) G + x \partial_x G \right) = 0.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:55:37.884095+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dcd93d1e0544ea1daffa434acc5b35fbe112c6cee144e6db065c9a762aa7ef6d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lm:L_convergence of arXiv:2605.09377, 'A factorization formula for the partition function in the semi-discrete parabolic Anderson'] For an inverse temperature $\beta \leq \beta_*$, the following is true: There is $\theta = \theta(\sigma) > 0$, independent of $\beta$, such that
