{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:00:15.225315+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dcf2b702c4cdd6fe98d539592fadb8c1e0ace4d50ad37949409d659f9877f9fd","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 5 (Airy convolution identity) of arXiv:2607.18619, 'Chernoff's Density Is Strongly Log-Concave'] Define the nonnegative integrable kernel
\begin{equation}
    r(u):=2u\sum_{j=1}^\infty e^{-\lambda_j u},
    \qquad u>0.
\end{equation}
Then, for every $x\in\R$,
\begin{equation}
    \int_0^\infty r(u)g(x+u)\dd u
      =x^2g(x)+\frac12g'(x).
\end{equation}
Consequently, if $g$ is log-concave and
$\curvg=-(\log g)''$, then
\begin{equation}
    \curvg(x)\ge4x, \qquad x\in\R.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

