{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:37.355331+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dcfeacd56dd90e7324a4aaa0de9aef29cd357b9d0547cf62a5843448fb625dcd","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 32 of arXiv:2606.17501, 'Critical spectral behavior and large deviations for geometric $\alpha$-stable processes'] \la{prop:Oshima-ineq}
  Let $h$ be the strictly positive continuous bounded ground state in \eqref{eq:22}. 
  There exists a positive function $g \in L^{1}(h^{2} dx)$ and a non-negative function
  $\psi \in C_{0}^{\infty}(\bR^{d})$ with $\int_{\bR^{d}}^{ } \psi h^{2} dx = 1$ such that
  for any $u \in \cF_{e}^{\alpha}$, 
  \begin{align}

    &\hspace{-1cm}
      \int_{\bR^{d}}^{ } \left| u(x) - h(x) L \left( \frac{u}{h} \right) \right| g(x) h(x) dx \\
    &\le C \mathcal{E}^{-\theta_{c} \mu}(u,u)^{1/2} 
    = C \left( \cE^{\alpha}(u,u) -
      \theta_{c} \int_{\bR^{d}}^{ } u(x)^{2} d\mu \right)^{1/2}
  \end{align}
  where $L(u)$ is a linear functional defined by 
  \begin{align}

    L(u) = \int_{\bR^{d}}^{ } u(x) \psi(x) h(x)^{2} dx.
  \end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:30:50.725850+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dd005d357577012699b1137c040e0a77b7b1448c8c1ea8f0c612f05e321bf454","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm111 of arXiv:2609.03091, 'Perfect numbers, Wieferich primes and the solutions of $\binom{2n}{n}\equiv 2^n \bmod n$'] Let $n=2^k p$, where $p$ is an odd prime and $k\in \mathbb{N}$, then $n$ is solution of the equation
\begin{equation} 
\binom{2n}{n} \equiv 2^n \bmod n
\end{equation}
if and only if $p$ satisfies the following conditions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item [a)] $p$ divides $\binom{2^{k+1}}{2^k}-2^{2^k}$;
    \item [b)] $p$ has at least $k$ digits $1$'s in its binary expansion.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:25:49.877465+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dd0076565ee577e7cd607270a9c255b1dffd6b55cb17fc4f34655ed27a1d1ce3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 7 of arXiv:2609.02065, '\textbf{From Bowditch's question to Goldman's conjecture for type-preserving representatio'] \cite[Theorem 1.1, Theorem 1.8]{ryu} 
