{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:11:11.707724+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dd76e959212f7c5ac1588f453b4da7c345f090ad0b558d9623a9dfb096cf3ec2","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:flat-milne-comp of arXiv:2609.00698, 'Kinetic Wedge Layers and Diffusive Limit of Neutron Transport in Polygonal Domains'] Fix $0<\kappa_{\rm in}<1$. Let $h\in\mathcal H_+$ and $F\in X_{\kappa_{\rm in}}$. For every $0<\kappa_{\rm out}<\kappa_{\rm in}$ there is a unique pair
\begin{align}
 E&\in\mathbb R,
 \qquad f^{\rm dec}\in X_{\kappa_{\rm out}},
 \qquad f:=E+f^{\rm dec},

\end{align}
for which $f$ is a bounded mild solution of (equation eq:milne-general-comp of the paper). As a consequence $f(\eta,\cdot)\to E$ uniformly in velocity at every smaller exponential rate. Here the uniqueness refers to the class (equation eq:Milne-output-class-rigorous of the paper).

The end state and the decaying part are the values of the end-state operator and of the solution operator,
\begin{align}
 \mathcal E_\mu(h,F)&:=E,
 \qquad
 \mathfrak M_\mu(h,F):=f^{\rm dec}.

\end{align}
They are linear, and they obey the mapping estimate
\begin{align}
 \abs{\mathcal E_\mu(h,F)}
 +\nm{\mathfrak M_\mu(h,F)}_{X_{\kappa_{\rm out}}}
 &\le C_{\kappa_{\rm out},\kappa_{\rm in}}
 \left(\nm h_{\mathcal H_+}+\nm F_{X_{\kappa_{\rm in}}}\right).

\end{align}

Both operators also inherit regularity in a parameter. Let $I$ be an interval and $q\in\mathbb N_0$, and assume that
\begin{align}
 h&\in C^q(I;\mathcal H_+),
 \qquad
 F\in C^q(I;X_{\kappa_{\rm in}}).

\end{align}
Then $\mathcal E_\mu(h,F)\in C^q(I)$ and $\mathfrak M_\mu(h,F)\in C^q(I;X_{\kappa_{\rm out}})$, and for $0\le a\le q$ the parameter derivatives pass inside the operators:
\begin{align}
 \partial_s^a\mathcal E_\mu(h,F)
 &=\mathcal E_\mu(\partial_s^ah,\partial_s^aF),\qquad
 \partial_s^a\mathfrak M_\mu(h,F)
 =\mathfrak M_\mu(\partial_s^ah,\partial_s^aF).

\end{align}
If, in addition, $0<\gamma<1$ and the stronger hypothesis
\begin{align}
 h&\in C^{q,\gamma}(I;\mathcal H_+),
 \qquad
 F\in C^{q,\gamma}(I;X_{\kappa_{\rm in}})

\end{align}
holds, then the two outputs belong respectively to $C^{q,\gamma}(I)$ and $C^{q,\gamma}(I;X_{\kappa_{\rm out}})$, with the corresponding boundedness estimate. Both conclusions hold with one-sided parameter regularity at an endpoint.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:20:15.701304+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dd7a1a43e94cbe87fb88851ea5d98d5e72977516cc0817a7e5e1e2b86231346c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lm:predual and ideal of arXiv:2609.00970, 'The unique predual problem for Lipschitz spaces, revisited'] Let $Y\subset\Lip_0(M)^*$ be a predual of $\Lip_0(M)$. Let $N\subset M$ be such that $0\in N$ and suppose that
