    \langle f, \xi_2(t)\rangle &= \langle f, \gamma\rangle + \langle f, \alpha(\psi(w_1)(t))\rangle + \langle f, \beta(t)\rangle - \int_0^t \langle f\times \iota, \xi_2(s)\rangle ds ,
\end{align}
for all $f\in \Lipbar{\mathbb{R}, L}(\mathbb{S})$, where $b_1\in \mathbb{R}_-$, $\gamma\in \mathcal{M}_f$, $y_1\in \mathbb{D}_\mathbb{R}[0,\infty)$, $c\in \mathbb{G}_{\bar{\mathbb{S}}}[0,\infty)$,  $\alpha\in \Lip{\mathcal{M}_f, L}[0,\infty)$ and $\beta\in \mathbb{D}_{\mathcal{M}_s}[0,\infty)$ are given such that $|\alpha|_{TV,t}^*<\infty$ and $|\beta|_{TV,t}^*<\infty$ for all $t\geq 0$. Then (equation eq:reflected_equation1 of the paper)-(equation eq:reflected_equation2 of the paper) has a unique solution $(w_1, \xi_2)\in \mathbb{D}_{\mathbb{R}\times\mathcal{M}_s}[0,\infty)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:49.288321+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_de461ed19cec78990f9e1cd639a2896614a4dc1a325f418e28b37d3545da4dc0","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 34 (L\'{e}pingle's inequality) of arXiv:2606.22964, 'Signature McKean--Vlasov stochastic differential equations'] For every $q\in[1,\infty)$, there exists a constant $C_q<\infty$ such that, for every $p>2$, and every martingale $M:[0,\infty)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^d$, we have
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathbb{E}\left[\sup_D\left(\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} \Big\lVert M_{t_{i+1}}-M_{t_i}\Big\rVert^p_{\mathbb{R}^d}\right)^{q/p}\right] \leq C_q\frac{p}{p-2}\mathbb{E}\left[\lVert M\rVert^q_{\infty}\right]
    \end{equation*}
    where $D$ is a partition of $[0,\infty)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:48:43.550677+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_de496b18c072a6159a0e0c8e0999615f75cd399fac34c293c089ea8b43887652","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 (Iterative oriented graph process) of arXiv:2608.07239, 'Unified framework for asymptotically uniform iterative construction of generalised random '] Consider the vertex set $V=\{v_1,\ldots,v_n\}$, consider degree sequences $\d=(d_1,\ldots,d_n)$ and $\d'=(d_1',\ldots,d_{n}')$ of non-negative integers with $m:=d_1+\ldots+d_n=d_1'+\ldots+d_{n}'>0$, and consider a set $X$ of forbidden arcs.
		\begin{itemize}
			\item Start with no edges $E_0=\emptyset$.
			\item For $r=0,\ldots,m-1$, let $d_{i,r}$ and $d_{i,r}'$ denote the out- and in-degrees of vertex $v_i$ in $(V,E_r)$, and consider those pairs of vertices $(v_i,v_j)\in V^2$ with $i\neq j$, $d_{i,r}<d_i$, $d_{j,r}'<d_j'$, $(v_i,v_j)\not\in E_r\cup X$, and $(v_j,v_i)\not\in E_r$.
			\begin{itemize}
				\item If no such pairs exist, let $E_{r+1}=E_r$.
				\item Otherwise, choose such a pair $(v_i,v_j)$ at random with probability proportional to $(d_i-d_{i,r})(d_j'-d_{j,r}')(1-\tfrac{d_id_j'}{2m})$, and let $E_{r+1}=E_r\cup\{(v_i,v_j)\}$.
			\end{itemize}
			\item Finally, apply rejection sampling to sample $E=E_m$ with $m$ arcs.
		\end{itemize}
		This guarantees that $(V,E)$ is an oriented graph with out- and in-degree sequences $\d$ and $\d'$ and with no arcs from $X$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:51:15.032996+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_de4a3b0c4c3d663b15828b2c4c9a911da319e3711878f94b98e6f88146d906c8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6tdiv2 of arXiv:2609.03926, 'Overcolored Partition $k$-tuples Restricted by Parity of the Parts'] Let $n$ be a non-negative integer and let $j,r,s,k,\alpha,m\ge 1$ be integers with $gcd(2,m)=1$. If $r=2^\alpha m$ or $k=2^\alpha m$, and $2^j>rk$, then there exists an integer $t\ge0$ such that for every $u\ge 1$ and distinct primes $p_1,p_2, \dots, p_{t+u}$ greater than $3$, we have
	\begin{align*}
		\bar{b}^k_{r,s}\left(\dfrac{p_1\cdots p_{t+u}\cdot n}{24}\right)\equiv 0\pmod{2^j},
	\end{align*}
	for every $n\ge 1$ coprime to $p_1,p_2, \dots, p_{t+u}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:45.032781+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_de4c9dc0a5279ddfa2b6217c0ba248aabfc29074533a365d2f54b0256782712f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 21 of arXiv:2606.07290, 'Boundary behaviour of the Volterra square-root process'] For $\gamma \in (0,1]$, the function space $S_{L, \gamma}$ consists of all functions $f \in C_{\mathrm{loc}}^{\gamma}(0, T] \cap L^1(0, T)$ such that $L \ast f \in C^1(0,T])$. For $f \in S_{L,\gamma}$, the generalized fractional derivative is defined for $t \in (0, T]$ as:
