\end{align}
then $z_\cdot\in M_\tau^p(0;{\mathbb R}^{d})$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:35:19.871702+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df2fbceee39ccdc4edcc30d353f0a85875e3e7b2cab43acd3a27aa262b228d6b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:fully_linear_val_sec2 of arXiv:2608.24963, 'Why and When Neural Networks Improve Local Approximation in Optimization'] A model $m_k$ is said to be \emph{fully linear} on $\Ball{x_k}{\Delta_k}$ if there exist constants $\kappa_f,\kappa_g>0$, independent of $k$, such that for all $x\in\Ball{x_k}{\Delta_k}$,
\begin{equation}
    |f(x)-m_k(x)| \le \kappa_f\Delta_k^2,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
    \|\nabla f(x)-\nabla m_k(x)\| \le \kappa_g\Delta_k.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:50:27.006811+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df2fe780eb65d95327aef1525b8dc7e983bb58428a3f8d08f02af938db0b5ce4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm thm7a of arXiv:2505.11700, 'Distribution of the cokernels of determinantal row-sparse matrices'] Let $k \ge 3$ be an odd integer, $k_n=k$ for all $n$ and $r$ be a positive integer. Then for all sufficiently large $n$, 
\begin{equation} 
\mathbb{P}(\dim_{\mathbb{F}_2} \ker \overline{A_n} \ge r) 
\ge \frac{1}{4r!} \left ( \frac{2(k-1)}{e^{k-1}} \right )^r.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:18:27.577868+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df319b8b4e48a8631a48e2d23d7274944eb12f9f5886d8020995edbf652df15f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Def 41 of arXiv:2510.23423, 'One-arm exponents of the high-dimensional Ising model'] Let $\n\in \Omega_{G_\fg}$ and $x,y\in V_\fg$.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[$(i)$] We say that $x$ is \emph{connected} to $y$ in $\n$ and write $x\overset{\n}{\longleftrightarrow} y$, if there is a sequence of points $x_0=x,x_1,\ldots, x_\ell=y$ such that $\n_{x_ix_{i+1}}>0$ for $0\leq i \leq \ell-1$.
    \item[$(ii)$] The \emph{cluster} of $x$ in $\n$, denoted by $\mathbf{C}_\n(x)$, is the set of points connected to $x$ in $\n$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:32:11.122679+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df33388b51db93395362b4652b66a32c41abe1886666b5421c5fc4b92f2dfe68","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem th:mainSPECTRUMgraph of arXiv:2510.00748, '\bf On irreducible central limit theorems'] Let $Z_n$ be as in (equation eq:bilformsubsec of the paper), and let $G_n=(V_n,\mathcal{E}_n)$ be its associated graph. Suppose that there exists $k\ge 2$ such that, as $n\to \infty$,
