{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:47.655930+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df4ee651b4a3d686bb7ad80dfb8b09280f1875c3038bc0194abc72e21cee8234","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 2 of arXiv:2605.07411, '\bf {A note on the equivalence of super-Poincar\'e inequality}'] The following statements hold.
\begin{itemize}
\item [(1)] Suppose that SPI \eqref{e1-1} holds with associated rate function
$\beta_{{\rm SP}}(\cdot)$ and the following weak Poincar\'e inequality holds,
\begin{equation}
\mu(f^2)-\mu(f)^2\le \beta_{{\rm WP}}(s)\E(f,f)+s\|f\|_\infty^2,\ s>0,\ f\in \D(\E),
\end{equation}
where $\beta_{{\rm WP}}:\R_+ \to \R_+$ is non-increasing.
Then $\mu$ satisfies the WLSI \eqref{e1-3}
with the rate function $\beta_{{\rm WL}}:\R_+ \to \R_+$ as
\begin{equation}
\beta_{{\rm WL}}(s):=
\begin{cases}
C_1\inf\left\{\sup_{n_0\le n \le k}n\xi_1(\delta^{-n+1});
\ k\ge n_0\ {\rm satisfies}\ C_2k\delta^{-k}\le s\right\},\ &s\in (0,s_0],\\
\beta_{{\rm WL}}(s_0),\ &s\in (s_0,+\infty).
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Here $C_1$, $C_2$, $s_0$, $\delta>2$, $n_0\ge 2$ are some positive constants,
\begin{equation}
\xi_1(t):=\inf\left\{\frac{r}{1-t\beta_{{\rm SP}}(r)};r>0\ {\rm such\ that}\ 1-t\beta_{{\rm SP}}(r)>0\right\},\ t>0,
\end{equation}
and we use the convention that $\inf \emptyset=-\infty$.

\medskip
\item [(2)] Suppose that WLSI \eqref{e1-3} holds with associated rate function $\beta_{{\rm WL}}(\cdot)$,
and there exist $\theta>0$, $\delta\ge 2$ such that
\begin{equation}
\lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{\beta_{{\rm WL}}\left(\delta^{-n}n^{-\theta}\right)}{n}=0,
\end{equation}
then
the SPI \eqref{e1-1} is true with the following rate function,
\begin{equation}
\beta_{{\rm SP}}(s)=
\begin{cases}
C_3\inf\left\{\delta^k; k\ge n_0\ {\rm\ such\ that}\ \sup_{n\ge k}\frac{\beta_{{\rm WL}}\left(\delta^{-n}n^{-\theta}\right)}{n}\le s\right\},
\ &\ s\in (0,s_0],\\
\beta_{{\rm SP}}(s_0),\ &\ s\in (s_0,+\infty),
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $C_3$, $n_0$, $s_0$ are some positive constants.
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:23.321436+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df53252fd733b3c864189d6ad1ec23024c7fc38019648f35dfb2190f8d73c3ec","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 17 of arXiv:2506.21087, 'Quasi-Stationary Distributions of Interacting Dynamical Systems and their approximation'] Assume \ref{condhzero}, \ref{condhun} and \ref{condhquatre}. Then, 
    \begin{itemize}
    \item[(i)]
     \begin{equation}
        \sup_{\mu,\alpha}\|\Phi^{\alpha}_{t}(\mu)-\mu^\star_\alpha\|_{\TV}\overset{t\to+\infty}{\longrightarrow}0.
    \end{equation}
    \item[(ii)] For any initial condition $\nu_0\in {\cal P}(\MM)$, there exists a probability ${\cal I}_{\nu_0}$ such that the unique solution  to $\dot{\nu}=-\nu+\Pi_\nu$ converges to ${\cal I}_{\nu_0}$ as $t\rightarrow+\infty$ for the $TV$-distance. Furthermore, the convergence is uniform in the initial condition:
    $$\sup_{\nu_0\in{\cal P}(\MM)}\|\nu_t^{\nu_0}-{\cal I}_{\nu_0}\|_{TV}\xrightarrow{t\rightarrow+\infty}0.$$
    \end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:58:40.869784+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df539bf9f0252a02ac144cfa3ef383e88cf46a095e2c61497794bbf71c5ea68d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:cdtn-proper-zcoup of arXiv:2605.10491, 'Zero-couplings of infinite measures with cyclically monotone support and multivariate regu'] Let $\mu, \nu \in \mathrm{M}_{0}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ and assume
    \begin{equation}

    \forall y \in \operatorname{spt}\nu\setminus\left\{0\right\}, 
    \exists \varepsilon >0: \qquad
    \mu\left(H_+(y,\varepsilon)\right)=\infty
    .
    \end{equation}
    Then every $\gamma \in \Gamma_{0,\mathrm{m}}(\mu,\nu)$ is proper, i.e., $\Gamma_{0,\mathrm{m}}(\mu,\nu) = \Gamma_{0,\mathrm{m}}^{\mathrm{p}}(\mu,\nu)$, and therefore also $\Gamma_{0,\mathrm{cm}}(\mu,\nu) = \Gamma_{0,\mathrm{cm}}^{\mathrm{p}}(\mu,\nu)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:39:18.520265+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df564e87c9db75d0b6ff8b5fac0738e695174a5eddd0d1b9f8fd73d192d78cb5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theoremd 4 of arXiv:2609.02267, 'Locally Conformally Flat Manifolds with Positive Scalar Curvature: Kleinian Groups, Moduli'] Let $M^n$ be a connected, closed, oriented smooth $n$-manifold. Denote by $X(M^n)$ the space of locally conformally flat metrics with positive scalar curvature on $M^n$, and by $\mathcal{M}(M^n)$ its moduli space. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] If $n = 3$ and either $\pi_1(M^3)$ is finite or $M^3$ is diffeomorphic to $S^2 \times S^1$, then both $X(M^3)$ and $\mathcal{M}(M^3)$ are contractible.
\item[(ii)] If $n \ge 4$ and $M^n$ is homeomorphic to a spherical space form but not diffeomorphic to $S^n$, then $\mathcal{M}(M^n)$ is either empty or contractible.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:01:54.283966+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df580d7c27c8669fee41783127bee22fdbba13caefee34246d628c183cb5ac47","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem cor of arXiv:2411.01731, '$L^2$-Wasserstein contraction of modified Euler schemes for SDEs with high diffusivity and'] Assume that $(i)$ $({\bf A}_0)$ with $\ell_0=0$, $({\bf A}_{4})$ and $({\bf A}_{5})$ with $\ell_0^\star=0$ for the EM scheme (equation WW of the paper);   $(ii)$ $({\bf A}_0)$, $({\bf A}_2)$ and $({\bf A}_{3})$ for the TEM scheme (equation EW0- of the paper);   $(iii)$ $({\bf A}_0)$, $({\bf A}_{4})$ and $({\bf A}_{5})$ for the PEM scheme (equation EWW of the paper). Then, there exist constants $C_0,\sigma_0,\lambda>0, \delta_2^{\star}\in(0,1]$ such that for all  $\delta\in(0,\delta_2^{\star}]$,   $\mu\in \mathscr P_2(\mathds R^d)$, and the noise intensity $\sigma$ satisfying $|\sigma|\ge\sigma_0,$
\begin{align}
\mathbb{W}_{2}\big(\pi_\8,\pi_\8^{(\delta)}\big)\le  C_0 \delta^{ \gamma_\star}  d^{\ell_\star},
\end{align}
and
\begin{align}
\mathbb{W}_2(\mu P^{(\delta)}_{n\delta},\pi_\8)\le C_0 \big(\text{\rm{e}}^{-\lambda n\delta}	\mathbb{W}_2(\mu,\pi_\8)+\delta^{\gamma_\star}d^{\ell_\star}\big),
\end{align}
where $\pi_\8$ $($resp. $\pi_\8^{(\delta)}$$)$ is the unique IPM  of $(X_t)_{t\ge0}$ $($resp. $(X_{n\delta}^{\delta})_{n\ge0}$$)$, and
 $\gamma_\star=1,\ell_\star=1/2$  for the EM scheme (equation WW of the paper); $\gamma_\star=\gamma, \ell_\star=\ell_0+1/2$ for the TEM scheme (equation EW0- of the paper); $\gamma_\star=1,\ell_\star=(\ell_0+1+{\ell_0^\star}/2)\vee((\ell_0+1)/2+\ell_0/\gamma)$ for the PEM scheme (equation EWW of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:06:18.009654+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df59e19dd46621f64e579c6e81bf0ae1ded6a68fe7a1473a9b4b30789e43cde7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 5 (Tree contraction) of arXiv:2608.14802, 'Dynamical Gibbs-non-Gibbs transitions for finite-alphabet models on trees'] Let $(U,d)$ be a metric space. A function
$G:U^s\to U$ is an \underline{$s$-tree contraction} on $U$ if there exists
$\lambda\in[0,1)$ such that
\begin{equation}
d\left(G(x_1,\dots,x_s),G(y_1,\dots,y_s)\right)
\leq \lambda \max_{i=1,\dots,s} d(x_i,y_i)
\end{equation}
for all $x_1,\dots,x_s,y_1,\dots,y_s\in U$. The constant $\lambda$ is called the \underline{contraction constant}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:58.355379+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df5c40e4abae747e8abee991a9801facad1395336d3d8db64a63c1846cdccc7e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 19 of arXiv:2506.18132, 'Throughput in inhomogeneous planar drainage networks'] For all \(j \in \{0, 1\}\) and \(\alpha \in \big(1/2, 2/3\big) \), we have that
    \begin{align*}
        \P\Big( \A^{\ell}_{\tilde\tau_\ell + j} - \B^{\ell}_{\tilde\tau_\ell + j} 
        \geq \ell^{\alpha} \Big) 
        \rightarrow
        0, \qquad\text{ as } \ell\to\infty.
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:27:32.385630+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df6075eb50f9e83baa2cbe1d71345d7987d523a5014a64a3995d09f03b26302a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:couplingtight of arXiv:2509.23086, 'On Optimal Markovian Couplings of L\'evy Processes'] Let $\mathcal{M},\mathcal{N}\subset \Lambda(\mathbb R^d)$ be two $\Lambda$-tight families, and define
	\begin{align*}
		\Gamma^\Lambda(\mathcal{M},\mathcal{N}) = \cb{\gamma\in\Lambda(\mathbb R^{2d}) : \gamma \in\Gamma^\Lambda(\mu,\nu),\ \mu\in\mathcal{M},\ \nu\in\mathcal{N} }.
	\end{align*}
	That is, $\Gamma^\Lambda(\mathcal{M},\mathcal{N})$ is the family of all L\'evy couplings $\gamma$ of $\mu\in\mathcal{M},\ \nu\in\mathcal{N}$. Then $\Gamma(\mathcal{M},\mathcal{N})$ is $\Lambda$-tight in $\Lambda(\mathbb R^{2d})$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:12:19.484320+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df61d40f8c41238f52ecf0463fc1ad8a368cc26eb2eba1037d58af7683c43ecf","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 18 (Dense sampling of stable Ornstein--Uhlenbeck processes) of arXiv:2608.17927, 'Near-unit-root persistence of symmetric stable \\ autoregressive sequences'] Let $Y$ solve \eqref{eq:ou-sde}, with $\theta,\eta,x > 0$. For $\Delta > 0$, define its sampled persistence rate per unit physical time by
\begin{equation}

\Gamma_{\alpha,\theta}(\Delta) \,\deq\, -\lim_{n \to \infty}\,\frac{1}{n\Delta} \log{\PP_{x}(Y_{j\Delta} > 0,\ 1 \leq j \leq n)}.
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}

\lim_{\Delta \downarrow 0}\Gamma_{\alpha,\theta}(\Delta) = \theta G_{\alpha}^{*} \in (0,\alpha\theta/2].
\end{equation}
Moreover, for every $m\in\NN$,
\begin{equation}

\Gamma_{\alpha,\theta}(\Delta/m) \geq \Gamma_{\alpha,\theta}(\Delta).
\end{equation}
The upper endpoint $\alpha\theta/2$ is the continuous-time persistence rate in Proposition~\ref{prop:continuous-ou}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:45.026176+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df62089e7b3cfded02103650ed468d175b9c094f05f1fb0cb7569f7c507c72ef","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 18 (Stability in $b$) of arXiv:2606.06757, 'Path-Dependent Ergodic Optimal Control \\ and Backward Stochastic Differential Equations'] There exists a constant $C>0$ depending only on $\eta_1,\eta_2,\theta_0,\theta$ such that, for any $\xi\in\mathcal C_{\theta}^0$ and $K\in\K,$
\begin{equation*}
\begin{array}{cc}
     \displaystyle \int_0^{\infty} K(s)|\mathcal{X}^{\xi}_s- \tilde{\mathcal{X}}^{\xi}_s|_{\theta} \, ds\leq \Big(\int_0^{\infty} K(s)|\mathcal{X}^{\xi}_s- \tilde{\mathcal{X}}^{\xi}_s|_{\theta} \, ds\Big)^{\frac{1}{2}}\leq  C  \,  \Big(\int_0^{\infty} K(s)(\delta b(s))^2 \,ds\Big)^{\frac{1}{2}} \, , & \mathbb{P}-\text{a.s.}
\end{array}
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:08.764286+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df6210c4f09b498138353006f45fe11c5c9f39e76b1bbe45646c6ccffc82f7eb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem thm:paley-zygmund of arXiv:2411.18155, 'Besov regularity of random wavelet series'] Let $X\geq 0$ a random variable with $0<\mathds{E}[X^2]<\infty$, and let $\sigma\in [0,1]$.
Then
\begin{equation*}
\mathds{P}(X>\sigma \mathds{E}[X])\geq (1-\sigma)^2\frac{\mathds{E}[X]^2}{\mathds{E}[X^2]}.
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:17:04.328331+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df6254f8266ee49faf8a4fcf2495487dd5a21cba27f535283115087866c4afdc","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:equivalenceofensembles of arXiv:2606.01937, 'Stationary fluctuations for an exclusion process with mass and energy conservation'] Let $f:\Omega\longrightarrow\mathbb{R}$ be a local function whose support is included in $\{0,\hdots ,\ell_0-1\}$ for some $\ell_0\ge 1$. Then, there exists a constant $C_1=C_1(f)>0$ such that for any $\ell\ge\ell_0$, 
    \begin{equation}
        \sup_{\eta\in\Omega}\left| \mathbb{E}_\ell [f] - \left(\psi_f(\xi_0^\ell ,\eta_0^\ell ) - \frac{1}{2\ell }\mathrm{Tr}\big( \chi \mathrm{Hess}(\psi_f)\big) (\xi_0^\ell ,\eta_0^\ell)\right)\right| \le \frac{C_1}{\ell^2}.
    \end{equation}
    where $\chi$ is the compressibility matrix defined in (defin:compressibilitymatrix of the paper), and $\mathrm{Hess}(\psi_f)$ denotes the Hessian matrix of $\psi_f$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:50:44.870661+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df62c7dec6fe82e974e312642d2af8cb9ba847ff8c6bee357baf01516627e96f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 12 (Cut majorant) of arXiv:2607.16724, 'Algebraic Transfer for Operator-Valued Gaussian Chaoses: Oriented Schatten Profiles and Si'] Let $\Theta$ be an $N$-linear operation
\[
 \Theta:\prod_{j=1}^N\cK_{m_j}^{\mathrm{alg}}
 \longrightarrow\cK_q^{\mathrm{alg}}.
\]
For $1\le r\le\infty$, an $r$-cut majorant is a nonnegative array
\[
 \mathsf M_\Theta^{(r)}(S;T_1,\ldots,T_N),
 \qquad
 S\subset[q],\quad T_j\subset[m_j],
\]
such that
\begin{align}

 \norm{\Flat_S(\Theta(K_1,\ldots,K_N))}_{\Sch_r}
 \le
 \sum_{T_1,\ldots,T_N}
 \mathsf M_\Theta^{(r)}(S;T_1,\ldots,T_N)
 \prod_{j=1}^N\norm{\Flat_{T_j}(K_j)}_{\Sch_r}.
\end{align}
An operation admitting such an array is called $r$-cut-admissible.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:19:42.623648+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df64ade1def25c6fa86d600fbbfb7da3574b8dcfb1661c79934d42a38db5c475","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 22 of arXiv:2609.03512, ''] Let $g(G)=3$ and $r(G)=5$. Then $\gamma(G)=\gamma_t(G)=2$ if and only if $G$ belongs to one of the following classes:
