{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:24:20.957527+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dfe455105a1705652fe3981da6851b99921d3647e465bf713dbe20cd387e0e83","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition ParisiFormula:NKP of arXiv:2508.12464, 'On the Fitness Landscape in the $\NK$ Model'] We have that for any $\beta>0,$ 
	$
	\lim_{l\to\infty}\mathbb{E} F_{{\text{\tiny MS}},l}^p(\beta)
	$
	exists.
	Furthermore, 
	\begin{align*}
		\lim_{l\to\infty}\mathbb{E} F_{{\text{\tiny MS}},l}^p(\beta)&=\ln 2+\frac{\beta^2}{2},\,\,\forall 0<\beta\leq \beta_p,\\
		\lim_{l\to\infty}\mathbb{E} F_{{\text{\tiny MS}},l}^p(\beta)&\geq \ln2+\beta\beta_p-\frac{\beta_p^2}{2},\,\,\forall \beta>\beta_p,
	\end{align*}
	where $\beta_p>0$ is defined as \begin{align}
		\beta_p^2=\inf_{0<u<1}(1+u^{-p})I(u)
	\end{align}
	for
	\begin{align*}
		I(u):=\frac{1}{2}\bigl((1+u)\ln (1+u)+(1-u)\ln (1-u)\bigr),\,\,\forall u\in [0,1].
	\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:52:17.581049+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dfe921ab4ea87ddafa7b5c26d401f3d694eef11f1b0a3678924cce68de6ca461","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma ConR of arXiv:2411.15485, '\bf\Large Stochastic Volterra Equations for Local Times of Spectrally Positive L\'evy Proc'] For each $\beta\geq 0$ and $y\geq 0$, we have  as $n\to\infty$, 
  \begin{eqnarray*}
  \int_0^t e^{-\beta s}R_{\varPi_n}(ns) \,ds \overset{\rm u.c.}\to \int_0^t e^{-\beta s} W'(s)\, ds
  \quad \mbox{and}\quad 
  \int_0^t e^{-\beta s} R_{n}(ns,y)\, ds \overset{\rm u.c.}\to y\cdot \int_0^t e^{-\beta s} W'(s)\, ds.
  \end{eqnarray*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:09:59.187097+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dfecb00ffb8373dca069f85f6fa62f4dff4190e038de6f651529ad86d9e6f270","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:root-perturb-short of arXiv:2608.05123, 'A central limit theorem for the random assignment problem'] There are weights \(\omega_i^{\rm root}\ge0\), summing to one and
satisfying \(\omega_i^{\rm root}\le1/(N_i+1)\), such that
\begin{equation}
 \frac{\mathcal K_{n,M}^{(0)}}{\mathcal T_n^{(0)}}
 =\sum_i\omega_i^{\rm root}\min\left\{\frac{MN_i}{n},1\right\}.
\end{equation}
Consequently
\begin{equation}
 0\le1-\frac{\mathcal K_{n,M}^{(0)}}{\mathcal T_n^{(0)}}
 \le\sum_{N_i<n/M}\frac1{N_i}.
\end{equation}
For fixed \(M\),
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal K_{n,M}-\mathcal K_{n,M}^{(0)}\longrightarrow0
\end{equation}
in probability under \(\mathbb P_n^{\mathrm{ref}}\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:48:49.902104+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dfeea4ae3a03a824c55cb62e47e16f7cfbc1bed61ffddf8b1f0d58db4da9ff0b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Multi-species 2-spin model of arXiv:2510.26960, 'Free energy fluctuations in SK and related spin glass models: \\ A literature survey'] Fix $k\geq 1$. Let $\Lambda = \diag(\alpha_1,\dots, \alpha_k)$ be a matrix satisfying $\alpha_i\in (0,1)$ for all $i$ and $\sum_{i=1}^k \alpha_i=1$. Let $\Delta^2$ be a $k\times k$ symmetric matrix of non-negative entries. 
    A multi-species model with species density matrix $\Lambda$ and variance profile matrix $\Delta^2$ satisfies the following assumptions.
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Given $N \geq k$, there is a partition of spins $\{1,2,\dots, N\} = \cup_{s=1}^k I_s$, where the species density
        \[
        \Lambda_N:=\frac1N\diag(|I_1|,...,|I_k|) \quad \text{satisfies} \quad \lim_{N\to\infty}\Lambda_N=\Lambda.
        \]
        \item Let $(g_{ij})_{1 \leq i, j\leq N}$ be independent centered Gaussian variables such that $\mathbb{E}[g_{ij}^2] = \Delta^2_{s,t}$ for $i\in I_s$ and $j\in I_t$. The Hamiltonian of the model, at each spin configuration $\sigma\in \Sigma_N$, is defined as
         \begin{equation*} 
        H_N(\sigma) = \frac1{\sqrt N}\sum_{i,j=1}^N g_{ij}\sigma_i\sigma_j.
         \end{equation*} 
    \end{itemize}
    Here, $\Sigma_N = \{\pm 1\}^N$ in the case of Ising spins and is the product of spheres $S_{|I_1|-1}  \times  \dots \times S_{|I_k|-1}$ in the case of spherical model.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:57:59.877750+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dff83e25a096a70e486a5a3f77364e73b128457194c7fcaba5d2bdd6d05a1187","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemmaprobsimple of arXiv:2607.06952, 'Universality for cokernels of partially random integral matrices'] Let $M$ be as in Theorem (thmmoments of the paper) or Theorem (thmmomentsband of the paper). Let $F \in \Sur_{\{n_k\},r}(V,G)$ with $\lambda >1$. Then, there exists a constant $K >0$ which is independent of $n$ and $\{n_k\}$ and $r$ such that \begin{equation}
        \mathbb{P}(FM = 0) \leq K |H_1|^{-n_1}|H_2|^{-n_2}\cdots|H_{s}|^{-n_s}(1-\varepsilon)^r.
    \end{equation}
Furthermore, if $M$ is as in Theorem (thmmoments of the paper), define $D_1,D_2,\theta$ using Definition (defkey of the paper). Then 
\begin{equation}
        \mathbb{P}(FM = 0) \leq K \left(\frac{D_1}{|G|}\right)^{\theta n}\left(\frac{D_2}{|G|}\right)^{(1-\theta)n}(1-\varepsilon)^r.
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:38.642506+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dff84b2e952504356a2286fb10e1c9652130a44081438d90646c7945e9aed17b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2506.18063, 'Reduced critical branching processes in non-favorable random environment \thanks{ This wor'] If Conditions B1 and B2 are valid, then
\begin{equation*}
