{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:45:10.068120+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e060934d6634f976f036e087a096f8f302b7d00a9bce67883d43521800fc2a7b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition wave kernel of arXiv:2504.14457, 'Abstract'] The fundamental solution of wave equation in dimension $d$ is given by
\begin{align}

p_w(t,x)=\left\{ \begin{array}{ll}
\frac{1}{2}\mathbbm{1}_{\{|x|<t\}}     & d=1, \\\rule{0ex}{1.5em}
\frac{1}{2\pi}\frac{1}{\sqrt{t^2-|x|^2}} \mathbbm{1}_{\{|x|<t\}}    & d=2,\\\rule{0ex}{1.5em}
\frac{1}{4\pi t}\sigma_t& d=3.
\end{array}\right.
\end{align}
Here $\sigma_t$ is the surface measure on $\partial \mathbf{B}(0,t)$. We notice that for all $d$,
\begin{align}

\displaystyle{\int_{\mathbb{R}^d}p_w(t,x)\,dx=t}.
\end{align}
For $d\geq 4$, $p_w(t,x)$ is not a function, but a Schwarz distribution, it will not satisfy one of our assumption ,hence the case will not be covered in this dissertation. The Fourier transform of the wave kernel is :
\begin{align}

    \hat{p}_w(t,\xi)=\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} e^{-i\xi x}p_w(t,x)dx=\frac{\sin(t|\xi|)}{|\xi|}.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:31:49.841394+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e061127f725463052bcc7efd9752754dd8ac130482ab87f431ac72065693fc41","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 4 of arXiv:2506.13661, 'Box-Covariances of Hyperuniform Point Processes'] Let $\eta$ be a stationary point process with truncated pair correlation measure $\beta.$ It holds that 
