{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:31:14.339041+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e10517b366a4205e6be9c50f2a12410a00941266cd582bc2024bc5b10fd6c03a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition Prop: upper bound for X epsilon of arXiv:2606.19207, 'Extrema of cooling branching Brownian motion and related Gaussian fields'] Fix $\alpha\in(0,1/2]$. For $\varepsilon\in(0,1)$, set $T:=\log(1/\varepsilon)$ and let $X_\varepsilon^\alpha$ be the Gaussian field defined in (equation eqModel: X epsilon alpha white noise integral of the paper). There exist constants $C,c>0$ and $T_*,\lambda_*>0$ such that
