{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:58.446298+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e12db37f6530861b762bb8278b76dcc35b6cd3433243da02fb049287d128e631","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition XY height function on a percolation configuration of arXiv:2604.24743, 'The impact of disorder and non-convex interactions on delocalisation of height functions'] Given an integer $L \in \ensuremath{\mathbb{N}}$, an integer $n\geq 2$, a parameter $\lambda > 0$, two inverse temperatures $\beta_1 , \beta_2 \geq 0$ and a percolation configuration $r \in \{0,1\}^{\Lambda_L},$ we define
  \begin{equation*}
     \mu_{L , n ,\beta_1, \beta_2 , \lambda, r}^{\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}} - \mathrm{XY}} (\left\{ \varphi \right\}) := \frac{1}{Z_{\Lambda_L,J, r}^{\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}}-\mathrm{XY}} }  I_{\left(\varphi(0)\right)} \left( \lambda \right)\prod_{x \sim y}  \left[ I_{\left(\varphi(x) - \varphi(y)\right)} \left( r_x \beta_1 \right) I_{\left(\varphi(x) - \varphi(y)\right)} \left( r_y \beta_1 \right) I_{\left(\varphi(x) - \varphi(y)\right)} \left(  n \beta_2 \right)^{n-2} \right].
 \end{equation*}
 We denote the variance associated with this measure by $\mathrm{Var}_{L , n ,\beta_1, \beta_2 , \lambda, r}^{\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}} - \mathrm{XY}}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:35:04.164786+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e12ed71b11faee49a5780250db861e1d1262d39dbe93bb3c2cf85ddf40560f3b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 40 of arXiv:2608.30059, 'Multihomogeneous Measures and Stochastic Polar Representations'] The function \(K_\nu\) is a finite positive semidefinite kernel on
\(G\times\mathsf T^q\) and
\begin{align}
K_\nu\bigl((ar,\boldsymbol h),(as,\boldsymbol k)\bigr)
&=
\chi_\alpha(a)^{-1}
K_\nu\bigl((r,\boldsymbol h),(s,\boldsymbol k)\bigr),
\\
K_\nu\bigl((r,\boldsymbol h),(s,\boldsymbol h)\bigr)
&=
p_{\boldsymbol h}\chi_\alpha(r\vee s)^{-1},

\end{align}
where \((r\vee s)_i=\max(r_i,s_i)\).  If \(\nu\) is
\(B\)-invariant, then
\begin{equation}

K_\nu\bigl((r,u\boldsymbol h),(s,u\boldsymbol k)\bigr)
=
K_\nu\bigl((r,\boldsymbol h),(s,\boldsymbol k)\bigr),
\qquad u\in\mathsf T.
\end{equation}

If \(Z\) is any stochastic representer of \(\nu\), then
\begin{equation}

K_\nu\bigl((r,\boldsymbol h),(s,\boldsymbol k)\bigr)
=
\E*{
\prod_{i=1}^q
\left[
\left(\frac{\gamma_{h_i,i}(Z)}{r_i}\right)^{\alpha_i}
\wedge
\left(\frac{\gamma_{k_i,i}(Z)}{s_i}\right)^{\alpha_i}
