{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:32:11.128518+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e1f12f953eaf5d048e33b0527115e90d69f087d6748f2b33e2195681b7b5ece0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 2q:moment of arXiv:2510.09818, 'On the Fourier dimension of fractional Brownian graphs'] [LL25].
Let $B$ be a fractional Brownian motion with Hurst parameter $H \in (0,1)$, and recall that the Fourier transform of $\mu_{G}$ is given by~(equation b1 of the paper). Then for any $q\in \mathbb N_+$ and $\xi_1, \xi_2 \in \mathbb R \setminus\{0\}$, we have
\begin{align}
	\mathbb E\left[ |\hat{\mu}_G(\xi_1,\xi_2)|^{2q} \right]
	= \frac{(q!)^2}{|\xi_2|^{2q/H}} \sum_{\varepsilon\in \mathcal{A}_{2q}} \mathcal{I}_{T}^{\lambda}[\varepsilon, G_\varepsilon],
\end{align}
where the parameters $\lambda, T$ and the function $G_{\varepsilon}$ are chosen as
\begin{align}
	\lambda = \frac{\xi_1}{|\xi_2|^{1/H}}, \qquad
	T = |\xi_2|^{1/H} ,\quad \text{ and }\quad
	G_\varepsilon(u) = e^{-\pi\mathrm{Var}(\sum_{i=1}^{2q} \varepsilon_i B_{u_i})}.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:19:00.873200+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e1f504cd6da278e67be60990daba3faf0089a5431c167a4c38808399ef89be60","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 25 (Coincidence-adjusted Ville bound / pooling benefit) of arXiv:2608.20337, 'Information on trajectories:\\ martingales and random times'] For $\alpha \in \Delta([W])$ and $x > 1$, let $\tau^{(\alpha)}_x := \inf\{t : M_t^{(\alpha)}
\geq x\}$ and write $g_x(\alpha) := \E\bigl[M_{\tau^{(\alpha)}_x}^{(\alpha)}\,
\ind\{\tau^{(\alpha)}_x < \infty\}\bigr]$ for the mass the geometric mixture
retains across level $x$.  Then
\begin{equation}
  \PP(\tau^{(\alpha)}_x < \infty)
  = \PP\!\left(\exists\,t : \prod_i \bigl(M_t^{(i)}\bigr)^{\alpha_i}
            \geq x\right)
  \leq \frac{g_x(\alpha)}{x}
  \leq \frac{1}{x},
\end{equation}
and the improvement over the plain Ville bound is the mass deficit $1 - g_x(\alpha) \geq 0$, strictly positive whenever the bettors disagree along the path. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:57:30.961110+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e1f9d093092e45d0beb8886663ea74cdc578583ddeff071b3b0edff261797b9d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 10 (Change of variable formula) of arXiv:2608.12225, 'Higher-order variation and\\ pathwise It\^o calculus on manifolds'] Let $p\in\mathbb N$, $p\geq 2$, $f\in C^p(M,\mathbb R)$, and let
$X\in V_p(M,\pi)$ be a continuous path with finite $p$-th
variation tensor $[X]_{\pi}^{(p)}$ along the sequence of partitions
