{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:12:08.988310+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2c87d5cf1e1ed10fe6699c82edd083c50336a88cfbd8362d03cb6421e343373","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem lem_rauchToponogovComparisonEstimate of arXiv:2608.17463, 'Persistence probabilities of fractional L\'evy fields indexed by hyperbolic space and othe'] Under the above assumptions on the manifold $\mathcal{M}$ there exists $r'>0$ and $\kappa', \mathcal{K}' \in\mathbb{R}$ with $\kappa'\leq\mathcal{K}'$, such that for any $\kappa\leq\kappa'$, any $\mathcal{K}'\leq\mathcal{K}$, any $0<r\leq\min\left\{r', \inj(\mathcal{M}_\mathcal{K})\right\}$, and any $\eta,\zeta\in\mathcal{B}_r^{\mathcal{M}}(O)$ we have
    \begin{align*}
        d_\mathcal{K}(\Psi_\mathcal{K}(\eta), \Psi_\mathcal{K}(\zeta)) \leq d(\eta, \zeta) \leq d_\kappa(\Psi_\kappa(\eta), \Psi_\kappa(\zeta)),
    \end{align*}
    where $d_\mathcal{K}(.,.)$ and $d_\kappa(.,.)$ are the shortest distance functions in the respective model spaces $\mathcal{M}_\mathcal{K}$ and $\mathcal{M}_\kappa$ and $\Psi_\mathcal{K}, \Psi_\kappa$ are the diffeomorphisms defined in (equation eq_exponentialTransferMap of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.344768+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2ca1384bf052b60305f773b727a70e34cb29a3baa1445a45f29ff031bef6375","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2411.05534, 'Inverse problems for stochastic partial differential equations'] We say the strongly measurable function $ f(\cdot) : \Omega \rightarrow \mathcal{X} $ is Bochner integrable with respect to $ \mu $ if there exists a sequence of Bochner integrable simple  functions $ \{ f_{i}(\cdot) \}_{i=1}^{\infty}    $ converging to $ f(\cdot) $ in $ X $, $ \mu $-a.e., such that 
    \begin{align*}
        \lim _{i, j \rightarrow \infty} \int_{\Omega}\left|f_i(s)-f_j(s)\right|_{\mathcal{X}} d \mu=0
        .
    \end{align*}
    For  $ E \in \mathcal{F} $, the Bochner integral of $ f(\cdot) $ over $ E $ is defined by 
    \begin{align*}
        \int_E f(s) d \mu=\lim _{i \rightarrow \infty} \int_{\Omega} \chi_E(s) f_i(s) d \mu(s) \quad \text { in } \mathcal{X} \text {. }
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:15:36.158483+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2cd24c64f8ab0d8bba296411240a2926cbc7974429a91c33ae0c13125464dc2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 15 (Energy equivalence) of arXiv:2609.01589, 'Uniform stability of expanding simple waves for Euler--Poisson--Boltzmann: a singular comp'] For $\eta$ and $\eps_0$ sufficiently small,
