{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.472033+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2e48a0c48cce064d2839f2421b1d609f8a912569039294816c2b6d31040515c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 43 (Completed same-field Wick map) of arXiv:2606.29292, 'Intrinsic Gaussian Tensor Geometry and Random Operator Spectra'] Fix $2\le r<\infty$.  The map $\operatorname{Dec}_m$ extends
isometrically from $\mathfrak W_{m,r}$ into
\[
  \mathfrak T_{m,r}
  (\cH_\C^{(1)},\ldots,\cH_\C^{(m)};\cC,\cE).
\]
For every $p\ge2$, the algebraic Wick map extends uniquely to
\begin{equation}

  \mathcal I_{m,p,r}:\mathfrak W_{m,r}
  \longrightarrow L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r(\cC,\cE))
\end{equation}
with
\begin{equation}

  \|\mathcal I_{m,p,r}(\mathbf K)\|_{L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)}
  \le m^{m/2}C_m(p+r)^{m/2}
  \|\mathbf K\|_{\mathfrak W_{m,r}}.
\end{equation}
For fixed $r$, the extensions are compatible across $p$.  More generally,
if $2\le r\le s<\infty$, $\mathbf K\in\mathfrak W_{m,r}$, and
$p,q\ge2$, then
\begin{equation}

  \mathcal I_{m,p,r}(\mathbf K)
  =\mathcal I_{m,q,s}(\iota^{\mathrm W}_{r,s}\mathbf K)
  \quad\text{in }L^0(\Omega;\cL(\cC,\cE)).
\end{equation}
At $p=r=2$,
\begin{equation}

  \|\mathcal I_{m,2,2}(\mathbf K)\|_{L^2(\Omega;\Sch_2)}
  =\sqrt{m!}\,\|\mathbf K\|_{\mathfrak W_{m,2}}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:36:16.003623+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2e58c6f1693e19616aa0f4890521e52bdf35eb27e8327f6f12b381bea323325","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition hull_well_defined of arXiv:2606.27357, '\textbf{Typical distances in high-genus triangulations}'] For any $\varepsilon > 0$ and $n \ge 1$, let us assume that the event $\mathbf{Iso}_{n}(\frac{\varepsilon}{2})$ occurs. Let us also fix $r \ge 0$ such that $|B_r^{+}| \le n^{1-\varepsilon}$. Then, we have $B_r^{\bullet} \neq T_{n,g_n}$. Moreover, if $K_{\theta}\log(n) \le |B_r^{\bullet}|$, then we have
\begin{align}
\big|\log(|B_r^{\bullet}|)-\log(|\partial^{*}B_r^{\bullet}|)\big| \le \log(3w_{\theta}^{-1}).
\end{align}
In particular, we have 
\[
\big|\log(|B_r^{+}|)-\log(|B_r^{\bullet}|)\big| \le \log(3w_{\theta}^{-1}).
\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:17:34.001446+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2e7940743ace8ffcb4268d6099be7d4a736b072c2e679139391c55a7af6d6b0","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 51 of arXiv:2609.03403, 'Hyperpositive functions, sector bounded functions and a trace formula'] The functions $S(z)$ and $\sigma(z)$ in  respectively \eqref{rep789} and \eqref{repre1} are related by
