{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:18:19.232522+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e3484460cd831065ac5d1bf17c53187fd933ddedeb406a06e009b731f63e991e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 59 of arXiv:2608.29086, '2608.29086'] [Vector field $\beta_{\sigma}$]
Given $M \subset H_0$ and edge-continuous $\sigma \in \Gamma(M)$. Then, a vector field $\beta_{\sigma} \in C^1(M)$ is obtained by
\begin{equation*}
\beta_{\sigma}(ab):= \Vert  e_{\sigma(abc)}(ab) \Vert \quad (ab \in C_1(M))),
\end{equation*}
where $abc \in Tri(M)$ is any triangle containing $ab$ as a side, and
\begin{equation*}
\Vert (x,y,z) \Vert :=x+y+z. 
\end{equation*}
 Since $\sigma$ is edge-continuous, the value dose not depend on the choice of $abc$. $\beta_{\sigma}$ is called the \textit{vector field defined by $\sigma$}.","status":"unverified"}

