{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:07:17.250140+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e47c3c19e6e2f76ddf39554ab41c2194eed065850e9cefdebf3169d2301fbfd9","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition ATTRACTOR of arXiv:2603.12774, 'Synchronization by noise for stochastic differential equations driven by fractional Browni'] Let $\Phi$ be an RDS. A random compact set $\mathcal{A}$ is { called pullback attractor} if 
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item [1)] $\mathcal{A}$ is invariant under \( \Phi \).
		\item [2)] For every compact set $K\subseteq  \mathcal{X}$ 
		we have
		\[ \lim\limits_{t\to\infty} \sup\limits_{x\in K} d(\Phi^t_{\theta_{-t}\omega}(x), \mathcal{A}(\omega)  )=0~~~\mathbb{P}-a.s. \]
	\end{enumerate}
	We call $\mathcal{A}$ a {weak attractor} if the convergence in 2) holds in probability instead of almost surely. The set $\mathcal{A}$ is called a {(weak) point attractor} if it satisfies the properties stated above, assuming that the compact set $K$ in condition 2) consists of a single point.~A (weak) point attractor is said to be {minimal} if it is contained in every other (weak) point attractor.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:52.735721+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e47d1b8f9fcf12e7db24c27b0fc9d35b4b7cddc8f730f097ef4ed9d350746c3b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo 15 of arXiv:2506.10213, 'Coupling of forward-backward stochastic differential equations on the Wiener space, and ap'] If the FBSDE \eqref{fbsde:equ:re}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item[(1)] satisfies Assumption \ref{coefficient:assume:general}, Assumption \ref{fbsde:basic:assume:p:general} with some $p\in [2,\fz)$ and Assumption \ref{ass:linear:diffusion};
        \item[(2)] admits a regular decoupling field $w$ with the Lipschitz constants $L_w$;
        \item[(3)] \eqref{p-condition} holds with $L_g$ replaced by $L_w$.
    \end{itemize}
    Let $\Tz := (X,Y,Z)$ be the solution of \eqref{fbsde:equ:re}.
    Then the estimation for $p$-coupling variance holds
    \begin{align}
        CV_p([t,T])
        &\leq c_{\eqref{equ:large:decoupling}} \left\{\E\left[|\xi - \xi^{\vz}|^p + |g(X_T) - g^{\vz}(X_T)|^p\right] \right.\notag\\
        &\quad \qquad \left. + \E\left[U_p([t,T])\right] + S_p([t,T])\right\},
    \end{align}
    where the constant $c_{\eqref{equ:large:decoupling}}$ only relies on $p$, $T - t$
    and all the Lipschitz constant in Assumption \ref{coefficient:assume:general},
    and $U_p([t,T])$ and $S_p([t,T])$ are defined as in Theorem \ref{thm:small:decoupling}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:54:15.659783+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e47f3a82dee410e64104e78011044643dc256a4d897dd45fafcad4fd173fa60a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2608.09700, 'A general paracontrolled ansatz for singular SPDEs'] We have the following identities (under Assumption 1).
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item For any $n_1,n_2\in\bfN^{d+1}$
			\begin{equation}
			D^{n_1} D^{n_2} = D^{n_1+n_2},  \quad 
			\downarrow^{n_1}\hspace{-1pt} \downarrow^{n_2}\hspace{-0pt} = \downarrow^{n_1+n_2}, \quad 
			\downarrow^{n_1}D^{n_2} = D^{n_2}\downarrow^{n_1}.
		\end{equation}
			\item  For any $\tau\in T \sqcup T^+$,  $\sigma\in\mcB^+$ and $n\in\bfN^{d+1}$
			\begin{equation} 
				\downarrow^n(\tau/\sigma) = (\downarrow^n\tau)/\sigma.
			\end{equation}
			\item  We have the Leibniz rules
			\begin{equation} 
			\begin{aligned}	D^n(\tau_1\tau_2) & = \sum_{n=n_1+n_2} \binom{n}{n_1}  D^{n_1}\tau_1 \, D^{n_2}\tau_2,
			\\
				\downarrow^n\hspace{-2pt}(\tau_1\tau_2) & = \sum_{n=n_1+n_2} \binom{n}{n_1} \downarrow^{n_1}\hspace{-2pt}\tau_1 \, \downarrow^{n_2}\hspace{-2pt}\tau_2.
				\end{aligned}
			\end{equation}
			\item For any $n\in\bfN^{d+1}$ and $\tau\in T$, we have the commutation relations
			\begin{equation*} 
				\Delta D^n\tau = (D^n\otimes\id)\Delta \tau, \quad \text{and}\quad
				\Delta \downarrow^n\tau = (\id \, \otimes \downarrow^n)\Delta \tau.
			\end{equation*}
			The same holds true for $\tau \in \CT^+$ and $\Delta$ replaced by $ \Delta^{\!+} $.
		\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:38:23.572443+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e482b2cb02b56509ef479d4d557b857567175a4fc4e8eb21bb5dc05f1abd2f98","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:third-bound of arXiv:2511.01058, 'Limit profiles and cutoff for the Burnside process on Sylow double cosets'] For \(p \ge 11\) and \(k \le a < 2k\),
