{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:35:04.165880+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e4cf2ee4de20f127b9600b87a61e9eb8f6e5f0e899f4ff6636bce820acfdbd52","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:CSZ20 of arXiv:2608.30829, 'Free energy and phase transition for\\ 2D directed polymers with critical spatial correlat'] Assume $\hat \omega=(\hat \omega_i)_{i \in \mathbb{N}}$ is a sequence of i.i.d.\ standard Gaussian random variables. There exist constants $c_1,c_2\in (0,\infty)$ such that, for any $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and for any differentiable and convex function $f : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}$, the following bound holds for any $b \in \mathbb{R}$ and $t,c \in (0,\infty)$:
    \begin{equation}

      \mathbb{P} \big(\, f(\hat \omega) \le b-t \,\big)\mathbb{P} \big(\, f(\hat \omega) \ge b \,, \ |\nabla f(\hat \omega)| \le c\, \big) \le c_1 \exp \Big\lbrace - \frac{(t/c)^2}{c_2} \Big\rbrace\,,
    \end{equation}
    where $\hat \omega=(\hat \omega_1,\ldots,\hat \omega_n)$ and $|\nabla f(\hat \omega)|=\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^n (\frac{\partial}{\partial \hat \omega_i} f)^2}$ is the norm of the gradient. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:24:20.980317+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e4d30c5b54bd08f7d4ff8939e18a667ce7035e3b3eb52a690bbcf679f0e5dbba","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:chemical_easy of arXiv:2508.18809, '{\bf Critical long-range percolation III: The upper critical dimension}'] Let $G=(V,E,J)$ be a transitive weighted graph. The following inequalities hold for every $0\leq \beta<\beta_c$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\mathbb E_\beta \sum_{x\in K} d_\mathrm{chem}(o,x)\leq (\mathbb E_\beta|K|)^2$.
\item $\mathbb E_\beta|K|^2\leq \mathbb E_\beta|K|\mathbb E_\beta \sum_{x\in K} (1+d_\mathrm{chem}(o,x))$.
\item $\frac{d}{d\beta}\mathbb E_\beta|K| \leq \sup_e \frac{J(e)e^{-\beta J(e)}}{1-e^{-\beta J(e)}}\mathbb E_\beta \sum_{x\in K} d_\mathrm{chem}(o,x)$. 
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:00.614992+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e4d6e27e57ef46c4120f157b5a65eb553087b51c33d0b3a6d8df82127d3cf59f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 28 of arXiv:2510.27589, 'Geodesic switches and exceptional times in dynamical Brownian last passage percolation'] Fix $\mu\in (0,1)$.  There exist positive constants $c,C,\delta_0$ such that for any fixed $0<\delta<\delta_0$, points $p\in [\![-(1+\gamma/4)n,-(1-\gamma/4)n]\!]_\RR, q\in [\![(1-\gamma/4)n,(1+\gamma/4)n]\!]_\RR$ with $\slope(p,q)\in (\mu,\mu^{-1})$, we have for all $n$ large enough,
