{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:49:49.202233+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e4e2f576b5a93c16ed1513f89ed54ed968ae4397e6bf0b1c39598470230b4e56","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2604.26898, '\bf Stochastic Scaling Limits and Synchronization \\by Noise in Deep Transformer Models'] Fix $N \in \mathbb N$, let $T>0$ and let the above assumptions hold. Then for every initial condition $\mathbf X_0 = \bar {\mathbf X}_0 \in (\mathbb S^{d-1})^N$ and every $m, L \in \mathbb N$ there exists a coupling\footnote{That is, a joint realization of $(G_\ell^m)_{\ell=1}^L$ and $(B^k)_{k=1}^\infty$ with the prescribed marginals, see Remark~\ref{rem:global_coupling}.} $\Gamma_{m,L}$ of $(G_\ell^m)_{\ell=1}^L$ and $(B^k)_{k\ge1}$ such that
    \begin{equ}
\E_{\Gamma_{m,L}}\left[\sup_{0\leq t\leq T}\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N |X^{i}_{\lfloor{Lt/T}\rfloor} - \bar X^i_t|^2\right] \leq  
    \mathsf c_{N,T}\left(\frac{\log L}{L} + \frac 1{ m}\right)
    \end{equ}
    where $\mathsf c_{N,T}$ is a constant depending on $N$ and $T$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:10.966500+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e4e378d69625196cbbda63931bd857e5fb8cdde626f81d75ad2a09cc3a1fa255","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 29 of arXiv:2606.12720, 'On McDiarmid's Inequality under Dependence \\ via Approximate Tensorization of Entropy'] {Cumulative Distribution Functions}


    Let $\nu$ be a probability measure on $\R^n$ and $X \sim \nu$. Respectively, define the empirical and average marginal cumulative distribution functions at $x \in \R$ as 
    \begin{align*}
        \hat F_n(x)
        := \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \indicator{X_i \leq x}, 
        \quad \text{and} \quad 
        \bar F(x)
        := \E[\hat F_n(x)]
        = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \P(X_i \leq x). 
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:39:41.408819+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e4e3a4bad90341a914d262bacd8a1eecaef059882c5b0a2aaf9abd2a864b9b3b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 3 of arXiv:2604.06823, 'On spectrum of sample correlation matrices from large fold tensor vectors'] (\cite{BYY2022}*{Proposition 2.4}) 
Let the assumptions in Theorem \ref{Thm} hold. Then there exists a probability distribution function $F_c$, such that
