Moreover, in the case that $\tau_\alpha \equiv 1$, the limiting distribution function $F_c$ is exactly the Mar\v{c}enko-Pastur law with density function \eqref{eq-MP law}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:58:38.059709+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e4e65afd031750de664cf0fd5a4278d3d0765cbbf7f47b9c0dd46c782ec4ea6d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Conjecture con:Diagonal of arXiv:2606.19313, 'Stability of Khintchine-type inequalities via log-monotonicity'] Suppose $p\geq 2$ is an even integer and $(a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n)\in \mathbb{R}^n$ satisfies $\sum_ka_k^2=1$. Furthermore, suppose $X$ is a symmetric random variable and $X_1, X_2, \ldots, X_n$ are iid copies of $X$. Let $r_k=r_k(X)$. Then there exist positive constants $C_p>0$ such that the following bounds hold for $S=\sum_ka_kX_k$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $(r_k)$ is log-concave, then 
\begin{align*}
\| S \|_p^p\leq \| Y_n \|_p^p-C_p\sum_k\textstyle (a_k^2-\frac{1}{n})^2;
\end{align*} 

\item If $(r_k)$ is log-convex, then 
\begin{align*}
\| S \|_p^p\geq \| Y_n \|_p^p+C_p\sum_k\textstyle (a_k^2-\frac{1}{n})^2.
\end{align*} 
\end{enumerate} (Stated as a conjecture by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:53:51.331657+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e4e6ec6992117f93e9795057332104e427875d82624ca99072d7ddb702b04d32","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:laplace-deterministic of arXiv:2605.03703, 'Scaling Limits of Bivariate Nearly-Unstable Hawkes Processes and Applications to Rough Vol'] Let $f\in C([0,1];\mathbb R)$ and let $X$ be an integrable solution with
input $f$.  Then, for every measurable $u:[0,1]\to[0,\varepsilon_0]$,
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}\Bigl[\exp\Bigl(-\int_0^1u(t)X_t\,dt\Bigr)\Bigr]
=\exp\Bigl(\int_0^1\chi_u(1-t)\,f(t)\,dt\Bigr),
\end{equation}
where $\varpi_u$ is the solution of~(equation eq:riccati of the paper) provided by
Lemma~(lem:riccati of the paper) and
$\chi_u=-u(1-\cdot)+\tfrac{\nu^2}{2}\varpi_u^2$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:07:35.021302+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e4e7890a1d5e7772d07568607ff899921f7b57ba173019ae607a37ce4f943a3e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 46 of arXiv:2609.02878, '2609.02878'] Fix a limiting point \(\xi=(\mu_t)_{t<1}\) based at
\(q\in Y^{\rm prod}\), let
\(\tau_i\searrow0\), and set
\[
 g_i(s)=\tau_i^{-1}g(1+\tau_i s).
\]
Choose \(H_{2m+2}\)-centres \(p_i\) of the time-\((-1)\) measures
\(\mu_{i,-1}:=\mu_{1-\tau_i}\) with respect to \(g_i(-1)\).
After passing to a subsequence realizing a tangent space at \(\xi\), fix
a finite \(A\ge0\) and any \(q_i\) satisfying
\begin{equation}
 d_\eta(q_i,q)\le A\sqrt{\tau_i}.
\end{equation}
There is \(D_A<\infty\) such that
\begin{align}
 \operatorname{diam}_{g_i(-1)}F_{q_i}&\le D_A,
 \\
 F_{q_i}&\subset B_{g_i(-1)}(p_i,D_A).

\end{align}
For every finite \(B\ge0\), there is \(C_{A,B}<\infty\) such that
\begin{equation}
 \sup_{B_{g_i(-1)}(p_i,D_A+B)}
 |\Rm(g_i(-1))|_{g_i(-1)}\le C_{A,B}.
\end{equation}
The constants may depend on the fixed limiting point and the realizing
subsequence, but not on \(i\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:50:48.853006+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e4e7f530503cef643b28e13df6bff9b1dbc182f3d9a96abcbfaf5845bd04b993","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:stat.dist.alpha>0.beta=lambda0.exp of arXiv:2607.10708, 'A class of Markov processes with resetting and applications to cybersecurity'] \addcontentsline{toc}{subsection}{Theorem \thetheorem\ {case \texorpdfstring{$\beta=\lambda_o$}{Theorem β=λₒ}} and \texorpdfstring{$\alpha>0$}{α>0}  and exponential jumps}
    Under Assumption (ass_ex of the paper), the invariant measure $\Pi$ for $\Lambda$ is given by the following:

\begin{equation} 
    \Pi(d\lambda) = \frac{K_0}{\beta} \delta_{\beta}(d\lambda) + \pi(\lambda) d\lambda

\end{equation}
    where the density is given by 

\begin{equation} 
    \pi(\lambda)
    = \frac{K_0}{\beta} \frac{\beta\theta}{\alpha(\lambda-\beta)} \int_\lambda^A \left(\frac{u-\beta}{\lambda-\beta}\right)^{-\frac{\beta}{\alpha}} e^{-(\frac{1}{\alpha}-\theta)(u-\lambda)} du \ ,

\end{equation}
    with $K_0/\beta = \frac{\alpha}{\alpha+ \beta \theta K_3}$ and 

\begin{equation}
  K_3 =
  \begin{cases}
    -{_1F_1}^{(1,0,0)}\left(0,\frac{\beta }{\alpha },\frac{(\alpha  \theta -1) (A-\beta )}{\alpha }\right), & \alpha \not= 1/\theta \\
    \frac{\alpha}{\beta}  \left( A -\beta\right) & \alpha = 1/\theta
  \end{cases}

\end{equation}
  where ${_1F_1}^{(1,0,0)}$ is the Kummer’s confluent hypergeometric function.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:56:17.366165+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e4ec03cf9c2e3cb31569cefc77e1e68ee8fda7f722952dca6449b8446c5b4649","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:bounded first moments of arXiv:2606.02472, 'Correlated uniform attachment trees'] Let $T_n \sim \mathrm{UA}(n)$, let $u \in \{0,\ldots,n\}$ and let $r \in \left[1,n\right]$. Then
    \begin{equation}
        \sum_{v=r}^{n} \mathbb{P} \left( v \sim u, F_{T_n}(v) > \frac{n}{r} \right) \leq 2,
    \end{equation}
    and
    \begin{equation}
        \sum_{v=u+1}^{r-1} \mathbb{P} \left( v \sim u, F_{T_n}(v) \leq \frac{n}{r} \right) \leq 1 .
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:45:10.133813+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e4edb82ed275f789ce586ea650082ada12ffdc730825de88f8dc400ad0bfeee9","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:free_product of arXiv:2505.07145, 'Nonamenable Poisson zoo'] Suppose that we are given two non-trivial connected transitive graphs $G_i = (V(G_i), E(G_i))$, $i = 1,2$, with disjoint vertex sets and some distinguished vertices $o_i$, $i = 1,2$. The {\bf free product}
	\begin{equation}

		G := (V(G), E(G)) :=
		G_1 \star G_2
	\end{equation}
	is constructed in the following way.
	The vertices of $G$ are all the finite {words}:
	\begin{equation}

		V(G) :=  \left\{
		x_1 \ldots x_n \, \left| \, 
		\parbox{17em}{
			$n \in \mathbb{N}; \, x_j \in V(G_1) \cup V(G_2) \setminus \{ o_1, o_2 \}; \\
			x_k \in V(G_i) \implies x_{k+1} \notin V(G_i), \, i=1,2$
		}		
		\right. \right\} 
		\cup
		\left\{ o \right\}
	\end{equation}
	where $o$ denotes the {empty word}, the distinguished vertex of $G$, obtained by identifying each $o_i$, $i = 1,2$ with $o$.
	If we are given words $x = x_1 \ldots x_k \in V(G)$ and $y = y_1 \ldots y_m \in V(G)$ with $x_k \in V(G_i)$ and $y_1 \notin V(G_i)$, then $xy$ stands for the concatenation as words. In addition, for all $x \in V(G)$ we define $x o = o x = x$.
	Furthermore, adjacency in $G$ is given as follows: for $i = 1,2$, if $\{ x,y \} \in E(G_i)$, then $\{ zx, zy \} \in E(G)$ for all $z = z_1 \ldots z_{\ell} \in V(G)$, with $z_{\ell} \notin V(G_i)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:25:55.420519+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e4ee71e87cec361dfa1e3e59653661e2efae87c6ac87c09d8af36eccf84779fc","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 of arXiv:2607.28215, 'Almost stochastic dominance via optimal transport'] For $X,Y\in \mathcal{L}_{c,g}(E)$ with $\E[g(X)]\le \E[g(Y)]$ we define 
\begin{align}
\gamma^\ast(X,Y;g)  := \frac{ \mathrm{OT}_c(X,Y)}{\E[g(Y)]-\E[g(X)]+\mathrm{OT}_c(X,Y)},
