{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:11:09.316014+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e517a19d2c591da0243019ea9cfacc72fd886ecb27fbedbb5f3fe2866a0b229d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Glem1 of arXiv:2411.06089, '{\Large \bf Averaging principle for semilinear slow-fast rough partial differential equati'] Let $T> 0$ and $\mathbf B \in \mathscr{C}^\gamma([0,T]; \mathbb R^d)$ for some $\gamma \in (1/3, \gamma_0)$. Then for any $(Y, Y^\prime) \in \mathcal{D} _{\mathbf B}^{2\gamma , \eta }([ 0, T] ; \mathcal{H} )$ with $\eta \in [0,\gamma)$ and $ G_1 \in C_{- 2\gamma , 0}^3( \mathcal{H} ; \mathcal{H}^d)$, we have
$$\Big(\int_0^\cdot S_{\cdot-u} G_1 (Y_u) d\mathbf{B}_u, G_1(Y) \Big) \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathbf B}^{2\gamma,\eta}([0,T];\mathcal{H}),$$
and
\begin{align}
&\Big\| \int_0^{\cdot} S_{\cdot-u} G_1 (Y_u) d\mathbf{B}_u , G_1 (Y) \Big\|_{\mathcal{D}_{\mathbf B}^{2\gamma,\eta}}  \nonumber\\
 \lesssim & \|G_1 (Y)_0^\prime\|_{\mathcal H_{-2\gamma}^{d \times d}}+\|G_1 (Y)\|_{\infty,0}
+T^{\gamma-\eta}(1+|B|_\gamma+|B^2|_{2\gamma})\|G_1 (Y),G_1 (Y)^{\prime}\|_{\mathscr{D}_{S,\mathbf B}^{2\gamma,2\gamma,0}}.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:31:14.336415+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e51c78ae49ff5aa8ff90508ad0bb6d9f92b7ae9ad1b6695dd1a3bbf6c9fbbd12","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm th1 of arXiv:2606.17792, 'The Loss of Tension in an Infinite Membrane with Holes of Decaying Spatial Density'] Suppose that all the holes are unit discs, with their natural centres being the holes' centres. Let $X$ be a PPP with the intensity $\lambda(\mathbf{z})$, such that for large~$\n{\mathbf{z}}$ we have $\lambda(\mathbf{z})\ge g(\n{\mathbf{z}})$ where
\begin{equation}
g(r)= \frac{1}{\log^{1/2-\delta} r}\qquad r>1    
\end{equation}
for some $\delta\in\left(0,\frac12\right)$. Then the membrane will eventually be destroyed a.s.,  i.e.\ $\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{L}_\infty(X)=\mathbb{R}^2)=1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

