{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.677168+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e536fb2045e5d32706f108301f55fd001c20059ec23fdabc6dfec7012a6f5d2e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:M_ell-better-bound of arXiv:2602.14998, 'Random geometric graphs with smooth kernels: sharp detection threshold and a spectral conj'] Let 
$\ell \triangleq \sum_{i<j}\ell_{ij}$ and $v \triangleq \sum_{i=1}^k {\mathbf{1}\left\{{\ell_i >0}\right\}}$.
Assume ~((eq:coeffs-assumption of the paper)) and \nbb{ 
$$
d \gtrsim B^2 r^2 + n^{1/2} B r^{3/2} + n^{1/2} (B + B^{1/2}) r + n^{1/3} (B^{4/3} + B^{2/3}) r^{4/3} + \log n + r^2 (\log n)^3.
$$}

For any constant $C_1 > 0$, there is a constant $C_2 > 0$ depending only on $C_1$ such that if $\ell \le C_1$, then
\begin{equation*}
M_{\boldsymbol{\ell}}
\le C_2 \left( \max \bigg\{ \frac{B(B+1) r^2 n^2}{d^2} , \frac{n (\log n)^{1/2}}{d} \bigg\} \right)^\ell \frac{1}{n^v} .

\end{equation*}

In the special case of a linear kernel $\kappa(t) = b_1 t$, assume 
\nbb{$d \gtrsim n^{1/2} b_1 + n^{1/3} b_1^{4/3} + \log n$.} 
For any constant $C_1 > 0$, there is a constant $C_2 > 0$ depending only on $C_1$ such that if $k \le C_1$, then
\begin{equation*}
M_{\boldsymbol{\ell}}
\le C_2 \left( \max \bigg\{ \frac{b_1^2}{d^2} , \frac{(\log n)^{1/2}}{n d} \bigg\} \right)^{k/2} .

\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:07:56.654808+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e53c533a78f506d05754e24e04ca08a2b70c60142cb1d561ae4c144c1dd09285","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma of arXiv:2411.04631, 'Existence of Martingale Solutions to Stochastic Constrained Heat Equation'] The space consisting of the continuous and square-integrable processes, which are martingales and have values in $\mathcal{H}$, is represented by $\mathbb{M}_{T}^{2}$. Moreover, it can be shown that $\mathbb{M}_{T}^{2}$ is a Banach space w.r.t following norm, 
