{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:17:17.075728+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e57e6336cba7a1384bcd547c35a88c86183b1f7f7884c42824ea75091b59489f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 (Finite-horizon error decomposition) of arXiv:2608.19762, '2608.19762'] Let
\(
    e_s:=\delta x_s-\widehat{\delta x}_s.
\)
Then, for every $h\geq1$,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
    e_{t+h}
    ={}&
    \Phi_{t+h,t+1}
    r_{B,t}(\xi_t)\\
    &+
    \sum_{j=t+1}^{t+h-1}
    \Phi_{t+h,j+1}
    \left(
        r_j^{\mathrm{sm}}
        +
        r_j^{\mathrm{sw}}
    \right).
\end{aligned}

\end{equation}

If $D\widetilde f_j$ is $L_j$-Lipschitz along the segment joining
$x_j^{\mathrm c}$ and $x_j^{\mathrm c}+\delta x_j$, then
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
    &\|e_{t+h}\|
    \leq{}
    \|\Phi_{t+h,t+1}\|
    \|r_{B,t}(\xi_t)\|\\
    &+
    \sum_{j=t+1}^{t+h-1}
    \|\Phi_{t+h,j+1}\|
    \left(
        \frac{L_j}{2}\|\delta x_j\|^2
        +
        \|r_j^{\mathrm{sw}}\|
    \right).
\end{aligned}

\end{equation}

If, in addition, $F_{t+h}$ has an $L_{F,t+h}$-Lipschitz gradient along the
segment joining $\theta_{t+h}^{\mathrm c}$ and
$\theta_{t+h}^{\mathrm s}$, then
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
    \bigl|
        \delta F_{t+h}
        -
        c_{t+h}^{\top}
        \Phi_{t+h,t+1}
        B_t\xi_t
    \bigr|
    &\leq{}
    \|c_{t+h}\|\,\|e_{t+h}\|\\
    &+
    \frac{L_{F,t+h}}{2}
    \|\delta\theta_{t+h}\|^2.
\end{aligned}

\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:54:18.236609+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e57ed4d844108f08fc57d4ebe05d399414916afb3ad907aa908d1aa225932f1c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor-cij of arXiv:2608.29147, 'The crossing matrix and the Burau matrix of pure braids'] For any pure braid $b$, the crossing matrix $C(b)=[c_{i j}]$ is obtained from the Burau matrix $f(b)=[f_{ij}(b;t)]$ as follows. 
\begin{align*}
