{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:20:07.907676+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e581f97c7846619b431ce829015c27151be7a86bf7d25d46cf1533bda8a2a84e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:global:majorization:onesided of arXiv:2608.23765, '\texorpdfstring{Tight Majorizations and Convergence Rates\\ of Nuclear Norm Minimization I'] Let $\varepsilon > 0$, let $\mathcal{J}_{\varepsilon}: \R^{d_1 \times d_2}  \to \R$ be the $\varepsilon$-smoothed nuclear norm (eq:smoothedell1:objective of the paper) and $Q_{\varepsilon}(\cdot \mid \mathbf{X}): \R^{d_1 \times d_2} \to \R$ be the quadratic model function of (eq:smoothedell1:IRLSmajorizer of the paper) defined by the left-sided or right-sided weight operator (eq:W:operator:action of the paper) using core matrix (eq:leftsided:mean of the paper) or (eq:rightsided:mean of the paper), respectively. Then, $Q_{\varepsilon}(\cdot \mid \mathbf{X})$ majorizes $\mathcal{J}_{\varepsilon}$ globally:
	\begin{equation} 
		Q_{\varepsilon}(\mathbf{Z} \mid\mathbf{X}) \geq \mathcal{J}_{\varepsilon}(\mathbf{Z}) \qquad \text{for each } \mathbf{Z},\mathbf{X} \in \R^{d_1 \times d_2}.
	\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.680473+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5827e3f0970de5ef75f67c6f95d682b710ec88b2df8854fbe758dee71518229","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:warm:start:condition of arXiv:2412.09235, 'A semiconcavity approach to stability of entropic plans and exponential convergence of Sin'] When the two marginals satisfy (equation conditio:caff of the paper) and $\Sigma\succ 0$, if $A_0\succeq A_\infty$ (defined at~(equation Ainfty of the paper)) then $B_n\preceq B_\infty$ for any $n\in\mathbb{N}$. Equivalently, if we start Sinkhorn's algorithm in $\varphi^{0}_\varepsilon$ such that 
 \begin{equation}
 \nabla^2\varphi^{0}_\varepsilon\succeq -\Sigma -\frac{\varepsilon\alpha_\rho}2+\biggl(\frac{\varepsilon^2\alpha_\rho^2}{4}+\frac{\alpha_\rho}{\beta_\nu}\,\Sigma^2\biggr)^{\nicefrac12}\,,
 \end{equation}
 then for any $n\in\mathbb{N}$
 \begin{equation}
 \nabla^2\psi^{n}_\varepsilon\preceq -\Sigma- \frac{\varepsilon\beta_\nu}2+\biggl(\frac{\varepsilon^2\beta_\nu^2}{4}+\frac{\beta_\nu}{\alpha_\rho}\,\Sigma^2\biggr)^{\nicefrac12}\,.
 \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:01:48.559448+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e587b60704797823389e2557351986687116f456033eb6e5fd5a1e8760252abb","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma enormous_bound_lite of arXiv:2606.29622, 'Affine Structure of the Brownian Signature'] Let $Y$ be a real-valued random variable. If the degree of the polynomial $N$ is even with leading coefficient $p_N$ such that  $\, \mathfrak{Re}(p_N) < 0$, then the map $x \mapsto \mathbb{E}\left[\exp({p(x + Y)})\right]$ is entire; that is, there exists a complex sequence $\bm{u} = (\bm{u}^0, \bm{u}^1, \ldots)$ such that
    \begin{align}
\mathbb{E}\left[\exp({p(x + Y)})\right] = \sum_{k\geq 0} \bm{u}^k \frac{x^k}{k!} = \bracket{\bm{u}}{\mathbbm{x}} \,, \quad x \in \mathbb{R}\,.
   \end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:35:56.811185+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e58c8105b7326c56e89a3b496cb7820e633121fe2796bdd1c25e80175e109a1c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma FAevent_and_unique_crossings of arXiv:2603.28520, 'The Supercritical Loop O(1) and Random Current Models: Uniqueness and Mixing'] Let $\omega \in \{0,1\}^{E(\Lambda_n)}$ and $A\subseteq \partial_v \Lambda_n$, then
