{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:03:59.344966+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5906c2bd4a94e183db8956e470cf8c1f51a41d6fe3a81d1df986b5f0befb0b9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:AT+LSI of arXiv:2507.08692, 'Some notes on higher order concentration of measure'] Let $\mu$ be a probability measure on a product of of finite sets $\mathcal{X} = \otimes_{i = 1}^n \mathcal{X}_i$. Assume that there are $\alpha_1, \alpha_2 \in (0,1)$ such that $\tilde{\beta}(\mu) \ge \alpha_1$ and that there is some interdependence matrix $J$ such that $\lvert J \rvert_\mathrm{op} \le 1 - \alpha_2$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item The approximate tensorization property (equation eq:AT of the paper) holds true with $C= 1/(\alpha_1\alpha_2^2)$.
\item $\mu$ satisfies a $\mathfrak{d}$-LSI with constant $\sigma^2 = \log(\alpha_1^{-1})/(2\log(2)\alpha_1\alpha_2^2)$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:40.355152+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e593a3349110966084f2a59440d8a95d6aa08c72140372f871f1b572b8040580","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2511.09280, 'Random Walk conditioned to stay above a non-flat floor: curvature effects'] There are \(n_0\geq 1\), \(c>0\) such that for any \(n\geq n_0\), any \(1\leq l< k\leq n\) with \(n^{2/3} \leq k-l \leq 2n^{2/3}\), and any \(x\geq 0\),
	\begin{equation*}
		\sum_{y\geq 0}\kerPF_{l,k}^{n}(x,y)
		\leq
		e^{-c},
	\end{equation*}
	where the sum is over \(y\) such that \(P(Z_k=y\given Z_l= x)>0\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:33.743860+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e59791bca500d1f43a5129be36c576c207f7120804152df22bf988f72e291d55","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 7 of arXiv:2604.03548, 'Degree-preserving conservative processes and a unified approach for their hydrodynamics'] Assume that the process $\{\eta(t)\}_{t\ge 0}$ satisfies all items in both Assumptions \ref{assmp:generator_element_basic} and \ref{assumption:second_order_polymonial}. 
Let $\rho_0\in L^2(\mathbb T)$ and  
let $\{\mu_N\}_{N\in\mathbb N}$ be a sequence of probability measures on $\mathscr X_N$ associated to  $\rho_0$.
Moreover, assume that  
\begin{equation}

\sup_{N\in\mathbb N}
E_{\mu_N}\bigg[\frac{1}{N} \sum_{x\in\mathbb T_N} \eta_x^2 \bigg] < C 
\end{equation}
for some constant $C>0$. 
Then, for every $t\in [0,T]$, the sequence of empirical measures $\{\pi^{N}_t\}_{N\in\mathbb N}$ converges in probability to the absolute continuous measure $\rho(t,u)du$, that is, for any $\delta>0$ and for any $G\in C^2(\mathbb{T})$ we have 
\begin{equation*}
\lim_{N\to\infty}
Q^{N}_m 
\bigg(\Big|\langle \pi^{N}_{t},G\rangle-\int_{\mathbb T} \rho(t,u)G(u)du \Big|>\delta\bigg) =0 
\end{equation*}
where $\rho(t,u)$ is the unique weak solution to the heat equation \eqref{eq:HDL_heat_equation} provided $m>5/2$, where recall that the measure $Q^{N}_m$ was defined  for the process taking values in Sobolev space $H^{-m}(\mathbb T)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:01:15.328659+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e599e4e3af8575a29ea737bd261f42ebaef353b277abf03f0cef445f39822d54","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Coro 12 of arXiv:2608.30357, 'Mean convergence for Banach space-valued random elements indexed in measure spaces'] Let the assumptions of \cref{cor:decay-mass}, excluding \eqref{eq:cor:decay-mass}, be satisfied. Let $\{M_n : n \ge 1\} \subseteq \bN\cup\{0\}$ be a non-decreasing sequence and assume that, for each $n \ge 1$, the  family $\{X^n_k :  k \in S_n\}$ is $M_n$-pairwise diagonally negatively dependent. If
\begin{align}
\lim_{n \to \infty} N(K_n, \ep)^{1 - (\frac{1}{p} \wedge \frac{1}{2})} \lt\|K_n\rt\| \BB((1+M_n) \sum_{k\in S_n} a_{n, k}^2\BB)^{\frac{1}{p} \wedge \frac{1}{2}} = 0\quad \trm{for all }  \ep >0,
\end{align}
then
	\begin{align*}
		\lim_{n \to \infty} \mathbb{E}  \,\BB\| \sum_{k \in S_n} a_{n, k} (X^n_k - \bE X^n_k) \BB\|^p  = 0.
	\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:47:01.668252+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e59a52266b34d88cea230b149b39238e66fee5d6bb3f6d8e3e1e3f2e5687151d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 13 of arXiv:2604.24189, 'Malliavin calculus and densities for chaos-driven stochastic differential equations'] Let $q$ be a positive integer, $e_0\in \HHH$, with $\|e_0\|_{\HHH}=1$, and  $(f_t)_{t\in[0,T]}$ be a family of vectors in  $\HHH ^{\odot q}$.   
	We denote by $V$ the orthogonal complement of $\operatorname{span}\{e_0\}$ in $\HHH$. 
	Hence, there exist $(f_{t,q-k})_{t\in [0,T]}\subset V^{\odot  {(q-k)}}$, with $0 \leq k \leq q$, such that
	\begin{equation}
		I_q(f_t)= \sum _{k=0}^{q} I_{k} (e_0^{\otimes k}) I_{q-k} (f_{t,q-k}),
	\end{equation}
	where $I_{0} (h^{\otimes 0})=1$. Furtheremore, for each $0 \leq k \leq q$, there exists a positive constant $C$ such that for any $s,t\in [0,T]$,
	\begin{equation}
		\|f_{t,q-k}-f_{s,q-k}\|_{\HHH^{\otimes {(q-k)}}}\leq C \|f_t-f_s\|_{\HHH^{\otimes q}}.
	\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:25:44.580138+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e59a9b77c3164bac411a6f0057d34b05bb06120191c5a08b3eae8a32f356abb7","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop conv_typical_to_mu of arXiv:2509.06730, 'Hyperbolic branching Brownian motion: \\ the empirical limit measure'] Almost surely, there exists a family of sub-probability measures $(\mu^K_\infty)_{K>0}$ such that for every $K>0$
    \begin{equation*}
        \mu_t^K \rightarrow \mu_\infty^K,
    \end{equation*}
    weakly, as $t\to \infty$. Furthermore, for $K<K'$ we have $\mu^K \leq \mu^{K'}$ and as $K \to \infty$,
    \begin{equation*}
        \mu_\infty^K \rightarrow \mu_\infty. 
    \end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.821684+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5a131872779c49809c405bbdea4b215853dee5e182a4f98e136f939a7edcddd","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:optimal-finite-modeling of arXiv:2609.01690, 'Iterated-sumset spectra: The complete exponent law and its rank geometry'] For all $h\geq1$ and $k\geq2$,
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal R_{\mathbb R}(h,k)=\mathcal R_{\mathbb Q}(h,k)=\mathcal R(h,k),
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 1+\left\lceil\frac{M_{h,k}-1}{h}\right\rceil
 \leq N(h,k)
 \leq 1+\max\left\{(k-2)h^{k-2},
                    \sum_{j=0}^{k-2}h^j\right\}.
\end{equation}
Consequently, for every fixed $k\geq2$,
\begin{equation}
 N(h,k)=\Theta_k(h^{k-2}).
\end{equation}
More finely, if
\[
 \nu_{h,k}(n)=\min\{\operatorname{ndiam}(A):A\subset\mathbb Z,\ |A|=k,\ |hA|=n\},
\]
then
\begin{equation}
 \nu_{h,k}(n)
 \leq\bigl(k-1-r_{h,k}(n)\bigr)h^{k-2}
 \qquad(n\in\mathcal R(h,k)).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:02:28.583479+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5a2d4e7f81800832d5c7ba88e6ee823c95e2ff91ed85b7e673ddd232d85b820","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2609.01333, '2609.01333'] For $i\in \{0,1\}$ we let $n\geq i$ and define

\begin{align*}
B_n = \frac{\sqrt{\pi}\,\Gamma\left(\frac{n}{2}\right)}{2\,\Gamma\left(\frac{n+1}{2}\right)} \qquad \text{and} \qquad C^i_n = \int_{0}^\infty r^{n} K_i(r) \mathrm{d}r.
\end{align*}
Then, the following asymptotic limits hold
\begin{align*}
&\lim_{\beta\to \infty} \beta^2(\beta^{n+1}T^i_n(\beta)- 2C^i_n)= 2^{-2}C^i_{n+2},\\
&\lim_{\beta\to 0} (T^1_n(\beta)-2^{n+1}B_n \beta^{-1})=0,\\
&\lim_{\beta\to 0} (T^0_n(\beta)+ 2^{n+2}B_{n+1} (\log(\beta)+\gamma_e))=-2^{n+2}\frac{d}{d n} B_{n+1}.
\end{align*}
In particular we find that
\begin{align}

T_n^i(\beta) &\sim  2\beta^{-n-1}C_n^i  +2^{-2}\beta^{-n-3}C_{n+2}^i
&&\textrm{ as $\beta\to \infty$},\\

T^1_n(\beta) &\sim 2^{n+1}B_n\beta^{-1}\quad \text{and}\quad T^0_n(\beta) \sim
-2^{n+2} B_{n+1}\log(\beta)
&&\textrm{ as $\beta\to 0$}.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:52.414105+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5a68699253b15bc20cdc493f4871059a380e4f73c1fdeb3f655214a2dd9aa69","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 76 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] We say that $\boldsymbol{\ell}$ has no holes in $[\mathfrak{a},\mathfrak{b}]$ if all spacings between particles indexed by $I$ and their immediate neighbors are minimal. In more detail, $I$ should be nonempty and
the following three conditions should be satisfied
\begin{enumerate}
\item Bulk: $\forall i \in \llbracket i_0+1,i_0 + \#I-1\rrbracket\quad \ell_{i + 1} = \ell_i + \theta_{h,h}$.
\item Left boundary: either\footnote{If $i_0=0$ and $a_1=-\infty$, then there is no way for this condition to be satisfied and there is no way for $\boldsymbol{\ell}$ to have no holes.} $\ell_{i_0+1}= a_h$ or $\ell_{i_0+1}=\ell_{i_0}+\theta_{h,h}$.

\item Right boundary: either\footnote{If $i_0+\#I=N$ and $b_H=+\infty$, then there is no way for this condition to be satisfied and no way for $\boldsymbol{\ell}$ to have no holes.}
$\ell_{i_0 + \#I}=b_h$ or $\ell_{i_0+\# I+1}= \ell_{i_0 + \#I}+\theta_{h,h}$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:54:28.056396+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5a75eca22ed7f4aae8421f627de8107e042e18efcef06ac15708a63a57212ca","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma prelim of arXiv:2608.12056, 'Deviation inequalities for U-processes of growing order, with applications to half-space d'] There exist absolute constants $c,C>0$ with the following properties. Assume that $\frac{\max(t,d)m}{N}\leq c$. Then 
\[
\hat \delta \leq C\sqrt{\frac{\max(d,t)}{N}}
\]
with probability at least $1-e^{-t}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:53:31.978168+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5a7c653c81f7e3ecc7d23ec8a5c17a41e81633ce9a0adebb295db42a889e29a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 2 of arXiv:2606.11389, 'Instability of a nonlinear oscillator with small friction and small additive noise'] For the system \eqref{add eps} and for all $(x_0,y_0,v_0)$ with $v_0 \neq 0$ the limit
      $$
       \lambda(\e^2 \beta,\e \sigma,1,1) = \lim_{t \to \infty} \frac{1}{t} \log \|v_t\|
      $$
exists almost surely and has the value
   \begin{equation} 
     \lambda(\e^2 \beta,\e \sigma,1,1) = \int \widetilde{Q}_\e(x,y,\theta)d\nu_\e(x,y,\theta)
     \end{equation}
where $\nu_\e$ is the unique invariant probability measure for the process $\{(x_t,y_t,\theta_t): t \ge 0\}$ on $\R^2 \times \R/(2\pi \Z)$ given by (\ref{add eps} ,\ref{theta}). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:52:57.731837+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5a81479b10738a846f740462a6e77974fbdba240a9810b1b0e705e7a9b3cbf3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 3 of arXiv:2609.02755, 'The Sharp Rate of Probabilistically Strong Convergence to the KPZ Equation'] For $0 < \delta \le \eps \le 1$, we let $\pi^{\eps, \delta} : \mfL_- \to C^\infty(\mathbb{R}^2)$ be the smooth, stationary random noise assignment defined by
	\begin{align*}
		\pi^{\eps,\delta} \<b0> = \xi_\eps, \quad \pi^{\eps,\delta} \<p0> = \xi_{\delta}, \quad \pi^{\eps,\delta} \<0t> = \eps^{-1/2} \xi_{\eps, \delta}
	\end{align*}
	where we have written $\xi_{\eps, \delta} = \xi_\eps - \xi_{\delta}$. 

	We let $Z^{\eps, \delta} = (\Pi^{\eps, \delta}, \Gamma^{\eps, \delta})$ denote the corresponding BPHZ model, as introduced in \cite[Theorem 6.18]{BHZ}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:40:59.372629+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e5a99a438cbd531cba026ee3b67d59d799573e721b238d49aa25b0d41e342436","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2609.03961, 'On the exponential sum over squarefree integers'] Let $N\ge2$ and $1\le Q\le N^{1/2}$.  Then
