{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:19:56.669122+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e69e5d9dae95fbd2444420c164e8ce8587697ce8f5208b2fca2829aa4f896b46","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 8 of arXiv:2608.30875, 'A Decomposition Theorem for Topological Semi-small Maps'] We define a \textbf{topologically stratified space} inductively on dimension. A 0-dimensional topologically stratified space $X$ is a countable set with the discrete topology. For $m > 0$ an \textbf{$m$-dimensional topologically stratified space} is a paracompact Hausdorff topological space $X$ equipped with a filtration
	\begin{align*}
		X=X_{m} \supseteq X_{m-1} \supseteq \dots \supseteq X_{1} \supseteq X_{0} \supseteq X_{-1}= \emptyset
	\end{align*}
	by closed subsets $X_{j}$ such that if $x \in X_{j}-X_{j-1}$ there exists a neighborhood $\mathcal{N}_{x}$ of $x$ in $X$, a compact $(m-j-1)$-dimensional topologically stratified space $L$ with filtration
	\begin{align*}
		L=L_{m-j-1} \supseteq \dots \supseteq L_{1} \supseteq  L_{0} \supseteq L_{-1} = \emptyset,
	\end{align*}  
	and a homeomorphism $\phi : \mathcal{N}_{x} \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}^{j} \times \mathcal{C}(L),$
	where $\mathcal{C}(L)$ is the open cone on $\mathcal{L}$, such that $\phi$ takes $\mathcal{N}_{x} \cap X_{j+i+1}$ homeomorphically onto
	\begin{align*}
		\mathbb{R}^{j} \times \mathcal{C}(L_{i}) \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{j} \times \mathcal{C}(L)
	\end{align*}
	for $m-j-1 \geq i \geq 0$, and $\phi$ takes $\mathcal{N}_{x} \cap X_{j}$ homeomorphically onto
	\begin{align*}
		\mathbb{R}^{j} \times \{ \text{vertex of }\; \mathcal{C}(L) \}.
	\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:38:23.515539+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6a19daae45e672b933fd67cff85045cfdc3d182da0e759567c51fcbbc35b73a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:wsrm-X of arXiv:2510.18392, 'Hyperuniformity of random measures, transport and rigidity'] Let $ \mathsf{M}$ a locally square integrable weakly $   \mathbb X$-stationary random measure on $   \mathbb X.$
 There is a complex translation bounded measure $ \mathsf{C}$ on $   \mathbb X$ and a non-negative translation-bounded  measure $ \mathsf{S}$ on $ \hat   \mathbb X$ such that for $ f,g$ bounded with compact support 
\begin{align*}
  \textrm{Cov}\left(\mathsf{M}(f),\mathsf{M}(g)\right) = \int_{   \mathbb X^{ 2}} f(x)   \overline{   g(x + y)}dx\mathsf{C}(dy) = \int_{ \hat   \mathbb X} \widehat{ f}^{   \mathbb X}(u)    \overline{  \widehat{ g}^{   \mathbb X}(u)}   \mathsf{S}(du) .
\end{align*}Also, $ \mathsf{C}$ is Hermitian, i.e. $ \mathsf{C}(-.) =  \bar \mathsf{C}$. If $ \mathsf{M}$ is real-valued, $ \mathsf{C}$ and $ \mathsf{S}$ are real-valued and symmetric.

This also holds for $ f,g$ Schwartz functions if $   \mathbb X = \mathbb{R}^{ d}.$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:57:07.304440+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6a24243c891d7b2325d9a575bb914894053a6fb86f89d5544e8354faababb1a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 of arXiv:2606.24090, 'Sparsity-adaptive concentration inequalities for random polynomials'] Let $f$ be the polynomial defined in \eqref{eq:tensor_poly}. Then, for every
