{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:34:33.535977+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6ad3372fd16292424f437b49e821da7f57e9543288ad80094c927336f0344f8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 4 of arXiv:2603.28212, 'Limit Laws for the Distance to Fr\'echet Means of Random Graphs'] Let $F_n$ be the Frobenius distance between $G_{n,p}$ and an arbitrary Fr\'echet mean graph.  
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] If $n^2p(1-p)\rightarrow 0$, then $F_n\xrightarrow{P}0$.
\item[(ii)] If $n^2p(1-p)\rightarrow \lambda$ for some constant $\lambda>0$, then
\[F_n\xrightarrow{D}2\sqrt{\mathrm{Poi}\Big(\frac{\lambda}2\Big)},\]
where $\mathrm{Poi}(\frac {\lambda}2)$ denotes a Poisson random variable with mean $\frac {\lambda}2$. 
\item[(iii)] If $n^2p(1-p)\rightarrow \infty$, then
\begin{equation}
    \frac{F_n}{\sqrt{\E[F_n^2]}} \xrightarrow{P} 1.
\end{equation}
In particular, if we further have that $np(1-p)$ converges to a non-negative limit or diverges to infinity, 
\begin{equation}
    \frac{F_n}{\sqrt{n^2p(1-p)}} \xrightarrow{P} \sqrt{a},
\end{equation}
where the constant
\begin{equation}
a=\begin{cases}
    2, & \text{if } np(1-p)\to 0;\\
    2+\dfrac{\big(c-\lfloor c\rfloor\big)^2+\lfloor c\rfloor}{c}, &\text{if }  np(1-p)\to c \text{ for some constant } c>0;\\
    3, & \text{if } np(1-p)\to\infty.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.826240+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6aeeb1523d6fdfcb30cd2c14be50ccfc725adc6b2beb629b8dc49726050c49e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:popularity of arXiv:2609.01690, 'Iterated-sumset spectra: The complete exponent law and its rank geometry'] Fix $h\geq2$.  There are explicit positive rational constants
$\kappa_2,\ldots,\kappa_h$ such that, as $q\to\infty$,
\begin{equation}
 F_{h,M_h-M_{h-r}}(q)=\kappa_rq^3+O_h(q^2)
 \qquad(2\leq r\leq h),
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 F_{h,M_h}(q)=\binom q4-
 \left(\sum_{r=2}^h\kappa_r\right)q^3+O_h(q^2).
\end{equation}
For every
\[
 n\in\mathcal R(h,4)\setminus
 \left(\{M_h\}\cup\{M_h-M_{h-r}:2\leq r\leq h\}\right)
\]
there is an explicit positive rational constant $\lambda_{h,n}$ such that
\begin{equation}
 F_{h,n}(q)=\lambda_{h,n}q^2+O_h(q).
\end{equation}
Thus exactly $h$ cardinalities are popular: the maximum has frequency of
order $q^4$, the other $h-1$ displayed values have frequency of order
$q^3$, and every remaining attainable value has frequency of order $q^2$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:57:20.690276+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6b8436949c63964fdc3a8aef9327d6ccefa1268afd420882def7f8324053c39","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:firstboundongradsth2 of arXiv:2411.10235, '\textbf{Smooth transport map via diffusion process}'] There exists  $0<C_\star<C_2$ such that if $t\in [0,1)$  and $x\in \mathbb{R}^d$ satisfy \begin{equation}
1-t^2\leq C_\star^{-1}\log(\epsilon^{-1})^{-C_\star} \text{ and } \|tx\|^2\leq 1-C_2^{-1}\log(\epsilon^{-1}+1)^{-K},
\end{equation}
    then for all $f\in L^2(\mathbb{R}^d)$, $i\in \{1,2\}$ and $\xi\in \mathbb{R}^d$, we have
    \begin{align*}
    \|\int f(y) \nabla_x^i p^{t,x}(y)d\lambda^d(y)\| 
    &\leq \frac{Ct^i}{(1-t^2)^{i/2}}\left(\int \|f(y)-\xi\|^2dp^{t,x}(y)\right)^{1/2}.
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:06:39.296924+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6b85a6c3005f6fe2fe902a0c3fae159e3d791442c6da3c6cb4da21047b7e7fa","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:lowerboundLRW of arXiv:2607.10801, 'Relaxation times of non-reversible\\ Markov processes'] There exists a universal constant $C\in (0,\infty )$ such that for all $d,n\in\mathbb N$ with $n\ge 2$ and all $\gamma\in (0,\infty )$, 
