\end{enumerate}
If (equation asdasda of the paper) or (equation asdasda2 of the paper) holds, then no global Markovian pure Nash equilibrium exists. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:51:11.536538+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e7036404fc1609765cbe9a8f6e9daad5124241ada585cc156eca333bead77dd7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 9 of arXiv:2609.02610, 'The frame set of the first Hermite function'] Let $N \in \N$, $N\geq1$, let $\Omega\subset\C$ be a connected open set, and let
  $f_0,\ldots,f_{N-1}$ be holomorphic on $\Omega$.  Then the functions
  $f_0,\ldots,f_{N-1}$ are linearly dependent over $\C$ if and only if
  \begin{equation}
    \Wr(f_0,\ldots,f_{N-1})\equiv0
    \quad\text{on }\Omega.
  \end{equation}
  Equivalently, they are linearly independent over $\C$ if and only if their
  Wronskian is not identically zero. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:25:40.046129+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e708bd3c2fb668e5de9630bf92115f8c495b81719dffbbc1d3652209e0985a46","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2510.24562, 'Hard wall repulsion for the discrete Gaussian free field in random environment on $\mathbb'] Assume that under $\bbQ$ the conductances are i.i.d.\ (or more generally that $\bbQ$ satisfies a mixing condition, see Remark~\ref{rem:Discussion}, 1)). Then, there exists $\Omega_{\mathrm{typ}}$ with full $\bbQ$-measure such that for all $\omega\in \Omega_{\mathrm{typ}}$
\begin{itemize}

    \item[i)] \begin{equation}
        \lim_{N\to\infty} \frac{1}{N^{d-2} \log N}\log \bbP^\omega[\cW_N^+] = - 2 \overline{g} \,\capa^{\mathrm{hom}} (V)\,,
    \end{equation}
    \item[ii)]
    \begin{equation}
        \lim_{N\to\infty} \sup_{x\in  \bbZ^d} \bigg|\frac{\bbE^\omega[\varphi_x|\cW^+_N]}{\sqrt{4 \overline{g} \log N}} - \mathscr{h}^{\mathrm{hom}}_V(x/N)\bigg| = 0\,,
    \end{equation}
    \item[iii)] setting $a_N = \bbE^{\omega} [\varphi_0 | \cW^+_N]$, we have
\begin{equation}
    \hat{\bbP}^\omega_N  \to \bbP^\omega 
\end{equation}
    weakly as $N \to \infty$,
where $\hat{\bbP}^\omega_N$ is the law of the field $\varphi - a_N$ under $\bbP^\omega[ \, \cdot \,| \cW^+_N]$. 
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:22:59.740127+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e70ae8c5520e8d7c1baea6abfe9ee6d4938f0e6bb36e88b6bf023914f3cf56b0","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem slconv of arXiv:2608.30031, 'Ziggurats, taut foliations, and contact structures'] Suppose we have a sequence of $\Phi$-horizontal $C^0$-foliations $\mathcal{G}_k$, $k \geq 0$, on $\mathbb{T}^2$, converging to a $\Phi$-horizontal foliation $\mathcal{G}$ in the $C^0_\mathrm{Fol}$ topology. Furthermore, assume that each $\mathcal{G}_k$ is Reebless with slope $s_k \in \mathbb{R}$, and that $(s_k)_k$ converges to some slope $s_\infty \in \mathbb{R}$.
    Then $\mathcal{G}$ is admissible, and either
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\mathcal{G}$ is of $\aleph$ type, or
        \item $\mathcal{G}$ has no Reeb annuli and has slope $s = s_\infty$.
    \end{itemize}

    Moreover, in the second case, if $(s_k)_k$ is strictly increasing (resp.~decreasing), then $\fs(\mathcal{G}) \in \{s^\natural, s^\flat\}$ (resp.~$\fs(\mathcal{G}) \in \{s^\natural, s^\sharp\}$).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:48:25.213231+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e70d36a5c8e79953abfde90d81feb608a9b32b2b0ccd9ff5e12f47722edf6d5a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:loctimemoments of arXiv:2607.05671, 'Bi-infinite systems of singularly interacting Brownian particles and the KPZ equation'] Under Assumption (assump:initdiff of the paper), for all $T \in [0,\infty)$, $\xi \in (0,\infty)$, and $r \in [1,\infty)$, the following bounds hold: 
