{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.612047+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e7af239e0142fa5f24c000cacb5c1c37a8ce16539b2157c3def14f803dd15d11","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 34 of arXiv:2607.08536, 'Soft edge limit of the Laguerre beta-ensemble at the lower edge'] Assume $1\ll a_n\le (\log n)^{1/2}$. There exists a coupling between $\Lambda_{n,\beta,2a_n}=\{\lambda_{1,2a_n}^{(n)}<\lambda_{2,2a_n}^{(n)}<\cdots<\lambda_{n,2a_n}^{(n)}\}$ and $\Bessel_{\beta,2a_n}=\{\lambda_{1,2a_n}^B<\lambda_{2,2a_n}^B<\cdots\}$ so that for any fixed $k$ and $0<\alpha<1$ we have 
\begin{align}
\lim_{n\to\infty}\pr\left(\sum_{j=1}^k |\lambda_{j,2a_n}^B-n\lambda_{j,2a_n}^{(n)}|^2\ge n^{-1+\alpha}\right) =0.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:25:12.187508+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e7b01e9b3101a61eb08a08e7dd243a453685566567a0c5c6ca6adf92b3fe1065","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 27 of arXiv:2510.20492, 'Heterochromatic two-arm probabilities for metric graph Gaussian free fields'] For any $d\ge 3$, there exist $C,c>0$ such for any $T>0$, any distinct $v_1,v_2,w_1,w_2\in \widetilde{\mathbb{Z}}^d$ with $|v_1-w_1|\land |v_2-w_2|\ge d$, and any $z\in \{v_1,v_2,w_1,w_2\}$, 
 	 	\begin{equation}
 		\begin{split}
 			\mathbb{P}\big(  \widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{1/2}^{z} \ge T  \mid \{v_1\xleftrightarrow{} w_1\Vert v_2\xleftrightarrow{} w_2 \} \big)\le Ce^{-cT}. 
 		\end{split}
 	\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:45:17.706307+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e7b233f0d76717f6153a1272f033a00e6060c8b7f77b3552f27d4b69dd8b85fa","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:local_time_0 of arXiv:2604.10214, 'Large deviations for maximum local time of simple random walk in dimensions \texorpdfstrin'] For any $m\in \mathbb N$ and $s\ge 0$,
\begin{align}
{\mathbb P}\big(\xi(\infty,0)> m\big)=(1-\gamma)^m;
\qquad
{\mathbb P}\big(\ell(\infty,0)> s\big)=e^{-\gamma s}.
\end{align}
In particular, for $\beta>0$, $n\in\mathbb N$ and $t>0$,
\begin{align}
\begin{aligned}
&{\mathbb P}\big(\xi(\infty,0)>\beta\alpha\log n + u\big)
=c_{\beta,n,u}(1-\gamma)^u n^{-\beta};\\ &{\mathbb P}\big(\ell(\infty,0)>\beta\gamma^{-1}\log t+u\big)=t^{-\beta}e^{-\gamma u}.    
\end{aligned}
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:10.891141+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e7b43353227c30e4ba05f14e5376d9ed2360cdc333191904cec565e0f8cfe963","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 2 of arXiv:2506.16873, 'Optimal matchings of randomly perturbed lattices'] Fix $d=1$ and $\alpha\in(0,1)$. Suppose that the distribution of the perturbation $\xi$ is symmetric around the origin and satisfies 
    \begin{equation}
       cr^{-\alpha } \le  \mathbb P (|\xi | \ge r) \le Cr^{-\alpha }
    \end{equation}
    for two constants $C,c>0$ and all $r \ge 1$. Then:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item 
        There is a perfect matching $M:\mathbb Z \to \Pi$ such that $$\mathbb P (|M(0)|>r)\le Cr^{-\frac{1+\alpha }{2}} \, .$$
        \item 
        Any invariant perfect matching $M:\mathbb Z \to \Pi $ has $$\E \big[ |M(0)|^{\frac{1+\alpha}{2}} \big] = \infty \, .$$
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:22:39.587267+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e7b5c408d1270da3203538a672d85c8989205a440bdddf038055610445bedfb5","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn of arXiv:2508.18807, '{\bf Critical long-range percolation I: High effective dimension}'] The results of this section will be proven under the assumption that at least one of the following four hypotheses hold, which together cover the hypotheses of both (thm:superprocess_main of the paper) (as listed in (equation HD of the paper)) and (thm:superprocess_main_regularly_varying of the paper). We refer to this list of hypotheses as \mytag[HD+]{{\color{blue}HD+}}\!\!.
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $d>3\alpha$.
    \item $d>6$ and  $\mathbb P_{\beta_c}(x\leftrightarrow y) \preceq \|x-y\|^{-d+2}$ for all $x\neq y$.
    \item $d=6$, $\alpha=2$, and  $\mathbb P_{\beta_c}(x\leftrightarrow y) \preceq (\log \|x-y\|)^{-1}\|x-y\|^{-d+2}$ for all $x\neq y$.
    \item $d=3\alpha<6$ and the hydrodynamic condition holds.
  \end{enumerate}
  We will often refer to these different hypotheses as Cases 1, 2, 3, and 4 of each relevant theorem, lemma, or proposition.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:30:14.227648+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e7b80c72887098ea5d2622e2578fef4edbce869a180ced32b631a4ce8d899095","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Bramson-converges-to-1 of arXiv:2608.23202, 'Locally finite Fixed points of Branching Brownian Motion'] \footnote{This claim can also be verified in the modern language of extremal point processes $\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}_\infty^\lambda$. Notably, the underlying reason for (equation other-main-input-from-Bramson of the paper) to hold is that the number of particles of $\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}_\infty^\lambda$ at a bounded distance from $x$ blows up as $x\to -\infty$: it grows like $ e^{-\mu x}$ for $\lambda >\sqrt{2}$ and $\approx (-x)e^{-\sqrt{2}x}$ for $\lambda = \sqrt{2}$.}
    Let $\phi:\mathbb{R}\to [0,1]$ be a measurable function such that $\phi(x) \geq \eta$ for all $x\in (a,b)$ \textcolor{black}{with} some $a,b \in \mathbb{R}$ and $\eta >0$. Let $u_\phi(t,x)$ be the solution to F-KPP equation with initial condition $\phi$. Then, for any $\delta >0$, as $t \to \infty$,
\begin{equation}
u_\phi\left(t, x\right) \to 1
\end{equation}
uniformly in $x$ such that $|x| \le \sqrt{2}t - \bigl(\frac{3}{2\sqrt{2}} + \delta\bigr)\log t$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:11:44.404783+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e7ba0ee4780029da9dbfc79bfe7cd38941f714dd1d800f484e92db1ffef6e1c9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 1 of arXiv:2609.03187, 'Duality for a Martingale Transport Problem with Moment Constraints'] Let $c=(c_1,\dots,c_N) \in \mathbb R^N$. Then $c\in\operatorname{int}\mathcal C$
if and only if the following conditions hold:
