{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:01:22.636803+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e7bd58d41099cb70df3ce99e47c1670eb020bafb8cb1dfe764e997b7a626592b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:Mmatrix of arXiv:2607.03639, '\vspace{-0.6in}An AI-Assisted Solution to the Signed BAR Conjecture: Uniqueness in the Har'] The normalized matrix has the Harrison--Reiman form
\begin{equation}
 \widehat R=I-P^T,
 \qquad P\ge0,
 \qquad \rho(P)<1.
\end{equation}
Every principal submatrix $R_{AA}$ is a nonsingular $M$-matrix and $R_{AA}^{-1}\ge0$. In particular, for every nonempty $A\subset J$, the active directions $\{d_i:i\in A\}$ are linearly independent. For $A\subset J$, define
\begin{equation}
 L_Av=v-R_A R_{AA}^{-1}v_A,
\end{equation}
with $L_\varnothing$ equal to the identity. If $A=I(x)$, then $L_A=\mathsf L_x$ is the (unique) linear map from $\mathbb R^d$ to $H_x$ such that $\mathsf L_xv-v\in\operatorname{span}\{R_i:i\in A\}$. Moreover,
\begin{equation}
 L_A R_i=0,
 \qquad i\in A,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 C_{\mathsf L}:=\max_{A\subset J}\left\|I-R_A R_{AA}^{-1}\pi_A\right\|<\infty,
\end{equation}
where $\pi_Av=v_A$ and the expression for $A=\varnothing$ is the identity.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:37:14.674952+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e7be92d43140c9499f827fe8f44e511da588891221fccca8ae8435b9a77a735c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 1 of arXiv:2607.11275, 'The limit law of the maximum of discrete partial-sums distribution II'] Assume that $\mathbb{E}S_N<0$. Then, for every $s\in\mathbb{C}$ such that $|s|<1$ and $G_{S_N}(s)
