        \int_{B_g(p,r)} R_\omega\,\frac{\omega^n}{n!}
    \]
    exists and is positive unless \(X\) is Gaussian.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:41:54.356683+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e8da8480c45199450420c660861237b79cb4457bc568e2eca373e1c1ec4b5ea5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma MaximalInequalityUnderMixing of arXiv:2412.01635, 'On the Weak Convergence of the Function-Indexed Sequential Empirical Process and its Smoot'] Let $\mathcal H$ be a set of Borel maps $\mathcal X \to \mathbb R.$ Assume there exist $\lambda, \tau > 0$ and an even integer $\nu \geq 2$ such that 
	\begin{itemize}
		\item[(i)] $\zeta(\alpha^X, \lambda, \nu) = \sum_{s = 1}^\infty s^{\nu-2} \alpha^X(s)^{\lambda/(2+\lambda)} < \infty,$

		\item[(ii)] $\E{\left|h(X_{t,n}) - \E{h(X_{t,n})}\right|^{l \frac{2+\lambda}{2}}} \leq \tau^{2+\lambda}$, for all $h \in \mathcal H$, $l = 2,...,\nu$ and $1 \leq t \leq n, n \in \mathbb N.$ 
	\end{itemize}

	Then there exists a constant $C \geq 0$ that only depends on $\nu$, $\lambda$ and the mixing coefficients such that for each $n \in \mathbb N$ and $1 \leq i \leq j \leq n$, with $m = j-i+1$, we have 
	$$ \sup_{h \in \mathcal H} \norm{S_{n,i,j}(h)}_{L_\nu} \leq C \sqrt{m} \max\left\{m^{-\frac12}, \tau \right\}.$$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:37:08.954829+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e8dc57fcf9963396c8107bf667fe19b6487d904322694724cfc8f6c17ec1fbed","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:subgamma of arXiv:2609.01581, 'Concentration of additive functionals of Stratonovich-type'] Let $\tilde\sigma^2 > 0$ and $c > 0$. 
Consider the convex function on $[0, 1/c)$
\begin{align}
\phi(k) = \frac{k^2\tilde\sigma^2}{2(1 - ck)}.
\end{align}
Its Legendre transform $\phi^\ast(a)$ is, for $a > 0$,
\begin{align}
  \phi^\ast(a)
  \equiv \sup_{0 \leq k < 1/c}\left[ka - \frac{\tilde\sigma^2 k^2}{2(1 - ck)}\right]
  = \frac{\tilde\sigma^2}{c^2}h\left(\frac{c a}{\tilde\sigma^2}\right),

\end{align}
with supremum attained at $k^\ast = c^{-1}\bigl(1 - \sqrt{\tilde\sigma^2/(\tilde\sigma^2 + 2ca)}\bigr) > 0$
and we define the auxiliary function $h(u) \equiv 1 + u - \sqrt{1 + 2u}$.
Moreover, we have $h(u) \geq u^2/[2(1+u)]$ for $u \geq 0$, i.e.,
\begin{align}
  \phi^\ast(a) \geq \frac{a^2}{2(\tilde\sigma^2 + ca)}.

\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:09:59.305305+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e8df8d25ba585fa135b555bd5e9ad0607e0604817422e00ff723f4d6443b038a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:entropy of arXiv:2608.07410, 'Extreme least singular values of random row submatrices\\ with bounded-density subgaussian'] Suppose $n_m/m\to\beta>1$ and let
\[
L_m:=\binom{n_m}{m-1}.
\]
Then
\begin{equation}
\frac1m\log L_m\longrightarrow h(\beta).
\end{equation}
Moreover, for every $\alpha\in(0,h(\beta))$ there are constants $\delta_0,\kappa>0$ such that, for all
sufficiently large $m$,
\begin{equation}
\frac1{L_m}
\sum_{1\le k\le\lfloor\delta_0m\rfloor}
\binom{m-1}{k}\binom{n_m-m+1}{k}
\le e^{-(\alpha+4\kappa)m}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:32:35.604247+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e8dfa72e24c680f0f3eb1ed98112bdaa7c62c9d0fd83703d6c2931bf613a97dc","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:MF1 of arXiv:2606.13264, 'Interference Queueing Networks: A Replica Mean-Field Approach in the Symmetric Setting'] Consider $A_{l,i}$ and $D_{l,i}$  Poisson processes of intensity $\lambda\rm{dx}$ and Poisson processes of intensity $\rm{dxdy}$ respectively, each being independent in $l,i$. The initial condition
\[
\left(q_i^{(MF1,l)}(0)\right)_{l\geq 1,\;0\leq i\leq N-1}
\]
is assumed to be invariant in law under translations of the index $i$ on the
discrete torus.
Define $Q^{MF1}=(q^{(MF1,l)}_i)_{l \in \mathbb{N}_*, i \in [1,N]}$
    \begin{align}
q_i^{(MF1,l)}(t)
&=
q_i^{(MF1,l)}(0)
+
A_{l,i}[0,t]
\nonumber \\
&\quad
-
\int_0^t \int_{\mathbb R_+}
\mathbf 1_{\{q_i^{(MF1,l)}(z^-)>0\}}
\mathbf 1_{\{u\leq \mu^{MF1}(z^-)\}}
\,D_{l,i}(\mathrm du,\mathrm dz),
\end{align}
where  $\mu^{MF1}(z)=B\log_2(1+\frac{1}{1+\mathbb{P}(q_1^{(MF1,1)}(z)>0)\xi_N})$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:32:58.301463+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e8e51348d8367e48b9eef90e6ce76e83851a5157848fd04f915700cd348ee5ce","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2505.10256, 'Stochastic Oscillators out of equilibrium:\\ scaling limits and correlation estimates'] Let $\{\mu_N\}_{N\in\mathbb N}$ be a sequence of probability measures in $\Omega_N$ and let $v_0$ and $e_0$ be measurable real-valued functions defined on $\mathbb T$.
We say the sequence $\{\mu_N\}_{N\in\mathbb N}$ is associated to $(v_0,e_0)$ if for every real-valued continuous function $G$ on $\mathbb T$ and for every $\delta>0$ we have that 
\begin{equation*}
\lim_{N\to\infty}\mu_N\bigg( \eta\in\Omega_N \,;\,  
\Big| \frac{1}{N} \sum_{x\in\mathbb T_N} \eta(x) G\Big(\frac xN\Big) - \int_{\mathbb{T}}G(u)v_0(u)du \Big| > \delta\bigg) =0
\end{equation*}
and 
\begin{equation*}
\lim_{N\to\infty}\mu_N\bigg( \eta\in\Omega_N \,;\,  
\Big| \frac{1}{N} \sum_{x\in\mathbb T_N} \eta(x)^2 G\Big(\frac xN \Big) - \int_{\mathbb{T}}G(u)e_0(u)du \Big| > \delta\bigg) =0.
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:04:59.426153+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e8e60ea897a235de33c45da5f6bc53258d819f7281092f1c2029fffa6700b2ca","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop Lowerbound of arXiv:2605.18744, 'Lattice random-field Widom--Rowlinson models'] Let $d\le 2$ and $\Lambda_n=\{-n,\ldots, n\}^d$. If $\mathbb{E}[\tau_x(\eta)]>0$ for some $x\in \mathbb{Z}^d$, there is $c >0$ such that for all $t>0$
    \begin{align*}
        \liminf_{n\to \infty }\, \mathbb{E}\Big[\exp\Big( t F_{\Lambda_n}(\eta)/\sqrt{|\Lambda_n|}\Big)\Big]\geq \exp( c t^2).
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:35:19.875730+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e8e6df4b064081cd4c5d9939cd2414f147dc5f2542ff6dfdffa27f3f6cb937f4","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem Lnetw of arXiv:2608.27939, 'Entropy and domination for quasi-Hitchin representations'] The matrix $M(t)$ in (equation eq:Tmat of the paper) has a decomposition \begin{equation} 
 M(t)
 =
 X_{i_1}(t)\cdot X_{i_2}(t)
 \cdots
 X_{i_N}(t) \cdot 
 D(t) 
\end{equation}
where $D(t)$ is a diagonal matrix with each entry a positive Laurent monomial in the $t$-parameters, and each $X_i(t)$ is the triangular matrix $I + m_iE_{i+1,i}$ where $m_i$ is a positive Laurent monomial in the triangle $t$-parameters.

Moreover, the unipotent matrix $L_\rho(P)$ for (equation eq:Lfactor of the paper) is obtained by conjugating $M(t)\cdot D(t)^{-1}$ by a diagonal matrix involving the shear parameters $e:=e_\rho(x,P)$. In particular, it is the weight matrix of a transparent weighted planar network whose non-constant edge-weights are positive Laurent monomials in $t$ and $e$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:25:41.598951+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e8ed02a8dec5c3b0ff357d91531c6eeb48b016c9c2badab909246ec0dcf0f8df","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:pointwise of arXiv:2608.11931, '\texorpdfstring{Nonlinear parabolic characterizations of stochastic completeness at infini'] Let $T>0$ and $u_0 \colon X\to\mathbb R$.  A \emph{pointwise solution of the (eq:GPME of the paper)
on $[0,T]\times X$ with initial datum $u_0$}
is a function $u \colon [0,T]\times X\to\mathbb R$ such that
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
    \item[\textup{(a)}] $u(t,\cdot)\in\mathcal{F}_\Phi$ for every $t\in(0,T)$;
    \item[\textup{(b)}] $u(\cdot,x)\in C([0,T])\cap C^1((0,T))$ for every $x\in X$;
    \item[\textup{(c)}] $(\partial_t +\Delta\Phi)u(t,x)=0$ for every $(t,x)\in(0,T)\times X$;
    \item[\textup{(d)}] $u(0,x)=u_0(x)$ for every $x\in X$.
\end{enumerate}
The solution $u$ is a \emph{bounded pointwise solution} if $u$ is bounded.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.750575+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e8eead40b8483297c5a96cbf4882a0a6fb68acd723124bf2b64e9c0c11092ef8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem th:Schauder_est of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] For any $\alpha,\gamma\geq 0 $ and $T>0$, there exists $C=C(\alpha,\gamma,T,B)>0$ such that 
\begin{equation}
\|\mathscr{P}_r \varphi\|_{B,\gamma+\alpha} + \|\mathscr{P}'_r \varphi\|_{B,\gamma+\alpha} \leq C\, r^{-\frac{\alpha}{2}} \|\varphi\|_{B,\gamma}, \qquad    r\in (0,T], 
\end{equation}
for any $\varphi\in C^{b}(\mathbb{R}^d)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:51:11.955066+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e8f2f4c392f187cd8ff25c4309f3417c97d40e3869628814a84691eea07a44e2","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 27 (Rank-one, diagonal, and mean-remainder response) of arXiv:2608.07820, 'Linear Spectral Statistics for Entrywise-Transformed Spiked Wigner Matrices under Shifted '] Let \(B_N^{(4)}=H_N^{(4)}+\theta xx^{\mathsf T}\).  Under pole avoidance,
uniformly on \(\Gamma\),
\begin{equation}
 \Tr(B_N^{(4)}-zI)^{-1}-\Tr G_N^{(4)}(z)
 =
 -\frac{\theta m'(z)}{1+\theta m(z)}
 +o_{\mathbb P}(1).
\end{equation}
Adding \(-\theta D_x+R_N^{(m)}\) changes the analytic LSS by
\begin{equation}
 c_{\varphi,\Gamma}^{\rm diag0}+o_{\mathbb P}(1),
\end{equation}
and \(R_N^{(m)}\) has zero response.","status":"unverified"}

