{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:53.168113+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea13f0b6506dac755874ab05179563d65a46fff3bcfed99cef350ce32cdce03c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 11 (Expected Cost and Value Function) of arXiv:2607.03318, 'Continuous Differentiability of the Value Function for Infinite-Dimensional Finite-Horizon'] For a given initial time $t \in [0, T]$, let $\mathcal{T}_{t, T}$ denote the class of all $\F$-stopping times $\tau \colon \Omega \to [t, T]$. The finite-horizon optimal stopping problem is characterized by the following quantities:
\begin{enumerate}[label={(\textit{\roman*})}, leftmargin=*]
    \item \emph{Expected Cost Functional.} For an initial state $x \in H$ and an admissible stopping strategy $\tau \in \mathcal{T}_{t, T}$, the expected cost functional $J \colon [0, T] \times H \times \mathcal{T}_{t, T} \to \R$ is defined by:
    \begin{equation} 

        J(t, x; \tau) := \E \left[ \int_t^\tau f(s, X(s)) \, \mathrm{d}s + \Phi(\tau, X(\tau))\ind_{\{\tau < T\}} + \Psi(X(T))\ind_{\{\tau = T\}} \right],
    \end{equation}
    where $X(\cdot) \equiv X(\cdot; t, x)$ denotes the continuous mild solution of the governing semilinear state equation \eqref{eq:mild_solution_eq}.

    \item \emph{Value Function.} The value function $v \colon [0, T] \times H \to \R$ is defined as the infimum of the expected cost evaluated over the entire class of admissible stopping strategies:
    \begin{equation} 

        v(t, x) := \inf_{\tau \in \mathcal{T}_{t, T}} J(t, x; \tau).
    \end{equation}
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:23:03.030916+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea16294f81bf943a37a2867b60d4d71c86d913d11fd018b8aa3aabe8f3193b87","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 14 of arXiv:2605.02713, '2605.02713'] For any $i=1,\dots, n$,
	\begin{align*}
		H_q \left( Z_{i,n} \right) =  \alpha_n^{qi}  H_q \left( Z_{0,n} \right) + R_q  \left( Z_{i,n} \right)
	\end{align*}
	with 
	\begin{align}
		R_q  \left( Z_{i,n} \right) = \sum_{r=0}^{q-1} \binom{q}{r} \alpha_n^{ri} \left( \sqrt{ 1 - \left( \alpha_n^{i} \right)^2 } \right)^{(q-r)}  \hspace{-2mm} H_r \left( Z_{0,n} \right) H_{q-r} \left(   Y_{i,n}  \right) 
	\end{align}
	and $Y_{i,n}=  \sqrt{ \frac{ 1 - \alpha_n^2}{ 1 - \left( \alpha_n^{i} \right)^2}} \displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{i} \varepsilon_k \, \alpha_n^{i-k}  $.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:04:39.826492+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea163e08d12b20a068d025cd8d1fbdfed0d24be586ac01322f20388804df5313","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop variational-representation-chi-square of arXiv:2504.02207, 'Finite-Time behaviors of M/M/$n$ Queue: Mixing Time, Mean Queue Length and Tail Bounds'] Given two distributions $P, Q$ with countable supports and let $\cG_Q$ be a collection of functions $g: \R \rightarrow \R$ such that $\E_Q\sqbr{g(X)^2} < \infty\}$, we have
