{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:16:49.561276+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eaa6109f0665272fbb22fc9ef8f418a32942a03497fa020a94bf58a67a27d611","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defs 60 ($\sigma_r$-local truncated Dirichlet forms) of arXiv:2509.06869, 'Infinite Interacting Brownian Motions and EVI Gradient Flows'] Assume~\ref{ass:CE} and~\ref{ass:ConditionalClos}. Define 
\begin{align}  
\mathcal D_r&:= \biggl\{u \in C(\U, \tau_\mrmv) \cap \mathcal B(\sigma_r):   u_{r}^\eta \in \mathcal D_{\U(B_r), \QP_r^\eta}\ \text{$\QP$-a.e.~$\eta$}\biggr\} \comma
\\
\E^{\U, \QP}_r(u) &:=\int_\dUpsilon  \E^{\U(B_r), \QP_r^\eta}(u_{r}^\eta) \diff\QP(\eta) \fstop \notag
\end{align}
Note that $\mathcal D_r$ satisfies the conditions (a)--(c) in~Defs.~\ref{d:core} (see (b) of~Example~\ref{ese:CC}) except the fact that $\mathcal D_r$ is {\it not dense in~$L^2(\U, \QP)$}, but dense in~the supspace~$L^2(\U, \sigma_r, \QP)$ of $\sigma_r$-measurable functions.  The form~$(\E^{\U, \QP}_r, \mathcal D_r)$ is closable by Prop.~\ref{t:ClosabilitySecond}, where the densely definedness is irrelevant to the proof of the closability.  The closure is denoted by~$\overline{\mathcal D}_r$. 
The form~$(\E^{\U, \QP}_r, \overline{\mathcal D}_r)$ is  Markovian as well, which follows by the same proof of the Markovianity of $(\E^{\U, \QP}_r, \dom{\E^{\U, \QP}})$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.636268+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eaa6a241e03c90fd24c49cb3e12827ecebc2eecc7d9830076c916c4ba1871784","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:density evolu of arXiv:2512.12124, 'A discontinuous percolation phase transition on the hierarchical lattice'] \emph{\textbf{Part (A):}}
    Let $\varpi \in \Lambda_{M_{g}+jr,\infty}$ for some $g \geq g_0$ and $jr \in \left\{ r,\ldots, \bar{r}_{g+1} \right\}$. 
    If $\varpi$ is good or mediocre, then there exists a non-bad set $U \subset \Lambda_{M_{g},\infty}$ with
    \begin{equation*}
        |U| \geq L^{jr} - \sum_{k=0}^{j-1} \# \operatorname{Def}_{M_g + kr}(\varpi) L^{kr}
    \end{equation*}
    such that $\sigma \subset \varpi$ for all $\sigma \in U$ and
    \begin{equation}
        S(\varpi) \geq \sum_{\sigma \in U} S(\sigma) \geq (1-\zeta)^2 \theta_g  L^{M_{g}+jr}.
    \end{equation}
    \emph{\textbf{Part (B):}}
    If $\varpi \in \Lambda_{M_g,\infty}$ is good, with a defect at level $n \in \{M_{g-1}, M_{g-1}+r,\ldots,M_{g} - r \}$, then
    \begin{equation}
        |K_{\max}(\varpi)| \geq \theta_g L^{M_{g}} - 2N L^n \geq (1-\zeta) \theta_g L^{M_g}  .
    \end{equation}
    If $\varpi \in \Lambda_{M_g,\infty}$ is mediocre, with a defect at level $n \in \{M_{g-1}, M_{g-1}+r,\ldots,M_{g} - 2r \}$, then
    \begin{equation}
        \sum_{\varphi \in \chi_{r}(\varpi)}  |K_{\max}(\varphi)| \geq \theta_g L^{M_{g}} - 2N L^n \geq (1-\zeta) \theta_g L^{M_g} .
    \end{equation}
    If $\varpi \in \Lambda_{M_g,\infty}$ is good or mediocre, without a defect, then 
    \begin{equation*}
        S(\varpi) \geq \theta_g L^{M_{g}}  .
    \end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

