Consequently, $\cplus(X)=\cminus(X)=0$ if and only if $X$ is Gaussian with
variance $V$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:45.027361+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eb872e04d85b5e3202bd00dc814ebdc056490a2652dc2fabdeb453a7212b51b6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 of arXiv:2606.06859, 'The rectangular finite free heat flow'] For any monic degree-$d$ polynomial $p(x)$, the polynomial $p(x,t)$ defined by (\ref{eqn:BC-FFC}) satisfies the partial differential equation
