{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:46:12.281844+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ebe940cae191436b05731993f37d5564e7a14aa4c59b20718b160976700cb80f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 12 of arXiv:2604.22567, 'Sign-balance of random Laplace eigenfunctions'] There exists a number $C = C(\Mcc) >0$ such that the following holds:
$\,$

\begin{enumerate}[i.]

\item {\bf Diagonal estimate:}
For every $T\ge 1$, $\eta\in [C,T]$, $x\in\Mcc$, one has
\begin{equation*}
 K_{T,\eta}(x,x) = 1 +  O\left( \frac{1}{\eta}  \right),
\end{equation*}
where the implicit constant in the `$O$'-notation depends only on $\Mcc$.

\item {\bf Off-diagonal estimate:} 
Define 
\begin{equation}

\gamma_{d}:= \frac{1}{4}(1-d)\pi \qquad \text{and} \qquad c_{d} = \sqrt{\frac{2}{\pi}}\cdot \frac{(2\pi)^{d/2}}{V_{d}} ,
\end{equation}
with $V_{d}$ the volume of the unit $d$-ball. For $x,y\in\Mcc$ denote $r:=d(x,y)$. Then for every $T\ge 1$, $\eta\in [C,T]$, $x,y\in\Mcc$, one has
\begin{equation}

K_{T,\eta}(x,y) = c_{d}\cdot (rT)^{-\frac{d-1}{2}} \Big(\cos\left( r\cdot T + \gamma_{d} \right) + O \Big(\eta r + \frac{1}{rT} + \frac{(rT)^{\frac{d-1}{2}}}{\eta}  \Big) \Big),
\end{equation}
where the implicit constant in the `$O$'-notation depends only on $\Mcc$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:27:11.365769+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ebe97dd24122112905a9c7a8db7774156ac541fba382495ad7b8bdfef0528c55","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem t: stealthy characterization of arXiv:2605.22803, 'Persistence of asymptotic variance under transport:\\ from hyperfluctuation to stealthy hy'] Suppose that $\Phi$ is a locally absolutely square-integrable invariant random complex measure. Then $\Phi$ is $\infty$-uniform iff there is a function $f\in L^1(\R^d, \C)$ with $\lambda_d(f)\neq0$ such that $\BV[\Phi(f)]=0$. Further, if $\hat{f}(k)\neq 0$ for all $k\in B_{\varepsilon}$ and some $\varepsilon>0$, then $\Phi$ is $\infty$-uniform with radius $\varepsilon$. Conversely, if $\Phi$ is $\infty$-uniform with radius $\varepsilon>0$, then $\BV[\Phi(f)]=0$ for all $f\in L^1(\R^d, \C)$ with $\hat{f}(k)=0$ for all $k\in B_\varepsilon^c$.
\begin{proof}
    Basically, the assertions follow directly from the definition of $\infty$-uniformity with radius $\varepsilon$ and the fact that by the covariance formula~(equation e: spectral covariance formula of the paper),
    \begin{equation}
        \BV[\Phi(f)] = \frac{1}{(2\pi)^d}\int |\hat{f}(k)|^2\, \hat\beta_\Phi(dk).
    \end{equation}
    For the first assertion, one should also note that if $\lambda_d(f)\neq 0$, then $|\hat{f}(0)|^2 > 0$, which stays true in a neighborhood of $0$ since $f\in L^1$, whereby $\hat{f}$ is continuous.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:35:57.404542+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ebeb56146d521daddb9fd049a0ad3616c98bc36da92d5531c3dcb0b156d62801","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Con con1 of arXiv:2604.10446, 'Least singular values of shifted sparse random combinatorial matrices'] Let $\Omega_0$, $\Omega_r$,  and $\Omega_c$  denote the events that $M$ has a zero column, 
    that $M$ contains two identical rows, and  $M$ contains two identical columns respectively. Then 
\begin{equation}
     \mathbb{P}(\mbox{$M$ is singular})=(1+o(1))\left(\mathbb{P}(\Omega_0) + \mathbb{P}(\Omega_r) + \mathbb{P}(\Omega_c) \right).
\end{equation} (Stated as a conjecture by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:20:15.586281+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ebed0aa5518dd6e4946ff3e75aa085cf1fbfee2fa230e012fd036571daa879fd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:Cao-Zhou of arXiv:2609.00840, 'Kählerity of complete almost-Kähler gradient shrinking Ricci solitons'] [Cao--Zhou [Cao-Zhou:10]] 
