{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:54:18.300311+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ee173dd9257a40bde38db1688e9f6413e40d15764729911426bdfe4f987c1820","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop prop_ii of arXiv:2608.30382, 'Convergence rates for the RMSprop optimizer with full control of the hyperparameters'] Let $ \beta \in [ \nicefrac{ 1 }{ 2 }, 1 ) $, $ \varepsilon \in [0,\infty) $, $ n \in {\mathbb N} \backslash \{ 1 \} $, $ i \in \{1, \dots, d \} $,
let $ A_n \in \mathcal F_{ n - 1 } $, and let $ C_X^{(2)}, C_v^{(-1)} \in (0,\infty) $.
Assume that
\bas{
{\mathbb E}[(X^{(i)}(\theta_{n-1},U_n))^2|\mathcal F_{n-1}]\le C_X^{(2)}
\qquad\text{on }A_n
}
and
\bas{
{\mathbb E}[\1_{A_n}(v_{n-1}^{(i)})^{-1}]\le C_v^{(-1)}.
}
The negative-moment assumption implies that $v_{n-1}^{(i)}>0$ almost surely
on $A_n$. If $\varepsilon=0$, inverse factors used in the proof are evaluated on
$A_n$ and extended by zero to $A_n^c$.
Then one has
\bas{
{\mathbb E}\Bigl[\1_{A_n}(\sqrt{v_n^{(i)}}+\varepsilon)^{-2}
X^{(i)}(\theta_{n-1},U_n)^2\Bigr]
\le 4 C_X^{(2)} C_v^{(-1)}.
}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:35:05.801424+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ee18cb574b334b833c48a1d6c3eeff0b3ac857372a736654767caab5b1085370","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:prn_integral of arXiv:2606.23547, 'The location of the largest exponential spacing and Euler’s generalized pentagonal numbers'] For $1\le r\le n$, the probability $p_{r,n}$ admits the integral representation
\begin{equation}
p_{r,n}
=\int_0^\infty r {\rm e}^{-r t}
\prod_{\substack{1\le k\le n\\k\neq r}}
\bigl(1-{\rm e}^{-k t}\bigr)\,{\rm d}t.
\end{equation}
Equivalently,
\begin{equation}
p_{r,n}
=\int_0^1 r\,u^{r-1}
\prod_{\substack{1\le k\le n\\k\neq r}}
(1-u^k)\,{\rm d}u.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.609830+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ee18eedd90c8855cd9035ab612bb2e60dac706aab111dc9f57982b5e64677185","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor-6.4 of arXiv:2412.08964, 'Phase transition and critical behavior in hierarchical\\integer-valued Gaussian and Coulom'] Suppose~$b\theta^3<1$. Then, under Assumption~(ass-1 of the paper), for all~$\boldsymbol\lambda_{0}\in\Sigma'$,
\begin{equation}

A_1:=\biggl(\,\sup_{n\ge1}\prod_{i=0}^n\max\{b\theta_i^3,1\}\biggr)\max\biggl\{1,\sup_{q\ge0}\frac{\lambda_{0}(q+1)}{\lambda_{0}(q)}\biggr\}<\infty
\end{equation}
and, for each~$n\ge1$, the iterations~$\{\boldsymbol\lambda_k\}_{k=0}^n$ generated from $\boldsymbol\lambda_{0}$ via (equation E:1.5 of the paper) obey
\begin{equation}

\sup_{n\ge k\ge0}\sup_{q\ge0}\frac{\lambda_k(q+1)}{\lambda_k(q)}\le A_1.
\end{equation}
In particular, $F^{(k)}(\Sigma')\subseteq\Sigma'$ for each~$k\ge0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:35:05.835952+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ee19dccbd68027afbee35e8a4da3719c489440e5deac1de5e5c43fb468c45d9c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:gaussian-equivalences of arXiv:2606.25976, 'Gaussian rigidity for infinite exchangeable sequences'] Suppose that \(X_1\sim\mathcal{N}(m,\sigma^2)\).
Set \(a:=2(\sigma^2-c)\), which is nonnegative by Lemma~(lem:scalar-second-moment of the paper), and let \(\gamma_a\) denote the centered Gaussian law \(\mathcal{N}(0,a)\) on \(\mathbb{R}\).  Then the following are equivalent.
