{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:28:53.620933+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ee1fc24aa1f30907d4c2c37ea715572b55cf78db1e302b721eaef410435977a7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 3 (Phase function and its maximizer) of arXiv:2605.11995, 'Intrinsic volumes of \(\ell_p\)-balls and a continuum of Maxwell--Poincar\'e--Borel laws f'] Fix \(1<p<\infty\) and \(\beta\in(0,1)\). Define the function $\Psi_{p,\beta}:(0,\infty)\to\mathbb R$
by
\begin{equation}
\Psi_{p,\beta}(\theta)
=
\frac{1-\beta}{2}\log \theta
+\beta\log \mathcal I_p(\theta)
+(1-\beta)\log \mathcal J_p(\theta),
\qquad \theta>0.
\end{equation}
Then \(\Psi_{p,\beta}\) has a unique global maximizer \(\theta_{p,\beta}\in(0,\infty)\). Moreover, $\Psi_{p,\beta}''(\theta_{p,\beta})<0$,
and \(\theta_{p,\beta}\) is the unique solution in \((0,\infty)\) of the equation
\begin{equation}
\theta_{p,\beta}\,\frac{\mathcal J_p(\theta_{p,\beta})}{\mathcal I_p(\theta_{p,\beta})}
=
\frac{(1-\beta)p}{2(p-1)\beta}.
\end{equation}
For $p=2$, this simplifies to $\theta_{2,\beta}=(1-\beta)/\beta$ and
\begin{equation}
\Psi_{2,\beta}(\theta)=\frac{1-\beta}{2}\log\theta+\frac{1}{2}\log\pi-\frac{1}{2}\log(1+\theta),\quad \theta>0.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:31:57.847328+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ee20b70eb635df92cbc0c94ae112a51d53d558c9e34d05a88730fde565ab340a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:WicksWigner of arXiv:2510.01021, '\Large Random Matrices, Intrinsic Freeness, and Sharp Non-Asymptotic Inequalities\relatedv'] Let $W_1,\dots,W_n$ be $N\times N$ independent standard Wigner matrices (recall Definition~(def:standardWignerMatrix of the paper)). Given a partition $\nu\in\mathbb{P}[2p]$ let $u_\nu$ be the $\nu$-assignment (see Definition~(def:pairpartition of the paper)). We have
    \begin{equation}
            \mathbb{E}\left[\tr W_{u_\nu(1)}\cdots W_{u_\nu(2p)}\right]  = 1,
    \end{equation}
    if $\nu\in \mathrm{NC}[2p]$ is non-crossing, and 
\begin{equation}
            \lim_{N\to\infty} \mathbb{E}\left[\tr W_{u_\nu(1)}\cdots W_{u_\nu(2p)}\right] = 0,
    \end{equation}
if $\nu\in \mathbb{P}[2p]\setminus \mathrm{NC}[2p]$ is crossing.

    Furthermore, $W_1,\dots,W_n$ enjoy (in the limit) a Wick's formula summing only over non-crossing partitions: For $u:[2p]\to[n]$ we have
    \begin{equation}
\lim_{N\to\infty}\mathbb{E}\left[\tr W_{u(1)}\cdots W_{u(2p)}\right] = \sum_{\nu\in \mathrm{NC}_2[2p]} 1_{u \sim \nu}.
    \end{equation}
The identities (equation eq:WignerNONcrossingpairings of the paper)--(equation eq:WicksforWigner of the paper) hold for a free semicircular family $s_1,\dots,s_n$ (without needing to take a limit). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:45:07.257085+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ee21a4008daf0e24e455a355d28ccbcbbcac2fea9e29dc9060666bcaa5348bf3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 1 of arXiv:2604.19308, '\textbf{On McKean--Vlasov SDEs with polynomial drifts for SIS epidemic models}'] Let $p=1$ and~\eqref{co:1} and~\eqref{co:2} be valid. Then pathwise uniqueness holds for~\eqref{eq:SIS epidemic model}, and any two solutions $I$ and $\tilde{I}$ to~\eqref{eq:SIS epidemic model} satisfy
\begin{equation}
\E\big[|I_{t} - \tilde{I}_{t}|\big] \leq e^{\int_{0}^{t}(\hat{b}_{k+1} + \hat{\lambda}_{k+1})(s,N(s))\,\mathrm{d}s}\E\big[|I_{0} - \tilde{I}_{0}|\big]
\end{equation}
for all $t\geq 0$. In particular, if $(\hat{b}_{k+1} + \hat{\lambda}_{k+1})^{+}(\cdot,N)$ is integrable, then $\sup_{t\geq 0} \E[|I_{t} -\tilde{I}_{t}|]$ is finite. If additionally
\begin{equation*}
\int_{0}^{\infty}(\hat{b}_{k+1}+\hat{\lambda}_{k+1})^{-}(s,N(s))\,\mathrm{d}s = \infty,\quad\text{then}\quad \lim_{t\uparrow\infty} \E\big[|I_{t}-\tilde{I}_{t}|\big] = 0.
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:11:57.342192+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ee21f82289586e31cf42ec401b367aca8656f6df94fc4f3aed4cfa2aad48600f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 29 of arXiv:2607.23791, 'Precise cover times for branching random walks on Hamming graphs: (iterated) logarithmic c'] For every fixed $a>0$, every $s\ge0$, and every integer $k\ge1$,
\begin{equation}
 \int_0^\infty e^{-a\lambda u}
 (1-e^{-\lambda u})^{a(k-1)}\dd u\ll_a(1+k)^{-a}.
\end{equation}
Moreover, for $k>e^{\lambda s}$,
\begin{equation}
 \int_0^s e^{-a\lambda u}
 (1-e^{-\lambda u})^{a(k-1)}\dd u
 \ll_a(1+k)^{-a}e^{-a k e^{-\lambda s}/4}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:51:33.319807+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ee22d60b0b692daf3db594391ac50c6fb63c5fba393ce1e90ef6a5edc289c8c2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma restr-cleft-sym-mon of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $i\colon S\hookrightarrow T$ be a cleft. Then the strong symmetric monoidal functor $i^*\colon\CAT_T^\times\to\CAT_S^\times$ restricts to a strong symmetric monoidal functor $\CAT_T^{\textup{$S$-cc},\otimes}\to\CAT_S^{\textup{$S$-cc},\otimes}$.
    \begin{proof}
        This proof is an elaboration on [CLL_Clefts]*{Remark 4.23}.  Unravelling definitions, the lemma amounts to proving the following:
        \begin{enumerate}
            \item The unique $S$-cocontinuous $T$-functor $\ul\Spc_S\to i^*(\ul\Spc_{S\triangleright T})$ preserving the terminal object is an equivalence.
            \item For any ${\mathcal{C}}_1,{\mathcal{C}}_2\in\CAT_T^\text{$S$-cc}$, `the' universal $S$-bilinear map $\beta\colon{\mathcal{C}}_1\times{\mathcal{C}}_2\to{\mathcal{D}}$ forgets to the universal $S$-bilinear map $i^*{\mathcal{C}}_1\times i^*{\mathcal{C}}_2\to i^*{\mathcal{D}}$.
        \end{enumerate}
        The first statement is clear, while for the second statement it will suffice to show that for any $S$-cocomplete $S$-$\infty$-category ${\mathcal{D}}$ the induced map 
        \[
            -\circ i^*\beta\colon{\textup{Fun}}_S^\text{$S$-bilinear}(i^*{\mathcal{C}}_1\times i^*{\mathcal{C}}_2,{\mathcal{E}})\to{\textup{Fun}}_S^\text{$S$-cc}(i^*{\mathcal{D}},{\mathcal{E}})
        \]
        is an equivalence. By [CLL_Clefts]*{Construction~4.21 and Corollary~4.22}, the equivalence $\ul{\textup{Fun}}_T({\mathcal{D}},i_*{\mathcal{E}})\xrightarrow{\;\smash{\raisebox{-0.25ex}{\ensuremath{\scriptstyle\sim}}}\;} i_*\ul{\textup{Fun}}_S(i^*{\mathcal{D}},{\mathcal{E}})$ obtained as the total mate of the obvious equivalence $i^*{\mathcal{D}}\times i^*(-)\cong i^*({\mathcal{D}}\times-)$ restricts to $\ul{\textup{Fun}}_T^\text{$S$-cc}({\mathcal{D}},i_*{\mathcal{E}})\xrightarrow{\;\smash{\raisebox{-0.25ex}{\ensuremath{\scriptstyle\sim}}}\;} i_*\ul{\textup{Fun}}_S^\text{$S$-cc}(i^*{\mathcal{D}},{\mathcal{E}})$, and evaluating at the terminal presheaf $1\in\PSh(T)$ yields a natural equivalence ${\textup{Fun}}_T({\mathcal{D}},i_*{\mathcal{E}})\xrightarrow{\;\smash{\raisebox{-0.25ex}{\ensuremath{\scriptstyle\sim}}}\;} {\textup{Fun}}_S(i^*{\mathcal{D}},{\mathcal{E}})$ restricting to ${\textup{Fun}}_T^\text{$S$-cc}({\mathcal{D}},i_*{\mathcal{E}})\xrightarrow{\;\smash{\raisebox{-0.25ex}{\ensuremath{\scriptstyle\sim}}}\;} {\textup{Fun}}_S(i^*{\mathcal{D}},{\mathcal{E}})$. On the other hand, applying this observation twice, Remark~(rk:charact-bilinear of the paper) shows that also the equivalence ${\textup{Fun}}_S(i^*{\mathcal{C}}_1\times i^*{\mathcal{C}}_2,{\mathcal{E}})\xrightarrow{\;\smash{\raisebox{-0.25ex}{\ensuremath{\scriptstyle\sim}}}\;}{\textup{Fun}}_T({\mathcal{C}}_1\times{\mathcal{C}}_2,i_*{\mathcal{E}})$ restricts to subcategories of $S$-bilinear functors. In summary, we obtain a commutative square
        \[
            \begin{tikzcd}
                 {\textup{Fun}}_S^\text{$S$-bilinear}(i^*{\mathcal{C}}_1\times i^*{\mathcal{C}}_2,{\mathcal{E}})\arrow[r,"-\circ i^*\beta"]\arrow[d,"\sim"'] &[.5em] {\textup{Fun}}_S^\text{$S$-cc}(i^*{\mathcal{D}},{\mathcal{E}})\arrow[d,"\sim"]\\[-.05ex]
                 {\textup{Fun}}_T^\text{$S$-bilinear}({\mathcal{C}}_1\times {\mathcal{C}}_2,i_*{\mathcal{E}})\arrow[r,"-\circ\beta"'] & {\textup{Fun}}_T^\text{$S$-cc}({\mathcal{D}},i_*{\mathcal{E}})
            \end{tikzcd}
        \]
        The bottom map is invertible by the defining property of $\beta$, whence so is the upper map by $2$-out-of-$3$, as desired.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

