and~\eqref{BC},~\eqref{forq},~\eqref{forw}, are satisfied.

 Then the conclusion of Proposition~\ref{prop:main} follows. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:22:39.538264+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ee35e353e216b4cbd790e431bfe01fd22bf282937f3ebc0ca4f68ee64a967701","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def:model_def of arXiv:2508.18807, '{\bf Critical long-range percolation I: High effective dimension}'] Let $d\geq 1$, let $\|\cdot\|$ be a norm on $\mathbb R^d$ and let $\alpha>0$. We consider translation-invariant kernels in which $J(x,y)$ is a decreasing, differentiable function of $\|x-y\|$ when $x\neq y$ (and vanishing otherwise), so that we can write
\begin{equation}
J(x,y) = \mathbbm{1}(x\neq y) J(\|x-y\|) = \mathbbm{1}(x\neq y) \int_{\|x-y\|}^\infty |J'(s)| \dif s.

\end{equation}
We will assume moreover that
\[
|J'(r)| = C(1+\delta_r) r^{-d-\alpha-1}
\]
for some constant $C>0$ and measurable, logarithmically integrable error function\footnote{This means that $\delta_r\to 0$ as $r\to \infty$, $\delta_r$ is locally integrable on some interval of the form $[r_0,\infty)$, and $\int_{r_0}^\infty |\delta_r|\frac{\dif r}{r}<\infty$. Our terminology regarding error estimates and regularity is discussed in more detail in (subsec:regular_variation_and_logarithmic_integrability of the paper).} $\delta_r$, as is the case when $J(r)=r^{-d-\alpha}$ and $\delta_r\equiv 0$. \textbf{We may assume without loss of generality that the unit ball in $\|\cdot\|$ has unit Lebesgue measure and that $C=1$, so that \begin{equation}

\tag{$*$}
|J'(r)|\sim r^{-d-\alpha-1} \qquad \text{ and } \qquad |B_r|:=|\{x\in \mathbb Z^d:\|x\|\leq r\}|\sim r^d,\end{equation}and will do this throughout the paper.} (Any other norm and kernel of the form we consider can be normalized to satisfy (equation eq:normalization_conventions of the paper), which changes the value of $\beta_c$ but does not change other features of the model at criticality.)","status":"unverified"}

