{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:44.553911+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ee3a32d4bf49ec1429a3d368e6f5de1a779c34bfad4051b043fd4a482224e2b4","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 (Standard weak transport costs) of arXiv:2604.02312, 'A weak transport approach to the Schrödinger--Bass bridge'] A function $C : \mathcal X \times \Pcal_p(\mathcal Y) \to \R \cup \{+\infty\}$ is called a standard weak transport cost function if it satisfies the following properties:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
    \item $C$ is lower semicontinuous;
    \item $C$ is bounded from below;
    \item for each $x \in \mathcal X$, the map $\rho \mapsto C(x,\rho)$ is convex;
    \item $C$ is proper, i.e., the effective domain of $C$,
    \[
        \operatorname{dom}(C) := \bigl\{ (x,\rho) \in \mathcal X \times \mathcal P_p(\mathcal Y) : C(x,\rho) < \infty \bigr\},
    \]
    is non-empty.
\end{enumerate}

Moreover, we say that $C$ admits a $p$-growth bound, if there exist $c>0$, $x_0 \in \mathcal{X}$, $y_0 \in \mathcal Y$ such that
\begin{equation}
    C(x,\rho) \leq c \left( 1 + d_\mathcal{X}(x_0,x)^p + \int d_\mathcal{Y}(y_0,y)^p \,\rho(\rmd y)\right), \qquad \forall \, (x,\rho) \in \mathcal X\times\cP_p(\mathcal Y).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

