{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:15:08.921498+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ee3c92d9097f26ff828f2a82bf9e06b46ff42f1ee9d45afb643c220c2b08b954","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn LDC of arXiv:2411.10633, 'A Geometric Perspective on the Injective Norm of Sums of Random Tensors'] A code $\mathcal{C} : \{0,1\}^n \rightarrow \{0,1\}^d$ is $(q, \delta, \varepsilon)$-locally decodable if there exists a randomized decoding algorithm $\mathsf{Dec}(\cdot)$, which takes an $i \in [k]$ as input and given oracle access to some $y \in \{0,1\}^d$ satisfies the following properties: 
