{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.475148+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ef365c24fa91d48d039fd4220a92c691884f727cc02701e79dfcb5c601faa321","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 67 (Order-continuous ultraviolet stability) of arXiv:2606.29292, 'Intrinsic Gaussian Tensor Geometry and Random Operator Spectra'] Let $1\le r<\infty$, let $E(I)$ be an order-continuous
$r$-reconstruction lattice, and let
$(K_{\nu;i})_{\nu\in\N,i\in I}$ be block kernels with fixed stochastic
and deterministic ambient spaces.  Assume:
\begin{enumerate}
\item for every $i\in I$, the sequence $(K_{\nu;i})_\nu$ is Cauchy in
the simultaneous profile norm;
\item there is $M=(M_i)\in E(I)$ such that
\begin{equation}

 \Prof_{m,r}(K_{\nu;i})\le M_i
 \qquad(\nu\in\N,\ i\in I).
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
Then the reconstructed kernels
\[
 K_\nu:=\sum_{i\in I}K_{\nu;i}
\]
are well defined and Cauchy in the simultaneous profile completion.
If $K_i=\lim_\nu K_{\nu;i}$ blockwise, then
\begin{equation}

 K_\nu\longrightarrow K:=\sum_{i\in I}K_i
\end{equation}
in that completion.

If $2\le r<\infty$, in the corresponding labelled or symmetric same-field
setting, the same convergence holds after decoupled Gaussian or same-field
Wick evaluation in $L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)$ for every $1\le p<\infty$.  The same
conclusion holds jointly for two regularization families realized over a
common stochastic Hilbert space and satisfying the two hypotheses above
with a common majorant.  If their blockwise limits coincide, then their
reconstructed and evaluated limits coincide.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.873521+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ef36dcaceb07f1f5f300070e4c253934b47aabbcaf3e8c32ded1b267789eca52","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop choquet inegral properties of arXiv:2411.00663, 'Ergodicity and Mixing of invariant capacities and applications'] Let $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mu)$ be a capacity space, $f$ and $g$ be two $\mathcal{F}$-measurable functions. Then
	\begin{longlist}
		\item (Positive homogeneity): $\int_\Omega\alpha fd\mu=\alpha \int_\Omega fd\mu$ for each $\alpha \geq 0$.
		\item (Translation invariance): $\int_\Omega (f+\alpha 1_{\Omega})d\mu=\int_\Omega fd\mu+\alpha$ for each $\alpha \in \mathbb{R}$.
		\item (Monotonicity): $\int_\Omega fd\mu \geq \int_\Omega gd\mu$ if $f \geq g$.
	\end{longlist} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

