{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:05.000234+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ef5ebe0dc9951880dd398549b127db6a42996eb0289caa1ee510296f036c690b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 92 of arXiv:2604.01685, 'Probability with Measure\footnote{Lecture notes. University of Ljubljana.}'] Let $(\Omega,\AA,\PP)$ be a probability space, $\BB$ a sub-$\sigma$-field of $\AA$,  $f\in \AA/\mathcal{B}_{[-\infty,\infty]}$, $\PP[f^+]\land \PP[ f^-]<\infty$.  Then there exists a up to $\PP$-a.s. equality unique $g\in \BB/\mathcal{B}_{[-\infty,\infty]}$ with $\PP [g^+]\land \PP [g^-]<\infty$ such that 
