{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:54:28.101201+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_efd3a948ded9b5aea6d77c6ebde05d6d0cfdc1c539b66c4ba6ec44daedc5bca7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:DAGthree_structures of arXiv:2608.13716, 'Graph Causal Optimal Transport \\ and Wasserstein Distances'] Let $G = (V, E)$ be a directed graph and $i, j, k \in V$.
    \begin{itemize}
        \item If $j \in \mathrm{pa}_{k}$ and $i \in \mathrm{pa}_{j}$ but $i \notin \mathrm{pa}_{k}$ we call the triplet $(i,j,k)$ a \emph{chain}. If no such triplet exists, we say $G$ is \emph{transitively closed}. 
        \item If $i \in \mathrm{pa}_{j} \cap \mathrm{pa}_{k}$ but $j \notin \mathrm{pa}_{k}$ and $k \notin \mathrm{pa}_{j}$, we call the triplet $(i,j,k)$ a \emph{split}. If no such triplet exists, we say $G$ contains \emph{no splits}.
        \item If $j \in \mathrm{pa}_{i}$ and $k\in \mathrm{pa}_{i}$ but $j \notin \mathrm{pa}_{k}$ and $k \notin \mathrm{pa}_{j}$, we call the triplet $(i,j,k)$ a \emph{merge}. If no such triplet exists, we say $G$ is \emph{perfect}.
    \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

