\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:24:45.970753+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eff1f38002dc429fd05e86f0f960bb34c5ea59ea9092c39d7f12bc00b48f4c5e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:R1a of arXiv:2509.17319, 'Non-directed polymers in random environments with range penalties: the high-dimensional ca'] Let $\alpha\in(2\vee\frac{d}{2},d)$ (in particular $d\geq 3$) and $(\zeta,\gamma)\in R_1$ (see Figure (diagram2 of the paper)). Then $(S_n)_{0\leq n \leq N}$ has end-to-end fluctuations of order $N^{\xi}$ under ${\ensuremath{\mathbf P}} _N$ with $\xi=\frac12$. Without loss of generality, we assume that ${\rm Var_\mathbb{P}}(\omega)=1$. Take $a_N=N^{1/4}$ for $d=3$, $a_N=(\log N)^{1/2}$ for $d=4$, and $a_N=1$ for $d\geq 5$. Then as $N\to\infty$,
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{a_N\beta_N}\left({\ensuremath{\mathbf Z}} _N-{\ensuremath{\mathbf E}} \left[e^{(\frac{1}{2}\beta_N^2-h_N)|{\ensuremath{\mathcal R}} _N|}\right]\right)\overset{(d)}{\longrightarrow}\begin{cases}
{\ensuremath{\mathcal N}} (0,\sigma_d^2),\quad&\text{for}~d=3,4,\\
{\ensuremath{\mathcal X}} ,\quad&\text{for}~d\geq5,
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where ${\ensuremath{\mathcal N}} (0,\sigma_d^2)$ is a centered Gaussian distribution with an explicit variance $\sigma_d^2$ and
\begin{equation}
{\ensuremath{\mathcal X}} :=\sum\limits_{x\in{\ensuremath{\mathbbm Z}} ^d}\omega_x{\ensuremath{\mathbf P}} (x\in{\ensuremath{\mathcal R}} _\infty)={\ensuremath{\mathbf E}} \left[\sum\limits_{x\in{\ensuremath{\mathcal R}} _\infty}\omega_x\right],
\end{equation}
which is a well-defined random variable. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.575981+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eff56ddc7873c1aef752c04f5ca1ec3266d91e593d31ef97ce2b3143d3ee2833","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem-inequality for H of arXiv:2412.08932, 'Gaussian heat kernel asymptotics for conditioned random walks'] For any $x\geqslant 0$ and $\varepsilon\geqslant0$, we have 
