{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:13:22.518670+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f0513858986b9b00b5c45305258c4423bdbe68d495bf8b1bfe902c1d93cc68a2","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Merger of arXiv:2505.16585, 'Expanded regimes of area law for lattice Yang--Mills theories'] Let $\ell_i, \ell_j$ be loops of $s$ which both contain some lattice edge $e$. Thus, $\ell_i = a \mbf{e} b$ and $\ell_j = c \mbf{e}' d$, where $\mbf{e}, \mbf{e}'$ are the same lattice edge. Define $\ell_i \oplus_{\mbf{e}, \mbf{e}'} \ell_j := a \mbf{e}' d c \mbf{e} b$. The \textit{positive merger} of $s$ at $(\mbf{e}, \mbf{e}')$ is defined to be the string $s' := ([\ell_i \oplus_{\mbf{e}, \mbf{e}'} \ell_j], \ell_1, \ldots, \hat{\ell}_i, \ldots, \hat{\ell}_j, \ldots, \ell_n)$, where here $\hat{\ell}$ denotes omission.

Similarly, if $\ell_i = a \mbf{e} b$, $\ell_j = c \mbf{e}' d$ where $\mbf{e}, (\mbf{e}')^{-1}$ are the same lattice edge, then define $\ell_i \ominus_{\mbf{e}, \mbf{e}'} \ell_j := adcb$. The \textit{negative merger} of $s$ at $(\mbf{e}, \mbf{e}')$ is defined to be the string $s' := ([\ell_i \oplus_{\mbf{e}, \mbf{e}'} \ell_j], \ell_1, \ldots, \hat{\ell}_i, \ldots, \hat{\ell}_j, \ldots, \ell_n)$.

Given an edge $\mbf{e}$ of $s$, we let $\mbb{M}_{+}(s, \mbf{e})/\mbb{M}_-(s, \mbf{e})$ be the sets of all positive/negative mergers of $s$ at $(\mbf{e}, \mbf{e}')$ for some other edge $\mbf{e}'$. We will frequently use the notation $\mbb{M}_{\pm}(s, \mbf{e})$ to denote the set of positive or negative mergers of $s$ at $\mbf{e}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:53:30.575163+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f05445c8eb9cf47ca4dc0cdfed0b35b3f25ca3d80dab23056628c8fb59fff921","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem-extension of probability space of arXiv:2505.24043, 'Weak solutions of Navier-Stokes Equation with purely discontinuous L\'evy Noise'] Let  $(\mathbf{X},\mathscr{B}_\mathbf{X})$ and $(\mathbf{U}, \mathcal{U})$ be two measurable spaces.
If
\[
\theta\colon  (0,\infty) \times \mathbf{U}\times \Omega \to \mathbf{X}
\]
is a generalized predictable  process, then
the process $\tilde{\theta}$ defined by
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\theta}\colon  (0,\infty) \times \mathbf{U}\times \tilde{\Omega} \ni (t,u,\tilde{\omega}) \mapsto \theta\bigl(t,u,\pi(\tilde{\omega})\bigr)\in  {\color{teal}\mathbf{X}}
\end{equation}
is a generalized predictable  process, see [Ikeda+Watanabe_1989].","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:38:11.778629+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f057416d43f0f5482aaa0133cba96556e16990c5e57795a632c1b6d46d7be45d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Théorème Fixation RMS of arXiv:2504.03627, 'Richardson’s model and the contact process with stirring: long time behavior.'] Let $\lambda>0$ and $(\xi_t)_{t\ge 0}$ be $\mathrm{RMS}(\lambda,1)$ starting from a non-empty initial configuration.
