For $\mathsf{P}\in \mathcal P_{s,1}(\Gamma)$ with $\int \xi(\Lambda_1)^2\mathsf{P}(d\xi)=\infty$ the limit is given by $\mathcal{W}^{int}(\mathsf{P})=\infty$.
For $\beta>0$ the free energy  $\mathcal F_{\beta}(\mathsf{P}):=\beta \mathcal{W}^{int}(\mathsf{P})+\mathcal E(\mathsf{P})$
is lower semicontinuous w.r.t.\ the topology $\mathcal T_{\mathcal L}$. Moreover, by assumption (assumption0 of the paper), the functional $\mathcal F_{\beta}$ is bounded from below by a finite constant. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:14:57.689520+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f06029be9b7a9a8fab2178c6cb71e16e1a0ce6b4810f0f9d9a386c0a2f8cb7d7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop conv_compositions of arXiv:2605.29073, 'Volterra clocks and their pure-jump limits: \\hitting times of curved boundaries'] Consider $x^n=\phi^n\circ \tau^n$ and $x=\phi\circ \tau$. If $(\phi^n,\tau^n) \rightarrow (\phi,\tau) $ in $D^\circ[0,T]$, then $\sup_{n\geq 1}\sup_{t\in[0,T]}|x_n(t)|<\infty$ and
