    where we used the construction of the space $\A_N$ of Lemma \ref{lem:bigspace} to define the product of $B^N_0$ and $b(t)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:19:42.477349+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f0a67e5dac78862baec6b1bdafd898ff56115f6a5d2cc37e12445334e8d99c24","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 (Single-edge expansion) of arXiv:2609.03466, 'Least-Favorable Location for Binomial Top-$t$ Selection'] The rate $I_1$ is invariant under $p\mapsto T(p)$ and has its unique minimum at $\pc$. At the center,
\begin{equation}
 \Jn:=I_1(\pc)=-\log(1-\delta^2),
 \qquad
 I_1''(\pc)=\frac{8\delta^2}{(1-\delta^2)^2}.

\end{equation}
On some closed interval $U\subset(0,1-\delta)$ centered at $\pc$, the single-edge probability has the strong expansion
\begin{equation}
 a_n(p)=n^{-1/2}e^{-nI_1(p)}
 \{A_1(p)+o_{C^2(U)}(1)\}.

\end{equation}
Here $A_1(p)$ is smooth, positive, and invariant under $p\mapsto T(p)$. At $p=\pc$,
\begin{align}
 a_n(\pc)
 &=A_{1,\delta}n^{-1/2}e^{-n\Jn}\{1+o(1)\},
 \notag\\
 a_n'(\pc)&=0,
 \\
 a_n''(\pc)
 &=-I_1''(\pc)A_{1,\delta}n^{1/2}e^{-n\Jn}\{1+o(1)\},

\end{align}
where
\begin{equation}
 A_{1,\delta}:=A_1(\pc)=\frac1{2\delta\sqrt\pi}.

\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:04:41.059194+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f0aa2acc2ffe3c1e23dd86d15faa416ebc9d7cb6c69c110316275e96b11fb022","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:flu_ssep of arXiv:2412.04032, 'Cutoff for the mixing time of the Facilitated Exclusion Process'] [lacoin_simple_2017]

There exists $c>0$ such that for $N$ sufficiently large, for all $\sigma \in \Gamma_{K,P}$ and $s \ge 16$, if $t \ge \frac{1}{8\pi^2}K^2\log P$,
 \eqs{\textnormal{\textbf{P}}_\sigma\left(\exists k,l \in \mathbb{T}_K, \left|\sum_{j=k}^l \left(\sigma_j(t) - \frac{P}{K}\right) \right| \ge s \sqrt{P}\right) \le 2 \exp(-cs^2).} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:45:10.135033+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f0ac12823f0c33f92172cad2cda8552376107b204754940cfd0b2d93fa438878","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:poisson_zoo of arXiv:2505.07145, 'Nonamenable Poisson zoo'] Given the $\Gamma$-rotation invariant measure $\nu := \nu_{o}$ and some intensity parameter $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}_{>0}$, let us consider independent Poisson random variables $N_{x,H}^{\lambda} \sim \mathsf{Poisson}( \lambda \cdot \nu_x(H) )$ indexed by $x \in V(G)$ and $H \in \mathcal{H}_x$.
	We say that $N_{x,H}^{\lambda}$ is the number of copies of the animal $H$ (rooted at $x$).
	We call the collection of random variables
	\begin{equation}

		\mathcal{N}_{\nu}^\lambda := 
		\left\{ 
			N^\lambda_{x,H} \, : \, x \in V(G), H \in \mathcal{H}_x 
		\right\}
	\end{equation}
	the {\bf Poisson zoo} at level $\lambda$ (with measure $\nu$) and the random set
	\begin{equation}

		\mathcal{S}_{\nu}^\lambda := 
		\bigcup_{x \in V} \bigcup_{H \in \mathcal{H}_x} \bigcup_{i = 1}^{ N_{x,H}^{\lambda} } \, H
	\end{equation}
	the {\bf trace} of the Poisson zoo at level $\lambda$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:36:16.064186+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f0acb111c3ef60fe19677487b5fc12b6ed5118635065ccabbca100039498931c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemmaNeu1 of arXiv:2606.28067, 'Ergodic approximation for the invariant distribution: An abstract framework for law-depend'] Let $t-s<1$, $x\in{\mathbb R}^+$ and $\mu\in\mathcal{P}_0({\mathbb R}^+)$ (i.e.  a probability measure on ${\mathbb R}_+$). For $q\geq 1$, the following $L^q$ estimates hold:
		\begin{align}
			&\big|{\mathbb E}[\mathcal{X}_{s,r}(x,\mu)^ q]- x^ q\big|\leq C_q(1+x^ q)(t-s)\\
			&{\mathbb E}[|\mathcal{X}_{s,r}(x,\mu)-x|^q]\leq C_q(1+x^ q)(t-s)
		\end{align}
		where $C_q$ depends on $q$, the drift $b$, $J$ and $\|f\|_\infty$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.434411+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f0b568f758dcb86583c96e1f03e73a6f11c823428d93c063f707a0ffe2864766","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: MainHaarDisc of arXiv:2603.26932, 'On the satisfaction frequency of spectral characterization conditions'] Let $\bM\sim \operatorname{SymHaar}(\widehat{R}^{n\times n})$. 
     Then, for every fixed prime $p$, 
     \begin{align}
        &\lim_{n\to \infty}\bbP\Bigl(\coker(\bM - x\bI ) \textnormal{ satisfies condition }\cD_p^1 \Bigr) = \Bigl( 1 - \frac{1}{p}\Bigr) \prod_{i=1}^\infty\Bigl(1 - \frac{1}{p^{2i}}\Bigr) , \\
        &\lim_{n\to \infty}\bbP\Bigl(\coker(\bM - x\bI ) \textnormal{ satisfies condition } \cD_p^1 \textnormal{ or }\cD_p^2 \Bigr)= \Bigl(1 - \frac{1}{p^2} \frac{3p-1}{p+1}\Bigr)\prod_{i=1}^\infty\Bigl(1 - \frac{1}{p^{2i}}\Bigr).  
     \end{align}
     Moreover, let $\cD^*$ denote the condition that $\cD_p^1$ or $\cD_p^{2}$ is satisfied for every odd prime $p$, and that $\cD_p^1$ is satisfied for $p=2$. 
     Then, 
     \begin{align}
        \lim_{n\to \infty}\bbP\Bigl(\coker(\bM - x\bI ) \textnormal{ satisfies condition }\cD^*    \Bigr) = \frac{6}{7} \prod_{\textnormal{primes }p}\Bigl(1 - \frac{1}{p^2} \frac{3p-1}{p+1}\Bigr)\prod_{i=1}^\infty\Bigl(1 - \frac{1}{p^{2i}}\Bigr). 
     \end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:44:28.010711+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f0bba656f7f137031b2b8a60c55902f63ada14f91e0276a3314e563b1a4ca05a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 15 of arXiv:2607.13308, 'No infinite clusters at criticality for two-dimensional dependent percolation under abstra'] Assume $\beta>3$. Given $\varepsilon \in (0, \beta-3)$, there exists $\uc{c:correlation_decay_gf}>0$ such that, for all $n \in \N$, if $A$ and $B$ are events supported in $B(0, 3n)$ and $B(0, 4n)^{c}$, respectively, then
    \begin{equation}
        \Cov_{\lambda} \big( \textnormal{\textbf{1}}_{A}, \textnormal{\textbf{1}}_{B}) \leq \uc{c:correlation_decay_gf} n^{-\beta+3+\varepsilon}.
    \end{equation}
    In particular,~\eqref{h4} is satisfied if $\beta> 3$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:51:30.071329+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f0c017558d28dc2c7645f7f3c2f9e8b187e2ea0d701a61af472abb3d9886aa79","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 13 (Theorem~\ref{maintheorem} (G)) of arXiv:2606.01832, 'MA(1) processes with uniform innovations conditioned to stay positive in the non-expanding'] For $y>\frac{1}{\theta}$, the limiting process $M'$ exists and is a Markov chain on $S$ with transition kernel
    \begin{align*}
    p'(x_1,\d x_2)&\coloneqq\mathds{1}_S(x_2,x_1)\frac{h(x_2)}{h(x_{1})}\frac{1}{\lambda}\frac{1}{a+1}\,\d x_2,~~~~~~~~x_1,x_2\in \bigg[\frac{1}{\theta},1\bigg],
\end{align*}
where $\lambda$ is the persistence exponent of $(p_n^{a,y}(\theta))$ and 
\begin{align*}
         h(x)=\begin{cases}
           E\left(\theta,\frac{-\theta x}{\lambda (a+1)}\right),&\frac{1}{\theta}\leq \theta x< 1,
            \\E\left(\theta,\frac{-1}{ \lambda \theta(a+1)}\right),&~ \theta x< \frac{1}{\theta},
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
where $\lambda:=\tilde \lambda \, \frac{1-\frac{1}{\theta}}{a+1}$ and $\tilde\lambda$ as defined in \eqref{root_blue} for $a=\frac{-1}{\theta}$.","status":"unverified"}

