{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:57:30.959647+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f124ba62d2c21e3dfdb85d193560bb2969104af113d4df96be6c77c92137d7bf","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 8 of arXiv:2608.12070, 'Central limit theorems for a heavy-tailed subcritical Galton-Watson process with state-dep'] Let $\{Z_n, n\geq 1\}$ be a sequence of i.i.d. random variables whose tails are regularly varying with index $1<\alpha<2$ (as in \eqref{add}), satisfying $\E Z_1=0$. Assume there exist $p, q \in [0, 1]$ such that 
\begin{align} 
\frac{\P(Z>x)}{\P(|Z|>x)} \to p, \quad \frac{\P(Z<-x)}{\P(|Z|>x)} \to q, \quad x\to \infty.
\end{align}
Let $\{t_n, n\geq1\}$ be a sequence of positive, integer-valued random variables. Suppose there exists a sequence of positive integers $\{b_n\}$ and a positive constant $c$ such that $t_n/b_n \overset{\P}{\to} c$ and $b_n \to \infty$. Define $S_n = \sum_{i=1}^n Z_i$. Then, as $n \to \infty$,
\begin{align*}
\frac{S_{t_n}}{a_{t_n}} \overset{d}{\to} S_\alpha(\rho, 0),
\end{align*}
where $\{a_n\}$ is a sequence satisfying $n a_n^{-\alpha}L(a_n) \to 1$, $\rho = p - q$, and $S_\alpha(\rho, 0)$ is an $\alpha$-stable random variable with characteristic function
\[
\exp\left(-|t|^\alpha \Gamma(1-\alpha)\cos\frac{\pi\alpha}{2} \left(1 + i\rho\, \mathrm{sgn}(t)\tan\frac{\pi\alpha}{2}\right)\right).
\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:27:08.851381+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f125041d0ae1e2ba6de8e7bb448e0c0be544477a42667585bdab005579422982","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 9 of arXiv:2511.11189, 'Sharp asymptotics for the maximal distance from the boundary to the nucleus of a typical P'] The expectation \(C_d\) given in identity (\ref{eq_C_d_as_expectation}) satisfies the recursive relation:
		\begin{equation}
			C_d = \frac{1}{2(d-1)} \left( \frac{B\left(\frac{d}{2},\frac{1}{2}\right)}{B\left(\frac{d-1}{2},\frac{1}{2}\right)} \right)^{d-1} C_{d-1}.
		\end{equation}
		As a consequence,
		\begin{equation}
			C_d = 		\frac{1}{2^{d-1}\sqrt{\pi}(d-1)!}\frac{\Gamma\left(\frac{d}{2}\right)^{d}}{\Gamma\left(\frac{d+1}{2}\right)^{d-1}}.
		\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:13:22.534305+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f1253e3029ecf8ce399341e0011b1b53675566ce62285860963d014407e33bd0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop pr:key_estimate of arXiv:2506.07158, 'The diffusivity of supercritical Bernoulli percolation is infinitely differentiable'] Given $i,j\in \mathbb{N}$ and $\alpha \geqslant 0$, then for every $\mathsf{r} \in (\frac{50}{27}, 2)$, and every $N,k \in \mathbb{N}$ satisfying
		\begin{align}
			N \geqslant i+j, \qquad k \geqslant \max\{h, h_*(\alpha,i,j)\} + 12(i+j) + 2,
		\end{align}
		there exists a constant $C(i,j,\alpha,h,N,k,d,\mathsf{r})$ independent of $m \in \mathbb{N}_+$ such that the following statement holds: for every ${\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m \in \mathcal{G}^{(N)}_{k}$, we have the estimate when the canonical grain (equation def.ConstantExtension_canonical of the paper) is applied to $v_m$ in ${\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m$ 
		\begin{multline}

			\frac{1}{|{\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m|}\sum_{\substack{|F|=i\\F\subseteq E_d({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m)}}\left(\sum_{\substack{{\scaleobj{1}{\square}} \in \mathcal{P}^{(N,k)}_{h}({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m)}} |{\scaleobj{1}{\square}}|^{\alpha}\sum_{e\in E_d({\scaleobj{1}{\square}})} |\nabla V_{m}(F, j)(e)|^2 \right) \\ \leqslant C(i,j,\alpha,h,N,k,d,\mathsf{r}) .
		\end{multline} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:42:55.309381+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f127427b42094807926601fdb8cb495d719044f799dd942d9e2cc937a5b32c1a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 3 of arXiv:2609.02392, 'Large values of cubic and quartic Dirichlet $L$-functions of prime moduli'] With the notation as above, let $j=3$, $4$ and $\psi$ be any ray class character modulo $9$ (resp. $16$) when $j=3$ (resp. $j=4$) and let $r$ be any primary element in $\mathcal O_K$. Then we have for real $Z>1$ and $\varepsilon>0$, we have
\begin{align*}

\begin{split}
  H_Z(r;\psi) := \sum_{\substack {c \odd \\ (c, r)=1 \\ N(c) \leq Z}} \frac {\Lambda_K(c)\psi(c) g_{K,j}(r, c) }{\sqrt{N(c)}}  \ll &
\begin{cases}
   \min (Z^{1+\varepsilon},  N(r)^{1/10+\varepsilon}Z^{4/5+\varepsilon}), & j=3, \\ \\
   \min (Z^{1+\varepsilon},  N(r)^{1/16+\varepsilon}Z^{7/8+\varepsilon}), & j=4.
\end{cases}
\end{split}
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:45.025084+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f127d1d2fb0fc6b0c9108139d12e9b7aed98ac5ec5d9d04b7175919569a0d693","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 5 of arXiv:2606.06757, 'Path-Dependent Ergodic Optimal Control \\ and Backward Stochastic Differential Equations'] We say that $(Y, Z, \Lambda) \in \mathbf{E}(\C_{\theta}) \times {\rm{UC}}$ is a solution of the \textnormal{ergodic backward SDE} if:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item[{\rm (i)}] For all $\xi \in \mathcal{C}_{\theta}$, the triple $(Y^{\xi}, Z^{\xi}, \Lambda)$ solves the infinite-horizon backward SDE \eqref{EBackwards SDE}.

        \item[{\rm (ii)}] There exists a constant $C > 0$ such that:
        \vspace{-6pt}
    \begin{equation}
    \begin{array}{cc}
         | Y^\xi_t | \leq C ( 1 + | \mathcal{X}^{\xi}_t |_{\theta}) \,  & \text{for all } \,  t \geq 0 \, .
    \end{array}
    \end{equation}
    \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:31:20.585402+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f12944c3bee481f52bb5934790387ea9b6409a3d910ca1deee4d1f03dd1fc5d9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 14 of arXiv:2608.25094, 'Noising-Denoising by Large Temperature Schr\"{o}dinger Bridges'] Let $(M,g)$ be a compact manifold with reference process having a spectral gap $\lambda_1> 0$. For $\mu,\nu \in \cP_2(M)$ satisfying Assumption \ref{assumption},   
    \begin{align}
        \limsup\limits_{T \to +\infty} \exp(\lambda_{1} T)(H(Q^{T}|P^{T})+H(P^{T}|Q^{T})) < +\infty. 
    \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:16:53.591274+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f12b9fcb72fef8d9d7a7c95da12e265f13ed45fe5d0bdad3af90c3ff26369b9e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:timeChange of arXiv:2412.04973, 'On the Laplace equation with non-local dynamical boundary conditions'] Let $\Lambda>0$, $l \geq 0$, $k <0$ and $\alpha \in (0,1]$. The solution to (equation ELambda of the paper) has the probabilistic representation
\begin{align*}
u(t,x) 
= & \mathbf{E}_x\left[\mathbf{E}_{X^+_\tau}[u_0(X^\Gamma \circ L_t)]\right]\\
= & \mathbf{E}_x\left[e^{-\Lambda L_t} \mathbf{E}_{X^+_\tau}[u_0(X^{0, \Gamma} \circ L_t)]\right], \quad t\geq 0,\; x \in \overline{\Omega}.
\end{align*}
In particular,
\begin{align*}
u(t,x) 
= & \int_0^\infty e^{-\Lambda s} \mathbf{E}_x\left[\mathbf{E}_{X^+_\tau}[u_0(X^{0, \Gamma}_s)]\right]  \mathbf{P}_0(L_t \in ds)\\
= & \int_0^\infty e^{-\Lambda s} w(s,x) \mathbf{P}_0(L_t \in ds)
\end{align*}
where $w$ is the solution to (equation EsolVVw of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:20:07.929352+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f12bcdbea605f0e703ed69950903460916014965d87170aab80303e26f398020","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prob prob_meet_terms of arXiv:2608.29914, 'Fixed points in abstract provability structures'] Let $S$ be a meet APS satisfying \textup{(C3*)}, and let $\triangle v$ be a one-variable meet APS term such that, for every subterm of the form $\triangle_0 v \land \triangle_1 v$, the degrees $d(\triangle_0)$ and $d(\triangle_1)$ have the same parity.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item If $d(\triangle)$ is odd and $\mathsf{E}_S(\triangle)$ holds, does $\mathsf{E}_S(\boxtimes)$ hold?

    \item If $S$ is consistent and $d(\triangle)$ is even, then is $\triangle\top$ non-refutable in $S$?

    \item If $S$ is consistent, $d(\triangle)$ is odd, and $p$ is a fixed point of $\triangle v$, then is $p$ non-refutable in $S$?
\end{enumerate} (Stated as a problem by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:43:44.239611+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f12c84865842cc3b16782d0c3a8325594830c1c5fed13b2fa20de06766f64a64","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 11 of arXiv:2604.14543, '2604.14543'] Under $\mathbf{Assumptions}$ $\mathbf{2}$-$\mathbf{4}$, there exists $h^{**}\in(0,1)$  with the following property: for any step size  $h\in(0,h^{**})$ and any two initial values $\hat{X}_{0}^{N,j,h}=x$, $\hat{Y}_{0}^{N,j,h}=y$, the EM scheme $\{\hat{X}_{kh}^{N,j,h}\}
$ and $\{\hat{Y}_{kh}^{N,j,h}\}$ defined by scheme (\ref{b-2}) satisfy, for $k=1,2,\cdots,\lfloor\frac{T}{h}\rfloor$ and $j=1,2,\cdots,N$,
$$
\mE\left|\hat{X}_{kh}^{N,j,h}-\hat{Y}_{kh}^{N,j,h}\right|^2\leq\mE|x-y|^2e^{-\xi_1 hk},
$$
where $\xi_1=\alpha-\beta-(a+b)h^{**}>0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:49:06.337572+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f12ce40759b8e20a58eaf953d0fd9a78173f1d07dd58c0caabafb7f5e6435b22","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:RiemannSums of arXiv:2601.19630, 'Mass generation for the two dimensional O(N) Linear Sigma Model in the large N limit'] Let $f: \xi \in \mathbb{R}^{2} \to f(\xi) \in [0, \infty)$ be smooth, radial, decreasing in $|\xi|$, and integrable.  Then it holds that
\begin{equation}
\left(\frac{2\pi}{L} \right)^{2}\sum_{\xi \in \Lambda_{L}^{*}}f(\xi) \geq \frac{1}{4}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{2}}f(\xi)d\xi. 
\end{equation}
Furthermore, the following bound holds
\begin{equation}
\bigg |\int_{\mathbb{R}^{2}}f(\xi)d\xi -\left(\frac{2\pi}{L} \right)^{2}\sum_{\xi \in \Lambda_{L}^{*}}f(\xi) \bigg | \leq 3 \cdot \left(\frac{2\pi}{L} \right)^{2}f(0)+4 \cdot\left(\frac{2\pi}{L} \right) \int_{0}^{\infty} f(\eta_{1},0)d \eta_{1}. 
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:38:13.637359+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f12d2b1f8a2dd174f2e208e7010525fccbc241b3d8efe902e7749095b9ef3cda","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 10 of arXiv:2609.00680, 'Sharp embeddings between quasi-Banach Besov spaces and shallow ReLU Variation spaces'] Fix $A>0$ and an integer $R_0>k+d+2$. Let $0<h\leq1$,
$x_0\in\mathbb R^d$, and $\psi\in C_c^\infty(\R^d)$ satisfy
$\supp\psi\subset B(0,A)$. Set
\begin{equation}

\psi_h(x):=\psi\left(\frac{x-x_0}{h}\right).
\end{equation}
The following estimate holds:
\begin{equation}
\|\psi_h\|_{\mathcal{L}_1(\mathcal{D})}
\lesssim h^{-k}
\max_{|\alpha|\leq R_0}\|D^\alpha\psi\|_{L^\infty(\mathbb R^d)}.
\end{equation}
The implicit constant may depend on $d$, $k$, $A$, $R_0$, and $\Omega$,
but is independent of $h$, $x_0$, and $\psi$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:49:15.415656+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f12e4101bdd970d23e5eb268a40a37c1cf75591e00914d401e97d3fdf28bddfc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma component-epsilon of arXiv:2608.27904, 'Instantons, indefinite 4-manifolds, and Dehn surgery'] If $W: (Y, \mathfrak a) \to (Y', \mathfrak a')$ is a nice cobordism, and $\theta \in \mathfrak Z(Y) \cong H^1(Y;\mathbb F_2)$, the map $\varepsilon_0$ is given by 
