{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:44.548273+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f151e1e3c46e2073e430223bdb694b5c5cfe9eae28712c54dabe458c86a125d9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 36 of arXiv:2604.01852, 'The on/off Brownian snake'] Let $x \in E$ and let $h>0$. The snake $X$ under the probability measure $\mathbb{N}_x(\cdot | T_h<\infty)$ can be expressed as the concatenation of the following two snakes.
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $(\zeta(X_t), 0 \leq t \leq T_h(X))$ has the distribution $P_0^{\text{Bes}(3)}$. Conditional on $(\zeta(X_t), 0 \leq t \leq T_h(X))$ being given by $g \coloneqq (g_t)_{0 \leq t \leq T_h(g)}$, the process $(X_t, 0 \leq t \leq T_h(X))$ is a time-inhomogeneous snake with distribution $\mathbb{P}_{x|g}(\cdot)$.
        \item Conditional on $X_{T_h(X)}=w$ for an $E$-valued killed path $w$, the processes $(X_t, 0 \leq t \leq T_h(X))$ and $(X_{T_h(X)+t}, t \geq 0)$ are independent and the latter has law $\mathbb{P}_w(\cdot)$.
    \end{enumerate}
    \begin{proof}
        The proof follows almost immediately from Theorem \ref{theorem bivariate brownian snake} and the representation of the excursion measure $n$ for Brownian motion given in Definition \ref{definition brownian excursion}. The second part follows by the Markov property of the time-inhomogeneous snake $X$ conditionally given the contour process and of the decomposition $\mathbb{P}_w(\cdot) = \int_g P^R_{\zeta(w)}(dg)\mathbb{P}_{w|g}(\cdot)$.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:24:25.987206+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f1550d974adc8777aeb3e2ddedea1cfb43d8038705d7948ca6e347fe5a705c98","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 53 (cf. {\cite[Theorem 1.4) of arXiv:2507.07702, 'Observables of random spanning trees in random environment'] {Mak24}} and {\cite[Theorem 1.8]{K24}}] 
      Let $\cT^\omega_{n, \beta}$ be the RSTRE as in Assumption \ref{As:K_n_U}. If $0 < \beta = \beta(n) \ll n/\log n$, then with high probability (and in $\P$-expectation)
    \begin{equation} 
       \bE^\omega_{n,\beta} \big[ L(\cT) \big]= (1 + o(1)) \frac{n}{\beta} \cdot \frac{1 - \beta e^{-\beta} - e^{-\beta}}{1- e^{-\beta}}.
    \end{equation}
    If on the other hand $\beta \gg n (\log n)^5$, then with high probability (and in $\P$-expectation)
    \begin{equation} 
        \bE^\omega_{n,\beta} \big[ L(\cT) \big] = (1 + o(1)) \zeta(3),
    \end{equation}
    where $\zeta(3) = \sum_{k=1}^\infty k^{-3} = 1.202\dots$ is Apéry's constant. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:45:17.711512+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f1552233599903e4280bbc5b06ad003d7179b8db4b1ca3d22852bdaeab0efc97","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem LOT of arXiv:2604.11761, 'The smallest singular value of signed random combinatorial matrices'] For any $b>0$ and $\gamma \in(0,1)$ there exists $C>0$ depending only on $b$ and $\gamma$ for which the following holds. Let $v \in \mathbb{R}^n$ such that $\|\mathrm{D}(v)\|_2 \geq b \sqrt{n}$. Then for every $\alpha>0$ and $\varepsilon \geq 0$, we have

\begin{align*}
\mathcal{L}\left(W_{v}, \varepsilon\right) \leq C \varepsilon+\frac{C}{\operatorname{CLCD}_{\alpha, \gamma}(v)}+C e^{-2 \alpha^2 / n}.
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:03.902306+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f15a020834cedd8c51ed1d5630751e9c415c0796a2d1afed8f61579e2848baba","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 17 of arXiv:2606.20908, 'On It\^o-Stratonovich formula for rough sheets'] Let $x\in \mathcal{P}_{1,1}^{\ga_1,\ga_2}$ with $1/2<\ga_1<1/3$, $\ga_2>1/2$ and  $\varphi\in C^4(\mathbb R)$.

    With our Hypotheses \ref{hyp:rs1} and \ref{hyp:rs2} in mind,  let $\zeta$ be a field controlled by $x$ as in Definition \ref{def:controlled-process}. Then
