{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:48:25.220107+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f1e7a1a5da525831f31811c2c60b31c90eb90414e45c2d1427ff212180042afe","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem: 378 of arXiv:2607.05848, 'Mesoscopic eigenvalue statistics for correlated random matrices'] {MR3678478}; [MR3941370]; [EHR25]}]
For a positive integer $L$, let $f:\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}\to\mathbb{C}$ be an $L$ times differentiable function with bounded derivatives. Let $W=(W_{\alpha})_{\alpha\in[N]^{2}}$ be an $N\times N$ random matrix, whose normalized cumulants satisfy Assumption (assump: 140 of the paper).
We use the notation $\partial_{\alpha}\coloneqq\frac{\partial}{\partial W_{\alpha}}$ for every $\alpha\in [N]^{2}$.
Let $\mathcal{N}(\alpha)$ be as in Assumption (assump: 140 of the paper) (iv).
Then, for any index $\alpha_{0}\in[N]^{2}$,
\begin{equation*}
	\mathbb{E}[W_{\alpha_{0}}f(W)] = \sum_{k=0}^{L-1} \sum_{\boldsymbol{\alpha}\in\mathcal{N}^{k}(\alpha_{0})}
	\frac{\kappa(\alpha_{0},\boldsymbol{\alpha})}{N^{(k+1)/2}k!}\mathbb{E}[\partial_{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}f(W)] + \mathcal{E}_{L}(f,\alpha_{0}),
\end{equation*}
where $\boldsymbol{\alpha}\coloneqq(\alpha_{1},\dots,\alpha_{k})$ and
$\partial_{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}\coloneqq\partial_{\alpha_{1}}\dots\partial_{\alpha_{k}}$.
Furthermore, the term $\mathcal{E}_{L}(f,\alpha_{0})$ satisfies, for some constant $C_{L}>0$ depending only on $L$,
\begin{equation}
	|\mathcal{E}_{L}(f,\alpha_{0})|
	\lesssim \frac{C_{L}}{N^{(L+1)/2}}\sum_{\boldsymbol{\alpha}\in\mathcal{N}^{L}(\alpha_{0})}
	\sup_{\lambda\in[0,1]} \bigg(\mathbb{E}\bigg[\Big|
	\partial_{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}
	f\big(\lambda W|_{\mathcal{N}(\alpha_{0})}+W|_{[N]^{2}\backslash\mathcal{N}(\alpha_{0})}\big)\Big|^{2}\bigg]\bigg)^{1/2},
\end{equation}
where the notation $W|_{\mathcal{N}}$ for $\mathcal{N}\subset [N]^{2}$ refers to the matrix defined by
\begin{equation*}
	W|_{\mathcal{N}}(\alpha) \coloneqq
	\begin{cases}
		W_{\alpha} & \alpha\in\mathcal{N},\\
		0 & \alpha\notin\mathcal{N}.
	\end{cases}
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

