{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:28:04.857823+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f275620eb16ceccce0b8d19dd6ac4e56ed002fb6bf8c3d24edf1c5fd87bf6aea","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 4 (Basse-O'Connor, Kramer-Bang, Svedsen, \cite{basseoconnor2025fourthmoment}) of arXiv:2511.20875, 'Double $q$-Wigner Chaos and the Fourth Moment'] Let $m,n \in\N$ have opposite parities.  For each $k\in\N$, let $f_k \in L^2(\mathbb{R}^m_+)$ and $g_k \in L^2(\mathbb{R}^n_+)$ be fully symmetric functions, and let $X_k = I^W(f_k)$ and $Y_k=I^W(g_k)$.  Assume $\mathbb{E}[X_k^2] + \mathbb{E}[Y_k^2] = \sigma^2$ for all $k\in\N$.  Then the following three conditions are equivalent.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\displaystyle{\lim_{k\to\infty} c_4[X_k+Y_k] = 0}$.
    \item $\displaystyle{\lim_{k\to\infty} f_k \overset{p}{\frown} f_k = 0}$ in $L^2(\mathbb{R}_+^{2(m-p)})$ for $p=1,\ldots,m-1$,  and $\displaystyle{\lim_{k\to\infty} g_k \overset{p}{\frown} g_k = 0}$ in $L^2(\mathbb{R}_+^{2(n-p)})$ for $p=1,\ldots,n-1$.
    \item The sequence $(X_k+Y_k)_{k\in\N}$ converges in distribution to a centered Gaussian random variable with variance $\sigma^2$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

