{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:21:28.777041+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f30aff307bcc8c8e0d25d84372b03da23ae048f8bba74ebda04da0540e6884e8","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:cutoff-gradient-product of arXiv:2605.03718, 'Potential Hessian Ascent III:\\ Sampling the Sherrington--Kirkpatrick Model at $\beta < 1/'] Let $N\in\mathbb N$, let $\operatorname{Sym}_N$ be the set of real symmetric $N \times N$ matrices, and let $(\operatorname{Sym}_N,\schnorm{\cdot})$ be the normalized Schatten-2 norm metric on $\operatorname{Sym}_N$. Let $\mathcal H$ be a real Hilbert space.
Let $f: \operatorname{Sym}_N\to\mathcal H$ be $L_f$-Lipschitz on $S$ with respect to the stated metric and let $\chi:\operatorname{Sym}_N\to\mathbb R$ be globally $L_{\chi}$-Lipschitz.
Fix open measurable sets $\mathcal L\subseteq S\subseteq \mathbb R^N$ and assume:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $0\leqslant \chi\leqslant 1$ everywhere and $\chi=0$ on $S^{c}$,
\item $d\chi=0$ a.e.\ on $\mathcal L^{c}$ (equivalently, $\supp(d\chi)\subseteq\mathcal L$),
\item $\sup_{x\in S}\|f(x)\|_{\mathcal H}\leqslant J$ for some $J<\infty$.
\end{enumerate}
Then $h:=\chi f$ is locally Lipschitz, and for every random variable $X$ with values in $M_N(\mathbb R)$,
\begin{equation}

\mathop{{}\mathbb{E}}\bigl[\|d(\chi f)(X)\|_{\mathrm{HS}(\operatorname{Sym}_N,\mathcal H)}^2\bigr]
\ \leqslant\
2(\dim \mathcal H)L_f^2 \;+\; 2\,J^2\,L_{\chi}^2\,\mathop{{}\mathbb{P}}\{X\in\mathcal L\},
\end{equation}
where $\left\lVert{\cdot}\right\rVert_{\mathrm{HS}(\operatorname{Sym}_N,\mathcal H)}$ is as in \pref{eq:intrinsic-gradient-norm-coordinates}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:58:38.058968+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f314990e8a59044c435b3b8032db5b009bded4efa2ce62daf7912e09acb89030","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2606.19313, 'Stability of Khintchine-type inequalities via log-monotonicity'] Fix $p\geq 1$, and let $a,b\in \mathbb{R}^n$. Let $X$ be a random variable and $X_1, X_2, \ldots, X_n$ iid copies of $X$. We say $X$ has the \emph{Schur-concave property for $p$} if 

\begin{align*}
(a_1^2, a_2^2,\ldots, a_n^2)\prec (b_1^2, b_2^2, \ldots, b_n^2)\Longrightarrow \|  \sum_i a_i X_i \|_p\geq \|  \sum_i b_i X_i \|_p,
\end{align*}in which $\| \cdot \|_p=(\mathbb{E}|\cdot |^p)^{\frac{1}{p}}$. We say $X$ has the \emph{Schur-convex property for $p$}\footnote{The implication $a\prec b\Longrightarrow \|  \sum_i a_i X_i \|_p\leq \|  \sum_i b_i X_i \|_p$ always holds for $p\geq 1$; see [Chavez1], for example.} if 

\begin{align*}
(a_1^2, a_2^2,\ldots, a_n^2)\prec (b_1^2, b_2^2, \ldots, b_n^2)\Longrightarrow \|  \sum_i a_i X_i \|_p\leq \|  \sum_i b_i X_i \|_p.
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:39:11.678231+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f3158873f35f8947c9ec8a4313e32bb09fbec512300a438e70463cea60be150f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 22 (Exact Brownian calibration) of arXiv:2608.04993, '2608.04993'] For every finite-dimensional real Hilbert space $E$,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \mathfrak c_A^E(K)=\|K\|_{\mathcal S_2},\qquad
 \mathfrak c_Q^E(S)=\|S\|_{\rm op}.}
\end{equation}
Thus the dual state gauges are exactly the Hilbert--Schmidt and trace norms,
and the compatible infinite-dimensional response completion is
\[
 H\times\mathcal S_2(H)_{\rm sk}\times\mathcal S_1(H)_{\rm sa}.
\]
The calibration is orthogonally natural.  For increasing finite-rank
orthogonal projections $P_n\uparrow I$, every
$K\in\mathcal S_2(H)_{\rm sk}$, and every
$S\in\mathcal L(H)_{\rm sa}$,
\begin{equation}
 \sup_n\mathfrak c_A^{P_nH}(P_nKP_n)=\|K\|_2,
 \qquad
 \sup_n\mathfrak c_Q^{P_nH}(P_nSP_n)=\|S\|_{\rm op}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:42:55.314252+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f31873ff621aaae7544780bb7b57b6bac70aedf18cd4751926e0093251010a9a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 13 (Variational identity) of arXiv:2609.02398, '\textbf{Convex Order Comparisons for Sub-Gamma Random Variables}'] Let $a > 0$ and $c > 0$. Then $X \lecx c\Lap$ holds for every $X \in \SE(1,a)$ if and only if
\begin{align*}
    p(1-p)\,\nu_{\max}(p,a) \le c\,\Lambda(p)
    \qquad \text{for all } p \in (0, 1/2],
\end{align*}
with $\Lambda$ as in \eqref{eqn:Lambda}. Consequently,
\begin{align}
    c^{\SE}(1, a) = \sup_{p \in (0,1/2]} R(p,a),
    \qquad
    R(p,a) \coloneqq \frac{(1-p)\,\nu_{\max}(p,a)}{1 + \log\frac{1}{2p}},

\end{align}
and the infimum in \eqref{eqn:defc} is a minimum: $c^{\SE}(1,a)$ is itself admissible.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:10:07.391109+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f319678434bac2df59071d0b33b0b8279fe8c6f9cec235e9846277ee14fa7494","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem t:uniqueness of arXiv:2606.11503, 'Percolation on hierarchical lattices'] For all $p > p_{c}$,
