{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:21:28.818645+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f3d1dd24f397a7fcbcf92b3992eb6207fb06ec7157ae2872da66a1bf47eec5d7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corr intro:RS larkin of arXiv:2605.05631, 'Wandering Exponents and the Free Energy of the High-Dimensional Elastic Polymer'] Fix the choice of $t$ and $\beta$, and consider ((eqn:Larkin equation of the paper)) as an equation in $\mu$.
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item If ((eqn:Larkin equation of the paper)) has no solutions, then the model is RS for all choices of $\mu$.
			\item If ((eqn:Larkin equation of the paper)) has one or more solutions, we will call the largest solution $\mu_{Lar}(\beta;t)$. Then if $\mu\ge \mu_{Lar}(\beta;t)$ the model is RS.
		\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:53:30.663079+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f3d1f0fc3e1c724271967b4198683e4e5e21f82f635f4016112b702701fabace","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Dfn $L^r$-optimal quantization error of arXiv:2506.01746, '\bf Optimal Bregman quantization: existence and uniqueness of optimal quantizers revisited'] The  $L^r$-optimal mean quantization error for $P$ at level $n\ge 1$ is defined by 
