    Then there exists $C = C(G,J) > 0$ such that for all $r \in \mathbf{N}$,

    \begin{equation}

        J(B(r), B(r)^{c})
        \leq 
        C
        r^{
        \max
        \left(
            d(2-\alpha),
            d - 1
        \right)  
        + 
        1
        -
        \delta
        }
        \log(r)^{
            \mathbf{1}
            \left(
                \alpha=1+1/d
            \right)
        }.
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:01:54.390285+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f4173065557a5eea19c429fc54c7ff97dba33da2135c6a0f5f00c168d3187e9e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma asymp_uniforme of arXiv:2411.02156, 'Martin boundary of a degenerate Reflected Brownian Motion in a wedge'] Let $\alpha^* < \alpha_1 < \alpha^{**}$ and $\epsilon > 0$. Then, there exist constants $\eta > 0$ and $r_0 >0$ such that
