{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:06:06.303181+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f4d2d7522cb795993e28f7312fb0e224f4ac3e330301f00274d2bef1839496a5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:girsanov of arXiv:2605.26652, 'Pathological Large Deviations of the KMP Process in Dimension $d\ge 2$'] Under the probability measures $\mathbb{Q}^{N,K}$ given by (equation eq:RND-tilt-3 of the paper), the processes $\xi^N_t$ are Markov with generator given by \begin{equation}
{\mathcal L_t^{N,H,K} = N^2\sum_{x\sim y} L^{H,K}_{x,y,t};}
\end{equation} \begin{equation}
{L^{H,K}_{x,y,t}F(\xi)
=\int_0^1 r_t(x,y,p,\xi)\big[F(\xi^{x,y,p})-F(\xi)\big] \,\mathrm d p.}
\end{equation} Moreover, under $\mathbb{Q}^{N,K}$, the empirical jump measure $\mu^N$ has predictable intensity
\[
{N^2 r_t(x,y,p,\xi^N_{t-})\,\mathrm d p\,\mathrm d t,}
