{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:22:39.687516+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f4ec1cb2b781cd2c1153e4853449fae4b18b57cc8fa490af0cd1bdc4b9efba62","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:signed_ODE_analysis of arXiv:2508.18807, '{\bf Critical long-range percolation I: High effective dimension}'] Let $a,b>0$, let $h:(0,\infty)\to (0,\infty)$ be a measurable, regularly varying function of index $b$, and suppose that 
$f:(0,\infty)\to \mathbb R$ is a (not necessarily positive) differentiable function such that 
\begin{equation}

f'(r) = \frac{(a \pm o(1)) f(r) + (A \pm o(1)) h(r)}{r}
\end{equation}
as $r\to \infty$ for some constant $A\in \mathbb R$. If $b>a$ and $|f(r)|=O(h(r))$ as $r\to \infty$ then
\[
  f(r) = \frac{A \pm o(1)}{b-a}h(r)
