{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:49:49.210219+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f62f4a7b1278b0ae14bb0a0e9383e79106ae9a92b6d96a06442bc39b51e1467d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 20 of arXiv:2604.27242, 'Statistical Inference for Homogenization Limits Driven by Wiener or Hermite Processes'] Let \(\beta(s)\) be a continuous non-increasing function satisfying \(\beta(0) = 1\) and \(|\beta(s)|\sim Ks^{\alpha}\) for some positive constant \(K\) and some \(\alpha<0\) as \(s\to\infty\). Then for any positive integer \(k\), as \(h\to\infty\):
\begin{enumerate}
    \item If \(\beta^k\) is not integrable along the positive half-axis (\(\alpha k>-1\)): \begin{equation}

        \int_{[a,a+h]^2}\beta(s-t)^k dsdt \sim \f{2K^k h^{\alpha k+2}}{(\alpha k+2)(\alpha k + 1)}
    \end{equation}
    \item If \(\alpha k<-1\):  \begin{equation}

        \int_{[a,a+h]^2}\beta(s-t)^k dsdt \sim 2h\int_0^{\infty}\beta(s)^k ds
    \end{equation}
    \item If \(\alpha k =-1\): \begin{equation}

        \int_{[a,a+h]^2}\beta(s-t)^k dsdt \sim 2Kh\log(h)
    \end{equation}
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:03:59.344754+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f63441824bd988d7151f81376d0d748e10209943e05c6025c513b697b20abc6a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:momentinequality of arXiv:2507.08692, 'Some notes on higher order concentration of measure'] Let $\mu$ be a measure on a product of finite sets satisfying a $\mathfrak{d}\mathrm{-LSI}(\sigma^2)$. Then, for any $f \in L^\infty(\mu)$ and any $r \ge 2$ we have
