{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:05:52.422596+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f639754bb7f69cc61fcd4826021f18b74a4e8fae91a6667484fc5681422b21aa","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary lemma4.22 of arXiv:2504.15992, 'Repeated singular values of a random symmetric matrix and decoupled singular value estimat'] For a given $p>1$, $B>0$, $\kappa>0$ and $\zeta\in\Gamma_B$, $A\in\operatorname{Sym}_n(\zeta)$. Then we can find a constant $C_P>0$ depending on $B$, $p$, $\kappa$ such that for any $\lambda_i\in[(-(2-\kappa)\sqrt{n},(2-\kappa)\sqrt{n}]$, we have
