\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:51:26.723303+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f69964fdfa71c71ff53800461d28b84c563d7994d27ae2640b54615fd4021367","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:retreat-gadget of arXiv:2606.06612, 'The limit shape and emergence of the \\ Discrete Gaussian level lines'] The following holds uniformly over all possible $Q, \xi$ as above. Fix $n \geq 0$. Let $\mathfrak{L}_n$ be the $(H+1-n)$ level line induced by $\xi$. Let $\theta$ denote the angle of $\overline{AB}$ with the $x$-axis. Then, with $\pi^\xi_Q$-probability $1 - o(1)$, $\mathfrak{L}_n$ lies above the point $X= (0, Y - \sigma e^{C\sqrt{\log L}} - \log L)$, where
