{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:21:28.791894+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f6f9e61879c9a0c151395fa0b57bb932cacf47ee1c43015dfbf9f373e402ad8c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem t:llt of arXiv:2605.03718, 'Potential Hessian Ascent III:\\ Sampling the Sherrington--Kirkpatrick Model at $\beta < 1/'] Let $X_i\sim \mathsf{Bernoulli}(p_i)$, $1\leqslant i\leqslant n$ be independent Bernoulli random variables. Let $\sigma_n^2 = \sumo in p_i(1-p_i)$. 
    Let $p_n(x)$ be the probability mass function of $S_n = \sumo in X_i$ and 
    $\gamma(x) = \rc{\sqrt{2\pi}}e^{-\fc{x^2}{2}}$. Then
