{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:25:12.190872+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f722db5d7432b77008f4bb4ae967ee56f1bc8dd28f89eea57954499eef2ea1ce","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 1 of arXiv:2510.21446, '{Uniqueness of adapted solutions to scalar BSDEs\\ with Peano-type generators}\tnoteref{fo'] For $\rho(\cdot)\in \Sbf$, let
\begin{equation}
\rho^*(q):=\sup_{x\in\R_+}\{\rho(x)-qx\},\ \ q\in \R_+.
\end{equation}
Then, $\rho^*(\cdot)$ is a nonincreasing convex function on $\R_+$ taking values in $\R_+\cup \{+\infty\}$, and
\begin{equation}
\rho(x)=\inf_{q\in\R_+,\rho^*(q)\in\R_+}\{\rho^*(q)+qx\},\ \ x\in\R_+.
\end{equation}
Moreover, for any $x_0\in \R_{++}$, by picking $q=\rho'(x_0)\in\R_+$ we have $\rho^*(q)\in\R_+$ and
\begin{equation}
\rho(x_0)=\rho^*(q)+qx_0.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:12:09.035759+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f726209ed2d826106160be38b27a9918ae959810683a5c8d1c91bb33b4c726eb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:nondeg-NS of arXiv:2608.21805, 'Logarithmic derivatives of variational and singular stochastic partial differential equati'] Consider this periodic-torus specialisation of the stochastic 2D Navier--Stokes equation (equation eq:NS-full of the paper) with additive noise $\mathcal{B} = Q^{1/2}$ where $Q$ excites finitely many eigenmodes of $A$, and write $g_k := Q^{1/2}f_k$ for the forcing directions. Suppose the noise satisfies the H\"ormander bracket condition of Hairer--Mattingly [hairer2011hormander]. Setting $\mathcal{A}_0 := \{g_k\}$ and $\mathcal{A}_{n+1} := \mathcal{A}_n \cup \{\widetilde{B}(h_1, h_2) : h_1, h_2 \in \mathcal{A}_n\}$ with $\widetilde{B}(h_1,h_2) := B(h_1,h_2) + B(h_2,h_1)$ the symmetrised nonlinearity, the linear span of $\mathcal{A}_\infty := \bigcup_{n \geq 0} \mathcal{A}_n$ is dense in $H$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\arabic*)}]
  \item \textup{(Finite-dimensional projected logarithmic derivatives)} For each $N \geq 1$, the projection $\Pi_N X(t)$ admits a smooth, everywhere strictly positive Lebesgue density on $H_N := \mathrm{ran}(\Pi_N) = \mathrm{span}\{e_1, \ldots, e_N\}$, with smooth, globally defined logarithmic derivative $\nabla\log p_N: H_N \to H_N$, by the Mattingly--Pardoux theorem for the 2D torus [mattinglypardoux2006malliavin] (smoothness and everywhere strict positivity of all finite-dimensional projected densities, for all $t > 0$, under viscosity-independent conditions), the torus instance of the general Hairer--Mattingly H\"ormander framework [hairer2011hormander] applied to the projection $\Pi_N\gamma_t\Pi_N$. The projected logarithmic derivative is given by the abstract Bismut--Fomin theorem (Theorem~(thm:abstract-bismut-fomin of the paper)) applied to $F = \Pi_N X(t)$ on the finite-dimensional Hilbert space $H_N$, with the compression $\Gamma_N = \Pi_N\gamma_t\Pi_N$ as its Malliavin covariance.
 \item \textup{(Admissible direction space for full $H$-directional logarithmic derivatives)} Define the \emph{admissible direction space}
  \begin{align*}
    \mathcal{H}^{\sharp}_t &\;:=\; \bigl\{h \in H : \text{Assumption~(ass:nondeg of the paper) holds for } h\bigr\} \\
    &\;=\; \bigl\{h \in H : h \in \operatorname{Ran}(\gamma_t) \text{ a.s.,}\;\; \mathbb{E}[\norm{\gamma_t^{\dagger}h}_H^q] < \infty \\
    &\qquad\qquad \text{ at the H\"older-triangle exponent } q, \\
    &\qquad\qquad\;\; \text{Cameron--Martin compatibility}~(equation eq:CM-compat-limit of the paper)\text{--}(equation eq:CM-compat-dominator of the paper)\bigr\}.
  \end{align*}
  Then for every $h \in \mathcal{H}^{\sharp}_t$ for which the domain hypothesis $v_h \in \operatorname{Dom}(\delta_U)$ of Theorem~(thm:main of the paper), Part~I, additionally holds, sufficient conditions being those of Remark~(rem:domain-vh-suffic of the paper), the full-$H$-directional Bismut--Fomin identity $\partial_h \mu_t = \beta_h\,\mu_t$ applies, with $\beta_h(X(t)) = -\mathbb{E}[\delta_U(v_h)\mid X(t)]$. If the further fixed-Tikhonov and raised structural hypotheses of Part~II hold, the explicit decomposition~(equation eq:skorokhod-decomp of the paper) applies as well.
  \item \textup{(Scope of (2))} The space $\mathcal{H}^{\sharp}_t$ is a deterministic subset of $H$, cut out by almost-sure and expectation conditions on $\gamma_t$. Verification of $h \in \mathcal{H}^{\sharp}_t$ for any specific $h$ is a separate quantitative input (verification of clauses~(i)--(ii)--(iii) of Assumption~(ass:nondeg of the paper) for the specific direction). The denseness of $\mathcal{H}^{\sharp}_t$ in $H$ is genuinely subtle and is open in full generality under H\"ormander's bracket condition alone; see Remark~(rem:nondeg-NS-gap of the paper) for the obstruction.
\end{enumerate}
\medskip\noindent The Mattingly--Pardoux/Hairer--Mattingly bounds control cone-restricted quadratic forms of $\gamma_t$ (all unit $\phi$ with a definite component in a fixed finite-dimensional range), and in particular the compressed matrices $\Pi_N\gamma_t\Pi_N$; for each fixed nonzero $\phi$ in the reachable span (dense under the bracket condition) they give $\langle \gamma_t \phi, \phi\ran (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:24.615901+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f7265c90ec58f57f73fb503800b0df623e9db9d12fcda2416b991d89384775d9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 11 of arXiv:2506.09592, 'Extremal process of the local time of simple\\random walk on a regular tree'] Recall the definition of $a_n(t)$ from \eqref{E:1.24a}. There exist~$c,\tilde c >0$ such that
