{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:52:23.605580+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f791058724157ae8d4b5cca09fccf599079d35333be329fb08ccd0081ecde5d8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Lem6.6z of arXiv:2602.23124, '\textsc{ Necessary and Sufficient Conditions for the Lacunary/Hereditary Laws of Large Num'] Consider natural numbers $\,m$ and $\,N_{2^{m-1}}^{(m-1)}\,,$ as well as a number $\,\delta >0$ and a rational $\varepsilon >0\,.$    There exist then natural numbers $\,N_{ 2^{\,m -1}}^{(m-1)} < N_{1}^{(m)} < \cdots < N_{{2^m} }^{(m)} \,,$ and positive numbers $\, p^{(m)}_1, \cdots, p^{(m)}_{2^m}\,$ with $\, \sum_{j=1}^{2^m} p^{(m)}_j < \delta\,,$ such that 
\begin{equation}

 p^{(m)}_j \, \Big( N^{(m)}_j \Big)^2 \,=\, m \cdot  N^{(m)}_{2^m}\,,\qquad j=1, \cdots, 2^m\,,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}

p^{(m)}_j \,  N^{(m)}_{j-1} \,=\, \varepsilon\,,\qquad j=	2, \cdots, 2^m\,.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:26:20.491134+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f791142c4dfd1bd8d7800c3e1624e8816ecf757bd604cc4326af8808f3d51140","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor almostdone of arXiv:2411.11676, 'Surface sums for lattice Yang--Mills in the large-$N$ limit'] Let $\Lambda_N$ be an increasing sequence converging to $\mathbb{Z}^d$.
There exists a number $\upbeta_1(d)>0$, depending only on the dimension $d$, such that the following is true. If $|\upbeta|\leq \upbeta_1(d)$, then, for any string $s\in\mathcal{S}$,
\begin{equation*}
	\text{$\phi_{\Lambda_N,N,\upbeta}(s)$ converges to a limit $\phi_{\infty}(s)$ as $N\to\infty$.}
\end{equation*} 
Moreover, $\phi_{\infty}(\emptyset)=1$, $\phi_{\infty}$ is invariant under backtrack erasures, $|\phi_{\infty}(s)|\leq 1$ for all $s\in\mathcal{S}$, and $\phi_{\infty}(s)$ is the unique solution to the master loop equation (equation eq:limiting_master_loop_eq of the paper) for all non-empty $s\in \mathcal{S}$.","status":"unverified"}

