{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:13:22.526578+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f7fc3c996fe286e5daf90d3fd4bb937fd1f364624e9e815f932387a30319b3ec","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm ab of arXiv:2506.07158, 'The diffusivity of supercritical Bernoulli percolation is infinitely differentiable'] Given $d \geqslant 2$ and $\mathfrak{p} \in (\mathfrak{p}_c, 1]$, the following statement is valid for the effective conductivity ${\overbracket[1pt][-1pt]{\mathbf{a}}}(\mathfrak{p})$: there exists an exponent $\alpha_*(d, \mathfrak{p}) > 0$, such that for every $k \in \mathbb{N}$  and $\alpha \in (0, \alpha_*)$, there exists a finite positive constant $C(d,k, \mathfrak{p}, \alpha)$ satisfying
