  u_\alpha(t,x)=\mathbb E_x\left[f(X(\mathcal{L}^\alpha(t) ))\right] ,\quad t>0,x\in\mathbb R^d. 
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:28:08.661232+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f8acdcb4a1bede531b134d6458d1a01713a0829623ed8b0463253eacd9a83f13","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 ($T^h$ and $h$-good) of arXiv:2607.29495, 'Fast Mixing for Low-Temperature Potts Models via Poisson Trees'] Let $T$ be a tree rooted at $r$, and $h,D>0$ be integers. Let $T^h$ be the subtree induced by the vertices of $T$ at depth $\leq h$. Moreover,
\begin{itemize}

\item All vertices at depth exactly~$h$ are $h$-good.
\item A vertex at depth less than $h$ is $h$-good if
it has at least~$D$  children that are $h$-good.
\end{itemize}
When   $T$ is fixed, we use
$ V^h_\mathrm{good}$ to denote the set of $h$-good vertices in~$V(T)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:48:24.316098+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f8ad5521c3a4c051e5e128898510ec01d1ca80bf56f2c234d4b4200f23dc87f0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:rectangular Appell polynomials of arXiv:2605.31356, 'P\'olya--Schur problems and free probability'] Let $\{f_{n}\}\subset\mathcal{LPI}$ and satisfy Assumption (assump:main assumption of the paper). If $\beta\equiv\beta_n$ and $\frac{n}{n+\beta}\rightarrow \lambda\in (0,1]$, then \begin{equation}
		\left\llbracket f_n\left(\frac{\lambda}{n}\mathscr{L}_\beta\right)z^{n}\right\rrbracket\Rightarrow\mathsf{MP}_{1,\lambda}\boxtimes \mu_{c,\Sigma},
	\end{equation} 
	 where $\mu_{c,\Sigma}$ is the $\boxplus$-ID limit of the Appell polynomials $A_{n,g_n}$ from Theorem (thm:main result, general of the paper) and $\mathsf{MP}_{1,\lambda}$ is the standard Marchenko--Pastur distribution with density 
	\begin{align*}
		\mathrm{d}\mathsf{MP}_{1,\lambda}(x)
		=\frac 1 {2\pi\lambda x} \sqrt{\big((1+\sqrt{\lambda})^2-x\big) \big(x-(1-\sqrt{\lambda}\big)^2  }\big)\mathrm{d} x \text{ on } x\in[(1-\sqrt{\lambda})^2, (1+\sqrt{\lambda})^2].
	\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:59:53.344572+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f8aee4409c6746dc8076b1ce658528c54f6d7d56850ef6183aea7cf99a68e366","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:dilation of arXiv:2607.24705, 'Derangetropy Operators'] Let $f \in \mathcal{D}$. Then:
		\begin{enumerate}[label=\normalfont(\roman*), leftmargin=*]
			\item $U_{f}$ is an isometry; for $f \in \mathcal{D}_{+}$ it is
			unitary onto $L^{2}(\operatorname{supp}f)$, with inverse
			\begin{equation*}
				\psi \;\longmapsto\; (\psi\circ Q)\,\sqrt{Q'}.
			\end{equation*}
			\item For every unit vector $g \in L^{2}(0,1)$,
			\begin{equation*}
				|U_{f}\,g|^{2} = \rho_{|g|^{2}}[f]:
			\end{equation*}
			the modulation family $\{\rho_{v}[f] : v \in \mathcal{W}\}$ is
			exactly the set of squared moduli of unit amplitudes transported by
			$U_{f}$. In particular,
			\begin{equation*}
				\rho_{w_{k}}[f] = \big|\psi_{k}[f]\big|^{2},
				\qquad
				\psi_{k}[f] := U_{f}\,\varphi_{k},
			\end{equation*}
			and, for $f \in \mathcal{D}_{+}$, the family
			$\{\psi_{k}[f]\}_{k\ge1}$ is an orthonormal basis of
			$L^{2}(\operatorname{supp}f)$; for general $f \in \mathcal{D}$ the
			support is read as $\{f > 0\}$ modulo null sets. The word dilation
			is used in the loose sense of a lift to a larger space.
			\item For every increasing differentiable homeomorphism $T$ with
			positive derivative, equivariance is unitarily implemented:
			\begin{equation*}
				U_{T_{\#}f} = V_{T}\,U_{f},
				\qquad
				V_{T}\,\psi = (\psi\circ T^{-1})\,\sqrt{\big|(T^{-1})'\big|};
			\end{equation*}
			for decreasing $T$ the rank reflection intervenes,
			$U_{T_{\#}f} = V_{T}\,U_{f}R$ with $(Rg)(z) = g(1-z)$.
		\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:49.282666+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f8af81b3a77c126757a172d93b0647ef0cc99cedfd1e3bcf4e1c75b41b775785","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 2 of arXiv:2606.22656, 'Fluctuations of Point Vortex Ensembles\\ at Small Negative Inverse Temperature'] For every $\beta_*<8\pi$ there exists $C_{\beta_*}<\infty$ such that, for all $0\leq \beta\leq \beta_*$ and $2\leq n\leq N$,
