    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:11:35.823725+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f9ce77e6332ed42494cbab12360dff9e32bdbe3f874a9d49e9d9ab5549ef1370","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:cascading of arXiv:2607.22908, 'Epidemic Phase Transitions in the Zero-Range Process'] For any $k \geq 0$,
  \begin{equation}
    H(B_{k+1}) \subset \bigcup_{j = 0}^{2} \bigcup_{(B, B') \in \mathcal{B}_j \times
    \mathcal{B}_j'} H(B) \cap H(B').
  \end{equation}
  In words, whenever $H(B_{k+1})$ occurs, there exists a pair of scale-$k$ boxes $B,B'$ that are separated vertically
  by at least $\ell_{k+1}/4$ or horizontally by at least $w_{k+1}/2$ such that $H(B)$ and $H(B')$ occur.
  The total number of candidate pairs of boxes $B$, $B'$ is at most
  \begin{equation}
