{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:45:07.262406+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f9da9edea70ba9417a68653cb6b7bf18d8c169f80707b4f1490aa29f85078caa","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 20 of arXiv:2604.19738, 'Phase Transitions in the Fluctuations of Functionals of Random Neural Networks \\'] Let $\beta_L$, $\beta$, $\lambda$ be as defined in \Cref{fixed-point:high}. Then, we have the following asymptotics for fixed $q\in\N$, $q\ge2$.

\smallskip
\noindent
If $q<\frac{d}{2\nu}$, then as $L\to\infty$
\begin{equation}

\int_{-1}^1 \beta_L^q(u)\,\di \mu(u)
=
\int_{-1}^1 \beta^q(u)\,\di \mu(u)\,(1+o(1))\,,
\end{equation}

\smallskip
\noindent
If $q\ge \frac{d}{2\nu}$, and $q$ is even whenever $\kappa$ is odd, then
\begin{align}
\int_{-1}^1 \beta_L^q(u)\,\di \mu(u)
\asymp
\begin{cases}
L & \textnormal{if } q=\frac{d}{2\nu},\\
\lambda^{(\frac{d}{2\nu}-q)L} & \textnormal{if } q>\frac{d}{2\nu}.
\end{cases}
\end{align}

\smallskip
\noindent
Moreover, we always have (with no restriction on $q, \kappa$)
\begin{align}
\int_{-1}^1 |\beta_L(u)|^q\,\di \mu(u)
\asymp
\begin{cases}
1 & \textnormal{if } q<\frac{d}{2\nu},\\
L & \textnormal{if } q=\frac{d}{2\nu},\\
\lambda^{(\frac{d}{2\nu}-q)L} & \textnormal{if } q>\frac{d}{2\nu}\,.
\end{cases}
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:44:17.357830+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f9db53da15ebedf187ed7c55ccbd650c4564ae2f77d8679f4f8765a4f1f8287e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Revelation depth of arXiv:2608.23860, 'Revelation Control'] For a decision-relevant direction $v$ at $x_0$, define its first revelation depth
