{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.768207+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fac6a86fd8f29e67f5468ffd299014d6b3e017121216bb2659ef7a06833f9169","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 19 of arXiv:2603.24616, 'Pinning in non-critical half-space geometric last passage percolation'] Fix a set $\Sigma = \llbracket 1, N \rrbracket$ with $N \in \mathbb{N} \cup \{\infty\}$ and $T_0, T_1\in \mathbb{Z}$ with $T_0 \leq T_1$. A $\Sigma$-indexed geometric line ensemble $\mathfrak{L} : \Sigma \times \llbracket T_0, T_1 \rrbracket \rightarrow \mathbb{Z}$ is said to satisfy the {\em interlacing Gibbs property} if it is interlacing, meaning that $L_i \succeq L_{i+1}$ for all $i \in \llbracket 1, N-1 \rrbracket$, and for any finite $K = \llbracket k_1, k_2 \rrbracket \subset \llbracket 1, N - 1 \rrbracket$ and $a,b \in \llbracket T_0, T_1 \rrbracket$ with $a < b$ the following holds.  Suppose that $f, g$ are two increasing paths drawn in $\{ (r,z) \in \mathbb{Z}^2 : a \leq r \leq b\}$ and $\vec{x}, \vec{y} \in \mathfrak{W}_k$ with $k=k_2-k_1+1$ are such that $\mathbb{P}(A) > 0$, where $A$ denotes the event $$A =\{ \vec{x} = ({L}_{k_1}(a), \dots, {L}_{k_2}(a)), \vec{y} = ({L}_{k_1}(b), \dots, {L}_{k_2}(b)), L_{k_1-1} \llbracket a,b \rrbracket = f, L_{k_2+1} \llbracket a,b \rrbracket = g \},$$
where if $k_1 = 1$ we adopt the convention $f = \infty = L_0$. Then, writing $k = k_2 - k_1 + 1$, we have for any $\{ B_i \in \Omega(a, b, x_i , y_i) \}_{i = 1}^k$ that
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}\left( L_{i + k_1-1}\llbracket a,b \rrbracket = B_{i} \mbox{ for $i \in \llbracket 1, k \rrbracket$} \, \vert  A \, \right) = \mathbb{P}_{\ice, \operatorname{Geom}}^{a,b, \vec{x}, \vec{y}, f, g} \left( \cap_{i = 1}^k\{ Q_i = B_i \} \right),
\end{equation}
where $\mathfrak{Q} = \{Q_i\}_{i = 1}^k$ has law $\mathbb{P}_{\ice, \operatorname{Geom}}^{a,b, \vec{x}, \vec{y}, f, g}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:17:13.063221+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_facf0ec9590f9b308e752bb961d1d0b63b2c28b1f643f470722323c5244d3c3b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:Pisigmaxi of arXiv:2508.04954, 'Conditional exponential directed last passage percolation under a one-point upper large de'] Let $\mathbf{n}\in {\mathbb N}^3$. For ${\boldsymbol \sigma}, {\boldsymbol \tau}\in \mathcal S_{\mb{n}}$, define the functions 
\begin{equation}  \begin{split}
    \Pi^{{\boldsymbol \sigma}}_{{\boldsymbol \tau}}(\boldsymbol{\xi}, \boldsymbol{\eta}) 
    = &\mathrm{K}(\boldsymbol{\eta}^{123}, \boldsymbol{\eta}^{12}, \boldsymbol{\eta}^1  | \boldsymbol{\xi}^{123}, \boldsymbol{\xi}^{12}, \boldsymbol{\xi}^1) \, 
    \mathrm{K}(\boldsymbol{\xi}^1, \boldsymbol{\eta}^{23}, \boldsymbol{\eta}^2 | \boldsymbol{\eta}^1,  \boldsymbol{\xi}^{23}, \boldsymbol{\xi}^2) \, \\
    &\times \mathrm{K}( \boldsymbol{\xi}^{12}, \boldsymbol{\xi}^2, \boldsymbol{\eta}^3 | \boldsymbol{\eta}^{12}, \boldsymbol{\eta}^2, \boldsymbol{\xi}^3) \,
    \mathrm{K}( \boldsymbol{\xi}^{123}, \boldsymbol{\xi}^{23}, \boldsymbol{\xi}^3 | \boldsymbol{\eta}^{123}, \boldsymbol{\eta}^{23},  \boldsymbol{\eta}^3) 
    \mathrm{S}( \boldsymbol{\xi}^{123}, \boldsymbol{\xi}^{23}, \boldsymbol{\xi}^3 | \boldsymbol{\eta}^{123}, \boldsymbol{\eta}^{23},  \boldsymbol{\eta}^3), 
\end{split} \end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
    {\mathsf{F}}_{L}^{{\boldsymbol \sigma}|{\boldsymbol \tau}} (\boldsymbol{\xi}, \boldsymbol{\eta}) 
    = \frac{\prod_{*\in \mathcal A_3} \prod_{i=1}^{a_*} {\mathsf{f}}_{L, *} (\xi_i^*)}{\prod_{*\in \mathcal A_3} \prod_{i=1}^{b_*} {\mathsf{f}}_{L, *} (\eta_i^*)}
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation*}   
    (a_{123}, a_{12}, a_{23}, a_1,a_2,a_3)=\type({\boldsymbol \sigma}), \qquad 
    (b_{123}, b_{12}, b_{23}, b_1,b_2,b_3)=\type({\boldsymbol \tau}). 
\end{equation*}
Here,  $\boldsymbol{\xi} = ( \boldsymbol{\xi}^{123}, \boldsymbol{\xi}^{12}, \boldsymbol{\xi}^{23}, \boldsymbol{\xi}^1, \boldsymbol{\xi}^2, \boldsymbol{\xi}^3)$ 
and $\boldsymbol{\eta} = ( \boldsymbol{\eta}^{123}, \boldsymbol{\eta}^{12}, \boldsymbol{\eta}^{23}, \boldsymbol{\eta}^1, \boldsymbol{\eta}^2, \boldsymbol{\eta}^3)$, 
with $\boldsymbol{\xi}^{*} = (\xi^*_1,\cdots,\xi^*_{a_*}) \in {\mathbb C}^{a_*}$ and $\boldsymbol{\eta}^{*} = (\eta^*_1,\cdots,\eta^*_{b_*}) \in {\mathbb C}^{b_*}$
for each $* \in \mathcal A_3$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:48:25.155354+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_facfb490385a4669c278c56f7f1150756286de340bb5421d92de1685584d0187","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lm:interp of arXiv:2607.05345, 'The local product condition implies cutoff'] Fix two measures $\mu,\pi\in\mathcal {P}({\mathbb{X}})$ with $\mathrm{d}_{\textsc{tv}}(\mu,\pi)<1$. Then, there is a   measure $\nu\in\mathcal {P}({\mathbb{X}})$ which is absolutely continuous w.r.t. both $\mu$ and $\pi$, such that
		\begin{eqnarray*}
			\left\|\frac{\mathrm{d} \nu}{\mathrm{d} \mu}\right\|_\infty\vee\ \ \left\|\frac{\mathrm{d}\nu}{\mathrm{d}\pi}\right\|_\infty	 & \leq & \frac{1}{1-\mathrm{d}_{\textsc{tv}}(\mu,\pi)}.
		\end{eqnarray*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:38:23.615426+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fad0868e7d2fc6af9ab928fb65e1d78805945756bc1d2ae26a320d04e1874124","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma EHI:l:CloseToSubmultiplicative of arXiv:2511.08956, '\textbf{Towards a characterization of elliptic Harnack inequality for jump processes}'] Let $X \in \mathcal{I}(d, r_0)$, and let $T$ be a random variable with Exponential($\lambda$) distribution, independent of $X$. For all $r > 0$ and $m \in \mathbb{N}$,
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathbb{P}_0 \left( \tau_{B(0, mr+(m-1)r_0)} < T \right) \leq \left( \mathbb{P}_0 \left( \tau_{B(0, r)}<T \right)\right)^m.
    \end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:38:17.800119+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fad4524abdd4f366e50387c4198d06595439fb4f83a62b93f1536cc816116eb9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: main of arXiv:2510.05850, 'Three-point connectivity constant for $q$-state Potts spin clusters'] For the continuum fuzzy Potts model with $q \in (1,4)$, $\kappa = 4\arccos(-\sqrt{q}/2)/\pi\in(\frac{8}{3},4)$, and parameter $r=1/q$, we have
\begin{align}
    R(q)=C(q) \cdot {C_{\beta=\frac{2}{\sqrt{\kappa}}}^{\rm ImDOZZ}\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft(\frac{1}{4\beta} - \frac{\beta}{2},\frac{1}{4\beta} - \frac{\beta}{2},\frac{1}{4\beta} - \frac{\beta}{2}\aftergroup\egroup\originalright)},\quad \text{where }C(q)=\sqrt{\kappa/2} \cdot\frac{\sin(\kappa\pi/2)}{\sin(4\pi/\kappa)}.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:51:11.632948+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fad6007322cdb929a085c064aed7c32a6b8d9385cfdb27b52e8ba01b02831cdf","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 (OBABO version of Theorem 2.1 of the supplement to \cite{ChakMonmarche:2025}) of arXiv:2605.31088, 'On couplings for kinetic Langevin diffusions'] Let $U\in C^2(\R^d)$ be twice continuously differentiable such that the Hessian of $U$ is globally Lipschitz continuous and bounded. Let $\pi_n(\delta_z)$ be the distribution after $n$ steps of the OBABO discretization of \eqref{eq: general kinetic Langevin} when starting at $z\in \R^{2d}$.  
    Then there exist $C,h_0>0$ such that for all $z,\tilde z\in \R^{2d}$, $h\in(0,h_0]$ and $n\in\N$ such that $hn\leq1$ we have
    \begin{equation}

        \dTV{\pi_n(\delta_z),\pi_n(\delta_{\tilde z})}\leq C\frac{h^{1/2}}{(1-e^{-\gamma h/2})^{1/2}}\br{\frac{e^{\gamma h/2}}{(hn)^{3/2}}+\frac{1-e^{-\gamma h/2}}{h}\frac{e^{\gamma h/2}}{(hn)^{1/2}}+(hn)^{1/2}}|\tilde z -z|.
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:34:45.684887+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fad8c3f0429c3244a529702746738451564bc69b342166213e9faa1cdc040d07","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:typeI of arXiv:2609.01920, 'Isospectral and not strongly isospectral spherical space forms with non-cyclic fundamental'] {Wolf-book}}]
		Let $m$, $n$, $r$ be positive integers satisfying $\gcd((r-1)n,m)=1$ and $r^n \equiv 1 \pmod{m}$. Then the group $\Gamma$ generated by elements $A$, $B$ with relations
		\begin{equation}
			A^m=B^n=1 \quad \text{and} \quad BAB^{-1}=A^r
		\end{equation}
		has order $mn$ and is of Type~I. Conversely, every Type~I group is isomorphic to one of this form. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:30:14.221793+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fadb9efbdb90777788491936aa75a76ab244901abbfbcba21ebf6397897ccd35","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:der-mart-lower of arXiv:2608.20537, 'Persistent anisotropy in multi-dimensional\\ branching Brownian motion'] For all $\underline{c} \in (0, 1]$, there exists $t_{\textnormal{max}} \leq -1$ such that the following holds.
  For all $\mathfrak{t} \leq t_{\textnormal{max}}$, there exists $\underline{T}(\mathfrak{t}) > \abs{\mathfrak{t}}$ and a function $\underline{u}(t,x; \mathfrak{t}) \geq 0$ defined where $t \in [-\underline{T}(\mathfrak{t}), \mathfrak{t}]$ and $x \geq 2 \log \left |t \right|$ satisfying the following properties:
  \begin{enumerate}[label = \textnormal{(\roman*)},itemsep = 2pt]
  \item $\underline{u} = 0$ where $x \geq \left |t \right|$;

