{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:54:28.101668+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fb34833cd0679ef29e085c2b66d4d7b029cb7fbcae44bf664ede7b3053daf1fe","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:Gcomp_characterisation of arXiv:2608.13716, 'Graph Causal Optimal Transport \\ and Wasserstein Distances'] Let $G$ be a DAG and $\mu \in \probabilities{\mathcal{X}}$. The following are equivalent:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item[1)] $\mu \in \Gprobabilities{\mathcal{X}}$;
        \item[2)] There exists a random variable $X \sim \mu$, independent $\mathbb{R}$-valued random variables $(U_i)_{i\in V}$, and measurable functions $f_i \colon \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{pa}_{i}} \times \mathbb{R} \to \mathcal{X}_i, i\in V$ such that $X_i = f_i(X_{\mathrm{pa}_{i}}, U_i), \, \forall \, i \in V$.
    \end{enumerate}
    If $G$ is a sorted DAG, the above are further equivalent to the following:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item[3)] $\mu$ admits a disintegration $\mu(dx) = \bigotimes_{i=1}^n \mu_i(dx_i \vert x_{\mathrm{pa}_{i}})$;
        \item[4)] If $X\sim \mu$ then 
        $$ \condindep{X_i}{X_{\mathrm{pa}_{i}}}{X_{1:i-1}},\qquad 2\leq i \leq n.$$ 
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:19:42.477858+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fb36743a986d48ea1f82a7cd4833ee830ce4d4727ec1b18f85d7dd5a558acbfb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9 (Graph-rate separation) of arXiv:2609.03466, 'Least-Favorable Location for Binomial Top-$t$ Selection'] \emph{Center classification and wedge-scale separation.} At $p=\pc$:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
\item a one-edge component has rate $\Jn$;
\item a two-edge connected component has rate $\Kn$;
\item a matching of two edges has rate $2\Jn>\Kn$;
\item every connected component containing at least three edges has rate strictly larger than $\Kn$.
\end{enumerate}
Consequently, for fixed $k,t,\delta$, there is a neighborhood $U$ of $\pc$ and a number $\eta>0$ such that every inclusion--exclusion term in $\mathcal R_n$ has probability at most
\begin{equation}
 C(n+1)^ke^{-n(\Kn+2\eta)},
 \qquad p\in U.

\end{equation}
Its first two derivatives with respect to $p$ are $O(e^{-n(\Kn+\eta)})$, uniformly on $U$. Therefore
\begin{equation}
 \|\mathcal R_n\|_{C^2(U)}=O(e^{-n(\Kn+\eta)}).

\end{equation}

\emph{Local edge-dominance separation.} After shrinking $U$ if necessary, there is also $\gamma>0$ such that every connected two-edge graph and every other non-single-edge graph occurring in \eqref{eq:exact-graph-decomp} satisfies
\begin{equation*}
 \mathcal I_p(G)\ge I_1(p)+\gamma,
 \qquad p\in U.
\end{equation*}
For $j=0,1,2$, the $j$th derivative of the sum of all such terms is bounded by
\begin{equation*}
 C_j n^{C_j}e^{-n(I_1(p)+\gamma)},
 \qquad p\in U.
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.666048+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fb3930e51a159b0f9bff5a65856e02d05a0be059fcbf73d7ff8cb583094ed642","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:restvk of arXiv:2412.09145, 'Asymptotic expansions for normal deviations of random walks conditioned to stay positive'] For every tuple $(j,q,s,\nu,\mu)$ satisfying $s+\nu+2\mu \le r+3$, $q\le 2j$ there exist polynomials $H_\eta$ of degree $\eta$, 
$\eta\le\lfloor \frac{r+3}{2} \rfloor$ such that 
\begin{multline*}
    z^{q-s+\nu}
    \sum_{k=2}^{n-1}
        \frac{
        v_k^{(s+\nu+2\mu)}
        e^{-\frac{z^2}{2(n-k)}}
        }
        {
        (n-k)^{j+\nu+\mu+1/2}
        }
=
    \frac{z^{q-s+\nu}}{n^{j+\nu+\mu+1/2}}
    e^{-\frac{z^2}{2n}}
    \sum_{\eta=0}^{\lfloor \frac{r+3}{2} \rfloor}
        \frac{H_\eta(z^2/n)}{n^{\eta}}
\\
+
    O\left(\frac{1}{(\min(\sqrt{n},1+|z|))^{r+2}}\right).
\end{multline*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.807022+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fb3a4b9d63f79c2ac498e1d95ece6dfbd6e40ef15c6566b5f001d3e6a09f55fa","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma priori-basic-1 of arXiv:2609.01488, 'Stabilization by a background magnetic field: global well-posedness of the full compressib'] Assume $(\rho,\mathbf u,p,\mathbf h)$ is a smooth solution to   the system (equation no-heat-mhd3 of the paper) on $\mathbb T^3\times[0,T]$ for some $T>0$, and
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{2}\underline{\rho}\leq\rho\leq2\bar\rho,\ \ \text{in}\ \mathbb T^3\times[0,T].
\end{equation}
Then there hold,
\begin{equation}
\frac{d}{dt}\mathcal A (t)+\|\sqrt\rho\mathbf u_t\|_0^2+\|\nabla\mathbf u\|_0^2\lesssim\big(1+\|(\mathbf u,p,\mathbf h)\|_2\big)\|(\mathbf u,p,\mathbf h)\|_2^3,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
A_1\big(\|(\mathbf u,p,\mathbf h)\|_0^2+\|\nabla\mathbf u\|_0^2\big)\leq\mathcal A(t)\leq A_2\big(\|(\mathbf u,p,\mathbf h)\|_0^2+\|\nabla\mathbf u\|_0^2\big),
\end{equation}
where the two positive constants $A_1,A_2$ depend only on $\mu,\lambda,\gamma,\bar P,|\mathbf n|,\underline{\rho},\bar\rho,$ and
\begin{align*}\mathcal A(t):=&\mu_3\|(\sqrt{\rho}\mathbf u(\cdot,t),(\gamma\bar P)^{-\frac12} p(\cdot,t),\mathbf h(\cdot,t))\|^2_{0}+\mu\|\nabla\mathbf u(\cdot,t)\|_0^2+(\mu+\lambda)\|\mathrm{div}\mathbf u(\cdot,t)\|_0^2\\
&-2\int \big(p(x,t)+\mathbf h(x,t)\cdot\mathbf n\big)\mathrm{div}\mathbf u(x,t)dx+2\int\mathbf h(x,t)\cdot (\mathbf n\cdot\nabla)\mathbf u(x,t)dx
\end{align*}
with $\mu_3:=\max\big\{(4|\mathbf n|^2+\gamma\bar P+1)\mu^{-1},\frac{4\gamma\bar P}{\mu}+1,\frac{16|\mathbf n|^2}{\mu}+1\big\}.$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:23:37.857506+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fb3a9031e6cf752525dbca278d674b14e3368fb8c0db6a683daebd29e851d280","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 14 (Eigenvalue scale function) of arXiv:2609.03647, 'Extension Problems for the Vladimirov--Taibleson Operator and the Hierarchical Laplacian'] For $B\in\mathcal{B}$, define the scale function
