{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:56:17.357955+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fbe6cf415044bd267bd07b721389a136bbbea6628b4eec374b19c8e0f21ab9bd","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem.dpp of arXiv:2605.14149, 'The mean-field limit of online stochastic vector balancing'] Fix integers $0\leq k_0 < k_1 \leq n$ and $\bm{x} \in {\mathbb R}^n$. Let $(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P})$ be a probability space, and $\mathbb{G} = (\mathcal{G}(k))_{k = k_0,\dots,k_1}$ be a filtration of $\mathcal{F}$. Furthermore, let $(\bm{Z}(k))_{k = k_0+1,\dots,k_1}$ be a sequence of $\mathbb{G}$-adapted, ${\mathbb R}^n$-valued random vectors such that $\bm{Z}(k) \sim \nu_{1/n}^{\otimes n}$ is independent of $\mathcal{G}(k-1)$ for all $k = k_0+1,\dots,k_1$.
    Then, we have
    \begin{align*}
        \mathcal{V}^n(k_0,\bm{x}) =  \inf_{\varepsilon(k_0+1),\dots,\varepsilon(k_1)} {\mathbb E}\bigg[ \mathcal{V}^n\Big(k_1, \bm{x} + \sum_{k = k_0+1}^{k_1} \varepsilon(k) \bm{Z}(k)\Big) \bigg],
    \end{align*}
    where the infimum is taken over all $\mathbb{G}$-adapted $\{\pm 1\}$-valued processes $(\varepsilon(k))_{k = k_0+1,\dots,k_1}$. In particular, if $k_1 = n$, we have
    \begin{align*}
    \mathcal{V}^n(k_0,\bm{x}) = \inf_{\varepsilon(k_0+1),\dots,\varepsilon(n)} {\mathbb E}\,\Big| \bm{x} + \sum_{k = k_0+1}^n \varepsilon(k) \bm{Z}(k) \Big|_{\infty},
    \end{align*}
    and thus, $V^n = \mathcal{V}^n(0,\bm{0})$, where $V^n$ was defined in (equation def.vn.intro of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:49:21.133716+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fbea1dba52d25c5d35900a8c3e27a9182d2ea8722208b592b794f362c87faa4d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 4 of arXiv:2608.26698, 'Mapping class groups of simply connected closed spin 5-manifolds with no 2- and 3-torsion '] Let $g \geq 1$.
        	\begin{itemize}
        		\item[(1)] The abelianization of $\mathrm{MCG}(M_g)$ is: \[H_1(\mathrm{MCG}(M_g)) \cong \begin{cases}
        			{(\mathbb{Z}/2)}^3, & g = 1,2; \\
        			{(\mathbb{Z}/2)}^2, & g = 3; \\
        			\mathbb{Z}/2, & g \geq 4.
        		\end{cases}\]
        		In particular, for $g \geq 4$ the homomorphism $\phi : \mathrm{MCG}(M_g) \rightarrow \mathrm{GL}_g(\mathbb{Z})$ induces an isomorphism $H_1(\mathrm{MCG}(M_g)) \rightarrow H_1(\mathrm{GL}_g(\mathbb{Z}))$.

