{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:47:32.878662+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fc12c45a7fcd1f3e49ed50757c85d7048ed7514ca5821f3aa599e9d3e253abf3","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 9 of arXiv:2609.02464, 'When does propagation of chaos in the critical Curie-Weiss model stop?'] For every $N$ and $k\leq N$, the following is true

\begin{enumerate}
\item If $k$ is even, then $(p_{N,k}(j))_{j=0}^k$ is log-concave, and hence
unimodal, if and only if
\begin{equation}
k\,\mathbb E_{\rho_{N,k}}[\sinh^2X]\le 1.
\end{equation}
If the reverse strict inequality holds, then the midpoint $k/2$ is a strict
local minimum and the distribution is not unimodal.

\item If $k$ is odd, then $(p_{N,k}(j))_{j=0}^k$ is log-concave, and hence
unimodal, if and only if
\begin{equation}
(k-1)\mathbb E_{\widehat\rho_{N,k}}[\sinh^2X]\le 1.
\end{equation}
If the reverse strict inequality holds, then the two midpoints $(k-1)/2$ and $(k+1)/2$ are a
local minimum and the distribution is not unimodal.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:11:57.313542+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fc137b264f5a5edb369d4c9ce5848ba2617aa5a87fe5155ce8a90d8ecabcd54b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 of arXiv:2607.23305, 'On extremes of a random walk with positive drift\\ over an intermediate regularly varying '] Let $\E\xi=-a<0$ and let 
