{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:26.254099+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fcdde6e329d30bd7b92e41d6447f583ef3ddb526043e417b9571295d400b7e59","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Dfn 12 (Hausdorff distance, {\cite[Section 7.3.1) of arXiv:2605.23308, 'Convergence rate estimates for semigroups and heat kernels associated with resistance form'] {BBI01}}]
Let $(Z,d^Z)$ be a metric space and let $\calC_c(Z)$ be the set of all compact subsets in $Z$.
For $A,B\in\calC_c(Z)$, define the Hausdorff distance between the two $d_{\rm{H}}^Z(A,B)$
by setting
 \begin{equation*}
  d^Z_{\mathrm{H}}(A,B):=\inf\{\varepsilon>0:A\subseteq B^{\varepsilon},B\subseteq A^{\varepsilon}\},
 \end{equation*}
 and
 \begin{equation*}
  C^{\varepsilon}:=\{z\in Z:\exists c\in C,\;d^Z(z,c)\leq \varepsilon\},\quad C\subseteq Z.
 \end{equation*}
 Here, we use a convention $\inf\emptyset:=\infty$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:28:33.768727+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fcdf9a2ddd9184fed4a63dbad189c4c65091e5fd43b639c15e5c572767e5d3b8","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem lA1Delta of arXiv:2504.07255, 'A Tail-Respecting Splitting Numerical Scheme\\ for L\'evy-Driven SDEs With Superlinear Dri'] Let $r\in[2,\infty)$. Then, for any $\gamma\in [0,1]$
\begin{equation}\begin{aligned}
\sup_{t\in[t_k,t_{k+1})}\mathbf{E}^{(N)}_{t_1,\dots,t_N}\Big[\|\Delta_t^n\|^r\Big|\mathcal{F}_{t_k}\Big]
\leq_C \|\Delta_{t_k}^n\|^r
&+h_n^{r\gamma +1}(1+\|X_{t_k}^{(n)}\|^{r\gamma\chi+r}) +h_n^{r\gamma }(1 + \|X^n_{t_k}\|^{r\gamma\chi+r})\\
&+h_n  (U_{t_k}^n)^r
+ h_n^{r\gamma+1} (U_{t_k}^n)^{r\gamma\chi}  + h_n
\end{aligned}\end{equation}
for $n$ large enough uniformly over
$N\in\mathbb{N}_0$, partitions $\{t_1,\dots, t_N\}$, and $k=0, \dots, N$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:01:01.725526+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fce029fbd10d2dbedd7272126f80a20407401f7030fc24a73d80796733fa5691","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma expansion-curvature-0 of arXiv:2609.00108, 'The double sphere solution in the liquid drop model'] Let $\Sigma_0^{\varepsilon, +,1}$ be the surface parametrized as in (equation defSigma0ve12 of the paper), with $P$ and $Q$ defined as in (equation defPQ of the paper). Let $h = h(r)$ be a $C^{2, \alpha}$-small normal perturbation of $\Sigma_0^{\varepsilon, +,1}$ as in (equation def-np of the paper). Then: for all $r \in (1,2)$ we have
		$$
		\begin{aligned}
			| {\mathcal Q}_{\Sigma_0^{\varepsilon, +,1}} [h] (r)
			| &\lesssim  (|h| + (r-1) |h'|^2 ) \, (r-1) \, (|h''| + |h'|) \\
			&+ |h|^2 + (r-1) |h'|^2 + (r-1) |h| |h'|;
		\end{aligned}
		$$
		for any $r \in [2, \varepsilon^{-\frac12})$ we have
		$$
		\begin{aligned}
			| {\mathcal Q}_{\Sigma_0^{\varepsilon, +,1}} [h] (r)| &\lesssim  ( \frac{|h|}{1+ r^2} + |h'|^2 )  \, ( |h''| + \frac{|h'|}{1+r} )\\
			&+ \frac{|h|^2}{1+ r^6} + \frac{|h'|^2}{1+ r^2} + \frac{|h| \, |h'|}{1+ r^3} ;
		\end{aligned}
		$$
		and any $r \in (\varepsilon^{-\frac12}, \varepsilon^{-1})$
		$$
		\begin{aligned}
			| {\mathcal Q}_{\Sigma_0^{\varepsilon, +,1}} [h] (r)| &\lesssim  (\varepsilon |h| + (1-\varepsilon^2 r^2)^2 |h'|^2) \, ( (1-\varepsilon^2 r^2) |h''| + \varepsilon^\frac12 (1-\varepsilon^2 r^2) |h'|)\\
			&+ \varepsilon^3 |h|^2 + \varepsilon (1-\varepsilon^2 r^2)^2 + \varepsilon^\frac{3}{2} (1-\varepsilon^2 r^2 ) |h| |h'|. 
		\end{aligned}
		$$
		Here ${\mathcal Q}_{\Sigma_0^{\varepsilon, +,1}}$ is the quadratic term obtained in Proposition (generalH of the paper) for the surface $\Sigma_0^{\varepsilon, +,1}$.
		We also recall that $R=\frac{1}{\varepsilon}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:59.502877+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fce1973ddd598ec7792818bf2fcadc39bc427da44f991b7aeb86a5a9bc129777","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 56 of arXiv:2605.10150, 'Rough path theory and an introduction to rough partial differential equations'] Let $\bfx = (X,\bbx) \in \scrc^{\alpha}([0,T],V)$ be a rough path for some index $\alpha \in (\frac{1}{3},\frac{1}{2})$, let $f_0 \in \Lip([0,T] \times W,W)$ and $f \in C_b^{2\alpha,2}([0,T] \times W, L(V,W))$ be mappings, and let $\xi \in W$ be arbitrary. Then there exists a constant $K > 0$, depending on $\xi$, $\alpha$, $T$, $\mathbf{X}$, $\| f_0 \|_{\Lip}$ and $\| f \|_{C_b^{2\alpha,2}}$, such that for every solution $(Y,Y') \in \scrd_X^{2\alpha}([0,T],W)$ to the RDE \eqref{RDE} with $Y_0 = \xi$ we have
\begin{align*}
| Y_t | \leq K \quad \text{for all $t \in [0,T]$.}
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:23:58.615126+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fce1ff400eac3eb28b6bf13cedf54b4b4b8ade2b75484c1cd29b4f5d8164e085","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 125 (Derivatives of logarithmic density) of arXiv:2609.03101, 'The emergence of the Cauchy horizon from the crease for $3D$ compressible Euler flow'] Let $\angularcoeffinmuweightedspatialcartesian_i^A$ be the $3\times 2$ matrix from  \eqref{E:ANGULARCOEFFINMUWEIGHTEDCARTESIAN}. Then the following identities hold:
\begin{subequations}	
	\begin{align}
		\upmu \Transport \LogDensity & = - X_i \muX v^i - \upmu \angularcoeffinmuweightedspatialcartesian_i^A \Yvf{A} v^i,  \\
		\Lunit \LogDensity & = X_i \Lunit v^i - \angularcoeffinmuweightedspatialcartesian_i^A \Yvf{A} v^i  + \exp(-\LogDensity)\frac{p_{;\Ent}}{\overline{\varrho}} \GradEnt^i X_i \\
		\muX \LogDensity & = - X_i \muX v^i - \upmu X_i \Lunit v^i - \upmu  \exp(-\LogDensity)\frac{p_{;\Ent}}{\overline{\varrho}} \GradEnt^i X_i 
	\end{align}
Additionally, if $W$ is any $\ell_{t,u}$-tangent vectorfield, we have: 
\begin{align}
	W \LogDensity & = - X_i W v^i - W_i \Lunit v^i - \Speed^{-2}\exp(\LogDensity) X^i W^j \upepsilon_{ijk} \vortrenormalized^k - \exp(-\LogDensity)\frac{p_{;\Ent}}{\overline{\varrho}}W_i \GradEnt^i  
\end{align} 
\end{subequations}Consequently, for any $1\le N \le \Ntop$, we have: 
\begin{subequations}
	\begin{align}
		\left| \tander^{[1,N+1]} \LogDensity \right| & \lesssim \left| \tander^{[1,N+1]} \velocityarray\right| +  \left|\tander^{\le N} (\vortrenormalized,\GradEnt)\right| + \fundbootsmall \left| \tander^{[1,N]} \controlvars\right|   \\
		\left| \comdersmall^{[1,N+1];1} \LogDensity \right| & \lesssim \left| \comdersmall^{[1,N+1];1} \velocityarray\right| + \upmu \left| \tander^{[1,N+1]}\velocityarray\right| +  \left|\tander^{\le N} (\vortrenormalized,\GradEnt)\right| + \fundbootsmall \left| \tandersmall^{[1,N]} \badcontrolvars\right|   \\		
		\left| \comdersmall^{[1,N+1]; 2} \LogDensity \right| & \lesssim \left|\comdersmall^{[1,N+1]; 2}\velocityarray\right| + \upmu \left| \comdersmall^{[1,N+1];\le 1} \velocityarray\right| + \upmu  \left|\tander^{\le N} (\vortrenormalized,\GradEnt)\right| + \fundbootsmall \left|\comderdoublesmall^{[1,N];\le 1} \badcontrolvars\right|
	\end{align}
\end{subequations}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:22:20.962566+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fce4b89727d57b53c3aa6ed276ea96370128071eb26cffb1adb837639fd6455d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 15 (Cycles and cliques) of arXiv:2605.28776, 'Limiting distributions of triangle counts in linear preferential attachment models'] Fix an integer $\ell \ge 4$. Let $C_\ell(n)$ and $K_\ell(n)$ denote, respectively, the number of $\ell$-cycles and $\ell$-cliques in the preferential attachment model $\mathrm{PAM}_{n}(m,\delta)$. Then the expectations $\E C_\ell(n)$ and $\E K_\ell(n)$ satisfy the following asymptotic regimes:

\begin{equation*}
    \E C_\ell(n)\cong \begin{cases}
 1 & \text{ if } \delta>0\\
 (\log n)^{\ell} & \text{ if } \delta=0\\
 n^{(1-2\beta)\lfloor \ell/2\rfloor} (\log n)^{\ell-2\lfloor \ell/2\rfloor} & \text{ if } \delta\in(-m,0)
 \end{cases},
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
    \E K_\ell(n)\cong \begin{cases}
 1 & \text{ if } \delta>-\frac{m(\ell-3)}{\ell-2}\\[1em]
 \log n & \text{ if } \delta=-\frac{m(\ell-3)}{\ell-2}\\[1em]
 n^{1-\beta(\ell-1)} & \text{ if } \delta\in\left(-m,-\frac{m(\ell-3)}{\ell-2}\right)
 \end{cases},
\end{equation*}
where $\beta:=(m+\delta)/(2m+\delta)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:25:06.127518+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fce7f9332a79deef21641631c0f0eab9ed231740e1c187c92b610d2925d5576e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 22 (Empirical Fourier CLT) of arXiv:2608.22342, 'Completely Positive Entropy and Fourier Central Limit Theorems for Stationary Random Measu'] There is a $\lambda_d$-conull set $E_\eta\subset\bR^d$ such that, for every
$\xi\in E_\eta$, every $m\geq1$, and every
$h_1,\ldots,h_m\in C_c^\infty(\bR^d;\bC)$,
\begin{equation}

    \bigl(Z_R(\xi;h_1),\ldots,Z_R(\xi;h_m)\bigr)
    \dto \bigl(G_\xi(h_1),\ldots,G_\xi(h_m)\bigr)
    \qquad\text{in }\bC^m,
\end{equation}
where the vector on the right is complex Gaussian with mean zero and
\begin{align}

    \E\!\left[G_\xi(h_a)\overline{G_\xi(h_b)}\right]
    &=s_\eta(\xi)
      \int_{\bR^d}h_a\overline{h_b}\,d\lambda_d,\\

    \E\!\left[G_\xi(h_a)G_\xi(h_b)\right]&=0.
\end{align}
In particular,
\begin{equation}

    Z_R(\xi;h)
    \dto \mathcal{CN}\bigl(0,s_\eta(\xi)\|h\|_2^2\bigr).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:18:13.204834+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fcead4c760c27aee9e5eea9331b00b9f85c503419e740f0cdfd3c5a5a282306b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemm Lemma_YO of arXiv:2506.08202, 'Stochastic dissipative systems in Banach spaces driven by L\'evy noise'] The following statements hold true.
\begin{align*}
\lim_{\delta{\rightarrow} 0}\norm{J_{\delta}(x)-x}_{\mathcal{K}}=0,\quad x\in {\rm Dom}(F).
\end{align*}
For every $0<\delta<|\zeta_F|^{-1}$ ($0<\delta<\infty$ if $\zeta_F=0$), the function $F_\delta-\zeta_F{\operatorname{I}}_{{\mathcal{K}}}$ is dissipative on ${\mathcal{K}}$. Moreover for every $0<\delta<|\zeta_F|^{-1}$ it holds
\begin{align}
{\left\|J_\delta(x)-x\right\|}_{\mathcal{K}}&\leq \delta\left(\|F(x)\|_K+\max\{0,\zeta_F\}\| x\|_{\mathcal{K}}\right),\qquad x\in {\rm Dom}(F);\\
\|F_{\delta}(x)\|_{\mathcal{K}}&\leq (1+\delta \max\{0,\zeta_F\})(\|F(x)\|_{\mathcal{K}}+2 \max\{0,\zeta_F\}{\left\|x\right\|}_{\mathcal{K}}),\qquad x\in {\rm Dom}(F);\\
{\left\|F_\delta(x)-F_\delta(y)\right\|}_K&\leq \left(2\delta^{-1}+ \max\{0,\zeta_F\}\right){\left\|x-y\right\|}_K,\qquad x,y\in{\mathcal{K}}.\\
{\left\|J_\delta(x)-J_\delta(y)\right\|}_{\mathcal{K}} &\leq {\left\|x-y\right\|}_K,\qquad x,y\in{\mathcal{K}}.
\end{align}
For every $x,y\in \mathcal{K}$ and $0<\delta,\tau<|\zeta_F|^{-1}$ we have
\begin{equation}
{\left\|J_\delta(x)-J_\tau(y)\right\|}_{\mathcal{K}}\leq \zeta_F{\left\|x-y\right\|}_{\mathcal{K}}+ (\delta+\tau)({\left\|F(y)\right\|}_{\mathcal{K}}+{\left\|F(x)\right\|}_{\mathcal{K}}+|\zeta_F|{\left\|y\right\|}_{\mathcal{K}}+|\zeta_F|{\left\|x\right\|}_{\mathcal{K}}).
\end{equation} 
If ${\mathcal{K}}$ is a Hilbert space, then for every $x,y\in {\rm Dom}(F)$ there exists $B:=B(\zeta_F)$ such that for every $\delta,\tau>0$ we have
\begin{equation}
\scal{F_{\delta}(x)-F_\tau(y)}{x-y}_{\mathcal{K}}\leq \zeta_F{\left\|x-y\right\|}_{\mathcal{K}}^2+(\delta+\tau)B({\left\|F(x)\right\|}_{\mathcal{K}}^2+{\left\|F(y)\right\|}_{\mathcal{K}}^2+{\left\|x\right\|}_{\mathcal{K}}^2+{\left\|y\right\|}_{\mathcal{K}}^2)
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:15:54.984059+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fcf063381931ec9ad28d1db3031d3b9f53ce15701d9e107a5b306d06aef42494","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition see, e.g., \cite{wijesekera_constructive_1990, simpson_proof_1994} of arXiv:2608.29708, 'Intuitionistic and Constructive Modal Logics for Classical Modal Logicians'] For an f-model $\mathfrak{M} = (W, \leq, \sqsubset, V)$, we define an \emph{intrinsic satisfaction relation} $\Vdash^i_\mathfrak{M}$ on $W \times \mathscr{L}_{\Box\Diamond}$ by:
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item $x \Vdash^i_\mathfrak{M} \psi_1 \to \psi_2 \mathrel{\;\ratio\Longleftrightarrow\;} \forall x' \geq x\, (x' \Vdash^i_\mathfrak{M} \psi_1 \mathrel{\Rightarrow} x' \Vdash^i_\mathfrak{M} \psi_2)$
    \item $x \Vdash^i_\mathfrak{M} \Box \varphi     \mathrel{\;\ratio\Longleftrightarrow\;} \forall x' \geq x\, \forall y' \sqsupset x'\, (y' \Vdash^i_\mathfrak{M} \varphi)$
    \item $x \Vdash^i_\mathfrak{M} \Diamond \varphi \mathrel{\;\ratio\Longleftrightarrow\;} \forall x' \geq x\, \exists y' \sqsupset x'\, (y' \Vdash^i_\mathfrak{M} \varphi)$
    \item The other cases are identical to the classical $\Vdash_\mathfrak{M}$.
  \end{enumerate}
  We write $\mathfrak{M} \vDash^i \varphi$ (``$\varphi$ is \emph{(i-)valid in} $\mathfrak{M}$'') to mean $\forall x \in W\,(x \Vdash^i_\mathfrak{M} \varphi)$,
  and $\mathfrak{F} \vDash^i \varphi$ (``$\varphi$ is \emph{(i-)valid on} $\mathfrak{F}$'') to mean that $\mathfrak{M} \vDash^i \varphi$ for every $\mathfrak{M}$ based on $\mathfrak{F}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.594051+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fcf0f0ef259472dfc59a09cd1f2be61900cd9413d9e166be0dc8c921c5b8a424","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop-4.1 of arXiv:2412.08964, 'Phase transition and critical behavior in hierarchical\\integer-valued Gaussian and Coulom'] Let~$\beta>0$ and assume that~$\{v_k'\}_{k=0}^n$ and~$\{v_k''\}_{k=0}^n$ are bounded uniformly in~$n\ge1$.
Then for all~$n\ge1$ and all $x,y\in\Lambda_n$,
