{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:38:13.637763+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fd7b8f0b45762305622675abf52cfe8ba12b0bed8722accec9a1b16182bfb85b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2609.00687, 'A unification of Euler-Maclaurin and Euler-Boole summation formulas'] Let $q\in\mathbb{N}$, $m\in\mathbb{N}_{0}$ and $a,b\in\mathbb{R}$ with $a<b$. Let $\chi$ be a Dirichlet character modulo $q$ and let $f(t)$ be an $m+1$
times continuously differentiable function on the interval $[a,b]$. Then, for $\lambda\in\mathbb{C}\setminus\{0\}$,
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\sum_{\substack{a<k\leqslant b\\k\in\mathbb{Z}}}\chi(k)\lambda^{-k}f(k)\nonumber\\
&&\qquad=\sum_{k=0}^{m}\frac{(-1)^{k+1}}{(k+1)!}\bigl(\beta_{k+1,\chi^{-}}(\{b\}_{\chi},\lambda)f^{(k)}(b)-\beta_{k+1,\chi^{-}}(\{a\}_{\chi},\lambda)f^{(k)}(a)\bigl)\nonumber\\
&&\qquad\quad+\delta_{1,\lambda^{q}}\beta_{0,\chi^{-}}(\lambda)\int_{a}^{b}f(t)dt\nonumber\\
&&\qquad\quad+\frac{(-1)^{m}}{(m+1)!}\int_{a}^{b}\beta_{m+1,\chi^{-}}(\{t\}_{\chi},\lambda)f^{(m+1)}(t)dt,
\end{eqnarray}
where
\begin{equation*}
\beta_{0,\chi^{-}}(\lambda)=\frac{1}{q}\sum_{r=1}^{q}\chi(-r)\lambda^{r},
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\beta_{m,\chi^{-}}(\{x\}_{\chi},\lambda)=q^{m-1}\sum_{r=1}^{q}\chi(-r)\lambda^{r-q[\frac{x+r}{q}]}\beta_{m}\biggl(\biggl\{\frac{x+r}{q}\biggl\},\lambda^{q}\biggl),
\end{equation*}
in which $\beta_{n}(x,\lambda)$ denotes the Apostol-Bernoulli polynomials defined for $\lambda\in\mathbb{C}\setminus\{0\}$ by the generating function (see, e.g., \cite{apostol2})
\begin{equation}
\frac{te^{xt}}{\lambda e^{t}-1}=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}
\beta_{n}(x,\lambda)\frac{t^{n}}{n!}\quad(|t+\log\lambda|<2\pi).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:28:08.658661+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fd7e378f527c7901b262d5f6e0ca3e25dd35116c728d95a708ddd97d2c426018","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 3 of arXiv:2607.29327, 'Fractal Random Variables\\ Defined by Probability Distributions of Digits\\ of Their $G_2$'] \emph{$G_2$-cylinder} of rank $m$ with base $c_1c_2...c_m$ is the set
