where $C$ depends only on $R$ and $n$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:32:56.078911+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fdbccd3d65cd66cd0ffc1588d24fd2b8863937fe48e7de8e9ed753d529b7a6c6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma le:talagrand of arXiv:2411.12192, 'Sample path properties and small ball probabilities for stochastic fractional diffusion eq'] Let $S$ be a set with the distance $d(s,t)=\mathbb E[|Z(s)-Z(t)|^2]^{1/2}$, where $\{Z(t)\}_{t\in S}$ is a Gaussian process. Let $\mathcal{N}_d(S,\varepsilon)$
denote the smallest number of (open) $d$-balls of radius $\varepsilon$ needed to cover $S$. If there is a decreasing function $\Psi: (0, \delta] \to (0, \infty)$
such that  $N(S,\varepsilon)\le\Psi(\varepsilon)$ for all $\varepsilon \in (0, \delta]$. Assume that for some constant $C_2 \ge C_1>1$  we have $C_1 \Psi(\varepsilon) \le \Psi(\varepsilon/2)\le C_2\Psi(\varepsilon)$
for all $\varepsilon\in (0, \delta]$. Then for all $\varepsilon \in (0, \delta]$,
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb{P}\bigg\{\sup_{s,t\in S}|Z(t)-Z(s)|\le \varepsilon \bigg\}  \ge \exp\left(-\frac{\Psi(\varepsilon)}{K}\right),
  \end{equation*}
where the constant $K$ depends on $C_1$ and $C_2$ only. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:37:51.458299+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fdbdabc7b7d7a5c6f46b054f4125d7c94bee5563d208bcfcc8c776fcbdc931e0","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 37 of arXiv:2604.03777, 'Equilibrium fluctuations for a multi-species particle system with long jumps'] Let $\gamma \in [1, \; 3/2)$ and fix $\lambda$ such that $0 < \lambda < 3-2 \gamma$. For every $n \in \mathbb{N}_+$, let $L_n:=n^{1 - \lambda}$. There exists some $f: \mathbb{N}_+ \mapsto \mathbb{R}$ satisfying $\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} f(n)=0$, such that for any $0 \leq s \leq t \leq T$,
	\begin{align*}
	\forall n \in \mathbb{N}_+, \quad	\mathbb{E}_{\nu_{\rho}} \Bigg[ \Bigg\{  \frac{\Theta(n) K_n}{n \sqrt{n} } \sum_{z=1}^{L_n-1}  za(z) \int_s^t \dd r \sum_{x}   \nabla H ( \tfrac{ \lfloor x - r v_n \rfloor }{n}  )   \Psi_{x,L_n}^{\alpha,\beta}(\eta_r^n)  \Bigg\}^{2} \Bigg] \leq \widehat{C} (t-s)^{2} f(n). 
	\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.676527+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fdc77e3ff18b13b8060defb69ca8676ddef16185ea7d4a459186fd7ae8f69035","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition USMR of arXiv:2605.28194, 'Delayed Blow-up in 3D Fluids via Pseudo-Transport Noise'] Fix $a\in [-1,0)$ and $p\in [2,\infty)$. Let $s,T,\tau$ be defined as above. Then there exists a unique solution to (equation Stokes-v of the paper) on $(s,\tau)$, which satisfies the following estimate: 
		\begin{itemize}
			\item[(1).] If $T<\infty$, then there exists $C_1=C_1(\nu,p,T-s)$ such that
			\begin{align*}
				&\mathbb{E}\bigg[ \sup_{t\in [s,\tau]}\|v^N(t)\|_{X_{tr}}^p\bigg] + \mathbb{E}\bigg[ \int_{s}^{\tau}\|v^N(t)\|_{X_{1}}^p\,{\,\rm d} t\bigg]\\
				&\leq C_1 \mathbb{E}\Big[\|v_s^N\|_{X_{tr}}^p\Big] + C_1\mathbb{E}\bigg[\int_{s}^{\tau}\Big(\|f(t)\|_{X_0}^p + \|g(t)\|_{L^p(\gamma(l^2;X_{1/2})}^p\Big)\,{\,\rm d} t\bigg].
			\end{align*}
			\item[(2).] If $v^N\in L^p(\Omega\times (s,\tau);X_{0})$, then there exists $C_2=C_2(\nu,p)$ such that
			\begin{align*}
				&\mathbb{E}\bigg[ \sup_{t\in [s,\tau]}\|v^N(t)\|_{X_{tr}}^p\bigg] + \mathbb{E}\bigg[ \int_{s}^{\tau}\|v^N(t)\|_{X_{1}}^p\,{\,\rm d} t\bigg]\\
				&\leq  C_1\mathbb{E}\bigg[\int_{s}^{\tau}\Big(\|f(t)\|_{X_0}^p + \|g(t)\|_{L^p(\gamma(l^2;X_{1/2})}^p\Big)\,{\,\rm d} t\bigg]\\ 
				&\quad+C_2 \mathbb{E}\Big[\|v_s^N\|_{X_{tr}}^p\Big] + C_2\mathbb{E}\bigg[ \int_{s}^{\tau}\|v^N(t)\|_{X_{0}}^p\,{\,\rm d} t\bigg].
			\end{align*}
		\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:11:35.816927+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fdc7e2824d08cd3b9fe3380983c736463e92cd01a978138fb5505c932b3a377e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:ratio of arXiv:2607.22220, 'Pathwise uniqueness for degenerate stochastic differential equations with H\"{o}lder conti'] For $r\le s$,
\begin{equation}
\frac{g(x_{\ell}(r))J_{r,s}}{g(x_{\ell}(s))}
=\exp\{-A_{r,s}-D_{r,s}\},
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation*}
A_{r,s}:=\int_r^s\lambda_\ell(u)\,\mathrm d u\ge0,
\quad
D_{r,s}:=\int_r^s
\rho_u q_{\ell\ell}
\left(\frac{x_{\ell}(u) g'(x_{\ell}(u))}{g(x_{\ell}(u))}-1\right)\,\mathrm d u.
\end{equation*}
Moreover,
\begin{equation}
\abs{D_{r,s}}\le C_R(s-r),
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\frac{\left|\widehat Y_\ell(s;r)\right|^2}{d_\ell(s)}
\le C_{T,R}\rho_r^2p(x_{\ell+1}(r)).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:30:28.830809+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fdcd2119be0339a82e22dff5a35102ca6272f5812c6e1a72596aa98f001a2b87","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 of arXiv:2609.02147, '2609.02147'] Let $\lambda\geq 0$, $p>2$ and $H$ be a half-space.  We say that a solution $u$ of \eqref{eqneH}
	\begin{itemize}
		\item 
		is stable if
		\[
		Q_{\lambda,u}(\varphi) \coloneq \int_{\R^N} |\nabla \varphi|^2 + \lambda\varphi^2 - (p-1)\chi_{{H}}|u|^{p-2}\varphi^2 \,dx\ge 0 
		\quad \text{for all $\varphi \in C^1_0(\R^N)$};
		\]
		\item 
		is stable outside a compact set $K$ if $Q_{\lambda, u}(\varphi ) \ge 0$ for any $\varphi \in C_0^1(\R^N \setminus K)$;
		\item 
		has Morse index $m_{\lambda,H}(u)$ equal to $k$ if $k$ is 
		the maximal dimension of a subspace $W \subset C_0^1(\R^N)$ so that $Q_{\lambda,u}(\varphi)< 0$ for any $\varphi \in W \setminus \{0\}$.
	\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:11:44.381103+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fdcdd45a5e1cf4eca72e2f129f2ce9e132c475e61b6f056cc8d66aad3857ba5b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 5 of arXiv:2609.03116, '\bf \boldmath On the reconstruction of $n$-quasigroups of order $4$ and upper bounds on th'] Let $n \geq 2$, and $(A,G'''_n,p)$ be a partial $n$-quasigroup. Then
\begin{enumerate}
\item if $(A,G'''_n,p)$ has more than one extension then all the extensions are cubical;
\item if $(A,G'''_n,p)$ has more than two extensions then it has a bicubical extension.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:37:28.982883+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fdcf86f4e81719f441273e19a199b799244dc818aff6869180d127be41678b3b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 1.2 of arXiv:2412.00708, 'Stochastic PDE approach to fluctuating interfaces'] We have
\begin{align}  
b_G^N(\eta,\varphi) & = \frac{K}{N^d} \sum_{p\in{\mathbb{T}}_N^d} 
\bar c_{p}(\eta)\varphi\left(\tfrac{p}N\right), \\

\Gamma_G^N(\eta,\varphi) & = \frac{K}{N^{2d}} \sum_{p\in{\mathbb{T}}_N^d} 
c_{p}(\eta) \varphi\left(\tfrac{p}N\right)^2.
\end{align}
where
\begin{align}  
\bar c_p(\eta) := c_p(\eta) (1-2\eta_p) = c_p(\eta) \Big(1_{\{\eta_p=0\}}
- 1_{\{\eta_p=1\}}\Big).
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:14:37.619526+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fdcfaac78d3c269d18ff3c4675b7797e78bf4486a4a09f685bf917db279f484f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 26 of arXiv:2608.19065, 'Boundedly exchangeable graphs'] There exists a unique Borel measure $\lambda_g$ on $M$ such that for every chart $(V, \varphi)$ of $M$, with $\varphi = (x^1, \dots, x^d)$, and every $B \in \Bcal(M)$ with $B \subseteq V$,
    \begin{equation} 
        \lambda_g(B) = \int_{\varphi(B)} \varphi_{\ast}\sqrt{\abs{G}} \; d\lambda_d,
    \end{equation}
    where $G \coloneqq \det[g_{jk}]$ is the determinant of the metric components $g_{jk} \coloneqq g\!\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial x^j}, \frac{\partial}{\partial x^k}\right)$ in the chart $(V, \varphi)$, and $\varphi_{\ast}\sqrt{\abs{G}} = \sqrt{\abs{G}} \circ \varphi^{-1}$ denotes the pushforward of $\sqrt{\abs{G}}$ on $V$ along $\varphi$. Moreover, $\lambda_g$ is diffuse, $\sigma$-finite, and satisfies $\lambda_g(B) < \infty$ for every $B \in \widehat{\Bcal}(M)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:46:25.365526+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fdd3fe469b3a9e3d37a646650617b0d4de3b3bd3d9845ea1acc535cc78620e7b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop compatibility of arXiv:2604.14122, 'The scaling limit of random walk and\\ the intrinsic metric on planar critical percolation'] Let $V,V'\in\mathfrak{C}$, $V\subset V'$, and $x,y\in\mathcal{X}\cap\overline{V}$ such that for every $u\in V'\setminus V$ there is a point $z\in\overline{V}$ that separates $u$ from $x,y$ in $\mathcal{X}\cap\overline{V'}$. Then
\begin{equation}
    \mathfrak{d}_{V'}(x,y)=\mathfrak{d}_V(x,y).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:09:49.801203+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fdd68b72cf64a8c3b715f4bbad57a4b56eb34864b20d4a090f058cc739188352","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 10 (Cf. Theorem 4.6, \cite{ab0}) of arXiv:2609.03070, 'Variants of order semicontinuity in Banach lattices'] The following conditions are equivalent:
\item[(i)] $F$ is semicontinuous;
\item[(ii)] $\Ba_{F}$ is order closed;
\item[(ii')] $\Ba_{F}$ is uo closed;
\item[(iii)] If $f_{p}\too f$, then $\|f\|\le\liminf\|f_{p}\|$;
\item[(iii')] If $f_{p}\touo f$, then $\|f\|\le\liminf\|f_{p}\|$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:23.330277+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fdd889537ebfbe3e3632936db34ac115db2ced47417775ca69bf9e3fc1b71e04","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 5 of arXiv:2506.22020, 'Norm-dependent Lamperti-type MAP representations of stable processes and Brownian motions '] Let $(\xi,\Xi)=(\xi_t,\Xi_t)_{t\geq0}$ be the underlying MAP of $Z$ from (\ref{item_a}) with respect to $\|\cdot\|_1$. The killing rate of $(\xi,\Xi)$ is given by
	\begin{equation}
		q(\boldsymbol{x})=\mathbbm{1}_{(0,\infty)^d}(\boldsymbol{x})\frac{1}{\alpha}\sum_{k=1}^d \frac{\Gamma(1+\alpha)\sin(\pi\alpha(1-\rho_k))}{\pi} \Big(\frac{x_k}{\|\boldsymbol{x}\|_1}\Big)^{-\alpha},\quad \boldsymbol{x}=(x_1,\ldots,x_d)\in(0,\infty)^d,

	\end{equation}
where $\rho_k\coloneqq\mathbb{P}(X^{(k)}_1>0)$ is the positivity parameter of $X^{(k)}$ from the description in  (\ref{item_a}). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:38:36.831497+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fdddb7b00d14434d30b99584753d9a264b8d8563114ed9b86c3d54ed367747f9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 0108390 of arXiv:2606.29143, 'Comonotonic and moment matching approximations for sums of lognormal random variables'] Let $A = (a_1, ..., a_m)^{'}$ and $Q = (q_1, ..., q_m)^{'}$ be two numerical vectors with $\min(a_1, ..., a_m) \geq 0, \max(a_1, ..., a_m) > 0$ and $0 = q_0 < q_1 < q_2 < ... < q_m = 1$. Then, for any continuous random variable $X$ the stop-loss premiums of the step-weighted distribution associated with the numerical vectors $A$ and $Q$ are given by  
\begin{align*}
E[(X_{A, Q} - d)_{+}] &= {a}^{-1}\Big[a_lE[(X - d)_{+}] + d\Big(a_l(1 - q_{l}) - \sum^{m - 1}_{k = l}a_{k + 1}(q_{k + 1} - q_{k})\Big)  \\ 
&\quad + \sum^{m - 1}_{k = l}(a_{k + 1} - a_{k})(1 - q_k)\text{TVaR}_{q_k}[X]\Big], \,\, d \in (F^{-1+}_{X_{A, Q}}(0), F^{-1}_{X_{A, Q}}(1))
\end{align*}
with $F_X(d) \in (q_{l - 1}, q_l]$ for some $l \in \{1, ..., m\}$ and $\sum_{k = m}^{m - 1} = 0$ by convention.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:19:46.237907+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fddeec1afd138b1ce8945c9044f60d592df909148b2765da0db08e741ae128f1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 113 of arXiv:2507.08909, 'Annealed almost periodic entropy'] If $\phi:\G\to\rmM_k$ is positive definite, then $\hann(\phi)$ is equal to
	\begin{multline}
	\rmH_\phi(B_0) - \frac{1}{2}\sum_{n=0}^\infty \sum_{s_{n+1}\cdots s_1 \in S_{n+1}}\big(\rmH_\phi(s_n\cdots s_1\,|\,P(s_n\cdots s_1)) \\ \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad - \rmH_\phi(s_{n+1}\cdots s_1\mid P(s_{n+1}\cdots s_1))\big).
	\end{multline} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:47:01.662777+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fde035618cd3f11128162e5991c7d150df406ac1b6bcea0e770fbf28c07f26f9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 3 (\cite{HJPS2}) of arXiv:2604.23656, 'Doubly Reflected Backward SDEs Driven by $G$-Brownian Motion with Quadratic Generator'] If $\eta\in H_G^{\alpha}(0,T)$ with $\alpha\geq 1$ and $p\in(0,\alpha]$, then we have

