{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:53:39.112259+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fe0ed2ec586525b2f71dccb6f8f95673a3fd93a8b8991c9d3cbba4449f7f41c7","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 1 (Contraction-specific second-moment refinement) of arXiv:2608.26877, 'When Is the Sharp Covariance Envelope Tight? Feature-Only Geometry for Volume-Sampled Leas'] Assume that $X$ has full column rank and that
$L^*>0$ and $d<s<m$.  Let $A,z,B$ and $\mathbb P_B$ be as in
\eqref{eq:residual-augmented-whitening}--\eqref{eq:residual-augmented-law},
and write
\begin{equation}
  \Omega_B=\{S\subseteq[m]: |S|=s,\ 
  \det(B_S^\top B_S)>0\}.

\end{equation}
The law $\mathbb P_B$ is analysis-only, and every selected inverse below is
restricted to $\Omega_B$.  Define
\begin{equation}
  H_s=\mathbb E_BK_S,\qquad
  \Delta_S=K_S-H_s,\qquad
  V_s=\mathbb E_B[\Delta_S^2],\qquad
  \mathcal Q_s=H_s^{-1}V_sH_s^{-1}.

\end{equation}
Then $0\prec K_S\preceq I_d$ on $\Omega_B$, and
\begin{equation}
  \mathbb E_B[K_S^{-1}]-H_s^{-1}
  =\mathcal Q_s+H_s^{-1}\mathbb E_B\!\left[
    \Delta_S(K_S^{-1}-I_d)\Delta_S
  \right]H_s^{-1}
  \succeq\mathcal Q_s\succeq0.

\end{equation}
The coefficient of $V_s$ is one: this is an exact ordered inverse remainder,
not a Taylor approximation.  If $X$ is in row general position, the exact
covariance transform gives
\begin{equation}
  \overline M_s\preceq
  L^*\left[I_d-\beta H_s^{-1}-\beta\mathcal Q_s\right]
  \preceq L^*\left[I_d-\beta H_s^{-1}\right].

\end{equation}

The correction is computable from singleton and pair exclusions.  Put
$T=S^c$, $k=m-s$, $q=m-d-1$, $D_i=a_ia_i^\top$, and retain
$h_i=\lVert b_i\rVert_2^2$.  The singleton exclusion probability is
\begin{align}
  \pi_i&=\mathbb P_B(i\in T)=\frac{k}{q}(1-h_i),

\end{align}
and, for $q\ge2$, the pair exclusion probability is
\begin{align}
  \pi_{ij}&=\mathbb P_B(i,j\in T)
  =\frac{k(k-1)}{q(q-1)}
  \left[(1-h_i)(1-h_j)-(b_i^\top b_j)^2\right].

\end{align}
When $m=d+2$, the strict domain forces $q=k=1$; in that branch set
$\pi_{ij}=0$ rather than evaluating
\eqref{eq:second-moment-pair-exclusion}.  In either branch,
\begin{equation}
  V_s=\sum_i\pi_i(1-\pi_i)D_i^2
  +\sum_{i<j}(\pi_{ij}-\pi_i\pi_j)
    (D_iD_j+D_jD_i).

\end{equation}

For every full-column-rank design in the same strict domain, without a row
general position assumption, define
\begin{equation}
  F_s=\mathbb E_BG_S,\qquad
  W_s=\mathbb E_B[(G_S-F_s)G^{-1}(G_S-F_s)]\succeq0.

\end{equation}
For the ordinary-law supported, full-fit-centered second moment $M_s$ in
\eqref{eq:residual-augmented-centered-second-moment}, only the one-sided
hierarchy
\begin{equation}
  M_s\preceq L^*\left[G^{-1}-\beta F_s^{-1}
    -\beta F_s^{-1}W_sF_s^{-1}\right]
  \preceq L^*\left[G^{-1}-\beta F_s^{-1}\right]

\end{equation}
is asserted.  Outside the row-general-position interior this is not an exact
inverse-moment covariance identity or an exact covariance-gap decomposition.
The proposition is not invoked at the existing zero branches $L^*=0$,
$s=m$, or $m=d$, nor at $s=d<m$; when $m=d+1$ the strict domain is empty.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:15:08.936798+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fe0f20f1a2b591ea235034f126ba576cb3bc5c0c8c461e578d964d6908270298","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Definition_block_process of arXiv:2411.10829, 'Airy$_\beta$ line ensemble and its Laplace transform'] Take $m\in \mathbb N$, $\vec \mathbf k=(\mathbf k_1,\ldots, \mathbf k_m)\in \mathbb R_+^m$, and denote $\mathbf Q_\ell=\sum_{\ell'=1}^\ell \mathbf k_{\ell'}$ for each $\ell \in \llbracket 0, m\rrbracket$.
A \emph{block process with times $\vec \mathbf k$}
is a family $\vec \mathbf p=\{\mathbf p_j\}_{j=1}^{m+\mathbf u}$, where  $\mathbf u\in \mathbb Z_{\ge 0}$, and each $\mathbf p_j:[0, \mathbf Q_m]\to \mathbb R_{\ge 0}$ is a function satisfying the following:
\begin{itemize}
    \item for each $\ell \in \llbracket 1, m\rrbracket$, $\mathbf p_\ell\in \mathfrak B([0,\mathbf Q_{m}])$, and $\mathbf p_\ell= 0$ on $[\mathbf Q_{\ell-1}, \mathbf Q_m]$;
    \item for each $j\in \llbracket m+1, m+\mathbf u\rrbracket$, $\mathbf p_j \in \mathfrak B([0,\mathbf Q_m])$ and $\mathbf p_j$ is not identical zero.
    \item for each $j\in \llbracket 1, m+\mathbf u\rrbracket$, $\ell \in \llbracket 1, m\rrbracket$, $\mathbf p_j$ is left-continuous at $x=\mathbf Q_{\ell-1}$ unless $j=\ell$.
    \item regularity condition spelled in Definition (defn:blp of the paper) below.\footnote{Here is some intuition for the definition of a block process. It corresponds to the degrees of polynomials in Dunkl operators expansion, as shown later in (sec:gme of the paper). The values of $\mathbf p_\ell$ at points $[0,\mathbf Q_{\ell-1}]$ for $\ell \in \llbracket 1, m\rrbracket$ relate to the degrees of variables $x_i$ with indices matching one of the Dunkl operators $\mathfrak D_i^N$ (to be introduced in (sec:dunkl of the paper)) we are expanding, and $\mathbf p_j$ for $j\in\llbracket m+1, m+\mathbf u\rrbracket$ are the degrees of the other variables. In particular, the variable corresponding to the first operator would have zero degree after $\mathbf Q_0=0$, so $\mathbf p_1$ is identical zero.}
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

