for all $|x|\geq R_2+2$, where $I_j\in{\mathcal J}$ is given by Theorem \ref{prop-alpha-decay}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:46:36.172063+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ff6bfc03e9c7c0bb6ce9377b45125af999edd0ebb9dec62c80d43fd4ea3ea81b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2609.02848, 'The universal moduli space of non-degenerate $\Z/2\Z$~harmonic spinors'] Let $({\mathbf{p}},[{\mathfrak l}]) \in \mathbf{Dir} \times \mathbf{Ram}$ and $\phi,\psi \in \Gamma\paren{X{\backslash} \mathrm{Br}({\mathfrak l}), S\otimes {\mathfrak l}}$.
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item
    The \defined{stress-energy tensor}
    $T_{\phi,\psi} \in \Gamma\paren{X{\backslash} Z,\Hom(S^2TX,\mathbb{R})}$ is defined by
    \begin{equation*}
      T_{\phi,\psi} (v,w)
      \coloneq
      -\frac18\paren{\Inner{\gamma(v)\nabla_w\phi,\psi} + \Inner{\gamma(w)\nabla_v\phi,\psi}
      + \Inner{\gamma(v)\nabla_w\psi,\phi} + \Inner{\gamma(w)\nabla_v\psi,\phi}}.
    \end{equation*}
  \item  
    Define
    $\mu(\phi,\psi) \in \Omega^1\paren{X{\backslash} Z,{\mathfrak o}_{\mathrm{C\ell}}(S)}$
    by
    \begin{equation*}
      \mu(\phi,\psi)
      \coloneq
      \frac12\bar\gamma^*\paren*{\phi\Inner{\psi,-} + \psi\Inner{\phi,-}}.
    \end{equation*}
    with $\bar\gamma^*$ denoting the adjoint of the linear map $\bar \gamma \mskip0.5mu\colon\thinspace T^*X \otimes {\mathfrak o}_{\mathrm{C\ell}}(S) \to \Sym(S)$ defined by $\bar\gamma(\alpha\otimes \xi) \coloneq \gamma(\alpha^\sharp)\xi$.
    \qedhere
  \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:35:10.788284+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ff6dbb4bd5d002012025d74c73b85dc70a24ad9b1534f20eb06f2d0800c64c26","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 23 of arXiv:2604.00630, 'The contact process on a bipartite spatial network'] There exists~$C > 0$ such that the following holds for~$\lambda$ small enough.
	Let~$k,\ell \in \mathbb N$ with~$k \ge 2$ and~$\ell \ge k-1$. Let~$Q(k,\ell)$ be the probability that there is an infection path in~$\mathcal G$ started at the root, having ordered trace~$\Gamma$ satisfying the following properties:
	\begin{itemize}
		\item $\Gamma$ has length~$\ell$ and visits~$k$ distinct vertices;
		\item the last vertex of~$\Gamma$ is distinct from all other vertices;
		\item all vertices of~$\Gamma$ are blue.
	\end{itemize}

	Then, taking~$M(\lambda,k,\ell)$ as in~\eqref{eq_def_of_M_f},
\begin{equation*}
	Q(k,\ell) \le \begin{cases}
		CM(\lambda,k,\ell)\cdot \lambda^{1+a}&\text{if $\ell$ is even};\\
		CM(\lambda,k,\ell)\cdot \lambda&\text{if $\ell$ is odd}.
	\end{cases}
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:50:27.007473+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ff6f4ae8daa1bef37cbc0abe3ed36103eb2584d04ba61544d900ca0189975830","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thmx leA of arXiv:2505.13382, 'The localization transition for the directed polymer in a random environment is smooth'] The following limits are  well defined and coincide almost-surely
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{f}(\beta):=\lim_{n\to \infty}  \frac{1}{n} {\ensuremath{\mathbb E}} \left[ \log W^{\beta}_n\right]= \lim_{n\to \infty}  \frac{1}{n} \log W^{\beta}_n.
 \end{equation}
The function $\mathfrak{f}(\beta)$ is referred to as the free energy of the directed polymer, it is continous and nonincreasing in $\beta$. Thus
there exists $\beta_c\in [0,\infty]$ such that
$$ \beta>\beta_c \quad \Leftrightarrow  \quad \mathfrak{f}(\beta)<0.$$
Furthermore the following holds:
\begin{itemize}
 \item [(i)] The function $\mathfrak{f}(\beta)+\lambda(\beta)=\lim_{n\to \infty} (1 /n)\log Z^{\beta,\omega}_n$ is convex in $\beta$.
 \item [(ii)] If $d=1,2$ we have $\beta_c=0$, while if $d\ge 3$ we have $\beta_c>0$.
\item [(iii)] When $d\ge 3$ we have $\beta_c\in (0,\infty)$  if ${\ensuremath{\mathbb P}} \left( \omega_{1,0}=\mathrm{ess\, sup}  \ \omega_{1,0}\right)<\frac{1}{2d}$.
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:31:45.276408+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ff717e5d398a6b8a928646b465871b993c3e09bfeb41b3db9ce9351b2a5b58f0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem theorem3.14goods of arXiv:2412.06246, 'The smallest singular value for rectangular random matrices with Lévy entries'] [[brailovskaya2024universality]] There exists a universal constant $C>0$ such that for any $t>0$, 
